const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Britain. The garden of Cymbeline's palace.","id":0},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["You do not meet a man but frowns; our bloods","No more obey the heavens than our courtiers","Still seem as does the King's."],"id":1,"m":"Every man you meet is frowning. Our moods follow the King's as closely as the courtiers' faces do."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["But what's the matter?"],"id":2,"m":"But what's wrong?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["His daughter, and the heir of's kingdom, whom","He purpos'd to his wife's sole son—a widow","That late he married—hath referr'd herself","Unto a poor but worthy gentleman. She's wedded;","Her husband banish'd; she imprison'd. All","Is outward sorrow, though I think the King","Be touch'd at very heart."],"id":3,"m":"His daughter, heir to his kingdom, whom he meant to marry to his new wife's only son (the Queen is a widow he married recently), has given herself to a poor but worthy gentleman. She's married; her husband is banished, and she's a prisoner. Everyone looks sad, though I think only the King is truly hurt to the heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["None but the King?"],"id":4,"m":"Only the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["He that hath lost her too. So is the Queen,","That most desir'd the match. But not a courtier,","Although they wear their faces to the bent","Of the King's looks, hath a heart that is not","Glad at the thing they scowl at."],"id":5,"m":"And the man who lost her too, and the Queen, who wanted the match most. But no courtier, however much they shape their faces to match the King's looks, isn't secretly glad about what they pretend to scowl at."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["And why so?"],"id":6,"m":"Why is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["He that hath miss'd the Princess is a thing","Too bad for bad report; and he that hath her—","I mean that married her, alack, good man!","And therefore banish'd—is a creature such","As, to seek through the regions of the earth","For one his like, there would be something failing","In him that should compare. I do not think","So fair an outward and such stuff within","Endows a man but he."],"id":7,"m":"The man who failed to win the Princess is too bad to describe, and the man who has her (I mean the one who married her, poor good man, and was banished for it) is someone without equal: search the whole earth for his match and any rival would fall short. I don't think any man but he combines such good looks with such inner worth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["You speak him far."],"id":8,"m":"You praise him highly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I do extend him, sir, within himself;","Crush him together rather than unfold","His measure duly."],"id":9,"m":"I'm only stretching him to his true size, sir. If anything I'm shrinking him rather than showing his full measure."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["What's his name and birth?"],"id":10,"m":"What's his name and family?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["I cannot delve him to the root; his father","Was call'd Sicilius, who did join his honour","Against the Romans with Cassibelan,","But had his titles by Tenantius, whom","He serv'd with glory and admir'd success,","So gain'd the sur-addition Leonatus;","And had, besides this gentleman in question,","Two other sons, who, in the wars o' th' time,","Died with their swords in hand; for which their father,","Then old and fond of issue, took such sorrow","That he quit being; and his gentle lady,","Big of this gentleman, our theme, deceas'd","As he was born. The King he takes the babe","To his protection, calls him Posthumus Leonatus,","Breeds him and makes him of his bed-chamber,","Puts to him all the learnings that his time","Could make him the receiver of; which he took,","As we do air, fast as 'twas minist'red,","And in's spring became a harvest, liv'd in court—","Which rare it is to do—most prais'd, most lov'd,","A sample to the youngest; to th' more mature","A glass that feated them; and to the graver","A child that guided dotards. To his mistress,","For whom he now is banish'd—her own price","Proclaims how she esteem'd him and his virtue;","By her election may be truly read","What kind of man he is."],"id":11,"m":"I can't trace his roots far. His father was Sicilius, who fought for honour alongside Cassibelan against the Romans but got his titles from Tenantius, whom he served with glory and great success, and so earned the extra name Leonatus. Besides this gentleman, he had two other sons, who died sword in hand in the wars of the time. Their father, old and devoted to his children, grieved so much that he died, and his gentle wife, pregnant with this gentleman, died as he was born. The King took the baby under his protection, named him Posthumus Leonatus, brought him up, made him a gentleman of his bedchamber, and gave him every kind of learning his age could offer. He took it in as fast as we breathe air and was ripe for harvest in his youth. He lived at court, which is rare, and was praised and loved by all: a model to the young, a mirror for the mature to dress themselves by, and to the old, a child who guided their foolish age. As for his lady, for whom he is now banished, her own worth shows how highly she valued him and his virtue. You can read what kind of man he is from her choice."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I honour him","Even out of your report. But pray you tell me,","Is she sole child to th' King?"],"id":12,"m":"I honour him just from your account. But tell me, is she the King's only child?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["His only child.","He had two sons—if this be worth your hearing,","Mark it—the eldest of them at three years old,","I' th' swathing clothes the other, from their nursery","Were stol'n; and to this hour no guess in knowledge","Which way they went."],"id":13,"m":"His only child now. He had two sons (this is worth hearing, mark it): the older at three years old, the other still in baby clothes, were stolen from their nursery, and to this day nobody knows which way they went."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["How long is this ago?"],"id":14,"m":"How long ago was this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Some twenty years."],"id":15,"m":"Some twenty years."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["That a king's children should be so convey'd,","So slackly guarded, and the search so slow","That could not trace them!"],"id":16,"m":"That a king's children could be stolen like that, so carelessly guarded, and the search so slow that it couldn't trace them!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Howsoe'er 'tis strange,","Or that the negligence may well be laugh'd at,","Yet is it true, sir."],"id":17,"m":"However strange it is, and however laughable the negligence, it's true, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["I do well believe you."],"id":18,"m":"I believe you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["We must forbear; here comes the gentleman,","The Queen, and Princess."],"id":19,"m":"We must stop talking. Here comes the gentleman, with the Queen and the Princess."},{"t":"sd","id":20,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"sd","id":21,"text":"Enter the Queen, Posthumus, and Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["No, be assur'd you shall not find me, daughter,","After the slander of most stepmothers,","Evil-ey'd unto you. You're my prisoner, but","Your gaoler shall deliver you the keys","That lock up your restraint. For you, Posthumus,","So soon as I can win th' offended King,","I will be known your advocate. Marry, yet","The fire of rage is in him, and 'twere good","You lean'd unto his sentence with what patience","Your wisdom may inform you."],"id":22,"m":"No, daughter, you can be sure you won't find me treating you with the evil eye that stepmothers are famous for. You're my prisoner, but your jailer will hand you the keys that lock you up. As for you, Posthumus, as soon as I can soften the angry King, I'll be known as your champion. But right now he's burning with rage, and you'd do well to accept his sentence with as much patience as your good sense allows."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Please your Highness,","I will from hence to-day."],"id":23,"m":"If it please your Highness, I'll leave today."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["You know the peril.","I'll fetch a turn about the garden, pitying","The pangs of barr'd affections, though the King","Hath charg'd you should not speak together."],"id":24,"m":"You know the danger. I'll take a walk round the garden, feeling for the pain of forbidden love, even though the King has ordered that you mustn't talk together."},{"t":"sd","id":25,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["O dissembling courtesy! How fine this tyrant","Can tickle where she wounds! My dearest husband,","I something fear my father's wrath, but nothing—","Always reserv'd my holy duty—what","His rage can do on me. You must be gone;","And I shall here abide the hourly shot","Of angry eyes, not comforted to live","But that there is this jewel in the world","That I may see again."],"id":26,"m":"Oh, false kindness! How sweetly this tyrant can tickle while she wounds! My dearest husband, I'm a little afraid of my father's anger, but (saving the duty I owe him) I'm not afraid of anything his rage can do to me. You must go, and I must stay here and endure the constant fire of angry looks, with no comfort in living except that this jewel, you, is somewhere in the world for me to see again."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["My queen! my mistress!","O lady, weep no more, lest I give cause","To be suspected of more tenderness","Than doth become a man. I will remain","The loyal'st husband that did e'er plight troth;","My residence in Rome at one Philario's,","Who to my father was a friend, to me","Known but by letter; thither write, my queen,","And with mine eyes I'll drink the words you send,","Though ink be made of gall."],"id":27,"m":"My queen, my lady! Don't cry any more, or I'll show more tenderness than a man should. I'll stay the most loyal husband who ever made a vow. I'll be living in Rome at the house of a man called Philario, a friend of my father's whom I know only by letter. Write to me there, my queen, and I'll drink in your words with my eyes, even if the ink is bitter gall."},{"t":"sd","id":28,"text":"Re-enter Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Be brief, I pray you.","If the King come, I shall incur I know not","How much of his displeasure. [Aside] Yet I'll move him","To walk this way. I never do him wrong","But he does buy my injuries, to be friends;","Pays dear for my offences."],"id":29,"m":"Be quick, please. If the King comes I'll be in more trouble than I can say. (Aside) Still, I'll get him to walk this way. Whenever I wrong him, he pays for my injuries to make friends again; he pays dearly for my offences."},{"t":"sd","id":30,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Should we be taking leave","As long a term as yet we have to live,","The loathness to depart would grow. Adieu!"],"id":31,"m":"If we spent the rest of our lives saying goodbye, the pain of parting would only grow. Goodbye!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Nay, stay a little.","Were you but riding forth to air yourself,","Such parting were too petty. Look here, love:","This diamond was my mother's; take it, heart;","But keep it till you woo another wife,","When Imogen is dead."],"id":32,"m":"No, wait a moment. If you were only riding out for some air, a goodbye like this would be too small. Look, love: this diamond was my mother's. Take it, my heart, but keep it until you court another wife, after Imogen is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["How, how? Another?","You gentle gods, give me but this I have,","And sear up my embracements from a next","With bonds of death! Remain, remain thou here"],"id":33,"m":"What? Another wife? Kind gods, let me keep the one I have, and seal me off from embracing anyone else with the bonds of death! Stay here, ring,"},{"t":"sd","id":34,"text":"Puts on the ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","cont":1,"lines":["While sense can keep it on. And, sweetest, fairest,","As I my poor self did exchange for you,","To your so infinite loss, so in our trifles","I still win of you. For my sake wear this;","It is a manacle of love; I'll place it","Upon this fairest prisoner. [Puts a bracelet on her arm]"],"id":35,"m":"as long as I'm alive to wear you. And, sweetest and fairest, just as I swapped my poor self for you, to your enormous loss, I go on winning in our little exchanges too. Wear this for my sake: it's a handcuff of love, and I'll put it on this lovely prisoner. (He puts a bracelet on her arm.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["O the gods!","When shall we see again?"],"id":36,"m":"Oh, gods! When will we see each other again?"},{"t":"sd","id":37,"text":"Enter Cymbeline and Lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Alack, the King!"],"id":38,"m":"Oh no, the King!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Thou basest thing, avoid; hence from my sight","If after this command thou fraught the court","With thy unworthiness, thou diest. Away!","Thou'rt poison to my blood."],"id":39,"m":"You lowest of creatures, get out of my sight! If after this order you burden the court with your worthless presence, you die. Go! You're poison in my blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["The gods protect you,","And bless the good remainders of the court!","I am gone."],"id":40,"m":"May the gods protect you and bless the good people left at court! I'm going."},{"t":"sd","id":41,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["There cannot be a pinch in death","More sharp than this is."],"id":42,"m":"Death can't hold a sharper pain than this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["O disloyal thing,","That shouldst repair my youth, thou heap'st","A year's age on me!"],"id":43,"m":"You disloyal thing, who should have made me young again, you're piling years of age on me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I beseech you, sir,","Harm not yourself with your vexation.","I am senseless of your wrath; a touch more rare","Subdues all pangs, all fears."],"id":44,"m":"I beg you, sir, don't harm yourself with this anger. I can't feel your rage; a rarer pain overpowers every pain and every fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Past grace? obedience?"],"id":45,"m":"Beyond grace? Beyond obedience?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Past hope, and in despair; that way past grace."],"id":46,"m":"Beyond hope, and in despair. In that sense, beyond grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["That mightst have had the sole son of my queen!"],"id":47,"m":"When you could have had my queen's only son!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["O blessed that I might not! I chose an eagle,","And did avoid a puttock."],"id":48,"m":"Blessed am I that I didn't! I chose an eagle and avoided a buzzard."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Thou took'st a beggar, wouldst have made my throne","A seat for baseness."],"id":49,"m":"You took a beggar, and would have made my throne a seat for low birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["No; I rather added","A lustre to it."],"id":50,"m":"No, I added a shine to it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["O thou vile one!"],"id":51,"m":"You vile girl!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Sir,","It is your fault that I have lov'd Posthumus.","You bred him as my playfellow, and he is","A man worth any woman; overbuys me","Almost the sum he pays."],"id":52,"m":"Sir, it's your fault that I've loved Posthumus. You brought him up as my playmate, and he is a man worth any woman. He's worth far more than I am; he overpays for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["What, art thou mad?"],"id":53,"m":"What, are you mad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Almost, sir. Heaven restore me! Would I were","A neat-herd's daughter, and my Leonatus","Our neighbour shepherd's son!"],"id":54,"m":"Nearly, sir. Heaven heal me! I wish I were a cowherd's daughter and my Leonatus our neighbour shepherd's son!"},{"t":"sd","id":55,"text":"Re-enter Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Thou foolish thing!","[To the Queen] They were again together. You have done","Not after our command. Away with her,","And pen her up."],"id":56,"m":"You foolish girl! (To the Queen) They've been together again. You didn't do as I ordered. Take her away and lock her up."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Beseech your patience.—Peace,","Dear lady daughter, peace!—Sweet sovereign,","Leave us to ourselves, and make yourself some comfort","Out of your best advice."],"id":57,"m":"Please be patient. (To Imogen) Quiet, dear daughter, quiet! (To Cymbeline) Sweet king, leave us alone and find some comfort in your own best judgment."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Nay, let her languish","A drop of blood a day and, being aged,","Die of this folly."],"id":58,"m":"No, let her waste away a drop of blood a day, and die of this folly when she's old."},{"t":"sd","id":59,"text":"Exit, with Lords."},{"t":"sd","id":60,"text":"Enter Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Fie! you must give way.","Here is your servant. How now, sir! What news?"],"id":61,"m":"Come, you must give in. Here's your servant. Well, sir, what news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["My lord your son drew on my master."],"id":62,"m":"My lord your son drew his sword on my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Ha!","No harm, I trust, is done?"],"id":63,"m":"What! No harm done, I hope?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["There might have been,","But that my master rather play'd than fought,","And had no help of anger; they were parted","By gentlemen at hand."],"id":64,"m":"There could have been, except my master was playing rather than fighting, and wasn't driven by anger. Some gentlemen nearby separated them."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["I am very glad on't."],"id":65,"m":"I'm very glad of that."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Your son's my father's friend; he takes his part","To draw upon an exile! O brave sir!","I would they were in Afric both together;","Myself by with a needle, that I might prick","The goer-back. Why came you from your master?"],"id":66,"m":"Your son is my father's friend; he takes his side by drawing on an exile! What a hero! I wish they were both in Africa together, with me standing by with a needle to stab whichever one ran away. Why did you leave your master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["On his command. He would not suffer me","To bring him to the haven; left these notes","Of what commands I should be subject to,","When't pleas'd you to employ me."],"id":67,"m":"He ordered me to. He wouldn't let me go with him to the harbour, and he left these written instructions for how I should serve you whenever you need me."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["This hath been","Your faithful servant. I dare lay mine honour","He will remain so."],"id":68,"m":"He has been a loyal servant to you. I'd stake my honour that he'll stay one."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["I humbly thank your Highness."],"id":69,"m":"I humbly thank your Highness."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Pray walk awhile."],"id":70,"m":"Please walk with me a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["About some half-hour hence,","Pray you speak with me. You shall at least","Go see my lord aboard. For this time leave me."],"id":71,"m":"In about half an hour, please come and talk to me. At least you can go and see my lord aboard ship. For now, leave me."},{"t":"sd","id":72,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Britain. A public place.","id":73},{"t":"sd","id":74,"text":"Enter Cloten and two Lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Sir, I would advise you to shift a shirt; the violence","of action hath made you reek as a sacrifice. Where air comes out,","air comes in; there's none abroad so wholesome as that you vent."],"id":75,"m":"Sir, I'd advise you to change your shirt. All that action has made you steam like a burnt sacrifice. When air goes out, air comes in, and no air outside is as healthy as what you breathe out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["If my shirt were bloody, then to shift it. Have I hurt him?"],"id":76,"m":"If my shirt were bloody, then I'd change it. Did I hurt him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] No, faith; not so much as his patience."],"id":77,"m":"(Aside) No, not even his patience."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Hurt him! His body's a passable carcass if he be not","hurt. It is a throughfare for steel if it be not hurt."],"id":78,"m":"Hurt him! If he isn't hurt, his body is a fine-looking corpse. It's a highway for swords if he isn't hurt."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] His steel was in debt; it went o' th' back","side the town."],"id":79,"m":"(Aside) His sword was in debt; it slunk out the back way of town to avoid paying."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["The villain would not stand me."],"id":80,"m":"The villain wouldn't stand and face me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] No; but he fled forward still, toward your","face."],"id":81,"m":"(Aside) No, but he kept advancing toward your face."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Stand you? You have land enough of your own; but he","added to your having, gave you some ground."],"id":82,"m":"Stand and face you? You've got plenty of land of your own, but he added to it: he gave you some ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] As many inches as you have oceans.","Puppies!"],"id":83,"m":"(Aside) About as many inches as you have oceans. Puppies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I would they had not come between us."],"id":84,"m":"I wish they hadn't come between us."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] So would I, till you had measur'd how long a","fool you were upon the ground."],"id":85,"m":"(Aside) So do I, until you'd measured on the ground how long a fool you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["And that she should love this fellow, and refuse me!"],"id":86,"m":"And to think she loves this fellow and refuses me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] If it be a sin to make a true election, she is","damn'd."],"id":87,"m":"(Aside) If it's a sin to make the right choice, she's damned."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Sir, as I told you always, her beauty and her brain go","not together; she's a good sign, but I have seen small reflection","of her wit."],"id":88,"m":"Sir, as I've always told you, her beauty and her brain don't go together. She's a fine-looking sign, but I've seen little reflection of wit in her."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] She shines not upon fools, lest the reflection","should hurt her."],"id":89,"m":"(Aside) She doesn't shine on fools, in case the reflection hurts her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Come, I'll to my chamber. Would there had been some hurt","done!"],"id":90,"m":"Come, I'm going to my room. I wish some real harm had been done!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] I wish not so; unless it had been the fall of","an ass, which is no great hurt."],"id":91,"m":"(Aside) I don't, unless it was the fall of an ass, which is no great harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["You'll go with us?"],"id":92,"m":"You'll come with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I'll attend your lordship."],"id":93,"m":"I'll attend your lordship."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Nay, come, let's go together."],"id":94,"m":"No, come on, let's go together."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Well, my lord."],"id":95,"m":"Very well, my lord."},{"t":"sd","id":96,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"Britain. Cymbeline's palace.","id":97},{"t":"sd","id":98,"text":"Enter Imogen and Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I would thou grew'st unto the shores o' th' haven,","And questioned'st every sail; if he should write,","And I not have it, 'twere a paper lost,","As offer'd mercy is. What was the last","That he spake to thee?"],"id":99,"m":"I wish you'd grown roots on the shore of the harbour and questioned every ship. If he writes and I don't get it, it's as much a loss as a pardon that never arrives. What was the last thing he said to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["It was: his queen, his queen!"],"id":100,"m":"It was: \"My queen, my queen!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Then wav'd his handkerchief?"],"id":101,"m":"Then he waved his handkerchief?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["And kiss'd it, madam."],"id":102,"m":"And kissed it, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Senseless linen, happier therein than I!","And that was all?"],"id":103,"m":"Senseless linen, luckier than I am! And was that all?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["No, madam; for so long","As he could make me with his eye, or care","Distinguish him from others, he did keep","The deck, with glove, or hat, or handkerchief,","Still waving, as the fits and stirs of's mind","Could best express how slow his soul sail'd on,","How swift his ship."],"id":104,"m":"No, madam. For as long as he could make me out with his eye, or I could pick him out from the others, he stayed on deck, waving his glove or hat or handkerchief, as the rush of his feelings could best show how slowly his soul sailed and how fast his ship."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Thou shouldst have made him","As little as a crow, or less, ere left","To after-eye him."],"id":105,"m":"You should have watched him until he was as small as a crow, or smaller, before you stopped looking."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Madam, so I did."],"id":106,"m":"Madam, I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I would have broke mine eyestrings, crack'd them but","To look upon him, till the diminution","Of space had pointed him sharp as my needle;","Nay, followed him till he had melted from","The smallness of a gnat to air, and then","Have turn'd mine eye and wept. But, good Pisanio,","When shall we hear from him?"],"id":107,"m":"I would have broken my eye muscles, cracked them, just to look at him until the distance made him as thin as my needle point. No, I'd have followed him with my eyes until he melted from a gnat into air, and then turned away and wept. But, good Pisanio, when will we hear from him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Be assur'd, madam,","With his next vantage."],"id":108,"m":"Be sure, madam, at his first chance."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I did not take my leave of him, but had","Most pretty things to say. Ere I could tell him","How I would think on him at certain hours","Such thoughts and such; or I could make him swear","The shes of Italy should not betray","Mine interest and his honour; or have charg'd him,","At the sixth hour of morn, at noon, at midnight,","T' encounter me with orisons, for then","I am in heaven for him; or ere I could","Give him that parting kiss which I had set","Betwixt two charming words, comes in my father,","And like the tyrannous breathing of the north","Shakes all our buds from growing."],"id":109,"m":"I didn't say goodbye to him properly, and I had such pretty things to say. Before I could tell him how I'd think of him at certain hours, with such and such thoughts; or make him swear the women of Italy wouldn't betray my rights and his honour; or tell him to meet me in prayer at six in the morning, at noon and at midnight, because then I'm in heaven for him; or before I could give him the parting kiss I had placed between two magic words, in comes my father and, like the cruel north wind, shakes all our buds before they can grow."},{"t":"sd","id":110,"text":"Enter a Lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["The Queen, madam,","Desires your Highness' company."],"id":111,"m":"Madam, the Queen would like your Highness's company."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Those things I bid you do, get them dispatch'd.","I will attend the Queen."],"id":112,"m":"Get those things I asked you to do done. I'll go to the Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Madam, I shall."],"id":113,"m":"I will, madam."},{"t":"sd","id":114,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"Rome. Philario's house.","id":115},{"t":"sd","id":116,"text":"Enter Philario, Iachimo, a Frenchman, a Dutchman, and a Spaniard."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Believe it, sir, I have seen him in Britain. He was then","of a crescent note, expected to prove so worthy as since he hath","been allowed the name of. But I could then have look'd on him","without the help of admiration, though the catalogue of his","endowments had been tabled by his side, and I to peruse him by","items."],"id":117,"m":"Believe me, sir, I saw him in Britain. His reputation was growing then, and people expected him to become as worthy as he's since been said to be. But I could have looked at him then without any special admiration, even with a list of his qualities posted beside him for me to check off item by item."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["You speak of him when he was less furnish'd than now he","is with that which makes him both without and within."],"id":118,"m":"You're speaking of him when he had less of what makes him what he is, inside and out, than he has now."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCHMAN","lines":["I have seen him in France; we had very many there could","behold the sun with as firm eyes as he."],"id":119,"m":"I've seen him in France. We had plenty of men there who could look at the sun as steadily as he does."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["This matter of marrying his king's daughter, wherein he","must be weighed rather by her value than his own, words him, I","doubt not, a great deal from the matter."],"id":120,"m":"This business of marrying his king's daughter, where he's being measured by her worth more than his own, inflates his reputation well beyond the truth, I'm sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCHMAN","lines":["And then his banishment."],"id":121,"m":"And then there's his banishment."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Ay, and the approbation of those that weep this lamentable","divorce under her colours are wonderfully to extend him, be it","but to fortify her judgment, which else an easy battery might lay","flat, for taking a beggar, without less quality. But how comes it","he is to sojourn with you? How creeps acquaintance?"],"id":122,"m":"Yes, and the people who mourn this sad separation on her side praise him to the skies, if only to defend her judgment, which could otherwise be easily attacked for taking a beggar with no qualities. But how does he come to be staying with you? How did you get to know him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["His father and I were soldiers together, to whom I have","been often bound for no less than my life."],"id":123,"m":"His father and I were soldiers together, and I often owed him my life."},{"t":"sd","id":124,"text":"Enter Posthumus."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes the Briton. Let him be so entertained amongst you as","suits with gentlemen of your knowing to a stranger of his","quality. I beseech you all be better known to this gentleman,","whom I commend to you as a noble friend of mine. How worthy he is","I will leave to appear hereafter, rather than story him in his","own hearing."],"id":125,"m":"Here comes the Briton. Gentlemen, welcome him as men of your breeding should welcome a stranger of his rank. Please get to know this gentleman, whom I recommend to you as a noble friend. I'll let his worth show itself later rather than describe it in his hearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCHMAN","lines":["Sir, we have known together in Orleans."],"id":126,"m":"Sir, we knew each other in Orleans."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Since when I have been debtor to you for courtesies,","which I will be ever to pay and yet pay still."],"id":127,"m":"And I've owed you for your kindnesses ever since; I'll keep paying that debt and still owe it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCHMAN","lines":["Sir, you o'errate my poor kindness. I was glad I did","atone my countryman and you; it had been pity you should have","been put together with so mortal a purpose as then each bore,","upon importance of so slight and trivial a nature."],"id":128,"m":"Sir, you overvalue my small kindness. I was glad to reconcile you and my countryman. It would have been a pity for you to fight with such deadly intent, as you both meant to then, over something so slight and trivial."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["By your pardon, sir. I was then a young traveller;","rather shunn'd to go even with what I heard than in my every","action to be guided by others' experiences; but upon my mended","judgment—if I offend not to say it is mended—my quarrel was not","altogether slight."],"id":129,"m":"Forgive me, sir. I was a young traveller then and preferred to act on my own judgment rather than be guided by other people's experience. But now that my judgment has improved (if it's not boastful to say so), I still think my quarrel wasn't so slight."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCHMAN","lines":["Faith, yes, to be put to the arbitrement of swords, and","by such two that would by all likelihood have confounded one the","other or have fall'n both."],"id":130,"m":"Indeed it was, to be settled by swords, and between two men who would most likely have destroyed each other or both been killed."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Can we, with manners, ask what was the difference?"],"id":131,"m":"May we politely ask what the quarrel was?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCHMAN","lines":["Safely, I think. 'Twas a contention in public, which","may, without contradiction, suffer the report. It was much like","an argument that fell out last night, where each of us fell in","praise of our country mistresses; this gentleman at that time","vouching—and upon warrant of bloody affirmation—his to be more","fair, virtuous, wise, chaste, constant, qualified, and less","attemptable, than any the rarest of our ladies in France."],"id":132,"m":"Safely, I think. It was a public argument, so it can be reported. It was much like the argument last night, when each of us praised the women of our own countries. This gentleman insisted then, and offered to prove it in blood, that his lady was more beautiful, virtuous, wise, chaste, faithful, accomplished and harder to seduce than the finest ladies of France."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["That lady is not now living, or this gentleman's opinion,","by this, worn out."],"id":133,"m":"Either that lady is dead by now, or this gentleman's opinion has worn out."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["She holds her virtue still, and I my mind."],"id":134,"m":"She still keeps her virtue, and I my opinion."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["You must not so far prefer her fore ours of Italy."],"id":135,"m":"You can't rank her so far above our Italian ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Being so far provok'd as I was in France, I would abate","her nothing, though I profess myself her adorer, not her friend."],"id":136,"m":"If I were provoked as I was in France, I wouldn't lower her one bit, though I'm her worshipper, not merely her lover."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["As fair and as good—a kind of hand-in-hand comparison—","had been something too fair and too good for any lady in Britain.","If she went before others I have seen as that diamond of yours","outlustres many I have beheld, I could not but believe she","excelled many; but I have not seen the most precious diamond that","is, nor you the lady."],"id":137,"m":"To call her as beautiful and as good as ours, side by side, would already be too much praise for any lady in Britain. If she outshone the women I've seen the way your diamond outshines many I've seen, I'd have to believe she excelled many. But I haven't seen the most precious diamond in the world, and you haven't seen the most precious lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I prais'd her as I rated her. So do I my stone."],"id":138,"m":"I praised her at the value I put on her. I do the same with my stone."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["What do you esteem it at?"],"id":139,"m":"What do you value it at?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["More than the world enjoys."],"id":140,"m":"More than the whole world owns."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Either your unparagon'd mistress is dead, or she's","outpriz'd by a trifle."],"id":141,"m":"Then either your matchless lady is dead, or she's worth less than a trinket."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["You are mistaken: the one may be sold or given, if there","were wealth enough for the purchase or merit for the gift; the","other is not a thing for sale, and only the gift of the gods."],"id":142,"m":"You're wrong. The ring can be sold or given, if anyone were rich enough to buy it or worthy enough to receive it. The lady is not for sale; she can only be a gift from the gods."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Which the gods have given you?"],"id":143,"m":"And the gods have given her to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Which by their graces I will keep."],"id":144,"m":"And by their grace I'll keep her."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["You may wear her in title yours; but you know strange fowl","light upon neighbouring ponds. Your ring may be stol'n too. So","your brace of unprizable estimations, the one is but frail and","the other casual; a cunning thief, or a that-way-accomplish'd","courtier, would hazard the winning both of first and last."],"id":145,"m":"She may be yours in name, but you know strange birds land on neighbouring ponds. Your ring may be stolen too. Of your two priceless treasures, one is frail and the other can be lost by chance. A clever thief, or a courtier skilled in that line, would risk trying to win both."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Your Italy contains none so accomplish'd a courtier to","convince the honour of my mistress, if in the holding or loss of","that you term her frail. I do nothing doubt you have store of","thieves; notwithstanding, I fear not my ring."],"id":146,"m":"Your Italy has no courtier skilled enough to overcome my lady's honour, if it's in keeping or losing that you call her frail. I don't doubt you have plenty of thieves; still, I'm not worried about my ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Let us leave here, gentlemen."],"id":147,"m":"Let's drop this, gentlemen."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Sir, with all my heart. This worthy signior, I thank","him, makes no stranger of me; we are familiar at first."],"id":148,"m":"Sir, with all my heart. This worthy gentleman, I thank him, doesn't treat me as a stranger; we're on familiar terms already."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["With five times so much conversation I should get ground","of your fair mistress; make her go back even to the yielding, had","I admittance and opportunity to friend."],"id":149,"m":"With five times as much talk I'd gain ground on your lovely lady, and make her retreat all the way to giving in, if I had access and opportunity on my side."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["No, no."],"id":150,"m":"No, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["I dare thereupon pawn the moiety of my estate to your","ring, which, in my opinion, o'ervalues it something. But I make","my wager rather against your confidence than her reputation; and,","to bar your offence herein too, I durst attempt it against any","lady in the world."],"id":151,"m":"I'd dare to bet half my fortune against your ring on it, which in my opinion is worth rather less. But I'm betting against your confidence, not her reputation. And to spare you offence, I'd dare to try it against any lady in the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["You are a great deal abus'd in too bold a persuasion,","and I doubt not you sustain what y'are worthy of by your attempt."],"id":152,"m":"You're badly mistaken in being so sure of yourself, and I'm sure your attempt would get you what you deserve."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["What's that?"],"id":153,"m":"What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["A repulse; though your attempt, as you call it, deserve","more—a punishment too."],"id":154,"m":"A rejection. Though your attempt, as you call it, deserves more: a punishment too."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Gentlemen, enough of this. It came in too suddenly; let","it die as it was born, and I pray you be better acquainted."],"id":155,"m":"Gentlemen, enough of this. It came up too suddenly; let it die as quickly as it was born, and please get to know each other better."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Would I had put my estate and my neighbour's on th'","approbation of what I have spoke!"],"id":156,"m":"I wish I'd bet my estate and my neighbour's on the truth of what I said!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["What lady would you choose to assail?"],"id":157,"m":"Which lady would you choose to attack?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Yours, whom in constancy you think stands so safe. I will","lay you ten thousand ducats to your ring that, commend me to the","court where your lady is, with no more advantage than the","opportunity of a second conference, and I will bring from thence","that honour of hers which you imagine so reserv'd."],"id":158,"m":"Yours, whom you think so safe in her faithfulness. I'll bet you ten thousand ducats against your ring that, if you give me an introduction to the court where your lady is, with no more advantage than the chance of a second meeting, I'll bring back from there that honour of hers which you think so well guarded."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I will wage against your gold, gold to it. My ring I","hold dear as my finger; 'tis part of it."],"id":159,"m":"I'll bet gold against your gold. My ring I hold as dear as my finger; it's part of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["You are a friend, and therein the wiser. If you buy","ladies' flesh at a million a dram, you cannot preserve it from","tainting. But I see you have some religion in you, that you fear."],"id":160,"m":"You're her lover, and so you're the wiser not to risk it. If you bought ladies' flesh at a million a gram, you still couldn't keep it from going bad. But I see you have some religious scruple: you're afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["This is but a custom in your tongue; you bear a graver","purpose, I hope."],"id":161,"m":"That's just a habit of your tongue. You have a more serious purpose, I hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["I am the master of my speeches, and would undergo what's","spoken, I swear."],"id":162,"m":"I'm the master of what I say, and I'll undertake what I've said, I swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Will you? I Shall but lend my diamond till your return.","Let there be covenants drawn between's. My mistress exceeds in","goodness the hugeness of your unworthy thinking. I dare you to","this match: here's my ring."],"id":163,"m":"Will you? Then I'll only be lending you my diamond till you come back. Let's have an agreement drawn up between us. My lady's goodness is greater than the size of your unworthy thoughts. I dare you to this bet: here's my ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["I will have it no lay."],"id":164,"m":"I won't allow it as a bet."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["By the gods, it is one. If I bring you no sufficient","testimony that I have enjoy'd the dearest bodily part of your","mistress, my ten thousand ducats are yours; so is your diamond","too. If I come off, and leave her in such honour as you have","trust in, she your jewel, this your jewel, and my gold are yours—","provided I have your commendation for my more free entertainment."],"id":165,"m":"By the gods, it is one. If I don't bring you enough proof that I've enjoyed the dearest part of your lady's body, my ten thousand ducats are yours, and so is your diamond. If I fail, and leave her as honourable as you trust she is, then she your jewel, this your jewel, and my gold are all yours, provided you give me a letter of introduction to get me a warmer welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I embrace these conditions; let us have articles betwixt","us. Only, thus far you shall answer: if you make your voyage upon","her, and give me directly to understand you have prevail'd, I am","no further your enemy—she is not worth our debate; if she remain","unseduc'd, you not making it appear otherwise, for your ill","opinion and th' assault you have made to her chastity you shall","answer me with your sword."],"id":166,"m":"I accept these terms; let's have a contract between us. But on one point you'll answer to me: if you succeed with her and tell me plainly that you've won, I'm no longer your enemy, because she isn't worth our quarrel. If she stays unseduced and you can't show otherwise, then for your low opinion and your attack on her chastity you'll answer to me with your sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Your hand—a covenant! We will have these things set down","by lawful counsel, and straight away for Britain, lest the","bargain should catch cold and starve. I will fetch my gold and","have our two wagers recorded."],"id":167,"m":"Your hand: it's a deal! We'll have it all written down by a lawyer, and I'll set off for Britain right away, before the bargain catches cold and dies. I'll fetch my gold and have both our stakes recorded."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Agreed."],"id":168,"m":"Agreed."},{"t":"sd","id":169,"text":"Exeunt Posthumus and Iachimo."},{"t":"sp","who":"FRENCHMAN","lines":["Will this hold, think you?"],"id":170,"m":"Do you think this will go ahead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Signior Iachimo will not from it. Pray let us follow 'em."],"id":171,"m":"Signor Iachimo won't back out of it. Let's follow them."},{"t":"sd","id":172,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"V","loc":"Britain. Cymbeline's palace.","id":173},{"t":"sd","id":174,"text":"Enter Queen, Ladies, and Cornelius."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Whiles yet the dew's on ground, gather those flowers;","Make haste; who has the note of them?"],"id":175,"m":"While the dew is still on the ground, gather those flowers. Hurry. Who has the list?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["I, madam."],"id":176,"m":"I do, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Dispatch."],"id":177,"m":"Go quickly."},{"t":"sd","id":178,"text":"Exeunt Ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["Now, Master Doctor, have you brought those drugs?"],"id":179,"m":"Now, Master Doctor, have you brought those drugs?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["Pleaseth your Highness, ay. Here they are, madam."],"id":180,"m":"Yes, if it please your Highness. Here they are, madam."},{"t":"sd","id":181,"text":"Presenting a box."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","cont":1,"lines":["But I beseech your Grace, without offence—","My conscience bids me ask—wherefore you have","Commanded of me these most poisonous compounds","Which are the movers of a languishing death,","But, though slow, deadly?"],"id":182,"m":"But I beg your Grace, without offence (my conscience tells me to ask), why have you ordered these very poisonous mixtures from me, which cause a slow, wasting death, slow but deadly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["I wonder, Doctor,","Thou ask'st me such a question. Have I not been","Thy pupil long? Hast thou not learn'd me how","To make perfumes? distil? preserve? yea, so","That our great king himself doth woo me oft","For my confections? Having thus far proceeded—","Unless thou think'st me devilish—is't not meet","That I did amplify my judgment in","Other conclusions? I will try the forces","Of these thy compounds on such creatures as","We count not worth the hanging—but none human—","To try the vigour of them, and apply","Allayments to their act, and by them gather","Their several virtues and effects."],"id":183,"m":"I'm surprised you ask me that, Doctor. Haven't I been your pupil for a long time? Haven't you taught me to make perfumes, to distil, to preserve, so well that the great King himself often begs me for my concoctions? Having come this far (unless you think I'm a devil), isn't it right that I should widen my knowledge into other experiments? I'll test the strength of your mixtures on creatures we don't think worth hanging, but no humans, to see how powerful they are, try out remedies against them, and so learn their various properties and effects."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["Your Highness","Shall from this practice but make hard your heart;","Besides, the seeing these effects will be","Both noisome and infectious."],"id":184,"m":"Your Highness will only harden your heart by this practice. Besides, watching these effects will be both disgusting and unhealthy."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["O, content thee."],"id":185,"m":"Oh, don't worry."},{"t":"sd","id":186,"text":"Enter Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["[Aside] Here comes a flattering rascal; upon him","Will I first work. He's for his master,","An enemy to my son.—How now, Pisanio!","Doctor, your service for this time is ended;","Take your own way."],"id":187,"m":"(Aside) Here comes a flattering rascal. I'll work on him first. He's on his master's side, and an enemy to my son. (Aloud) Hello, Pisanio! Doctor, I don't need you any more for now. Off you go."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["[Aside] I do suspect you, madam;","But you shall do no harm."],"id":188,"m":"(Aside) I suspect you, madam, but you won't do any harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["[To Pisanio] Hark thee, a word."],"id":189,"m":"(To Pisanio) Listen, a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["[Aside] I do not like her. She doth think she has","Strange ling'ring poisons. I do know her spirit,","And will not trust one of her malice with","A drug of such damn'd nature. Those she has","Will stupefy and dull the sense awhile,","Which first perchance she'll prove on cats and dogs,","Then afterward up higher; but there is","No danger in what show of death it makes,","More than the locking up the spirits a time,","To be more fresh, reviving. She is fool'd","With a most false effect; and I the truer","So to be false with her."],"id":190,"m":"(Aside) I don't like her. She thinks she has strange slow poisons. I know her nature and won't trust anyone so spiteful with a drug that deadly. What I've given her will stun and deaden the senses for a while. She'll probably test it first on cats and dogs, then on higher creatures. But there's no danger in the appearance of death it causes, beyond shutting down the vital spirits for a time, so they revive all the fresher. She's fooled by a completely false effect, and I'm all the more honest for being false to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["No further service, Doctor,","Until I send for thee."],"id":191,"m":"I don't need you any more, Doctor, until I send for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["I humbly take my leave."],"id":192,"m":"I humbly take my leave."},{"t":"sd","id":193,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Weeps she still, say'st thou? Dost thou think in time","She will not quench, and let instructions enter","Where folly now possesses? Do thou work.","When thou shalt bring me word she loves my son,","I'll tell thee on the instant thou art then","As great as is thy master; greater, for","His fortunes all lie speechless, and his name","Is at last gasp. Return he cannot, nor","Continue where he is. To shift his being","Is to exchange one misery with another,","And every day that comes comes comes to","A day's work in him. What shalt thou expect","To be depender on a thing that leans,","Who cannot be new built, nor has no friends","So much as but to prop him?"],"id":194,"m":"Is she still crying, you say? Don't you think that in time she'll cool off and let good advice in where foolishness rules now? Work on her. When you bring me word that she loves my son, I'll tell you on the spot that you're as great as your master; greater, because his fortunes are struck dumb and his name is at its last gasp. He can't come back, and he can't stay where he is. To move would be to swap one misery for another, and every new day only undoes another day's work in him. What can you hope for by depending on a falling thing that can't be rebuilt and hasn't a friend to prop it up?"},{"t":"sd","id":195,"text":"The Queen drops the box. Pisanio takes it up."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["Thou tak'st up","Thou know'st not what; but take it for thy labour.","It is a thing I made, which hath the King","Five times redeem'd from death. I do not know","What is more cordial. Nay, I prithee take it;","It is an earnest of a further good","That I mean to thee. Tell thy mistress how","The case stands with her; do't as from thyself.","Think what a chance thou changest on; but think","Thou hast thy mistress still; to boot, my son,","Who shall take notice of thee. I'll move the King","To any shape of thy preferment, such","As thou'lt desire; and then myself, I chiefly,","That set thee on to this desert, am bound","To load thy merit richly. Call my women.","Think on my words."],"id":196,"m":"You're picking up you don't know what, but keep it for your trouble. It's something I made that has saved the King from death five times. I don't know a better restorative. No, please take it. It's a down payment on more good things I mean to do for you. Tell your mistress how things stand for her; say it as if it came from you. Think what a chance you're getting to change sides; and think that you'll still have your mistress, and my son into the bargain, who'll take notice of you. I'll persuade the King to promote you any way you like, and I myself, who set you on to earn it, will be bound to reward you richly. Call my women. Think about what I've said."},{"t":"sd","id":197,"text":"Exit Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["A sly and constant knave,","Not to be shak'd; the agent for his master,","And the remembrancer of her to hold","The hand-fast to her lord. I have given him that","Which, if he take, shall quite unpeople her","Of leigers for her sweet; and which she after,","Except she bend her humour, shall be assur'd","To taste of too."],"id":198,"m":"A sly, loyal rogue who can't be shaken: his master's agent, and the one who reminds her to keep her vow to her husband. I've given him something which, if he takes it, will rid her of all her sweetheart's messengers; and which she'll be sure to taste too afterwards, unless she changes her mind."},{"t":"sd","id":199,"text":"Re-enter Pisanio and Ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["So, so. Well done, well done.","The violets, cowslips, and the primroses,","Bear to my closet. Fare thee well, Pisanio;","Think on my words."],"id":200,"m":"Good, good. Well done. Take the violets, cowslips and primroses to my room. Goodbye, Pisanio. Think about what I've said."},{"t":"sd","id":201,"text":"Exeunt Queen and Ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["And shall do.","But when to my good lord I prove untrue","I'll choke myself—there's all I'll do for you."],"id":202,"m":"And I will. But when I prove untrue to my good lord, I'll strangle myself. That's all I'll do for you."},{"t":"sd","id":203,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"VI","loc":"Britain. The palace.","id":204},{"t":"sd","id":205,"text":"Enter Imogen alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["A father cruel and a step-dame false;","A foolish suitor to a wedded lady","That hath her husband banish'd. O, that husband!","My supreme crown of grief! and those repeated","Vexations of it! Had I been thief-stol'n,","As my two brothers, happy! but most miserable","Is the desire that's glorious. Blessed be those,","How mean soe'er, that have their honest wills,","Which seasons comfort. Who may this be? Fie!"],"id":206,"m":"A cruel father and a false stepmother; a foolish suitor chasing a married lady whose husband has been banished. Oh, that husband, the supreme crown of my grief, and all its repeated torments! If I'd been stolen by thieves like my two brothers, I'd be happy. But a desire set on a high, glorious thing is the most miserable. Blessed are those, however humble, who can have their honest wishes; that makes comfort sweet. Who could this be? Oh, no!"},{"t":"sd","id":207,"text":"Enter Pisanio and Iachimo."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Madam, a noble gentleman of Rome","Comes from my lord with letters."],"id":208,"m":"Madam, a noble gentleman from Rome has come with letters from my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Change you, madam?","The worthy Leonatus is in safety,","And greets your Highness dearly. [Presents a letter]"],"id":209,"m":"You change colour, madam? The worthy Leonatus is safe and sends your Highness loving greetings. (He gives her a letter.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Thanks, good sir.","You're kindly welcome."],"id":210,"m":"Thank you, good sir. You're very welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["[Aside] All of her that is out of door most rich!","If she be furnish'd with a mind so rare,","She is alone th' Arabian bird, and I","Have lost the wager. Boldness be my friend!","Arm me, audacity, from head to foot!","Or, like the Parthian, I shall flying fight;","Rather, directly fly."],"id":211,"m":"(Aside) Everything on the outside of her is splendid! If she has a mind as rare as that, she's the one and only phoenix, and I've lost the bet. Boldness, be my friend! Daring, arm me from head to foot! Or I'll have to fight while retreating, like the Parthians; or rather, simply run."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["[Reads] 'He is one of the noblest note, to whose","kindnesses I am most infinitely tied. Reflect upon him","accordingly, as you value your trust. Leonatus.'","So far I read aloud;","But even the very middle of my heart","Is warm'd by th' rest and takes it thankfully.","You are as welcome, worthy sir, as I","Have words to bid you; and shall find it so","In all that I can do."],"id":212,"m":"(Reading) \"He is a man of the highest reputation, to whose kindness I am bound beyond measure. Treat him accordingly, as you value your loyalty to me. Leonatus.\" That much I read aloud, but the rest warms the very centre of my heart, and it accepts it gratefully. You are as welcome, worthy sir, as I have words to say, and you'll find it so in everything I can do."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Thanks, fairest lady.","What, are men mad? Hath nature given them eyes","To see this vaulted arch and the rich crop","Of sea and land, which can distinguish 'twixt","The fiery orbs above and the twinn'd stones","Upon the number'd beach, and can we not","Partition make with spectacles so precious","'Twixt fair and foul?"],"id":213,"m":"Thank you, fairest lady. (As if to himself) Are men mad? Has nature given them eyes to see the arch of the sky and the rich harvest of sea and land, eyes that can tell the blazing stars above from the identical pebbles on the beach, and can't we, with such precious instruments, tell fair from foul?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["What makes your admiration?"],"id":214,"m":"What are you so amazed at?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["It cannot be i' th' eye, for apes and monkeys,","'Twixt two such shes, would chatter this way and","Contemn with mows the other; nor i' th' judgment,","For idiots in this case of favour would","Be wisely definite; nor i' th' appetite;","Sluttery, to such neat excellence oppos'd,","Should make desire vomit emptiness,","Not so allur'd to feed."],"id":215,"m":"The fault can't be in the eye, because apes and monkeys, between two such women, would chatter in favour of this one and pull faces at the other. Nor in the judgment, because even idiots would decide wisely on this question of beauty. Nor in the appetite: filth, set against such pure excellence, would make desire throw up on an empty stomach, not tempt it to feed."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["What is the matter, trow?"],"id":216,"m":"What's the matter, I wonder?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["The cloyed will—","That satiate yet unsatisfied desire, that tub","Both fill'd and running—ravening first the lamb,","Longs after for the garbage."],"id":217,"m":"The overfed appetite, that desire which is stuffed but never satisfied, like a tub that's both full and leaking, first devours the lamb and then craves the garbage."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["What, dear sir,","Thus raps you? Are you well?"],"id":218,"m":"What, dear sir, has carried you away like this? Are you well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Thanks, madam; well.—Beseech you, sir,","Desire my man's abode where I did leave him.","He's strange and peevish."],"id":219,"m":"Thank you, madam, I'm well. (To Pisanio) Please, sir, go to my servant where I left him. He's a stranger here and easily upset."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["I was going, sir,","To give him welcome."],"id":220,"m":"I was just going, sir, to welcome him."},{"t":"sd","id":221,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Continues well my lord? His health beseech you?"],"id":222,"m":"Is my lord still well? How is his health, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Well, madam."],"id":223,"m":"Well, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Is he dispos'd to mirth? I hope he is."],"id":224,"m":"Is he in good spirits? I hope he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Exceeding pleasant; none a stranger there","So merry and so gamesome. He is call'd","The Britain reveller."],"id":225,"m":"Extremely cheerful. No foreigner there is so merry and playful. They call him the British party-goer."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["When he was here","He did incline to sadness, and oft-times","Not knowing why."],"id":226,"m":"When he was here he tended to be sad, and often without knowing why."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["I never saw him sad.","There is a Frenchman his companion, one","An eminent monsieur that, it seems, much loves","A Gallian girl at home. He furnaces","The thick sighs from him; whiles the jolly Briton—","Your lord, I mean—laughs from's free lungs, cries 'O,","Can my sides hold, to think that man—who knows","By history, report, or his own proof,","What woman is, yea, what she cannot choose","But must be—will's free hours languish for","Assured bondage?'"],"id":227,"m":"I never saw him sad. He has a companion there, a Frenchman, a distinguished gentleman who apparently loves a French girl back home very much. He pumps out heavy sighs like a furnace, while the jolly Briton (your lord, I mean) laughs from the bottom of his lungs and cries, \"Oh, my sides will split, to think that a man who knows from history, gossip or his own experience what woman is, yes, what she can't help being, would waste his free hours pining for certain slavery!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Will my lord say so?"],"id":228,"m":"My lord says that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Ay, madam, with his eyes in flood with laughter.","It is a recreation to be by","And hear him mock the Frenchman. But heavens know","Some men are much to blame."],"id":229,"m":"Yes, madam, with his eyes streaming with laughter. It's a treat to be there and hear him mock the Frenchman. But heaven knows some men are much to blame."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Not he, I hope."],"id":230,"m":"Not he, I hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Not he; but yet heaven's bounty towards him might","Be us'd more thankfully. In himself, 'tis much;","In you, which I account his, beyond all talents.","Whilst I am bound to wonder, I am bound","To pity too."],"id":231,"m":"Not he; but still, he could be more grateful for heaven's generosity to him. What he has in himself is a lot; what he has in you, which I count as his, is beyond all wealth. While I have to wonder at it, I also have to pity."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["What do you pity, sir?"],"id":232,"m":"What do you pity, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Two creatures heartily."],"id":233,"m":"Two people, with all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Am I one, sir?","You look on me: what wreck discern you in me","Deserves your pity?"],"id":234,"m":"Am I one of them, sir? You're looking at me. What ruin do you see in me that deserves your pity?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Lamentable! What,","To hide me from the radiant sun and solace","I' th' dungeon by a snuff?"],"id":235,"m":"How sad! What, to hide from the radiant sun and take comfort in a dungeon by a candle stub?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I pray you, sir,","Deliver with more openness your answers","To my demands. Why do you pity me?"],"id":236,"m":"Please, sir, answer my questions more openly. Why do you pity me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["That others do,","I was about to say, enjoy your—But","It is an office of the gods to venge it,","Not mine to speak on't."],"id":237,"m":"That others, I was about to say, enjoy your... But it's for the gods to take revenge for it, not for me to speak of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["You do seem to know","Something of me, or what concerns me; pray you—","Since doubting things go ill often hurts more","Than to be sure they do; for certainties","Either are past remedies, or, timely knowing,","The remedy then born—discover to me","What both you spur and stop."],"id":238,"m":"You seem to know something about me, or something that concerns me. Please, since suspecting that things are wrong often hurts more than knowing they are (because certainties are either past cure or, if known in time, bring their cure with them), tell me what you keep starting to say and then stopping."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Had I this cheek","To bathe my lips upon; this hand, whose touch,","Whose every touch, would force the feeler's soul","To th' oath of loyalty; this object, which","Takes prisoner the wild motion of mine eye,","Fixing it only here; should I, damn'd then,","Slaver with lips as common as the stairs","That mount the Capitol; join gripes with hands","Made hard with hourly falsehood—falsehood as","With labour; then by-peeping in an eye","Base and illustrious as the smoky light","That's fed with stinking tallow—it were fit","That all the plagues of hell should at one time","Encounter such revolt."],"id":239,"m":"If I had this cheek to bathe my lips on, this hand whose every touch would force the toucher's soul to swear loyalty, this face that captures my wandering eye and fixes it here alone, would I then, damned for it, slobber on lips as common as the steps up to the Capitol, clasp hands made hard by hourly lying (lying as if it were hard work), then peep sideways into an eye as low and as bright as a smoky candle fed on stinking fat? It would be right for all the plagues of hell to strike such betrayal at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["My lord, I fear,","Has forgot Britain."],"id":240,"m":"My lord, I'm afraid, has forgotten Britain."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["And himself. Not I","Inclin'd to this intelligence pronounce","The beggary of his change; but 'tis your graces","That from my mutest conscience to my tongue","Charms this report out."],"id":241,"m":"And himself. I'm not eager to spread this news of how low he's sunk, but your graces charm this report from my silent conscience onto my tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Let me hear no more."],"id":242,"m":"Don't tell me any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["O dearest soul, your cause doth strike my heart","With pity that doth make me sick! A lady","So fair, and fasten'd to an empery,","Would make the great'st king double, to be partner'd","With tomboys hir'd with that self exhibition","Which your own coffers yield! with diseas'd ventures","That play with all infirmities for gold","Which rottenness can lend nature! such boil'd stuff","As well might poison poison! Be reveng'd;","Or she that bore you was no queen, and you","Recoil from your great stock."],"id":243,"m":"Oh dearest soul, your situation strikes my heart with a pity that makes me sick! A lady so beautiful, and heir to an empire that would double the greatest king, to be put on a level with whores hired with the very allowance your own treasury pays him! With diseased prostitutes who gamble for gold with every sickness rottenness can give a body! Such stewed filth that it could poison poison! Get revenge, or the woman who gave birth to you was no queen, and you fall short of your great family."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Reveng'd?","How should I be reveng'd? If this be true—","As I have such a heart that both mine ears","Must not in haste abuse—if it be true,","How should I be reveng'd?"],"id":244,"m":"Revenge? How should I get revenge? If this is true (and my heart is such that I mustn't let my ears deceive it in a hurry), if it's true, how should I get revenge?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Should he make me","Live like Diana's priest betwixt cold sheets,","Whiles he is vaulting variable ramps,","In your despite, upon your purse? Revenge it.","I dedicate myself to your sweet pleasure,","More noble than that runagate to your bed,","And will continue fast to your affection,","Still close as sure."],"id":245,"m":"Should he make me live like a priestess of Diana between cold sheets, while he leaps on changing whores, to spite you, and with your money? Get revenge for it. I offer myself to your sweet pleasure, nobler than that runaway from your bed, and I'll stay faithful to your love, as secret as I am loyal."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["What ho, Pisanio!"],"id":246,"m":"Pisanio, come here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Let me my service tender on your lips."],"id":247,"m":"Let me offer my service on your lips."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Away! I do condemn mine ears that have","So long attended thee. If thou wert honourable,","Thou wouldst have told this tale for virtue, not","For such an end thou seek'st, as base as strange.","Thou wrong'st a gentleman who is as far","From thy report as thou from honour; and","Solicits here a lady that disdains","Thee and the devil alike.—What ho, Pisanio!—","The King my father shall be made acquainted","Of thy assault. If he shall think it fit","A saucy stranger in his court to mart","As in a Romish stew, and to expound","His beastly mind to us, he hath a court","He little cares for, and a daughter who","He not respects at all.—What ho, Pisanio!"],"id":248,"m":"Get away! I blame my ears for listening to you so long. If you were honourable, you'd have told this story to serve virtue, not for the purpose you're after, as low as it is strange. You slander a gentleman who is as far from your report as you are from honour, and you try to seduce a lady who despises you and the devil alike. Pisanio! The King my father will be told of your attack. If he thinks it right for an insolent stranger to bargain in his court as if it were a Roman brothel, and to lay out his beastly mind to us, then he has a court he cares little for, and a daughter he doesn't respect at all. Pisanio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["O happy Leonatus! I may say","The credit that thy lady hath of thee","Deserves thy trust, and thy most perfect goodness","Her assur'd credit. Blessed live you long,","A lady to the worthiest sir that ever","Country call'd his! and you his mistress, only","For the most worthiest fit! Give me your pardon.","I have spoke this to know if your affiance","Were deeply rooted, and shall make your lord","That which he is new o'er; and he is one","The truest manner'd, such a holy witch","That he enchants societies into him,","Half all men's hearts are his."],"id":249,"m":"Oh, lucky Leonatus! I can say your lady's faith in you deserves your trust, and your perfect goodness deserves her certain faith. Long may you both live happily, a lady married to the worthiest gentleman any country ever called its own, and you his lady, fit only for the worthiest! Forgive me. I said all that to find out whether your faith was deeply rooted, and I'll make your lord what he already is all over again. He's the most truly well-bred of men, such a holy enchanter that he charms company to him; half of every man's heart is his."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["You make amends."],"id":250,"m":"You're making amends."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["He sits 'mongst men like a descended god:","He hath a kind of honour sets him of","More than a mortal seeming. Be not angry,","Most mighty Princess, that I have adventur'd","To try your taking of a false report, which hath","Honour'd with confirmation your great judgment","In the election of a sir so rare,","Which you know cannot err. The love I bear him","Made me to fan you thus; but the gods made you,","Unlike all others, chaffless. Pray your pardon."],"id":251,"m":"He sits among men like a god come down to earth. He has a kind of honour that makes him look more than mortal. Don't be angry, most mighty Princess, that I dared to test how you'd take a false report, which has proved again your great judgment in choosing so rare a gentleman, a judgment you know can't go wrong. My love for him made me winnow you like this, but the gods made you, unlike everyone else, without chaff. Please forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["All's well, sir; take my pow'r i' th' court for yours."],"id":252,"m":"All's well, sir. Use my power at court as if it were your own."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["My humble thanks. I had almost forgot","T' entreat your Grace but in a small request,","And yet of moment too, for it concerns","Your lord; myself and other noble friends","Are partners in the business."],"id":253,"m":"My humble thanks. I almost forgot to ask your Grace a small favour, though an important one, since it concerns your lord. I and some other noble friends are partners in the business."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Pray what is't?"],"id":254,"m":"Please, what is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Some dozen Romans of us, and your lord—","The best feather of our wing—have mingled sums","To buy a present for the Emperor;","Which I, the factor for the rest, have done","In France. 'Tis plate of rare device, and jewels","Of rich and exquisite form, their values great;","And I am something curious, being strange,","To have them in safe stowage. May it please you","To take them in protection?"],"id":255,"m":"About a dozen of us Romans, and your lord (the best feather in our wing), have pooled money to buy a present for the Emperor. I bought it in France as agent for the others. It's silver plate of rare design and jewels of rich and exquisite form, of great value, and being a stranger here I'm rather anxious to have them stored safely. Would you be willing to keep them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Willingly;","And pawn mine honour for their safety. Since","My lord hath interest in them, I will keep them","In my bedchamber."],"id":256,"m":"Gladly, and I'll stake my honour on their safety. Since my lord has a share in them, I'll keep them in my bedroom."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["They are in a trunk,","Attended by my men. I will make bold","To send them to you only for this night;","I must aboard to-morrow."],"id":257,"m":"They're in a trunk, guarded by my men. I'll be bold enough to send it to you just for tonight. I must go aboard ship tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["O, no, no."],"id":258,"m":"Oh, no, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Yes, I beseech; or I shall short my word","By length'ning my return. From Gallia","I cross'd the seas on purpose and on promise","To see your Grace."],"id":259,"m":"Yes, I'm afraid so, or I'll break my word by delaying my return. I crossed the sea from France on purpose, and as I'd promised, to see your Grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I thank you for your pains.","But not away to-morrow!"],"id":260,"m":"Thank you for your trouble. But don't leave tomorrow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["O, I must, madam.","Therefore I shall beseech you, if you please","To greet your lord with writing, do't to-night.","I have outstood my time, which is material","'To th' tender of our present."],"id":261,"m":"Oh, I must, madam. So, if you'd like to send your lord a letter, please write it tonight. I've already overstayed my time, which matters for the delivery of our present."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I will write.","Send your trunk to me; it shall safe be kept","And truly yielded you. You're very welcome."],"id":262,"m":"I'll write. Send your trunk to me. It will be kept safe and faithfully returned to you. You're very welcome."},{"t":"sd","id":263,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Britain. Before Cymbeline's palace.","id":264},{"t":"sd","id":265,"text":"Enter Cloten and the two Lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Was there ever man had such luck! When I kiss'd the jack,","upon an up-cast to be hit away! I had a hundred pound on't; and","then a whoreson jackanapes must take me up for swearing, as if I","borrowed mine oaths of him, and might not spend them at my","pleasure."],"id":266,"m":"Was there ever a man with such luck! When I'd kissed the jack, to have my bowl knocked away at the last throw! I had a hundred pounds on it. And then some son-of-a-whore of a monkey has to tell me off for swearing, as if I'd borrowed my oaths from him and couldn't spend them as I like."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["What got he by that? You have broke his pate with your","bowl."],"id":267,"m":"What did he get for it? You broke his head with your bowl."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] If his wit had been like him that broke it, it","would have run all out."],"id":268,"m":"(Aside) If his brains had been like the man who broke it, they'd all have run out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["When a gentleman is dispos'd to swear, it is not for any","standers-by to curtail his oaths. Ha?"],"id":269,"m":"When a gentleman feels like swearing, it's not for bystanders to cut his oaths short. Right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["No, my lord; [Aside] nor crop the ears of them."],"id":270,"m":"No, my lord. (Aside) Nor to crop their ears either."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Whoreson dog! I give him satisfaction? Would he had been","one of my rank!"],"id":271,"m":"Son-of-a-whore dog! Me give him satisfaction in a duel? I wish he'd been of my rank!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] To have smell'd like a fool."],"id":272,"m":"(Aside) So he'd smell like a fool too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I am not vex'd more at anything in th' earth. A pox on't! I","had rather not be so noble as I am; they dare not fight with me,","because of the Queen my mother. Every jackslave hath his bellyful","of fighting, and I must go up and down like a cock that nobody","can match."],"id":273,"m":"Nothing on earth annoys me more. Damn it! I'd rather not be as noble as I am. They don't dare fight me because of my mother the Queen. Every low servant gets his fill of fighting, and I have to strut about like a fighting cock nobody can match."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] You are cock and capon too; and you crow,","cock, with your comb on."],"id":274,"m":"(Aside) You're a cock and a capon too, and you crow like a cock with your coxcomb on."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Sayest thou?"],"id":275,"m":"What did you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["It is not fit your lordship should undertake every","companion that you give offence to."],"id":276,"m":"It's not right for your lordship to take on every fellow you offend."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["No, I know that; but it is fit I should commit offence to","my inferiors."],"id":277,"m":"No, I know that. But it's right for me to offend my inferiors."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Ay, it is fit for your lordship only."],"id":278,"m":"Yes, that's right for your lordship alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Why, so I say."],"id":279,"m":"That's what I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Did you hear of a stranger that's come to court","to-night?"],"id":280,"m":"Did you hear about a stranger who came to court tonight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["A stranger, and I not known on't?"],"id":281,"m":"A stranger, and I wasn't told?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] He's a strange fellow himself, and knows it","not."],"id":282,"m":"(Aside) He's a strange fellow himself and doesn't know it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["There's an Italian come, and, 'tis thought, one of","Leonatus' friends."],"id":283,"m":"An Italian has arrived, thought to be one of Leonatus's friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Leonatus? A banish'd rascal; and he's another, whatsoever","he be. Who told you of this stranger?"],"id":284,"m":"Leonatus? A banished scoundrel; and this one's another, whoever he is. Who told you about this stranger?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["One of your lordship's pages."],"id":285,"m":"One of your lordship's pages."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Is it fit I went to look upon him? Is there no derogation","in't?"],"id":286,"m":"Would it be suitable for me to go and look at him? Would it lower my dignity?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["You cannot derogate, my lord."],"id":287,"m":"You can't be lowered, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Not easily, I think."],"id":288,"m":"Not easily, I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["[Aside] You are a fool granted; therefore your issues,","being foolish, do not derogate."],"id":289,"m":"(Aside) You're an acknowledged fool, so your actions, being foolish, can't lower you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Come, I'll go see this Italian. What I have lost to-day at","bowls I'll win to-night of him. Come, go."],"id":290,"m":"Come on, I'll go and see this Italian. What I've lost at bowls today I'll win off him tonight. Come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I'll attend your lordship."],"id":291,"m":"I'll attend your lordship."},{"t":"sd","id":292,"text":"Exeunt Cloten and First Lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","cont":1,"lines":["That such a crafty devil as is his mother","Should yield the world this ass! A woman that","Bears all down with her brain; and this her son","Cannot take two from twenty, for his heart,","And leave eighteen. Alas, poor princess,","Thou divine Imogen, what thou endur'st,","Betwixt a father by thy step-dame govern'd,","A mother hourly coining plots, a wooer","More hateful than the foul expulsion is","Of thy dear husband, than that horrid act","Of the divorce he'd make! The heavens hold firm","The walls of thy dear honour, keep unshak'd","That temple, thy fair mind, that thou mayst stand","T' enjoy thy banish'd lord and this great land!"],"id":293,"m":"That such a crafty devil as his mother should give the world this ass! A woman who overpowers everything with her brain, and this son of hers can't take two from twenty and leave eighteen to save his life. Alas, poor princess, divine Imogen, what you endure: between a father ruled by your stepmother, a stepmother hatching plots every hour, and a suitor more hateful than the cruel banishment of your dear husband or the horrible divorce he'd make! May the heavens keep the walls of your honour strong and that temple, your pure mind, unshaken, so that you may live to enjoy your banished lord and this great country!"},{"t":"sd","id":294,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Britain. Imogen's bedchamber in Cymbeline's palace; a trunk in one corner.","id":295},{"t":"sd","id":296,"text":"Enter Imogen in her bed, and a Lady attending."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Who's there? My woman? Helen?"],"id":297,"m":"Who's there? My maid? Helen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Please you, madam."],"id":298,"m":"Here, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["What hour is it?"],"id":299,"m":"What time is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Almost midnight, madam."],"id":300,"m":"Almost midnight, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I have read three hours then. Mine eyes are weak;","Fold down the leaf where I have left. To bed.","Take not away the taper, leave it burning;","And if thou canst awake by four o' th' clock,","I prithee call me. Sleep hath seiz'd me wholly."],"id":301,"m":"Then I've been reading for three hours. My eyes are tired. Fold down the page where I stopped. Go to bed. Don't take the candle away; leave it burning. And if you can wake at four o'clock, please call me. Sleep has taken hold of me completely."},{"t":"sd","id":302,"text":"Exit Lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","cont":1,"lines":["To your protection I commend me, gods.","From fairies and the tempters of the night","Guard me, beseech ye!"],"id":303,"m":"I put myself in your protection, gods. Guard me from fairies and the tempters of the night, I beg you!"},{"t":"sd","id":304,"text":"Sleeps. Iachimo comes from the trunk."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["The crickets sing, and man's o'er-labour'd sense","Repairs itself by rest. Our Tarquin thus","Did softly press the rushes ere he waken'd","The chastity he wounded. Cytherea,","How bravely thou becom'st thy bed! fresh lily,","And whiter than the sheets! That I might touch!","But kiss; one kiss! Rubies unparagon'd,","How dearly they do't! 'Tis her breathing that","Perfumes the chamber thus. The flame o' th' taper","Bows toward her and would under-peep her lids","To see th' enclosed lights, now canopied","Under these windows white and azure, lac'd","With blue of heaven's own tinct. But my design","To note the chamber. I will write all down:","Such and such pictures; there the window; such","Th' adornment of her bed; the arras, figures—","Why, such and such; and the contents o' th' story.","Ah, but some natural notes about her body","Above ten thousand meaner movables","Would testify, t' enrich mine inventory.","O sleep, thou ape of death, lie dull upon her!","And be her sense but as a monument,","Thus in a chapel lying! Come off, come off;"],"id":305,"m":"The crickets are singing, and man's overworked senses are restoring themselves by rest. This is how our Tarquin stepped softly on the rushes before he woke the chastity he wounded. Venus, how beautifully you suit your bed! Fresh lily, whiter than the sheets! If only I could touch! Just kiss; one kiss! Matchless rubies, how sweetly they press together! It's her breath that perfumes the room like this. The candle flame bends toward her and wants to peep under her eyelids to see the lights shut in there, now curtained under these white and blue lids, laced with veins the colour of the sky. But my plan is to note the room. I'll write it all down: such and such pictures; the window there; her bed decorated like this; the tapestry, its figures, so and so, and what story it tells. Ah, but some natural marks on her body would be better evidence than ten thousand lesser furnishings, and would enrich my list. Oh sleep, you imitation of death, lie heavy on her, and let her senses be like a stone effigy lying in a chapel! Come off, come off;"},{"t":"sd","id":306,"text":"Taking off her bracelet."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","cont":1,"lines":["As slippery as the Gordian knot was hard!","'Tis mine; and this will witness outwardly,","As strongly as the conscience does within,","To th' madding of her lord. On her left breast","A mole cinque-spotted, like the crimson drops","I' th' bottom of a cowslip. Here's a voucher","Stronger than ever law could make; this secret","Will force him think I have pick'd the lock and ta'en","The treasure of her honour. No more. To what end?","Why should I write this down that's riveted,","Screw'd to my memory? She hath been reading late","The tale of Tereus; here the leaf's turn'd down","Where Philomel gave up. I have enough.","To th' trunk again, and shut the spring of it.","Swift, swift, you dragons of the night, that dawning","May bare the raven's eye! I lodge in fear;","Though this a heavenly angel, hell is here. [Clock strikes]","One, two, three. Time, time!"],"id":307,"m":"as slippery as the Gordian knot was tight! It's mine, and this will be outward proof, as strong as his conscience is within, to drive her lord mad. On her left breast there's a mole with five spots, like the red drops at the bottom of a cowslip. Here's proof stronger than any law could produce. This secret will force him to think I've picked the lock and taken the treasure of her honour. No more. What for? Why write down what's riveted and screwed into my memory? She's been reading late: the story of Tereus. Here's the page turned down where Philomel gave in. I have enough. Back into the trunk, and shut its spring. Hurry, hurry, you dragons that pull the night, so that dawn may open the raven's eye! I'm lodged in fear. Though this is a heavenly angel, hell is here. (A clock strikes.) One, two, three. Time, time!"},{"t":"sd","id":308,"text":"Exit into the trunk."},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"Cymbeline's palace. An ante-chamber adjoining Imogen's apartments.","id":309},{"t":"sd","id":310,"text":"Enter Cloten and Lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Your lordship is the most patient man in loss, the most","coldest that ever turn'd up ace."],"id":311,"m":"Your lordship is the most patient man when losing, the coolest who ever threw a one."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["It would make any man cold to lose."],"id":312,"m":"Losing would make any man cool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["But not every man patient after the noble temper of","your lordship. You are most hot and furious when you win."],"id":313,"m":"But not every man as patient as your lordship's noble temper. You're hot and furious only when you win."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Winning will put any man into courage. If I could get this","foolish Imogen, I should have gold enough. It's almost morning,","is't not?"],"id":314,"m":"Winning puts courage into any man. If I could get this foolish Imogen, I'd have gold enough. It's almost morning, isn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Day, my lord."],"id":315,"m":"It's day, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I would this music would come. I am advised to give her","music a mornings; they say it will penetrate."],"id":316,"m":"I wish those musicians would come. I've been advised to play her music in the mornings; they say it will get through to her."},{"t":"sd","id":317,"text":"Enter musicians."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","cont":1,"lines":["Come on, tune. If you can penetrate her with your fingering, so.","We'll try with tongue too. If none will do, let her remain; but","I'll never give o'er. First, a very excellent good-conceited","thing; after, a wonderful sweet air, with admirable rich words to","it—and then let her consider."],"id":318,"m":"Come on, tune up. If you can get through to her with your fingering, good; we'll try with the voice too. If nothing works, let her be, but I'll never give up. First a very fine, cleverly made piece; then a wonderful sweet tune with admirable rich words to it, and then let her think it over."},{"t":"sp","who":"MUSICIAN","lines":["~Hark, hark! the lark at heaven's gate sings,","~And Phoebus 'gins arise,","~His steeds to water at those springs","~On chalic'd flow'rs that lies;","~And winking Mary-buds begin","~To ope their golden eyes.","~With everything that pretty bin,","~My lady sweet, arise;","~Arise, arise!"],"id":319,"m":"(Song) Listen, the lark is singing at heaven's gate, and the sun god is rising to water his horses at the springs that lie in the cup-shaped flowers. The marigold buds are opening their sleepy golden eyes. With every pretty thing there is, my sweet lady, get up; get up, get up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","cont":1,"lines":["So, get you gone. If this penetrate, I will consider your music","the better; if it do not, it is a vice in her ears which","horsehairs and calves' guts, nor the voice of unpaved eunuch to","boot, can never amend."],"id":320,"m":"That'll do, off you go. If this gets through to her, I'll think the better of your music. If it doesn't, it's a defect in her ears that neither horsehair and catgut nor a eunuch's voice can ever fix."},{"t":"sd","id":321,"text":"Exeunt musicians."},{"t":"sd","id":322,"text":"Enter Cymbeline and Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Here comes the King."],"id":323,"m":"Here comes the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I am glad I was up so late, for that's the reason I was up","so early. He cannot choose but take this service I have done","fatherly.—Good morrow to your Majesty and to my gracious mother."],"id":324,"m":"I'm glad I stayed up so late, because that's why I was up so early. He can't help taking this effort of mine in a fatherly way. Good morning to your Majesty and to my gracious mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Attend you here the door of our stern daughter?","Will she not forth?"],"id":325,"m":"Are you waiting here at the door of our stubborn daughter? Won't she come out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I have assail'd her with musics, but she vouchsafes no","notice."],"id":326,"m":"I've attacked her with music, but she won't take any notice."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["The exile of her minion is too new;","She hath not yet forgot him; some more time","Must wear the print of his remembrance out,","And then she's yours."],"id":327,"m":"The banishment of her darling is too recent; she hasn't forgotten him yet. A little more time must wear away the imprint of his memory, and then she's yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["You are most bound to th' King,","Who lets go by no vantages that may","Prefer you to his daughter. Frame yourself","To orderly soliciting, and be friended","With aptness of the season; make denials","Increase your services; so seem as if","You were inspir'd to do those duties which","You tender to her; that you in all obey her,","Save when command to your dismission tends,","And therein you are senseless."],"id":328,"m":"You owe a great deal to the King, who lets no chance go by to recommend you to his daughter. Make yourself a proper suitor and choose your moments well. Let every refusal make you serve her more. Seem as if you were inspired to do the services you offer her; obey her in everything, except when she orders you to leave, and then act as if you hadn't heard."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Senseless? Not so."],"id":329,"m":"Not heard? Not likely."},{"t":"sd","id":330,"text":"Enter a Messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["So like you, sir, ambassadors from Rome;","The one is Caius Lucius."],"id":331,"m":"If you please, sir, ambassadors from Rome. One of them is Caius Lucius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["A worthy fellow,","Albeit he comes on angry purpose now;","But that's no fault of his. We must receive him","According to the honour of his sender;","And towards himself, his goodness forespent on us,","We must extend our notice. Our dear son,","When you have given good morning to your mistress,","Attend the Queen and us; we shall have need","T' employ you towards this Roman. Come, our queen."],"id":332,"m":"A worthy man, though he comes on an angry errand now; but that's not his fault. We must receive him as the honour of his master requires, and as for him personally, we must show him attention for his past kindness to us. Dear son, when you've said good morning to your lady, come to the Queen and me. We'll need you to deal with this Roman. Come, my queen."},{"t":"sd","id":333,"text":"Exeunt all but Cloten."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["If she be up, I'll speak with her; if not,","Let her lie still and dream. By your leave, ho! [Knocks]","I know her women are about her; what","If I do line one of their hands? 'Tis gold","Which buys admittance; oft it doth-yea, and makes","Diana's rangers false themselves, yield up","Their deer to th' stand o' th' stealer; and 'tis gold","Which makes the true man kill'd and saves the thief;","Nay, sometime hangs both thief and true man. What","Can it not do and undo? I will make","One of her women lawyer to me, for","I yet not understand the case myself.","By your leave. [Knocks]"],"id":334,"m":"If she's up, I'll speak to her; if not, let her lie and dream. Excuse me, hello! (He knocks.) I know her women are with her. What if I tip one of them? It's gold that buys entry; it often does, yes, and makes Diana's own gamekeepers betray her, and drive their deer to the poacher's stand. And it's gold that gets the honest man killed and saves the thief; no, sometimes hangs both thief and honest man. What can't it do and undo? I'll make one of her women my lawyer, because I don't understand the case myself yet. Excuse me. (He knocks.)"},{"t":"sd","id":335,"text":"Enter a Lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Who's there that knocks?"],"id":336,"m":"Who's knocking there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["A gentleman."],"id":337,"m":"A gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["No more?"],"id":338,"m":"Nothing more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Yes, and a gentlewoman's son."],"id":339,"m":"Yes, a gentlewoman's son."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["That's more","Than some whose tailors are as dear as yours","Can justly boast of. What's your lordship's pleasure?"],"id":340,"m":"That's more than some whose tailors cost as much as yours can truthfully claim. What does your lordship want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Your lady's person; is she ready?"],"id":341,"m":"Your lady in person. Is she dressed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Ay,","To keep her chamber."],"id":342,"m":"Yes: to stay in her room."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["There is gold for you; sell me your good report."],"id":343,"m":"Here's some gold for you. Sell me your good word."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["How? My good name? or to report of you","What I shall think is good? The Princess!"],"id":344,"m":"What? My good name? Or to say about you what I think good? The Princess!"},{"t":"sd","id":345,"text":"Enter Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Good morrow, fairest sister. Your sweet hand."],"id":346,"m":"Good morning, fairest sister. Your sweet hand."},{"t":"sd","id":347,"text":"Exit Lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Good morrow, sir. You lay out too much pains","For purchasing but trouble. The thanks I give","Is telling you that I am poor of thanks,","And scarce can spare them."],"id":348,"m":"Good morning, sir. You take too much trouble to buy nothing but trouble. My only thanks is to tell you I'm short of thanks and can hardly spare any."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Still I swear I love you."],"id":349,"m":"Still, I swear I love you."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["If you but said so, 'twere as deep with me.","If you swear still, your recompense is still","That I regard it not."],"id":350,"m":"If you only said so, it would mean as much to me. If you keep swearing, your reward will always be that I don't care."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["This is no answer."],"id":351,"m":"That's no answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["But that you shall not say I yield, being silent,","I would not speak. I pray you spare me. Faith,","I shall unfold equal discourtesy","To your best kindness; one of your great knowing","Should learn, being taught, forbearance."],"id":352,"m":"I wouldn't speak at all, except that you'd say my silence meant I'd given in. Please leave me alone. Honestly, I'll show you as much rudeness as you show me kindness. A man of your great understanding should learn, once taught, to hold back."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["To leave you in your madness 'twere my sin;","I will not."],"id":353,"m":"It would be a sin to leave you in your madness. I won't."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Fools are not mad folks."],"id":354,"m":"Fools don't cure mad people."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Do you call me fool?"],"id":355,"m":"Are you calling me a fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["As I am mad, I do;","If you'll be patient, I'll no more be mad;","That cures us both. I am much sorry, sir,","You put me to forget a lady's manners","By being so verbal; and learn now, for all,","That I, which know my heart, do here pronounce,","By th' very truth of it, I care not for you,","And am so near the lack of charity","To accuse myself I hate you; which I had rather","You felt than make't my boast."],"id":356,"m":"Since I'm mad, yes. If you'll be patient, I'll stop being mad; that cures us both. I'm very sorry, sir, that you make me forget a lady's manners by speaking so plainly, but learn once and for all that I, who know my own heart, declare here, by its very truth, that I don't care for you, and I'm so close to lacking charity that I'd accuse myself of hating you, which I'd rather you felt than heard me boast of."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["You sin against","Obedience, which you owe your father. For","The contract you pretend with that base wretch,","One bred of alms and foster'd with cold dishes,","With scraps o' th' court—it is no contract, none.","And though it be allowed in meaner parties—","Yet who than he more mean?—to knit their souls—","On whom there is no more dependency","But brats and beggary—in self-figur'd knot,","Yet you are curb'd from that enlargement by","The consequence o' th' crown, and must not foil","The precious note of it with a base slave,","A hilding for a livery, a squire's cloth,","A pantler—not so eminent!"],"id":357,"m":"You sin against the obedience you owe your father. As for the marriage you claim with that low wretch, brought up on charity and fed on cold leftovers, scraps from the court: it's no marriage, none. And though lower people (but who's lower than he?) are allowed to tie the knot themselves, when all they have to depend on is brats and poverty, you are held back from that freedom by the importance of the crown, and mustn't stain its precious name with a low slave, a lackey fit for a servant's uniform, a squire's cast-off, a pantry boy, and not even that grand!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Profane fellow!","Wert thou the son of Jupiter, and no more","But what thou art besides, thou wert too base","To be his groom. Thou wert dignified enough,","Even to the point of envy, if 'twere made","Comparative for your virtues to be styl'd","The under-hangman of his kingdom, and hated","For being preferr'd so well."],"id":358,"m":"Blasphemous man! If you were the son of Jupiter, and otherwise no more than you are, you'd be too low to be his groom. You'd be honoured enough, even to the point of being envied, if, judging by your merits, you were made under-hangman of his kingdom, and hated for being promoted so high."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["The south fog rot him!"],"id":359,"m":"May the foggy south wind rot him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["He never can meet more mischance than come","To be but nam'd of thee. His mean'st garment","That ever hath but clipp'd his body is dearer","In my respect than all the hairs above thee,","Were they all made such men. How now, Pisanio!"],"id":360,"m":"He can never meet worse luck than to be named by you. His humblest garment that ever touched his body is more precious to me than all the hairs on your head, even if every one were turned into a man like you. Hello, Pisanio!"},{"t":"sd","id":361,"text":"Enter Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["'His garments'! Now the devil—"],"id":362,"m":"\"His garment\"! Now the devil..."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["To Dorothy my woman hie thee presently."],"id":363,"m":"Go to Dorothy, my maid, at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["'His garment'!"],"id":364,"m":"\"His garment\"!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I am sprited with a fool;","Frighted, and ang'red worse. Go bid my woman","Search for a jewel that too casually","Hath left mine arm. It was thy master's; shrew me,","If I would lose it for a revenue","Of any king's in Europe! I do think","I saw't this morning; confident I am","Last night 'twas on mine arm; I kiss'd it.","I hope it be not gone to tell my lord","That I kiss aught but he."],"id":365,"m":"I'm haunted by a fool, frightened and worse, angered. Go and tell my maid to look for a jewel that has slipped off my arm by accident. It was your master's. Curse me if I'd lose it for the income of any king in Europe! I think I saw it this morning; I'm certain it was on my arm last night: I kissed it. I hope it hasn't gone to tell my lord that I kiss anything but him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["'Twill not be lost."],"id":366,"m":"It won't be lost."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I hope so. Go and search."],"id":367,"m":"I hope not. Go and search."},{"t":"sd","id":368,"text":"Exit Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["You have abus'd me.","'His meanest garment'!"],"id":369,"m":"You've insulted me. \"His humblest garment\"!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Ay, I said so, sir.","If you will make 't an action, call witness to 't."],"id":370,"m":"Yes, I said so, sir. If you want to take me to court for it, call witnesses."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I will inform your father."],"id":371,"m":"I'll tell your father."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Your mother too.","She's my good lady and will conceive, I hope,","But the worst of me. So I leave you, sir,","To th' worst of discontent."],"id":372,"m":"And your mother too. She's my good friend and will think only the worst of me, I expect. So I leave you, sir, to the worst of bad moods."},{"t":"sd","id":373,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I'll be reveng'd.","'His mean'st garment'! Well."],"id":374,"m":"I'll get revenge. \"His humblest garment\"! Very well."},{"t":"sd","id":375,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"Rome. Philario's house.","id":376},{"t":"sd","id":377,"text":"Enter Posthumus and Philario."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Fear it not, sir; I would I were so sure","To win the King as I am bold her honour","Will remain hers."],"id":378,"m":"Don't worry, sir. I wish I were as sure of winning over the King as I am confident her honour will stay hers."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["What means do you make to him?"],"id":379,"m":"What steps are you taking with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Not any; but abide the change of time,","Quake in the present winter's state, and wish","That warmer days would come. In these fear'd hopes","I barely gratify your love; they failing,","I must die much your debtor."],"id":380,"m":"None; I just wait for times to change, shiver in this present winter, and wish warmer days would come. With these fearful hopes I can barely repay your kindness; if they fail, I'll die deeply in your debt."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Your very goodness and your company","O'erpays all I can do. By this your king","Hath heard of great Augustus. Caius Lucius","Will do's commission throughly; and I think","He'll grant the tribute, send th' arrearages,","Or look upon our Romans, whose remembrance","Is yet fresh in their grief."],"id":381,"m":"Your goodness and your company more than repay anything I can do. By now your king will have heard from the great Augustus. Caius Lucius will carry out his orders thoroughly, and I think your king will agree to the tribute and send the back payments, or else face our Romans, whom the Britons still remember with pain."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I do believe","Statist though I am none, nor like to be,","That this will prove a war; and you shall hear","The legions now in Gallia sooner landed","In our not-fearing Britain than have tidings","Of any penny tribute paid. Our countrymen","Are men more order'd than when Julius Caesar","Smil'd at their lack of skill, but found their courage","Worthy his frowning at. Their discipline,","Now mingled with their courages, will make known","To their approvers they are people such","That mend upon the world."],"id":382,"m":"I believe, though I'm no statesman and never will be, that this will turn into a war, and that you'll hear of the legions now in France landing in our unafraid Britain before you hear of a penny of tribute paid. Our countrymen are better organised than when Julius Caesar smiled at their lack of skill but found their courage worth frowning at. Their discipline, now joined to their courage, will show anyone who tests them that they're a people who improve with time."},{"t":"sd","id":383,"text":"Enter Iachimo."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["See! Iachimo!"],"id":384,"m":"Look! Iachimo!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["The swiftest harts have posted you by land,","And winds of all the comers kiss'd your sails,","To make your vessel nimble."],"id":385,"m":"The swiftest deer must have carried you over land, and the winds from every quarter kissed your sails to speed your ship."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Welcome, sir."],"id":386,"m":"Welcome, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I hope the briefness of your answer made","The speediness of your return."],"id":387,"m":"I hope a short answer is what made your return so quick."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Your lady","Is one of the fairest that I have look'd upon."],"id":388,"m":"Your lady is one of the most beautiful women I've ever seen."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["And therewithal the best; or let her beauty","Look through a casement to allure false hearts,","And be false with them."],"id":389,"m":"And the best as well; otherwise let her beauty lean out of a window to lure false hearts, and be false with them."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Here are letters for you."],"id":390,"m":"Here are letters for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Their tenour good, I trust."],"id":391,"m":"Good news in them, I trust."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["'Tis very like."],"id":392,"m":"Very likely."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Was Caius Lucius in the Britain court","When you were there?"],"id":393,"m":"Was Caius Lucius at the British court when you were there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["He was expected then,","But not approach'd."],"id":394,"m":"He was expected, but hadn't arrived."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["All is well yet.","Sparkles this stone as it was wont, or is't not","Too dull for your good wearing?"],"id":395,"m":"All's well so far. Does this stone sparkle as it used to, or is it too dull for you to wear?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["If I have lost it,","I should have lost the worth of it in gold.","I'll make a journey twice as far t' enjoy","A second night of such sweet shortness which","Was mine in Britain; for the ring is won."],"id":396,"m":"If I'd lost it, I'd have lost its value in gold. I'd travel twice as far to enjoy a second night as sweet and short as the one I had in Britain; because the ring is won."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["The stone's too hard to come by."],"id":397,"m":"The stone is too hard to get."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Not a whit,","Your lady being so easy."],"id":398,"m":"Not at all, since your lady is so easy."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Make not, sir,","Your loss your sport. I hope you know that we","Must not continue friends."],"id":399,"m":"Don't make a joke of your loss, sir. I hope you know we can't remain friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Good sir, we must,","If you keep covenant. Had I not brought","The knowledge of your mistress home, I grant","We were to question farther; but I now","Profess myself the winner of her honour,","Together with your ring; and not the wronger","Of her or you, having proceeded but","By both your wills."],"id":400,"m":"Good sir, we must, if you keep our agreement. If I hadn't brought back knowledge of your lady, I grant we'd have to fight it out. But now I declare myself the winner of her honour, and of your ring, and I haven't wronged her or you, since I only acted with both your consent."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["If you can make't apparent","That you have tasted her in bed, my hand","And ring is yours. If not, the foul opinion","You had of her pure honour gains or loses","Your sword or mine, or masterless leaves both","To who shall find them."],"id":401,"m":"If you can prove you've had her in bed, my hand and ring are yours. If not, your foul opinion of her pure honour will win or lose your sword or mine, or leave both without owners for whoever finds them."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Sir, my circumstances,","Being so near the truth as I will make them,","Must first induce you to believe—whose strength","I will confirm with oath; which I doubt not","You'll give me leave to spare when you shall find","You need it not."],"id":402,"m":"Sir, my details, which I'll make so close to the truth, must first make you believe. I'll back them with an oath, which I'm sure you'll let me skip when you see you don't need it."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Proceed."],"id":403,"m":"Go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["First, her bedchamber,","Where I confess I slept not, but profess","Had that was well worth watching-it was hang'd","With tapestry of silk and silver; the story,","Proud Cleopatra when she met her Roman","And Cydnus swell'd above the banks, or for","The press of boats or pride. A piece of work","So bravely done, so rich, that it did strive","In workmanship and value; which I wonder'd","Could be so rarely and exactly wrought,","Since the true life on't was—"],"id":404,"m":"First, her bedroom (where, I admit, I didn't sleep, but I declare I had what was well worth staying awake for). It was hung with tapestry of silk and silver showing proud Cleopatra meeting her Roman, and the river Cydnus swelling over its banks, either from the crowd of boats or from pride. A piece of work so finely and richly done that its craftsmanship competed with its value. I wondered how it could be made so rarely and exactly, since the true life of it was..."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["This is true;","And this you might have heard of here, by me","Or by some other."],"id":405,"m":"That's true. But you could have heard about it here, from me or someone else."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["More particulars","Must justify my knowledge."],"id":406,"m":"More details must prove what I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["So they must,","Or do your honour injury."],"id":407,"m":"They must, or you injure your honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["The chimney","Is south the chamber, and the chimneypiece","Chaste Dian bathing. Never saw I figures","So likely to report themselves. The cutter","Was as another nature, dumb; outwent her,","Motion and breath left out."],"id":408,"m":"The fireplace is on the south side of the room, and the carving over it shows chaste Diana bathing. I never saw figures so close to speaking for themselves. The sculptor was like a second nature, only silent; he outdid her, except that he left out movement and breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["This is a thing","Which you might from relation likewise reap,","Being, as it is, much spoke of."],"id":409,"m":"That's also something you could have picked up from hearsay, since it's much talked about."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["The roof o' th' chamber","With golden cherubins is fretted; her andirons—","I had forgot them—were two winking Cupids","Of silver, each on one foot standing, nicely","Depending on their brands."],"id":410,"m":"The ceiling of the room is decorated with golden cherubs. Her andirons (I'd forgotten them) were two silver Cupids with their eyes shut, each standing on one foot, neatly leaning on their torches."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["This is her honour!","Let it be granted you have seen all this, and praise","Be given to your remembrance; the description","Of what is in her chamber nothing saves","The wager you have laid."],"id":411,"m":"And this is her honour! Suppose you've seen all this, and well done for remembering it: describing what's in her room doesn't win the bet you made."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Then, if you can, [Shows the bracelet]","Be pale. I beg but leave to air this jewel. See!","And now 'tis up again. It must be married","To that your diamond; I'll keep them."],"id":412,"m":"Then turn pale, if you can. (He shows the bracelet.) Just let me give this jewel some air. Look! And now it's put away again. It must be married to that diamond of yours; I'll keep them both."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Jove!","Once more let me behold it. Is it that","Which I left with her?"],"id":413,"m":"By Jupiter! Let me look at it once more. Is it the one I left with her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Sir—I thank her—that.","She stripp'd it from her arm; I see her yet;","Her pretty action did outsell her gift,","And yet enrich'd it too. She gave it me, and said","She priz'd it once."],"id":414,"m":"Yes, sir (and I thank her for it), that one. She slipped it off her arm; I can still see her. Her pretty way of doing it was worth more than the gift, and yet added to its value too. She gave it to me and said she had once treasured it."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["May be she pluck'd it of","To send it me."],"id":415,"m":"Maybe she took it off to send it to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["She writes so to you, doth she?"],"id":416,"m":"She writes and tells you so, does she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["O, no, no, no! 'tis true. Here, take this too;"],"id":417,"m":"Oh, no, no, no! It's true. Here, take this too."},{"t":"sd","id":418,"text":"Gives the ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","cont":1,"lines":["It is a basilisk unto mine eye,","Kills me to look on't. Let there be no honour","Where there is beauty; truth where semblance; love","Where there's another man. The vows of women","Of no more bondage be to where they are made","Than they are to their virtues, which is nothing.","O, above measure false!"],"id":419,"m":"It's a basilisk to my eye; it kills me to look at it. Let there be no honour where there's beauty, no truth where there's an appearance of it, no love where there's another man. Let women's vows be no more binding to those they're made to than women are to their own virtues, which is not at all. Oh, false beyond measure!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Have patience, sir,","And take your ring again; 'tis not yet won.","It may be probable she lost it, or","Who knows if one her women, being corrupted","Hath stol'n it from her?"],"id":420,"m":"Be patient, sir, and take your ring back. It isn't won yet. She may well have lost it; or who knows whether one of her women, bribed, stole it from her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Very true;","And so I hope he came by't. Back my ring.","Render to me some corporal sign about her,","More evident than this; for this was stol'n."],"id":421,"m":"Very true, and I hope that's how he got it. Give me back my ring. Give me some physical mark on her body, clearer than this, because this was stolen."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["By Jupiter, I had it from her arm!"],"id":422,"m":"By Jupiter, I took it from her arm!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Hark you, he swears; by Jupiter he swears.","'Tis true—nay, keep the ring, 'tis true. I am sure","She would not lose it. Her attendants are","All sworn and honourable—they induc'd to steal it!","And by a stranger! No, he hath enjoy'd her.","The cognizance of her incontinency","Is this: she hath bought the name of whore thus dearly.","There, take thy hire; and all the fiends of hell","Divide themselves between you!"],"id":423,"m":"Listen, he swears; by Jupiter he swears. It's true. No, keep the ring; it's true. I'm sure she wouldn't lose it. Her attendants are all under oath and honourable. Them, persuaded to steal it! And by a stranger! No, he's had her. This is the badge of her lust: she has bought the name of whore at this high price. There, take your fee, and may all the devils of hell share themselves between you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Sir, be patient;","This is not strong enough to be believ'd","Of one persuaded well of."],"id":424,"m":"Sir, be patient. This isn't strong enough to make you believe it of someone so well thought of."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Never talk on't;","She hath been colted by him."],"id":425,"m":"Don't talk about it. He's ridden her like a colt."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["If you seek","For further satisfying, under her breast—","Worthy the pressing—lies a mole, right proud","Of that most delicate lodging. By my life,","I kiss'd it; and it gave me present hunger","To feed again, though full. You do remember","This stain upon her?"],"id":426,"m":"If you want more proof: under her breast, which is worth pressing, there's a mole, very proud of its delicate lodging. On my life, I kissed it, and it made me hungry at once to feed again, though I was full. You remember this mark on her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Ay, and it doth confirm","Another stain, as big as hell can hold,","Were there no more but it."],"id":427,"m":"Yes, and it confirms another stain, as big as hell can hold, even if there were no other."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Will you hear more?"],"id":428,"m":"Will you hear more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Spare your arithmetic; never count the turns.","Once, and a million!"],"id":429,"m":"Spare your arithmetic; don't count the times. Once, and a million!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["I'll be sworn—"],"id":430,"m":"I'll swear..."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["No swearing.","If you will swear you have not done't, you lie;","And I will kill thee if thou dost deny","Thou'st made me cuckold."],"id":431,"m":"No swearing. If you swear you haven't done it, you're lying, and I'll kill you if you deny you've made me a cuckold."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["I'll deny nothing."],"id":432,"m":"I'll deny nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["O that I had her here to tear her limb-meal!","I will go there and do't, i' th' court, before","Her father. I'll do something—"],"id":433,"m":"Oh, if I had her here, I'd tear her limb from limb! I'll go there and do it, at court, in front of her father. I'll do something..."},{"t":"sd","id":434,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILARIO","lines":["Quite besides","The government of patience! You have won.","Let's follow him and pervert the present wrath","He hath against himself."],"id":435,"m":"Completely beyond the control of patience! You've won. Let's follow him and turn aside the rage he has now against himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["With all my heart."],"id":436,"m":"With all my heart."},{"t":"sd","id":437,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"V","loc":"Rome. Another room in Philario's house.","id":438},{"t":"sd","id":439,"text":"Enter Posthumus."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Is there no way for men to be, but women","Must be half-workers? We are all bastards,","And that most venerable man which I","Did call my father was I know not where","When I was stamp'd. Some coiner with his tools","Made me a counterfeit; yet my mother seem'd","The Dian of that time. So doth my wife","The nonpareil of this. O, vengeance, vengeance!","Me of my lawful pleasure she restrain'd,","And pray'd me oft forbearance; did it with","A pudency so rosy, the sweet view on't","Might well have warm'd old Saturn; that I thought her","As chaste as unsunn'd snow. O, all the devils!","This yellow Iachimo in an hour—was't not?","Or less!—at first? Perchance he spoke not, but,","Like a full-acorn'd boar, a German one,","Cried 'O!' and mounted; found no opposition","But what he look'd for should oppose and she","Should from encounter guard. Could I find out","The woman's part in me! For there's no motion","That tends to vice in man but I affirm","It is the woman's part. Be it lying, note it,","The woman's; flattering, hers; deceiving, hers;","Lust and rank thoughts, hers, hers; revenges, hers;","Ambitions, covetings, change of prides, disdain,","Nice longing, slanders, mutability,","All faults that man may name, nay, that hell knows,","Why, hers, in part or all; but rather all;","For even to vice","They are not constant, but are changing still","One vice but of a minute old for one","Not half so old as that. I'll write against them,","Detest them, curse them. Yet 'tis greater skill","In a true hate to pray they have their will:","The very devils cannot plague them better."],"id":440,"m":"Is there no way for men to be born without women doing half the work? We're all bastards, and that most respected man I called my father was who knows where when I was minted. Some forger with his tools made me a fake coin; and yet my mother seemed the Diana of her day, as my wife seems the one and only of this one. Oh, revenge, revenge! She held me back from my lawful pleasure and often begged me to be patient, and did it with such rosy modesty that the sweet sight of it could have warmed old Saturn, so that I thought her as chaste as snow the sun has never touched. Oh, all the devils! This sallow Iachimo, in an hour, wasn't it? Or less, right from the start? Maybe he didn't even speak but, like a German boar stuffed with acorns, cried \"Oh!\" and mounted, and found no resistance except what he expected to meet, the part she should have guarded. If only I could find the woman's part in me! For there's no impulse toward vice in a man that isn't, I swear, the woman's part. If it's lying, mark it, it's the woman's; flattering, hers; deceiving, hers; lust and filthy thoughts, hers, hers; revenge, hers; ambition, greed, changing vanities, scorn, fussy cravings, slander, fickleness, every fault a man can name, no, that hell knows: hers, in part or all; but rather all. Because they aren't even constant in their vices, but keep swapping one that's a minute old for one not half as old. I'll write against them, hate them, curse them. And yet the cleverest true hatred is to pray they get what they want: the devils themselves couldn't punish them better."},{"t":"sd","id":441,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Britain. A hall in Cymbeline's palace.","id":442},{"t":"sd","id":443,"text":"Enter in state, Cymbeline, Queen, Cloten, and Lords at one door, and at another Caius Lucius and attendants."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Now say, what would Augustus Caesar with us?"],"id":444,"m":"Now tell me, what does Augustus Caesar want with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["When Julius Caesar—whose remembrance yet","Lives in men's eyes, and will to ears and tongues","Be theme and hearing ever—was in this Britain,","And conquer'd it, Cassibelan, thine uncle,","Famous in Caesar's praises no whit less","Than in his feats deserving it, for him","And his succession granted Rome a tribute,","Yearly three thousand pounds, which by thee lately","Is left untender'd."],"id":445,"m":"When Julius Caesar (whose memory still lives in men's eyes, and will always be a subject for tongues to tell and ears to hear) was here in Britain and conquered it, your uncle Cassibelan, as famous for Caesar's praise as for the deeds that earned it, granted Rome a yearly tribute for himself and his heirs of three thousand pounds, which you have recently failed to pay."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And, to kill the marvel,","Shall be so ever."],"id":446,"m":"And, to end the surprise, never will again."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["There be many Caesars","Ere such another Julius. Britain is","A world by itself, and we will nothing pay","For wearing our own noses."],"id":447,"m":"There'll be many Caesars before another Julius. Britain is a world of its own, and we'll pay nothing for wearing our own noses."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["That opportunity,","Which then they had to take from 's, to resume","We have again. Remember, sir, my liege,","The kings your ancestors, together with","The natural bravery of your isle, which stands","As Neptune's park, ribb'd and pal'd in","With rocks unscalable and roaring waters,","With sands that will not bear your enemies' boats","But suck them up to th' top-mast. A kind of conquest","Caesar made here; but made not here his brag","Of 'came, and saw, and overcame.' With shame—","The first that ever touch'd him—he was carried","From off our coast, twice beaten; and his shipping—","Poor ignorant baubles!—on our terrible seas,","Like egg-shells mov'd upon their surges, crack'd","As easily 'gainst our rocks; for joy whereof","The fam'd Cassibelan, who was once at point—","O, giglot fortune!—to master Caesar's sword,","Made Lud's Town with rejoicing fires bright","And Britons strut with courage."],"id":448,"m":"That chance they had then to take it from us, we now have to take it back. Remember, sir, my king, the kings your ancestors, and the natural strength of your island, which stands like Neptune's park, ribbed and fenced with rocks that can't be climbed and roaring waters, with sands that won't hold up your enemies' boats but suck them down to the top of the mast. Caesar made a sort of conquest here, but he didn't make his boast of \"I came, I saw, I conquered\" here. With shame, the first he ever felt, he was carried off our coast, beaten twice; and his ships, poor ignorant toys, were tossed on our terrible seas like eggshells on the waves and cracked just as easily against our rocks. To celebrate, the famous Cassibelan, who was once on the point (oh, fickle fortune!) of mastering Caesar's sword, lit up London with bonfires and made Britons strut with courage."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Come, there's no more tribute to be paid. Our kingdom is","stronger than it was at that time; and, as I said, there is no","moe such Caesars. Other of them may have crook'd noses; but to","owe such straight arms, none."],"id":449,"m":"Come on, there's no more tribute to pay. Our kingdom is stronger than it was then, and as I said, there are no more Caesars like that one. Others may have hooked noses, but none of them has such strong arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Son, let your mother end."],"id":450,"m":"Son, let your mother finish."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["We have yet many among us can gripe as hard as Cassibelan.","I do not say I am one; but I have a hand. Why tribute? Why should","we pay tribute? If Caesar can hide the sun from us with a blanket,","or put the moon in his pocket, we will pay him tribute for light;","else, sir, no more tribute, pray you now."],"id":451,"m":"We still have plenty of men who can grip as hard as Cassibelan. I don't say I'm one of them, but I have a hand. Why tribute? Why should we pay tribute? If Caesar can hide the sun from us with a blanket or put the moon in his pocket, we'll pay him tribute for light. Otherwise, sir, no more tribute, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["You must know,","Till the injurious Romans did extort","This tribute from us, we were free. Caesar's ambition—","Which swell'd so much that it did almost stretch","The sides o' th' world—against all colour here","Did put the yoke upon's; which to shake of","Becomes a warlike people, whom we reckon","Ourselves to be."],"id":452,"m":"You must know that until the unjust Romans extorted this tribute from us, we were free. Caesar's ambition, which swelled so much it nearly stretched the sides of the world, put the yoke on us here with no right to do it. Shaking it off is fitting for a warlike people, which we consider ourselves to be."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["We do."],"id":453,"m":"We do."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Say then to Caesar,","Our ancestor was that Mulmutius which","Ordain'd our laws—whose use the sword of Caesar","Hath too much mangled; whose repair and franchise","Shall, by the power we hold, be our good deed,","Though Rome be therefore angry. Mulmutius made our laws,","Who was the first of Britain which did put","His brows within a golden crown, and call'd","Himself a king."],"id":454,"m":"Then tell Caesar this. Our ancestor was that Mulmutius who made our laws, laws Caesar's sword has badly mangled. Restoring them and their freedom, by the power we hold, will be our good deed, even if Rome is angry about it. Mulmutius made our laws, and he was the first in Britain to put a golden crown on his head and call himself king."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I am sorry, Cymbeline,","That I am to pronounce Augustus Caesar—","Caesar, that hath moe kings his servants than","Thyself domestic officers—thine enemy.","Receive it from me, then: war and confusion","In Caesar's name pronounce I 'gainst thee; look","For fury not to be resisted. Thus defied,","I thank thee for myself."],"id":455,"m":"I'm sorry, Cymbeline, that I have to declare Augustus Caesar, who has more kings as servants than you have household officers, your enemy. Take it from me, then: in Caesar's name I declare war and destruction against you. Expect a fury that can't be resisted. Having defied you like this, I thank you for my own treatment."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Thou art welcome, Caius.","Thy Caesar knighted me; my youth I spent","Much under him; of him I gather'd honour,","Which he to seek of me again, perforce,","Behoves me keep at utterance. I am perfect","That the Pannonians and Dalmatians for","Their liberties are now in arms, a precedent","Which not to read would show the Britons cold;","So Caesar shall not find them."],"id":456,"m":"You're welcome, Caius. Your Caesar knighted me; I spent much of my youth serving him. From him I gained honour, and since he's now trying to take it back from me by force, I'm bound to defend it to the death. I know for certain that the Pannonians and Dalmatians are now in arms for their freedom, an example the Britons would be cold not to follow. Caesar won't find them so."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Let proof speak."],"id":457,"m":"Let the outcome speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["His majesty bids you welcome. Make pastime with us a day or","two, or longer. If you seek us afterwards in other terms, you","shall find us in our salt-water girdle. If you beat us out of it,","it is yours; if you fall in the adventure, our crows shall fare","the better for you; and there's an end."],"id":458,"m":"His majesty welcomes you. Enjoy yourself with us for a day or two, or longer. If you come looking for us later on other terms, you'll find us inside our belt of salt water. If you beat us out of it, it's yours. If you die trying, our crows will eat better because of you; and that's the end of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["So, sir."],"id":459,"m":"Very well, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["I know your master's pleasure, and he mine;","All the remain is, welcome."],"id":460,"m":"I know your master's wishes, and he knows mine. All that's left is to say welcome."},{"t":"sd","id":461,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Britain. Another room in Cymbeline's palace.","id":462},{"t":"sd","id":463,"text":"Enter Pisanio reading of a letter."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["How? of adultery? Wherefore write you not","What monsters her accuse? Leonatus!","O master, what a strange infection","Is fall'n into thy ear! What false Italian—","As poisonous-tongu'd as handed—hath prevail'd","On thy too ready hearing? Disloyal? No.","She's punish'd for her truth, and undergoes,","More goddess-like than wife-like, such assaults","As would take in some virtue. O my master!","Thy mind to her is now as low as were","Thy fortunes. How? that I should murder her?","Upon the love, and truth, and vows, which I","Have made to thy command? I, her? Her blood?","If it be so to do good service, never","Let me be counted serviceable. How look I","That I should seem to lack humanity","So much as this fact comes to? [Reads] 'Do't. The letter","That I have sent her, by her own command","Shall give thee opportunity.' O damn'd paper,","Black as the ink that's on thee! Senseless bauble,","Art thou a fedary for this act, and look'st","So virgin-like without? Lo, here she comes."],"id":464,"m":"What? Adultery? Why don't you write what monsters accuse her? Leonatus! Oh, master, what strange infection has got into your ear! What false Italian, as poisonous of tongue as of hand, has worked on your too-willing hearing? Disloyal? No. She's punished for her faithfulness and endures, more like a goddess than a wife, attacks that would conquer most virtue. Oh, my master! Your opinion of her is now as low as your fortunes were. What? I should murder her? Because of the love and loyalty and vows I've given to serve you? I, kill her? Shed her blood? If that's good service, never call me a good servant. How do I look, that I should seem to lack humanity as much as this deed requires? (Reading) \"Do it. The letter I've sent her will, by her own wish, give you the chance.\" Oh, damned paper, as black as the ink on you! Senseless thing, are you an accomplice in this act, and look so virgin-white outside? Look, here she comes."},{"t":"sd","id":465,"text":"Enter Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","cont":1,"lines":["I am ignorant in what I am commanded."],"id":466,"m":"I'll pretend I don't know what I've been ordered to do."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["How now, Pisanio!"],"id":467,"m":"Hello, Pisanio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Madam, here is a letter from my lord."],"id":468,"m":"Madam, here's a letter from my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Who? thy lord? That is my lord—Leonatus?","O, learn'd indeed were that astronomer","That knew the stars as I his characters—","He'd lay the future open. You good gods,","Let what is here contain'd relish of love,","Of my lord's health, of his content; yet not","That we two are asunder—let that grieve him!","Some griefs are med'cinable; that is one of them,","For it doth physic love—of his content,","All but in that. Good wax, thy leave. Blest be","You bees that make these locks of counsel! Lovers","And men in dangerous bonds pray not alike;","Though forfeiters you cast in prison, yet","You clasp young Cupid's tables. Good news, gods!"],"id":469,"m":"From whom? Your lord? That's my lord, Leonatus? Oh, that astronomer would be learned indeed who knew the stars as well as I know his handwriting: he'd lay the future open. Kind gods, let what's in here taste of love, of my lord's health, of his happiness; but not happiness that we're apart; let that grieve him! Some griefs are healing, and that's one, because it keeps love healthy. Of his happiness in everything but that. Good wax, excuse me. Blessed be you bees who make these seals for secrets! Lovers and men in dangerous debt don't pray alike: though you throw debtors in prison by sealing their bonds, you also seal young Cupid's love letters. Good news, gods!"},{"t":"sd","id":470,"text":"Reads."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","cont":1,"lines":["'Justice and your father's wrath, should he take me in his","dominion, could not be so cruel to me as you, O the dearest of","creatures, would even renew me with your eyes. Take notice that I","am in Cambria, at Milford Haven. What your own love will out of","this advise you, follow. So he wishes you all happiness that","remains loyal to his vow, and your increasing in love","Leonatus Posthumus.'","O for a horse with wings! Hear'st thou, Pisanio?","He is at Milford Haven. Read, and tell me","How far 'tis thither. If one of mean affairs","May plod it in a week, why may not I","Glide thither in a day? Then, true Pisanio—","Who long'st like me to see thy lord, who long'st—","O, let me 'bate!—but not like me, yet long'st,","But in a fainter kind—O, not like me,","For mine's beyond beyond!-say, and speak thick—","Love's counsellor should fill the bores of hearing","To th' smothering of the sense—how far it is","To this same blessed Milford. And by th' way","Tell me how Wales was made so happy as","T' inherit such a haven. But first of all,","How we may steal from hence; and for the gap","That we shall make in time from our hence-going","And our return, to excuse. But first, how get hence.","Why should excuse be born or ere begot?","We'll talk of that hereafter. Prithee speak,","How many score of miles may we well ride","'Twixt hour and hour?"],"id":471,"m":"(Reading) \"Justice and your father's anger, if he caught me in his kingdom, could not be so cruel to me that you, dearest of creatures, could not restore me with your eyes. Know that I am in Wales, at Milford Haven. Follow whatever your own love advises you from this. So wishes you all happiness he who remains loyal to his vow, and grows in love for you, Leonatus Posthumus.\" Oh, for a horse with wings! Do you hear, Pisanio? He's at Milford Haven. Read it, and tell me how far it is there. If someone on ordinary business can plod it in a week, why can't I glide there in a day? So, faithful Pisanio, who longs like me to see your lord, who longs (oh, let me take that back) not like me, but still longs, in a fainter way (oh, not like me, because mine is beyond beyond!), tell me, and speak fast (love's adviser should cram the ears till they choke), how far it is to this blessed Milford. And while you're at it, tell me how Wales was lucky enough to own such a harbour. But first of all, how we can slip away from here, and how to excuse the gap our absence will leave between going and coming back. But first, how to get away. Why invent an excuse before there's anything to excuse? We'll talk about that later. Please tell me how many miles we can ride in an hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["One score 'twixt sun and sun,","Madam, 's enough for you, and too much too."],"id":472,"m":"Twenty miles between sunrise and sunset, madam, is enough for you, and too much."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Why, one that rode to's execution, man,","Could never go so slow. I have heard of riding wagers","Where horses have been nimbler than the sands","That run i' th' clock's behalf. But this is fool'ry.","Go bid my woman feign a sickness; say","She'll home to her father; and provide me presently","A riding suit, no costlier than would fit","A franklin's huswife."],"id":473,"m":"Why, man, a man riding to his own execution couldn't go so slowly. I've heard of horse races where horses ran faster than the sand in an hourglass. But this is silly. Go and tell my maid to pretend to be ill and say she's going home to her father; and get me a riding outfit at once, no more expensive than a well-off farmer's wife would wear."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Madam, you're best consider."],"id":474,"m":"Madam, you'd better think about this."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I see before me, man. Nor here, nor here,","Nor what ensues, but have a fog in them","That I cannot look through. Away, I prithee;","Do as I bid thee. There's no more to say;","Accessible is none but Milford way."],"id":475,"m":"I can see only what's in front of me, man. This way and that, and what comes after, are all in a fog I can't see through. Go, please; do as I tell you. There's nothing more to say: the only road open is the road to Milford."},{"t":"sd","id":476,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Wales. A mountainous country with a cave.","id":477},{"t":"sd","id":478,"text":"Enter from the cave Belarius, Guiderius, and Arviragus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["A goodly day not to keep house with such","Whose roof's as low as ours! Stoop, boys; this gate","Instructs you how t' adore the heavens, and bows you","To a morning's holy office. The gates of monarchs","Are arch'd so high that giants may jet through","And keep their impious turbans on without","Good morrow to the sun. Hail, thou fair heaven!","We house i' th' rock, yet use thee not so hardly","As prouder livers do."],"id":479,"m":"A fine day not to stay indoors, for people with a roof as low as ours! Bend down, boys: this doorway teaches you how to worship the heavens and bows you down for your morning prayers. The gates of kings are arched so high that giants can strut through with their wicked turbans on and never say good morning to the sun. Hail, lovely sky! We live in a rock, but we don't treat you as harshly as prouder people do."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Hail, heaven!"],"id":480,"m":"Hail, heaven!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Hail, heaven!"],"id":481,"m":"Hail, heaven!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Now for our mountain sport. Up to yond hill,","Your legs are young; I'll tread these flats. Consider,","When you above perceive me like a crow,","That it is place which lessens and sets off;","And you may then revolve what tales I have told you","Of courts, of princes, of the tricks in war.","This service is not service so being done,","But being so allow'd. To apprehend thus","Draws us a profit from all things we see,","And often to our comfort shall we find","The sharded beetle in a safer hold","Than is the full-wing'd eagle. O, this life","Is nobler than attending for a check,","Richer than doing nothing for a bribe,","Prouder than rustling in unpaid-for silk:","Such gain the cap of him that makes him fine,","Yet keeps his book uncross'd. No life to ours!"],"id":482,"m":"Now for our mountain hunting. Up that hill: your legs are young. I'll walk these flat lands. When you look down and see me as small as a crow, remember that it's position that makes things look small or great, and then you can think over the stories I've told you about courts, princes, and the tricks of war. Service isn't valued for being done, but for being recognised. Thinking like this, we can learn from everything we see, and we'll often find, to our comfort, that the beetle in its shell is safer than the eagle with its full wings. Oh, this life is nobler than waiting on a great man for a scolding, richer than doing nothing for a bribe, prouder than rustling in unpaid-for silk: such a man gets a bow from the tailor who dresses him, but never pays his bill. There's no life like ours!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Out of your proof you speak. We, poor unfledg'd,","Have never wing'd from view o' th' nest, nor know not","What air's from home. Haply this life is best,","If quiet life be best; sweeter to you","That have a sharper known; well corresponding","With your stiff age. But unto us it is","A cell of ignorance, travelling abed,","A prison for a debtor that not dares","To stride a limit."],"id":483,"m":"You speak from experience. We poor fledglings have never flown out of sight of the nest and don't know what the air is like away from home. Maybe this life is best, if a quiet life is best; sweeter to you who've known a harsher one, and well suited to your stiff old age. But to us it's a cell of ignorance, travelling only in our beds, a prison for a debtor who doesn't dare step past its boundary."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["What should we speak of","When we are old as you? When we shall hear","The rain and wind beat dark December, how,","In this our pinching cave, shall we discourse.","The freezing hours away? We have seen nothing;","We are beastly: subtle as the fox for prey,","Like warlike as the wolf for what we eat.","Our valour is to chase what flies; our cage","We make a choir, as doth the prison'd bird,","And sing our bondage freely."],"id":484,"m":"What will we talk about when we're as old as you? When we hear rain and wind beating against dark December, how will we pass the freezing hours talking in this cramped cave? We've seen nothing. We're like animals: cunning as the fox for prey, fierce as the wolf for what we eat. Our courage is to chase what runs away. We make our cage a choir, like a caged bird, and sing freely about our captivity."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["How you speak!","Did you but know the city's usuries,","And felt them knowingly—the art o' th' court,","As hard to leave as keep, whose top to climb","Is certain falling, or so slipp'ry that","The fear's as bad as falling; the toil o' th' war,","A pain that only seems to seek out danger","I' th'name of fame and honour, which dies i' th'search,","And hath as oft a sland'rous epitaph","As record of fair act; nay, many times,","Doth ill deserve by doing well; what's worse—","Must curtsy at the censure. O, boys, this story","The world may read in me; my body's mark'd","With Roman swords, and my report was once","first with the best of note. Cymbeline lov'd me;","And when a soldier was the theme, my name","Was not far off. Then was I as a tree","Whose boughs did bend with fruit; but in one night","A storm, or robbery, call it what you will,","Shook down my mellow hangings, nay, my leaves,","And left me bare to weather."],"id":485,"m":"How you talk! If you only knew the city's money-lending and had felt it yourself; the skill of the court, as hard to leave as to keep, where climbing to the top means certain falling, or the top's so slippery that the fear is as bad as the fall; the hard work of war, a pain that seems only to look for danger in the name of fame and honour, which dies in the search and as often gets a slanderous epitaph as a record of good deeds; no, often gets punished for doing well; and what's worse, has to bow to the judgment. Oh, boys, the world can read this story in me. My body is marked by Roman swords, and my reputation was once among the very best. Cymbeline loved me, and whenever soldiers were talked of, my name wasn't far off. Then I was like a tree whose branches bent with fruit; but in one night a storm, or a robbery, call it what you like, shook down my ripe fruit, no, my leaves too, and left me bare to the weather."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Uncertain favour!"],"id":486,"m":"Fickle favour!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["My fault being nothing—as I have told you oft—","But that two villains, whose false oaths prevail'd","Before my perfect honour, swore to Cymbeline","I was confederate with the Romans. So","Follow'd my banishment, and this twenty years","This rock and these demesnes have been my world,","Where I have liv'd at honest freedom, paid","More pious debts to heaven than in all","The fore-end of my time. But up to th' mountains!","This is not hunters' language. He that strikes","The venison first shall be the lord o' th' feast;","To him the other two shall minister;","And we will fear no poison, which attends","In place of greater state. I'll meet you in the valleys."],"id":487,"m":"My only fault, as I've often told you, was that two villains, whose lying oaths beat my perfect honour, swore to Cymbeline that I was in league with the Romans. So I was banished, and for twenty years this rock and these lands have been my world. Here I've lived in honest freedom and paid more devout debts to heaven than in all the earlier part of my life. But up to the mountains! This isn't hunters' talk. Whoever kills the deer first will be lord of the feast, and the other two will serve him. And we won't fear poison, which waits in places of greater state. I'll meet you in the valleys."},{"t":"sd","id":488,"text":"Exeunt Guiderius and Arviragus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","cont":1,"lines":["How hard it is to hide the sparks of nature!","These boys know little they are sons to th' King,","Nor Cymbeline dreams that they are alive.","They think they are mine; and though train'd up thus meanly","I' th' cave wherein they bow, their thoughts do hit","The roofs of palaces, and nature prompts them","In simple and low things to prince it much","Beyond the trick of others. This Polydore,","The heir of Cymbeline and Britain, who","The King his father call'd Guiderius—Jove!","When on my three-foot stool I sit and tell","The warlike feats I have done, his spirits fly out","Into my story; say 'Thus mine enemy fell,","And thus I set my foot on's neck'; even then","The princely blood flows in his cheek, he sweats,","Strains his young nerves, and puts himself in posture","That acts my words. The younger brother, Cadwal,","Once Arviragus, in as like a figure","Strikes life into my speech, and shows much more","His own conceiving. Hark, the game is rous'd!","O Cymbeline, heaven and my conscience knows","Thou didst unjustly banish me! Whereon,","At three and two years old, I stole these babes,","Thinking to bar thee of succession as","Thou refts me of my lands. Euriphile,","Thou wast their nurse; they took thee for their mother,","And every day do honour to her grave.","Myself, Belarius, that am Morgan call'd,","They take for natural father. The game is up."],"id":489,"m":"How hard it is to hide the sparks of nature! These boys have no idea they're the King's sons, and Cymbeline doesn't dream they're alive. They think they're mine; and though they've been brought up so humbly in this cave where they have to stoop, their thoughts hit the roofs of palaces, and nature prompts them, even in simple, humble things, to act far more like princes than others do. This Polydore, the heir of Cymbeline and Britain, whom the King his father named Guiderius: by Jove! When I sit on my three-legged stool and tell of my brave deeds in war, his spirit flies into my story. When I say, \"So my enemy fell, and so I put my foot on his neck,\" at once the royal blood flows into his cheek; he sweats, strains his young muscles, and takes up a stance that acts out my words. The younger brother, Cadwal, once Arviragus, brings my story to life in the same way, and shows even more of his own imagination. Listen, the game is up! Oh, Cymbeline, heaven and my conscience know you banished me unjustly! And so I stole these babies at three and two years old, meaning to rob you of heirs as you robbed me of my lands. Euriphile, you were their nurse; they thought you were their mother, and they honour her grave every day. Me, Belarius, now called Morgan, they think their real father. The game's up."},{"t":"sd","id":490,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"Wales, near Milford Haven.","id":491},{"t":"sd","id":492,"text":"Enter Pisanio and Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Thou told'st me, when we came from horse, the place","Was near at hand. Ne'er long'd my mother so","To see me first as I have now. Pisanio! Man!","Where is Posthumus? What is in thy mind","That makes thee stare thus? Wherefore breaks that sigh","From th' inward of thee? One but painted thus","Would be interpreted a thing perplex'd","Beyond self-explication. Put thyself","Into a haviour of less fear, ere wildness","Vanquish my staider senses. What's the matter?","Why tender'st thou that paper to me with","A look untender! If't be summer news,","Smile to't before; if winterly, thou need'st","But keep that count'nance still. My husband's hand?","That drug-damn'd Italy hath out-craftied him,","And he's at some hard point. Speak, man; thy tongue","May take off some extremity, which to read","Would be even mortal to me."],"id":493,"m":"You told me, when we got off the horses, that the place was close by. My mother never longed to see me for the first time as much as I long now. Pisanio! Man! Where is Posthumus? What's on your mind that makes you stare like that? Why does that sigh break from deep inside you? Someone painted looking like that would be taken for a person confused beyond explaining himself. Look less frightened, before wild fear overcomes my calmer senses. What's the matter? Why do you hand me that paper with such an unkind look? If it's summer news, smile first; if it's wintry, you only need to keep that face. My husband's handwriting? That poison-cursed Italy has outwitted him, and he's in some terrible trouble. Speak, man. Your tongue may soften some disaster that would kill me to read."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Please you read,","And you shall find me, wretched man, a thing","The most disdain'd of fortune."],"id":494,"m":"Please read it, and you'll find me, wretched man, the most scorned of fortune's creatures."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["[Reads] 'Thy mistress, Pisanio, hath play'd the strumpet in","my bed, the testimonies whereof lie bleeding in me. I speak not","out of weak surmises, but from proof as strong as my grief and as","certain as I expect my revenge. That part thou, Pisanio, must act","for me, if thy faith be not tainted with the breach of hers. Let","thine own hands take away her life; I shall give thee opportunity","at Milford Haven; she hath my letter for the purpose; where, if","thou fear to strike, and to make me certain it is done, thou art","the pander to her dishonour, and equally to me disloyal.'"],"id":495,"m":"(Reading) \"Your mistress, Pisanio, has played the whore in my bed. The proofs of it lie bleeding in my heart. I'm not speaking from weak suspicion but from proof as strong as my grief and as certain as I expect my revenge to be. You, Pisanio, must carry out that part for me, if your loyalty isn't tainted by her betrayal. Take her life with your own hands. I'll give you the chance at Milford Haven: she has my letter for the purpose. If you're afraid to strike, and to assure me it's done, you're the pimp of her dishonour, and just as disloyal to me.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["What shall I need to draw my sword? The paper","Hath cut her throat already. No, 'tis slander,","Whose edge is sharper than the sword, whose tongue","Outvenoms all the worms of Nile, whose breath","Rides on the posting winds and doth belie","All corners of the world. Kings, queens, and states,","Maids, matrons, nay, the secrets of the grave,","This viperous slander enters. What cheer, madam?"],"id":496,"m":"Why should I need to draw my sword? The paper has already cut her throat. No, it's slander, whose edge is sharper than the sword, whose tongue is more poisonous than all the snakes of the Nile, whose breath rides on the speeding winds and spreads lies to every corner of the world. Kings, queens and states, virgins, wives, even the secrets of the grave: this viper slander gets into all of them. How are you, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["False to his bed? What is it to be false?","To lie in watch there, and to think on him?","To weep twixt clock and clock? If sleep charge nature,","To break it with a fearful dream of him,","And cry myself awake? That's false to's bed,","Is it?"],"id":497,"m":"False to his bed? What does it mean to be false? To lie awake there thinking of him? To cry from hour to hour? If sleep overpowers me, to break it with a frightening dream about him and wake myself up crying? Is that being false to his bed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Alas, good lady!"],"id":498,"m":"Alas, good lady!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I false! Thy conscience witness! Iachimo,","Thou didst accuse him of incontinency;","Thou then look'dst like a villain; now, methinks,","Thy favour's good enough. Some jay of Italy,","Whose mother was her painting, hath betray'd him.","Poor I am stale, a garment out of fashion,","And for I am richer than to hang by th' walls","I must be ripp'd. To pieces with me! O,","Men's vows are women's traitors! All good seeming,","By thy revolt, O husband, shall be thought","Put on for villainy; not born where't grows,","But worn a bait for ladies."],"id":499,"m":"I, false? Your conscience is my witness! Iachimo, when you accused him of lust, you looked like a villain; now I think your face looks honest enough. Some flashy Italian bird, whose mother was her makeup, has seduced him. Poor me, I'm stale, a dress gone out of fashion, and since I'm too rich to hang on the wall, I must be ripped up. Tear me to pieces! Oh, men's vows betray women! Because of your betrayal, husband, every good appearance will be thought to be put on for wickedness; not natural, but worn as bait for ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Good madam, hear me."],"id":500,"m":"Good madam, listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["True honest men being heard, like false Aeneas,","Were, in his time, thought false; and Sinon's weeping","Did scandal many a holy tear, took pity","From most true wretchedness. So thou, Posthumus,","Wilt lay the leaven on all proper men:","Goodly and gallant shall be false and perjur'd","From thy great fail. Come, fellow, be thou honest;","Do thou thy master's bidding; when thou seest him,","A little witness my obedience. Look!","I draw the sword myself; take it, and hit","The innocent mansion of my love, my heart.","Fear not; 'tis empty of all things but grief;","Thy master is not there, who was indeed","The riches of it. Do his bidding; strike.","Thou mayst be valiant in a better cause,","But now thou seem'st a coward."],"id":501,"m":"Truly honest men, because of what was said about false Aeneas, were thought false in his time, and Sinon's weeping brought shame on many holy tears and took pity away from real misery. So you, Posthumus, will taint all good men: the handsome and the gallant will be thought false and lying because of your great failure. Come on, fellow, be honest: do what your master orders. When you see him, testify a little to my obedience. Look, I'll draw the sword myself. Take it, and strike the innocent house of my love, my heart. Don't be afraid; it's empty of everything but grief. Your master isn't there, who used to be its whole treasure. Do as he says; strike. You may be brave in a better cause, but right now you look like a coward."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Hence, vile instrument!","Thou shalt not damn my hand."],"id":502,"m":"Get away, vile weapon! You won't damn my hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Why, I must die;","And if I do not by thy hand, thou art","No servant of thy master's. Against self-slaughter","There is a prohibition so divine","That cravens my weak hand. Come, here's my heart—","Something's afore't. Soft, soft! we'll no defence!—","Obedient as the scabbard. What is here?","The scriptures of the loyal Leonatus","All turn'd to heresy? Away, away,","Corrupters of my faith! you shall no more","Be stomachers to my heart. Thus may poor fools","Believe false teachers; though those that are betray'd","Do feel the treason sharply, yet the traitor","Stands in worse case of woe. And thou, Posthumus,","That didst set up my disobedience 'gainst the King","My father, and make me put into contempt the suits","Of princely fellows, shalt hereafter find","It is no act of common passage but","A strain of rareness; and I grieve myself","To think, when thou shalt be disedg'd by her","That now thou tirest on, how thy memory","Will then be pang'd by me. Prithee dispatch.","The lamp entreats the butcher. Where's thy knife?","Thou art too slow to do thy master's bidding,","When I desire it too."],"id":503,"m":"Why, I must die, and if it isn't by your hand, you're no servant of your master's. There's a law against suicide so holy that it makes my weak hand a coward. Come, here's my heart. Something's in front of it. Wait, wait! We'll have no protection, obedient as a scabbard. What's this? The scriptures of the loyal Leonatus, all turned to heresy? Away, away, corrupters of my faith! You'll be no armour for my heart any more. That's how poor fools believe false teachers; and though the betrayed feel the treason sharply, the traitor is in a worse state of misery. And you, Posthumus, who made me disobey the King my father and scorn the proposals of princes, will find later that it was no ordinary act but a rare one; and it grieves me to think how, when you've grown tired of the woman you now feed on, your memory will be tormented by me. Please, get it over with. The lamb begs the butcher. Where's your knife? You're too slow to do your master's bidding, when I want it too."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["O gracious lady,","Since I receiv'd command to do this busines","I have not slept one wink."],"id":504,"m":"Oh, gracious lady, since I got the order to do this, I haven't slept a wink."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Do't, and to bed then."],"id":505,"m":"Do it, and then go to bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["I'll wake mine eyeballs first."],"id":506,"m":"I'll stay awake till my eyes fall out first."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Wherefore then","Didst undertake it? Why hast thou abus'd","So many miles with a pretence? This place?","Mine action and thine own? our horses' labour?","The time inviting thee? the perturb'd court,","For my being absent?—whereunto I never","Purpose return. Why hast thou gone so far","To be unbent when thou hast ta'en thy stand,","Th' elected deer before thee?"],"id":507,"m":"Then why did you take it on? Why have you wasted so many miles with a pretence? This place? What I've done and what you've done? Our horses' effort? The chance this time gives you? The court in turmoil over my absence, where I never mean to go back? Why have you come so far only to unstring your bow when you've taken your position and the chosen deer is in front of you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["But to win time","To lose so bad employment, in the which","I have consider'd of a course. Good lady,","Hear me with patience."],"id":508,"m":"Only to win time to get out of such a wicked job, and meanwhile I've thought of a plan. Good lady, hear me patiently."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Talk thy tongue weary—speak.","I have heard I am a strumpet, and mine ear,","Therein false struck, can take no greater wound,","Nor tent to bottom that. But speak."],"id":509,"m":"Talk till your tongue's tired. Speak. I've heard I'm a whore, and my ear, wrongly struck there, can't take a greater wound, nor any probe reach the bottom of that one. But speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Then, madam,","I thought you would not back again."],"id":510,"m":"Then, madam, I thought you wouldn't go back."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Most like—","Bringing me here to kill me."],"id":511,"m":"Very likely, since you brought me here to kill me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Not so, neither;","But if I were as wise as honest, then","My purpose would prove well. It cannot be","But that my master is abus'd. Some villain,","Ay, and singular in his art, hath done you both","This cursed injury."],"id":512,"m":"Not that either. But if I were as wise as I am honest, my plan would work. It must be that my master has been deceived. Some villain, and an exceptional one at his trade, has done you both this cursed wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Some Roman courtezan!"],"id":513,"m":"Some Roman whore!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["No, on my life!","I'll give but notice you are dead, and send him","Some bloody sign of it, for 'tis commanded","I should do so. You shall be miss'd at court,","And that will well confirm it."],"id":514,"m":"No, on my life! I'll just send word that you're dead, and send him some bloody token of it, because I've been ordered to. You'll be missed at court, and that will confirm it nicely."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Why, good fellow,","What shall I do the while? where bide? how live?","Or in my life what comfort, when I am","Dead to my husband?"],"id":515,"m":"But, good fellow, what will I do meanwhile? Where will I stay? How will I live? What comfort will there be in my life when I'm dead to my husband?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["If you'll back to th' court—"],"id":516,"m":"If you'll go back to court..."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["No court, no father, nor no more ado","With that harsh, noble, simple nothing—","That Cloten, whose love-suit hath been to me","As fearful as a siege."],"id":517,"m":"No court, no father, and no more trouble with that harsh, noble, empty nothing, that Cloten, whose courting has been as terrifying to me as a siege."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["If not at court,","Then not in Britain must you bide."],"id":518,"m":"If not at court, then you mustn't stay in Britain."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Where then?","Hath Britain all the sun that shines? Day, night,","Are they not but in Britain? I' th' world's volume","Our Britain seems as of it, but not in't;","In a great pool a swan's nest. Prithee think","There's livers out of Britain."],"id":519,"m":"Where then? Does Britain have all the sun that shines? Are day and night only in Britain? In the book of the world, our Britain seems part of it but not in it: a swan's nest in a great pool. Please believe there are people living outside Britain."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["I am most glad","You think of other place. Th' ambassador,","Lucius the Roman, comes to Milford Haven","To-morrow. Now, if you could wear a mind","Dark as your fortune is, and but disguise","That which t' appear itself must not yet be","But by self-danger, you should tread a course","Pretty and full of view; yea, happily, near","The residence of Posthumus; so nigh, at least,","That though his actions were not visible, yet","Report should render him hourly to your ear","As truly as he moves."],"id":520,"m":"I'm very glad you're thinking of somewhere else. The ambassador, Lucius the Roman, comes to Milford Haven tomorrow. Now, if you could make your mind as dark as your fortune, and disguise what you are, which can't show itself yet without danger to you, you could take a path that's promising and full of prospects; yes, perhaps near where Posthumus is living; so close, at least, that even if you couldn't see what he does, news would bring him to your ear every hour as truly as he moves."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["O! for such means,","Though peril to my modesty, not death on't,","I would adventure."],"id":521,"m":"Oh, for a way like that I'd take the risk, even if it endangered my modesty, though not my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Well then, here's the point:","You must forget to be a woman; change","Command into obedience; fear and niceness—","The handmaids of all women, or, more truly,","Woman it pretty self—into a waggish courage;","Ready in gibes, quick-answer'd, saucy, and","As quarrelous as the weasel. Nay, you must","Forget that rarest treasure of your cheek,","Exposing it—but, O, the harder heart!","Alack, no remedy!—to the greedy touch","Of common-kissing Titan, and forget","Your laboursome and dainty trims wherein","You made great Juno angry."],"id":522,"m":"Well then, here's the point. You must forget you're a woman: change commanding into obeying, and fear and delicacy, the servants of all women, or more truly, woman's own pretty self, into a cheeky courage; quick with jokes, quick with answers, saucy, and as quarrelsome as a weasel. No, you must forget that rarest treasure, your complexion, and expose it (oh, how hard-hearted! but there's no help for it) to the greedy touch of the sun, who kisses everyone, and forget the elaborate, dainty clothes that made great Juno jealous."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Nay, be brief;","I see into thy end, and am almost","A man already."],"id":523,"m":"No, be quick. I can see where you're going, and I'm almost a man already."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["First, make yourself but like one.","Fore-thinking this, I have already fit—","'Tis in my cloak-bag—doublet, hat, hose, all","That answer to them. Would you, in their serving,","And with what imitation you can borrow","From youth of such a season, fore noble Lucius","Present yourself, desire his service, tell him","Wherein you're happy—which will make him know","If that his head have ear in music; doubtless","With joy he will embrace you; for he's honourable,","And, doubling that, most holy. Your means abroad—","You have me, rich; and I will never fail","Beginning nor supplyment."],"id":524,"m":"First, just make yourself look like one. Foreseeing this, I've already got ready (it's in my cloak-bag) a doublet, hat, breeches, everything that goes with them. If you'd dress in them and, with whatever imitation you can borrow from a boy of that age, present yourself to noble Lucius, ask to serve him, tell him what you're good at (and he'll find that out if he has any ear for music), he'll surely take you on gladly, because he's honourable and, more than that, very religious. As for your money while abroad, you have me, who has plenty, and I'll never fail to provide what you need now and later."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Thou art all the comfort","The gods will diet me with. Prithee away!","There's more to be consider'd; but we'll even","All that good time will give us. This attempt","I am soldier to, and will abide it with","A prince's courage. Away, I prithee."],"id":525,"m":"You're all the comfort the gods will feed me with. Please go! There's more to think about, but we'll fit ourselves to whatever good time brings us. I'm a soldier in this attempt and I'll see it through with a prince's courage. Go, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Well, madam, we must take a short farewell,","Lest, being miss'd, I be suspected of","Your carriage from the court. My noble mistress,","Here is a box; I had it from the Queen.","What's in't is precious. If you are sick at sea","Or stomach-qualm'd at land, a dram of this","Will drive away distemper. To some shade,","And fit you to your manhood. May the gods","Direct you to the best!"],"id":526,"m":"Well, madam, we must say a quick goodbye, in case I'm missed and suspected of taking you from court. My noble mistress, here's a box. I got it from the Queen. What's in it is precious. If you're seasick, or queasy on land, a sip of this will cure the sickness. Find somewhere shady and dress yourself as a man. May the gods guide you to the best!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Amen. I thank thee."],"id":527,"m":"Amen. Thank you."},{"t":"sd","id":528,"text":"Exeunt severally."},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"V","loc":"Britain. Cymbeline's palace.","id":529},{"t":"sd","id":530,"text":"Enter Cymbeline, Queen, Cloten, Lucius, and Lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Thus far; and so farewell."],"id":531,"m":"This far, then; and so goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Thanks, royal sir.","My emperor hath wrote; I must from hence,","And am right sorry that I must report ye","My master's enemy."],"id":532,"m":"Thank you, royal sir. My emperor has written; I must leave, and I'm truly sorry I must report you as my master's enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Our subjects, sir,","Will not endure his yoke; and for ourself","To show less sovereignty than they, must needs","Appear unkinglike."],"id":533,"m":"Our subjects, sir, won't bear his yoke, and if I showed less independence than they do, I'd look unkingly."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["So, sir. I desire of you","A conduct overland to Milford Haven.","Madam, all joy befall your Grace, and you!"],"id":534,"m":"Very well, sir. I ask you for an escort overland to Milford Haven. Madam, may all joy come to your Grace, and to you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["My lords, you are appointed for that office;","The due of honour in no point omit.","So farewell, noble Lucius."],"id":535,"m":"My lords, you are appointed to that duty. Leave out no part of the honour due to him. Goodbye, noble Lucius."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Your hand, my lord."],"id":536,"m":"Your hand, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Receive it friendly; but from this time forth","I wear it as your enemy."],"id":537,"m":"Take it as a friend; but from now on I wear it as your enemy."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Sir, the event","Is yet to name the winner. Fare you well."],"id":538,"m":"Sir, the outcome has yet to name the winner. Farewell."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Leave not the worthy Lucius, good my lords,","Till he have cross'd the Severn. Happiness!"],"id":539,"m":"Don't leave the worthy Lucius, my good lords, until he's crossed the Severn. Good luck!"},{"t":"sd","id":540,"text":"Exeunt Lucius and Lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["He goes hence frowning; but it honours us","That we have given him cause."],"id":541,"m":"He leaves frowning, but it's to our honour that we've given him reason to."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["'Tis all the better;","Your valiant Britons have their wishes in it."],"id":542,"m":"All the better: your brave Britons get their wish."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Lucius hath wrote already to the Emperor","How it goes here. It fits us therefore ripely","Our chariots and our horsemen be in readiness.","The pow'rs that he already hath in Gallia","Will soon be drawn to head, from whence he moves","His war for Britain."],"id":543,"m":"Lucius has already written to the Emperor about how things stand here. So it's high time our chariots and cavalry were ready. The forces he already has in France will soon be gathered, and from there he'll launch his war on Britain."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["'Tis not sleepy business,","But must be look'd to speedily and strongly."],"id":544,"m":"This is no business to sleep on. It must be dealt with quickly and strongly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Our expectation that it would be thus","Hath made us forward. But, my gentle queen,","Where is our daughter? She hath not appear'd","Before the Roman, nor to us hath tender'd","The duty of the day. She looks us like","A thing more made of malice than of duty;","We have noted it. Call her before us, for","We have been too slight in sufferance."],"id":545,"m":"We expected this, so we're prepared. But, my gentle queen, where is our daughter? She hasn't appeared before the Roman or paid us her daily respects. She seems to us more full of spite than duty; we've noticed it. Call her to us; we've been too lenient."},{"t":"sd","id":546,"text":"Exit a Messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Royal sir,","Since the exile of Posthumus, most retir'd","Hath her life been; the cure whereof, my lord,","'Tis time must do. Beseech your Majesty,","Forbear sharp speeches to her; she's a lady","So tender of rebukes that words are strokes,","And strokes death to her."],"id":547,"m":"Royal sir, since Posthumus was exiled she has lived very much alone. Time must cure that, my lord. I beg your Majesty, don't speak sharply to her. She's so sensitive to scolding that words are blows to her, and blows are death."},{"t":"sd","id":548,"text":"Re-enter Messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Where is she, sir? How","Can her contempt be answer'd?"],"id":549,"m":"Where is she, sir? How can her contempt be explained?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Please you, sir,","Her chambers are all lock'd, and there's no answer","That will be given to th' loud of noise we make."],"id":550,"m":"If you please, sir, her rooms are all locked, and there's no answer, however loudly we knock."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["My lord, when last I went to visit her,","She pray'd me to excuse her keeping close;","Whereto constrain'd by her infirmity","She should that duty leave unpaid to you","Which daily she was bound to proffer. This","She wish'd me to make known; but our great court","Made me to blame in memory."],"id":551,"m":"My lord, when I last visited her, she asked me to excuse her keeping to her rooms. Her illness forced her to leave unpaid the respects she owed you daily. She asked me to tell you, but the business of our great court made me forget."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Her doors lock'd?","Not seen of late? Grant, heavens, that which I fear","Prove false!"],"id":552,"m":"Her doors locked? Not seen lately? Heavens, let what I fear be false!"},{"t":"sd","id":553,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Son, I say, follow the King."],"id":554,"m":"Son, I tell you, follow the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["That man of hers, Pisanio, her old servant,","I have not seen these two days."],"id":555,"m":"That man of hers, Pisanio, her old servant, I haven't seen for two days."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Go, look after."],"id":556,"m":"Go and look."},{"t":"sd","id":557,"text":"Exit Cloten."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["Pisanio, thou that stand'st so for Posthumus!","He hath a drug of mine. I pray his absence","Proceed by swallowing that; for he believes","It is a thing most precious. But for her,","Where is she gone? Haply despair hath seiz'd her;","Or, wing'd with fervour of her love, she's flown","To her desir'd Posthumus. Gone she is","To death or to dishonour, and my end","Can make good use of either. She being down,","I have the placing of the British crown."],"id":558,"m":"Pisanio, you who stand up so for Posthumus! He has a drug of mine. I pray his absence is because he swallowed it, since he thinks it's very precious. But as for her, where has she gone? Maybe despair has taken hold of her, or, carried on the wings of her passionate love, she's flown to her beloved Posthumus. She's gone to death or to dishonour, and my purposes can make good use of either. With her out of the way, the British crown is mine to place."},{"t":"sd","id":559,"text":"Re-enter Cloten."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["How now, my son?"],"id":560,"m":"Well, my son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["'Tis certain she is fled.","Go in and cheer the King. He rages; none","Dare come about him."],"id":561,"m":"It's certain she's run away. Go in and calm the King. He's raging; nobody dares go near him."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["All the better. May","This night forestall him of the coming day!"],"id":562,"m":"All the better. I hope this night robs him of the coming day!"},{"t":"sd","id":563,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I love and hate her; for she's fair and royal,","And that she hath all courtly parts more exquisite","Than lady, ladies, woman. From every one","The best she hath, and she, of all compounded,","Outsells them all. I love her therefore; but","Disdaining me and throwing favours on","The low Posthumus slanders so her judgment","That what's else rare is chok'd; and in that point","I will conclude to hate her, nay, indeed,","To be reveng'd upon her. For when fools","Shall—"],"id":564,"m":"I love her and hate her. I love her because she's beautiful and royal, and has every courtly grace more exquisitely than any lady, all ladies, or womankind. She has the best of each of them and, made up of them all, outshines them all. That's why I love her. But scorning me and throwing her favours on that low Posthumus so disgraces her judgment that her other rare qualities are choked. On that point I've decided to hate her; no, in fact, to get revenge on her. For when fools shall..."},{"t":"sd","id":565,"text":"Enter Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","cont":1,"lines":["Who is here? What, are you packing, sirrah?","Come hither. Ah, you precious pander! Villain,","Where is thy lady? In a word, or else","Thou art straightway with the fiends."],"id":566,"m":"Who's this? What, are you sneaking off, sirrah? Come here. Ah, you precious pimp! Villain, where's your lady? In a word, or you go straight to the devils."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["O good my lord!"],"id":567,"m":"Oh, my good lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Where is thy lady? or, by Jupiter—","I will not ask again. Close villain,","I'll have this secret from thy heart, or rip","Thy heart to find it. Is she with Posthumus?","From whose so many weights of baseness cannot","A dram of worth be drawn."],"id":568,"m":"Where's your lady? Or, by Jupiter... I won't ask again. Secretive villain, I'll have this secret out of your heart, or rip your heart out to find it. Is she with Posthumus, from whose great weight of lowness not one gram of worth can be squeezed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Alas, my lord,","How can she be with him? When was she miss'd?","He is in Rome."],"id":569,"m":"Alas, my lord, how can she be with him? When was she missed? He's in Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Where is she, sir? Come nearer.","No farther halting! Satisfy me home","What is become of her."],"id":570,"m":"Where is she, sir? Come closer. No more stalling! Tell me fully what's become of her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["O my all-worthy lord!"],"id":571,"m":"Oh, my most worthy lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["All-worthy villain!","Discover where thy mistress is at once,","At the next word. No more of 'worthy lord'!","Speak, or thy silence on the instant is","Thy condemnation and thy death."],"id":572,"m":"Most worthy villain! Tell me where your mistress is at once, with your next word. No more \"worthy lord\"! Speak, or your silence is your sentence and your death on the spot."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Then, sir,","This paper is the history of my knowledge","Touching her flight. [Presenting a letter]"],"id":573,"m":"Then, sir, this paper is everything I know about her escape. (He hands over a letter.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Let's see't. I will pursue her","Even to Augustus' throne."],"id":574,"m":"Let me see it. I'll chase her even to Augustus's throne."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["[Aside] Or this or perish.","She's far enough; and what he learns by this","May prove his travel, not her danger."],"id":575,"m":"(Aside) It was this or die. She's far enough away, and what he learns from this may only cost him a journey, not put her in danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Humh!"],"id":576,"m":"Hmm!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["[Aside] I'll write to my lord she's dead. O Imogen,","Safe mayst thou wander, safe return again!"],"id":577,"m":"(Aside) I'll write to my lord that she's dead. Oh, Imogen, may you wander safely and come safely back!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Sirrah, is this letter true?"],"id":578,"m":"Sirrah, is this letter genuine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Sir, as I think."],"id":579,"m":"Sir, I think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["It is Posthumus' hand; I know't. Sirrah, if thou wouldst","not be a villain, but do me true service, undergo those","employments wherein I should have cause to use thee with a","serious industry—that is, what villainy soe'er I bid thee do, to","perform it directly and truly—I would think thee an honest man;","thou shouldst neither want my means for thy relief nor my voice","for thy preferment."],"id":580,"m":"It's Posthumus's handwriting; I know it. Sirrah, if you'd stop being a villain and serve me faithfully, and carry out seriously whatever jobs I have for you (that is, whatever villainy I order you to do, do it directly and properly), I'd think you an honest man. You wouldn't lack my money to help you or my word to promote you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Well, my good lord."],"id":581,"m":"Well, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Wilt thou serve me? For since patiently and constantly thou","hast stuck to the bare fortune of that beggar Posthumus, thou","canst not, in the course of gratitude, but be a diligent follower","of mine. Wilt thou serve me?"],"id":582,"m":"Will you serve me? Since you've stuck so patiently and loyally to the miserable fortune of that beggar Posthumus, you can't help, out of gratitude, being a hard-working follower of mine. Will you serve me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Sir, I will."],"id":583,"m":"Sir, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Give me thy hand; here's my purse. Hast any of thy late","master's garments in thy possession?"],"id":584,"m":"Give me your hand. Here's my purse. Do you have any of your old master's clothes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["I have, my lord, at my lodging, the same suit he wore when","he took leave of my lady and mistress."],"id":585,"m":"I have, my lord, in my lodging: the same suit he wore when he said goodbye to my lady and mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["The first service thou dost me, fetch that suit hither. Let","it be thy first service; go."],"id":586,"m":"The first thing you do for me: bring that suit here. Let that be your first service. Go."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["I shall, my lord."],"id":587,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sd","id":588,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Meet thee at Milford Haven! I forgot to ask him one thing;","I'll remember't anon. Even there, thou villain Posthumus, will I","kill thee. I would these garments were come. She said upon a","time—the bitterness of it I now belch from my heart—that she","held the very garment of Posthumus in more respect than my noble","and natural person, together with the adornment of my qualities.","With that suit upon my back will I ravish her; first kill him,","and in her eyes. There shall she see my valour, which will then","be a torment to her contempt. He on the ground, my speech of","insultment ended on his dead body, and when my lust hath dined—","which, as I say, to vex her I will execute in the clothes that","she so prais'd—to the court I'll knock her back, foot her home","again. She hath despis'd me rejoicingly, and I'll be merry in my","revenge."],"id":589,"m":"Meet him at Milford Haven! I forgot to ask him one thing; I'll remember it in a moment. Right there, you villain Posthumus, I'll kill you. I wish those clothes would come. She once said (I belch the bitterness of it up from my heart now) that she valued Posthumus's mere clothes more than my noble person and all my fine qualities. With that suit on my back I'll rape her: but first kill him, in front of her. There she'll see my courage, which will then torment her scorn. With him on the ground and my insults spoken over his dead body, and when my lust has dined (which, as I say, I'll do in the clothes she praised so much, to spite her), I'll beat her back to court and kick her home. She's taken pleasure in scorning me, and I'll be merry in my revenge."},{"t":"sd","id":590,"text":"Re-enter Pisanio, with the clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","cont":1,"lines":["Be those the garments?"],"id":591,"m":"Are those the clothes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Ay, my noble lord."],"id":592,"m":"Yes, my noble lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["How long is't since she went to Milford Haven?"],"id":593,"m":"How long is it since she left for Milford Haven?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["She can scarce be there yet."],"id":594,"m":"She can hardly be there yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Bring this apparel to my chamber; that is the second thing","that I have commanded thee. The third is that thou wilt be a","voluntary mute to my design. Be but duteous and true, preferment","shall tender itself to thee. My revenge is now at Milford, would","I had wings to follow it! Come, and be true."],"id":595,"m":"Take these clothes to my room; that's the second thing I've ordered you. The third is that you keep quiet about my plan of your own accord. Just be obedient and loyal, and promotion will come to you. My revenge is now at Milford; I wish I had wings to follow it! Come, and be loyal."},{"t":"sd","id":596,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Thou bid'st me to my loss; for true to thee","Were to prove false, which I will never be,","To him that is most true. To Milford go,","And find not her whom thou pursuest. Flow, flow,","You heavenly blessings, on her! This fool's speed","Be cross'd with slowness! Labour be his meed!"],"id":597,"m":"You order me to my ruin: to be loyal to you would be to prove false to him who is most loyal, which I never will be. Go to Milford, and don't find the one you're chasing. Flow, flow, heavenly blessings, on her! May this fool's speed be blocked with slowness, and may his only reward be wasted effort!"},{"t":"sd","id":598,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"VI","loc":"Wales. Before the cave of Belarius.","id":599},{"t":"sd","id":600,"text":"Enter Imogen alone, in boy's clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I see a man's life is a tedious one.","I have tir'd myself, and for two nights together","Have made the ground my bed. I should be sick","But that my resolution helps me. Milford,","When from the mountain-top Pisanio show'd thee,","Thou wast within a ken. O Jove! I think","Foundations fly the wretched; such, I mean,","Where they should be reliev'd. Two beggars told me","I could not miss my way. Will poor folks lie,","That have afflictions on them, knowing 'tis","A punishment or trial? Yes; no wonder,","When rich ones scarce tell true. To lapse in fulness","Is sorer than to lie for need; and falsehood","Is worse in kings than beggars. My dear lord!","Thou art one o' th' false ones. Now I think on thee","My hunger's gone; but even before, I was","At point to sink for food. But what is this?","Here is a path to't; 'tis some savage hold.","I were best not call; I dare not call. Yet famine,","Ere clean it o'erthrow nature, makes it valiant.","Plenty and peace breeds cowards; hardness ever","Of hardiness is mother. Ho! who's here?","If anything that's civil, speak; if savage,","Take or lend. Ho! No answer? Then I'll enter.","Best draw my sword; and if mine enemy","But fear the sword, like me, he'll scarcely look on't.","Such a foe, good heavens!"],"id":601,"m":"I can see a man's life is a hard one. I've tired myself out, and for two nights running I've slept on the ground. I'd be ill, except my determination keeps me going. Milford, when Pisanio showed you to me from the mountain top, you were in sight. Oh, Jove! I think places of refuge run away from the wretched; I mean the places where they should get help. Two beggars told me I couldn't miss my way. Would poor people lie, when they're suffering and know it's a punishment or a test? Yes; no wonder, when rich people hardly tell the truth. To sin when you have plenty is worse than lying out of need, and lying is worse in kings than in beggars. My dear lord! You're one of the false ones. Now that I think of you my hunger's gone, though just before I was about to collapse for lack of food. But what's this? Here's a path to it; it's some wild man's den. I'd better not call; I don't dare call. But hunger, before it overthrows nature completely, makes it brave. Plenty and peace breed cowards; hardship is always the mother of hardiness. Hello! Who's here? If you're anything civilised, speak; if you're savage, take from me or lend to me. Hello! No answer? Then I'll go in. I'd better draw my sword; and if my enemy fears a sword as much as I do, he'll hardly dare look at it. Give me such an enemy, good heavens!"},{"t":"sd","id":602,"text":"Exit into the cave."},{"t":"sd","id":603,"text":"Enter Belarius, Guiderius, and Arviragus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["You, Polydore, have prov'd best woodman and","Are master of the feast. Cadwal and I","Will play the cook and servant; 'tis our match.","The sweat of industry would dry and die","But for the end it works to. Come, our stomachs","Will make what's homely savoury; weariness","Can snore upon the flint, when resty sloth","Finds the down pillow hard. Now, peace be here,","Poor house, that keep'st thyself!"],"id":604,"m":"You, Polydore, have proved the best hunter and are master of the feast. Cadwal and I will be cook and servant: that was our bargain. The sweat of hard work would dry up and die, if not for the purpose it serves. Come, our appetites will make plain food tasty. Tiredness can snore on a stone, while lazy idleness finds a feather pillow hard. Now, peace be with you, poor house that looks after yourself!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["I am thoroughly weary."],"id":605,"m":"I'm worn out."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["I am weak with toil, yet strong in appetite."],"id":606,"m":"I'm weak from work but strong in appetite."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["There is cold meat i' th' cave; we'll browse on that","Whilst what we have kill'd be cook'd."],"id":607,"m":"There's cold meat in the cave. We'll nibble on that while what we've killed is cooked."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["[Looking into the cave] Stay, come not in.","But that it eats our victuals, I should think","Here were a fairy."],"id":608,"m":"(Looking into the cave) Wait, don't go in. If it weren't eating our food, I'd think there was a fairy here."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["What's the matter, sir?"],"id":609,"m":"What's the matter, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["By Jupiter, an angel! or, if not,","An earthly paragon! Behold divineness","No elder than a boy!"],"id":610,"m":"By Jupiter, an angel! Or if not, an earthly perfection! Look: divinity no older than a boy!"},{"t":"sd","id":611,"text":"Re-enter Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Good masters, harm me not.","Before I enter'd here I call'd, and thought","To have begg'd or bought what I have took. Good troth,","I have stol'n nought; nor would not though I had found","Gold strew'd i' th' floor. Here's money for my meat.","I would have left it on the board, so soon","As I had made my meal, and parted","With pray'rs for the provider."],"id":612,"m":"Good sirs, don't hurt me. Before I came in I called out, and I meant to beg or buy what I've taken. Honestly, I've stolen nothing, and I wouldn't even if I'd found gold scattered on the floor. Here's money for my food. I'd have left it on the table as soon as I'd eaten and gone away with prayers for the person who provided it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Money, youth?"],"id":613,"m":"Money, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["All gold and silver rather turn to dirt,","As 'tis no better reckon'd but of those","Who worship dirty gods."],"id":614,"m":"Let all gold and silver turn to dirt instead, since it's valued no higher than dirt except by those who worship dirty gods."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I see you're angry.","Know, if you kill me for my fault, I should","Have died had I not made it."],"id":615,"m":"I see you're angry. Know that if you kill me for my offence, I'd have died if I hadn't committed it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Whither bound?"],"id":616,"m":"Where are you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["To Milford Haven."],"id":617,"m":"To Milford Haven."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["What's your name?"],"id":618,"m":"What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Fidele, sir. I have a kinsman who","Is bound for Italy; he embark'd at Milford;","To whom being going, almost spent with hunger,","I am fall'n in this offence."],"id":619,"m":"Fidele, sir. I have a relative who's bound for Italy; he sailed from Milford. On my way to him, almost dead with hunger, I fell into this offence."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Prithee, fair youth,","Think us no churls, nor measure our good minds","By this rude place we live in. Well encounter'd!","'Tis almost night; you shall have better cheer","Ere you depart, and thanks to stay and eat it.","Boys, bid him welcome."],"id":620,"m":"Please, fair youth, don't think we're rough peasants, and don't judge our good hearts by this rough place we live in. Well met! It's almost night. You'll have better food before you leave, and thanks for staying to eat it. Boys, welcome him."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Were you a woman, youth,","I should woo hard but be your groom. In honesty","I bid for you as I'd buy."],"id":621,"m":"If you were a woman, young man, I'd court you hard to be your husband. Honestly, I'd bid for you as I'd want to buy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["I'll make't my comfort","He is a man. I'll love him as my brother;","And such a welcome as I'd give to him","After long absence, such is yours. Most welcome!","Be sprightly, for you fall 'mongst friends."],"id":622,"m":"It'll be my comfort that he's a man. I'll love him as my brother, and the welcome I'd give my brother after a long absence is yours. Most welcome! Cheer up, you've fallen among friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["'Mongst friends,","If brothers. [Aside] Would it had been so that they","Had been my father's sons! Then had my prize","Been less, and so more equal ballasting","To thee, Posthumus."],"id":623,"m":"Among friends, if brothers. (Aside) I wish they had been my father's sons! Then my value as heir would have been less, and I'd have been a more equal weight to balance you, Posthumus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["He wrings at some distress."],"id":624,"m":"He's struggling with some distress."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Would I could free't!"],"id":625,"m":"I wish I could free him from it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Or I, whate'er it be,","What pain it cost, what danger! Gods!"],"id":626,"m":"Or I, whatever it is, whatever pain or danger it cost! Gods!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["[Whispering] Hark, boys."],"id":627,"m":"(Whispering) Listen, boys."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["[Aside] Great men,","That had a court no bigger than this cave,","That did attend themselves, and had the virtue","Which their own conscience seal'd them, laying by","That nothing-gift of differing multitudes,","Could not out-peer these twain. Pardon me, gods!","I'd change my sex to be companion with them,","Since Leonatus' false."],"id":628,"m":"(Aside) Great men with a court no bigger than this cave, who served themselves and had only the virtue their own consciences gave them, setting aside the worthless flattery of fickle crowds, couldn't outshine these two. Forgive me, gods! I'd change my sex to be their companion, since Leonatus is false."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["It shall be so.","Boys, we'll go dress our hunt. Fair youth, come in.","Discourse is heavy, fasting; when we have supp'd,","We'll mannerly demand thee of thy story,","So far as thou wilt speak it."],"id":629,"m":"That's settled. Boys, let's go and prepare our game. Fair youth, come in. Talking is hard work on an empty stomach. When we've eaten, we'll politely ask you for your story, as much as you want to tell."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Pray draw near."],"id":630,"m":"Please come closer."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["The night to th' owl and morn to th' lark less welcome."],"id":631,"m":"Night is less welcome to the owl and morning to the lark than you are to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Thanks, sir."],"id":632,"m":"Thank you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["I pray draw near."],"id":633,"m":"Please come closer."},{"t":"sd","id":634,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"VII","loc":"Rome. A public place.","id":635},{"t":"sd","id":636,"text":"Enter two Roman Senators and Tribunes."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["This is the tenour of the Emperor's writ:","That since the common men are now in action","'Gainst the Pannonians and Dalmatians,","And that the legions now in Gallia are","Full weak to undertake our wars against","The fall'n-off Britons, that we do incite","The gentry to this business. He creates","Lucius proconsul; and to you, the tribunes,","For this immediate levy, he commands","His absolute commission. Long live Caesar!"],"id":637,"m":"This is the gist of the Emperor's order: since the common soldiers are now fighting the Pannonians and Dalmatians, and the legions now in France are too weak to take on our war against the rebel Britons, we are to call on the gentlemen to take up this business. He makes Lucius proconsul, and to you tribunes he gives full authority for this immediate call-up. Long live Caesar!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRIBUNE","lines":["Is Lucius general of the forces?"],"id":638,"m":"Is Lucius general of the forces?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Ay."],"id":639,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRIBUNE","lines":["Remaining now in Gallia?"],"id":640,"m":"Who are now in France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["With those legions","Which I have spoke of, whereunto your levy","Must be supplyant. The words of your commission","Will tie you to the numbers and the time","Of their dispatch."],"id":641,"m":"With the legions I mentioned, which your recruits must reinforce. The words of your orders will fix the numbers and the time to send them."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRIBUNE","lines":["We will discharge our duty."],"id":642,"m":"We'll do our duty."},{"t":"sd","id":643,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Wales. Near the cave of Belarius.","id":644},{"t":"sd","id":645,"text":"Enter Cloten alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I am near to th' place where they should meet, if Pisanio","have mapp'd it truly. How fit his garments serve me! Why should","his mistress, who was made by him that made the tailor, not be","fit too? The rather—saving reverence of the word—for 'tis said","a woman's fitness comes by fits. Therein I must play the workman.","I dare speak it to myself, for it is not vain-glory for a man and","his glass to confer in his own chamber—I mean, the lines of my","body are as well drawn as his; no less young, more strong, not","beneath him in fortunes, beyond him in the advantage of the time,","above him in birth, alike conversant in general services, and","more remarkable in single oppositions. Yet this imperceiverant","thing loves him in my despite. What mortality is! Posthumus, thy","head, which now is growing upon thy shoulders, shall within this","hour be off; thy mistress enforced; thy garments cut to pieces","before her face; and all this done, spurn her home to her father,","who may, haply, be a little angry for my so rough usage; but my","mother, having power of his testiness, shall turn all into my","commendations. My horse is tied up safe. Out, sword, and to a","sore purpose! Fortune, put them into my hand. This is the very","description of their meeting-place; and the fellow dares not","deceive me."],"id":646,"m":"I'm near the place where they're supposed to meet, if Pisanio's map is accurate. How well his clothes fit me! Why shouldn't his lady, who was made by the same God who made the tailor, fit me too? All the more since (pardon the expression) they say a woman's fitness comes in fits. I'll have to work at that. I can say it to myself, because it isn't vanity for a man to consult his mirror in his own room: I mean, my body is as well drawn as his: no less young, stronger, not below him in fortune, ahead of him in present favour, above him in birth, just as experienced in battle, and more outstanding in single combat. And yet this undiscerning thing loves him to spite me. What a thing human life is! Posthumus, your head, which is now growing on your shoulders, will be off within the hour; your lady raped; your clothes cut to pieces in front of her; and when all that's done, I'll kick her home to her father, who may be a little angry at my rough treatment; but my mother, who can control his temper, will turn it all into praise of me. My horse is tied up safely. Out, sword, to a cruel purpose! Fortune, put them into my hands. This is exactly the place described for their meeting, and the fellow wouldn't dare deceive me."},{"t":"sd","id":647,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Wales. Before the cave of Belarius.","id":648},{"t":"sd","id":649,"text":"Enter, from the cave, Belarius, Guiderius, Arviragus, and Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["[To Imogen] You are not well. Remain here in the cave;","We'll come to you after hunting."],"id":650,"m":"(To Imogen) You're not well. Stay here in the cave. We'll come back to you after hunting."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["[To Imogen] Brother, stay here.","Are we not brothers?"],"id":651,"m":"(To Imogen) Brother, stay here. Aren't we brothers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["So man and man should be;","But clay and clay differs in dignity,","Whose dust is both alike. I am very sick."],"id":652,"m":"That's how man and man should be; but clay and clay differ in rank, though their dust is just the same. I'm very ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Go you to hunting; I'll abide with him."],"id":653,"m":"You go hunting. I'll stay with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["So sick I am not, yet I am not well;","But not so citizen a wanton as","To seem to die ere sick. So please you, leave me;","Stick to your journal course. The breach of custom","Is breach of all. I am ill, but your being by me","Cannot amend me; society is no comfort","To one not sociable. I am not very sick,","Since I can reason of it. Pray you trust me here.","I'll rob none but myself; and let me die,","Stealing so poorly."],"id":654,"m":"I'm not so very ill, yet I'm not well; but I'm not such a spoilt city boy as to act as if I'm dying before I'm sick. Please, leave me; keep to your daily routine. Breaking a habit breaks everything. I'm ill, but your being with me can't cure me. Company is no comfort to someone not in the mood for it. I'm not very ill, since I can reason about it. Please trust me here. I'll rob no one but myself, and let me die if I steal that poorly."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["I love thee; I have spoke it.","How much the quantity, the weight as much","As I do love my father."],"id":655,"m":"I love you; I've said it. As much in quantity and weight as I love my father."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["What? how? how?"],"id":656,"m":"What? How? How?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["If it be sin to say so, sir, I yoke me","In my good brother's fault. I know not why","I love this youth, and I have heard you say","Love's reason's without reason. The bier at door,","And a demand who is't shall die, I'd say","'My father, not this youth.'"],"id":657,"m":"If it's a sin to say so, sir, I share my good brother's fault. I don't know why I love this boy, and I've heard you say love has no reason. If the coffin were at the door and someone asked who should die, I'd say, \"My father, not this boy.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["[Aside] O noble strain!","O worthiness of nature! breed of greatness!","Cowards father cowards and base things sire base.","Nature hath meal and bran, contempt and grace.","I'm not their father; yet who this should be","Doth miracle itself, lov'd before me.—","'Tis the ninth hour o' th' morn."],"id":658,"m":"(Aside) Oh, noble blood! Oh, natural worth! The breeding of greatness! Cowards father cowards, and low things beget low things. Nature has fine flour and bran, the despised and the graceful. I'm not their father; and yet who this boy is, that they love him more than me, is a miracle. (Aloud) It's nine in the morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Brother, farewell."],"id":659,"m":"Goodbye, brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I wish ye sport."],"id":660,"m":"I wish you good hunting."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Your health. [To Belarius] So please you, sir."],"id":661,"m":"Get well. (To Belarius) Ready when you are, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["[Aside] These are kind creatures. Gods, what lies I have","heard!","Our courtiers say all's savage but at court.","Experience, O, thou disprov'st report!","Th' imperious seas breed monsters; for the dish,","Poor tributary rivers as sweet fish.","I am sick still; heart-sick. Pisanio,","I'll now taste of thy drug. [Swallows some]"],"id":662,"m":"(Aside) These are kind creatures. Gods, what lies I've heard! Our courtiers say everything is savage except the court. Experience, how you disprove gossip! The mighty seas breed monsters, while poor little rivers that flow into them give fish just as sweet for eating. I'm still sick, heart-sick. Pisanio, I'll try your drug now. (She swallows some.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["I could not stir him.","He said he was gentle, but unfortunate;","Dishonestly afflicted, but yet honest."],"id":663,"m":"I couldn't get anything out of him. He said he was well born but unlucky; dishonourably wronged, but still honourable."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Thus did he answer me; yet said hereafter","I might know more."],"id":664,"m":"That's what he told me too; but he said I might know more later."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["To th' field, to th' field!","We'll leave you for this time. Go in and rest."],"id":665,"m":"To the fields, to the fields! We'll leave you for now. Go in and rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["We'll not be long away."],"id":666,"m":"We won't be long."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Pray be not sick,","For you must be our huswife."],"id":667,"m":"Please don't be ill, because you have to be our housekeeper."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Well, or ill,","I am bound to you."],"id":668,"m":"Well or ill, I'm in your debt."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["And shalt be ever."],"id":669,"m":"And always will be."},{"t":"sd","id":670,"text":"Exit Imogen into the cave."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","cont":1,"lines":["This youth, howe'er distress'd, appears he hath had","Good ancestors."],"id":671,"m":"This boy, however troubled he is, seems to come of good ancestors."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["How angel-like he sings!"],"id":672,"m":"How like an angel he sings!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["But his neat cookery! He cut our roots in characters,","And sauc'd our broths as Juno had been sick,","And he her dieter."],"id":673,"m":"And his neat cooking! He cut our root vegetables into letters and seasoned our broths as if Juno were sick and he were her dietician."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Nobly he yokes","A smiling with a sigh, as if the sigh","Was that it was for not being such a smile;","The smile mocking the sigh that it would fly","From so divine a temple to commix","With winds that sailors rail at."],"id":674,"m":"He joins a smile and a sigh so nobly, as if the sigh were sorry it wasn't such a smile, and the smile mocked the sigh for wanting to fly from such a divine temple to mix with the winds sailors curse."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["I do note","That grief and patience, rooted in him both,","Mingle their spurs together."],"id":675,"m":"I notice that grief and patience, both rooted in him, tangle their roots together."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Grow patience!","And let the stinking elder, grief, untwine","His perishing root with the increasing vine!"],"id":676,"m":"Grow, patience! And let that stinking elder tree, grief, untwine its dying root from the growing vine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["It is great morning. Come, away! Who's there?"],"id":677,"m":"It's broad daylight. Come on, let's go! Who's there?"},{"t":"sd","id":678,"text":"Enter Cloten."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["I cannot find those runagates; that villain","Hath mock'd me. I am faint."],"id":679,"m":"I can't find those runaways. That villain has fooled me. I'm worn out."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Those runagates?","Means he not us? I partly know him; 'tis","Cloten, the son o' th' Queen. I fear some ambush.","I saw him not these many years, and yet","I know 'tis he. We are held as outlaws. Hence!"],"id":680,"m":"\"Those runaways\"? Doesn't he mean us? I half know him: it's Cloten, the Queen's son. I'm afraid of an ambush. I haven't seen him for many years, but I know it's him. We're considered outlaws. Let's go!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["He is but one; you and my brother search","What companies are near. Pray you away;","Let me alone with him."],"id":681,"m":"He's only one man. You and my brother search for any troops nearby. Please go. Leave me alone with him."},{"t":"sd","id":682,"text":"Exeunt Belarius and Arviragus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Soft! What are you","That fly me thus? Some villain mountaineers?","I have heard of such. What slave art thou?"],"id":683,"m":"Wait! Who are you, running away from me like that? Some villainous mountain men? I've heard of such people. What sort of slave are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["A thing","More slavish did I ne'er than answering","'A slave' without a knock."],"id":684,"m":"I never did anything more slavish than to answer to \"slave\" without hitting back."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Thou art a robber,","A law-breaker, a villain. Yield thee, thief."],"id":685,"m":"You're a robber, a lawbreaker, a villain. Surrender, thief."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["To who? To thee? What art thou? Have not I","An arm as big as thine, a heart as big?","Thy words, I grant, are bigger, for I wear not","My dagger in my mouth. Say what thou art;","Why I should yield to thee."],"id":686,"m":"To whom? To you? Who are you? Haven't I got an arm as strong as yours, and a heart as brave? Your words, I'll grant, are bigger, because I don't carry my dagger in my mouth. Tell me who you are and why I should surrender to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Thou villain base,","Know'st me not by my clothes?"],"id":687,"m":"You low villain, don't you know me by my clothes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["No, nor thy tailor, rascal,","Who is thy grandfather; he made those clothes,","Which, as it seems, make thee."],"id":688,"m":"No, rascal, nor your tailor, who's your grandfather. He made those clothes, which seem to be what made you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Thou precious varlet,","My tailor made them not."],"id":689,"m":"You precious rogue, my tailor didn't make them."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Hence, then, and thank","The man that gave them thee. Thou art some fool;","I am loath to beat thee."],"id":690,"m":"Then go away and thank the man who gave them to you. You're some sort of fool. I don't want to beat you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Thou injurious thief,","Hear but my name, and tremble."],"id":691,"m":"You insolent thief, just hear my name and tremble."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["What's thy name?"],"id":692,"m":"What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Cloten, thou villain."],"id":693,"m":"Cloten, you villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Cloten, thou double villain, be thy name,","I cannot tremble at it. Were it toad, or adder, spider,","'Twould move me sooner."],"id":694,"m":"Cloten, you double villain, let that be your name: I can't tremble at it. If it were Toad, or Adder, or Spider, it would move me sooner."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["To thy further fear,","Nay, to thy mere confusion, thou shalt know","I am son to th' Queen."],"id":695,"m":"To frighten you more, no, to destroy you completely, know that I'm the Queen's son."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["I'm sorry for't; not seeming","So worthy as thy birth."],"id":696,"m":"I'm sorry about that; you don't seem as worthy as your birth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Art not afeard?"],"id":697,"m":"Aren't you afraid?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Those that I reverence, those I fear—the wise:","At fools I laugh, not fear them."],"id":698,"m":"I fear those I respect: the wise. Fools I laugh at, I don't fear them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOTEN","lines":["Die the death.","When I have slain thee with my proper hand,","I'll follow those that even now fled hence,","And on the gates of Lud's Town set your heads.","Yield, rustic mountaineer."],"id":699,"m":"Then die. When I've killed you with my own hand, I'll go after those who just ran off, and set your heads on the gates of London. Surrender, you country bumpkin."},{"t":"sd","id":700,"text":"Exeunt, fighting."},{"t":"sd","id":701,"text":"Re-enter Belarius and Arviragus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["No company's abroad."],"id":702,"m":"There are no troops about."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["None in the world; you did mistake him, sure."],"id":703,"m":"None at all. You must have mistaken him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["I cannot tell; long is it since I saw him,","But time hath nothing blurr'd those lines of favour","Which then he wore; the snatches in his voice,","And burst of speaking, were as his. I am absolute","'Twas very Cloten."],"id":704,"m":"I can't be sure. It's a long time since I saw him, but time hasn't blurred the features he had then. The jerks in his voice and the bursts of speech were his. I'm certain it was Cloten himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["In this place we left them.","I wish my brother make good time with him,","You say he is so fell."],"id":705,"m":"This is where we left them. I hope my brother deals with him well, since you say he's so fierce."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Being scarce made up,","I mean to man, he had not apprehension","Or roaring terrors; for defect of judgment","Is oft the cease of fear."],"id":706,"m":"Being hardly grown into a man, I mean, he had no sense of fear's roaring terrors; lack of judgment often means lack of fear."},{"t":"sd","id":707,"text":"Re-enter Guiderius with Cloten's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","cont":1,"lines":["But, see, thy brother."],"id":708,"m":"But look, your brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["This Cloten was a fool, an empty purse;","There was no money in't. Not Hercules","Could have knock'd out his brains, for he had none;","Yet I not doing this, the fool had borne","My head as I do his."],"id":709,"m":"This Cloten was a fool, an empty purse with no money in it. Not even Hercules could have knocked out his brains, because he had none. Still, if I hadn't done this, the fool would be carrying my head as I'm carrying his."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["What hast thou done?"],"id":710,"m":"What have you done?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["I am perfect what: cut off one Cloten's head,","Son to the Queen, after his own report;","Who call'd me traitor, mountaineer, and swore","With his own single hand he'd take us in,","Displace our heads where—thank the gods!—they grow,","And set them on Lud's Town."],"id":711,"m":"I know exactly what: cut off the head of one Cloten, the Queen's son, by his own account, who called me traitor and mountain man and swore he'd capture us single-handed, take our heads off where (thank the gods!) they grow, and set them up on the gates of London."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["We are all undone."],"id":712,"m":"We're all ruined."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Why, worthy father, what have we to lose","But that he swore to take, our lives? The law","Protects not us; then why should we be tender","To let an arrogant piece of flesh threat us,","Play judge and executioner all himself,","For we do fear the law? What company","Discover you abroad?"],"id":713,"m":"Why, worthy father, what have we to lose except what he swore to take, our lives? The law doesn't protect us, so why should we be so soft as to let an arrogant lump of flesh threaten us and play judge and executioner all by himself, just because we're afraid of the law? Did you find any troops about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["No single soul","Can we set eye on, but in an safe reason","He must have some attendants. Though his humour","Was nothing but mutation—ay, and that","From one bad thing to worse—not frenzy, not","Absolute madness could so far have rav'd,","To bring him here alone. Although perhaps","It may be heard at court that such as we","Cave here, hunt here, are outlaws, and in time","May make some stronger head—the which he hearing,","As it is like him, might break out and swear","He'd fetch us in; yet is't not probable","To come alone, either he so undertaking","Or they so suffering. Then on good ground we fear,","If we do fear this body hath a tail","More perilous than the head."],"id":714,"m":"We can't see a single soul. But common sense says he must have some attendants. Though his mood was nothing but changes, and those from one bad thing to worse, not even frenzy or total madness could have driven him to come here alone. Perhaps it's been heard at court that people like us live in caves here, hunt here, are outlaws, and might one day gather a stronger force; and hearing that, he might, as is just like him, burst out swearing he'd bring us in. But it's not likely he'd come alone, either that he'd undertake it or that they'd allow it. So we have good reason to fear that this body has a tail more dangerous than its head."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Let ordinance","Come as the gods foresay it. Howsoe'er,","My brother hath done well."],"id":715,"m":"Let what's fated come as the gods decree. However it turns out, my brother did well."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["I had no mind","To hunt this day; the boy Fidele's sickness","Did make my way long forth."],"id":716,"m":"I didn't feel like hunting today. The boy Fidele's sickness made the way feel long."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["With his own sword,","Which he did wave against my throat, I have ta'en","His head from him. I'll throw't into the creek","Behind our rock, and let it to the sea","And tell the fishes he's the Queen's son, Cloten.","That's all I reck."],"id":717,"m":"With his own sword, which he waved at my throat, I've cut off his head. I'll throw it into the creek behind our rock and send it out to sea to tell the fishes he's the Queen's son, Cloten. That's all I care."},{"t":"sd","id":718,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["I fear'twill be reveng'd.","Would, Polydore, thou hadst not done't! though valour","Becomes thee well enough."],"id":719,"m":"I'm afraid it will be avenged. I wish you hadn't done it, Polydore, though bravery suits you well enough."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Would I had done't,","So the revenge alone pursu'd me! Polydore,","I love thee brotherly, but envy much","Thou hast robb'd me of this deed. I would revenges,","That possible strength might meet, would seek us through,","And put us to our answer."],"id":720,"m":"I wish I'd done it, so that the revenge would come after me alone! Polydore, I love you like a brother, but I'm very jealous that you've robbed me of this deed. I wish any revenge that human strength could meet would come looking for us and make us answer for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Well, 'tis done.","We'll hunt no more to-day, nor seek for danger","Where there's no profit. I prithee to our rock.","You and Fidele play the cooks; I'll stay","Till hasty Polydore return, and bring him","To dinner presently."],"id":721,"m":"Well, it's done. We'll hunt no more today, and not look for danger where there's nothing to gain. Please go back to our rock. You and Fidele do the cooking. I'll wait for hasty Polydore to come back, and bring him to dinner at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Poor sick Fidele!","I'll willingly to him; to gain his colour","I'd let a parish of such Cloten's blood,","And praise myself for charity."],"id":722,"m":"Poor sick Fidele! I'll go to him gladly. To bring back his colour I'd shed the blood of a whole parish of Clotens and call it charity."},{"t":"sd","id":723,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["O thou goddess,","Thou divine Nature, thou thyself thou blazon'st","In these two princely boys! They are as gentle","As zephyrs blowing below the violet,","Not wagging his sweet head; and yet as rough,","Their royal blood enchaf'd, as the rud'st wind","That by the top doth take the mountain pine","And make him stoop to th' vale. 'Tis wonder","That an invisible instinct should frame them","To royalty unlearn'd, honour untaught,","Civility not seen from other, valour","That wildly grows in them, but yields a crop","As if it had been sow'd. Yet still it's strange","What Cloten's being here to us portends,","Or what his death will bring us."],"id":724,"m":"Oh, goddess, divine Nature, how you display yourself in these two princely boys! They're as gentle as the breeze blowing under the violet without even stirring its sweet head; and yet, when their royal blood is heated, as rough as the wildest wind that seizes the mountain pine by its top and bends it to the valley. It's a wonder that an invisible instinct should shape them to royalty without learning it, honour without teaching, courtesy they've never seen in others, and courage that grows wild in them but yields a crop as if it had been sown. Still, it's strange what Cloten's coming here means for us, or what his death will bring us."},{"t":"sd","id":725,"text":"Re-enter Guiderius."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Where's my brother?","I have sent Cloten's clotpoll down the stream,","In embassy to his mother; his body's hostage","For his return. [Solemn music]"],"id":726,"m":"Where's my brother? I've sent Cloten's blockhead down the stream as an ambassador to his mother; his body stays as hostage for its return. (Solemn music.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["My ingenious instrument!","Hark, Polydore, it sounds. But what occasion","Hath Cadwal now to give it motion? Hark!"],"id":727,"m":"My ingenious instrument! Listen, Polydore, it's playing. But what reason does Cadwal have to play it now? Listen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Is he at home?"],"id":728,"m":"Is he home?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["He went hence even now."],"id":729,"m":"He went just now."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["What does he mean? Since death of my dear'st mother","It did not speak before. All solemn things","Should answer solemn accidents. The matter?","Triumphs for nothing and lamenting toys","Is jollity for apes and grief for boys.","Is Cadwal mad?"],"id":730,"m":"What does he mean by it? It hasn't sounded since my dear mother died. Solemn things should answer solemn events. What's the matter? Celebrating nothing and mourning trifles is merriment for apes and grief for boys. Is Cadwal mad?"},{"t":"sd","id":731,"text":"Re-enter Arviragus, with Imogen as dead, bearing her in his arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Look, here he comes,","And brings the dire occasion in his arms","Of what we blame him for!"],"id":732,"m":"Look, here he comes, carrying in his arms the terrible reason for what we were blaming him for!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["The bird is dead","That we have made so much on. I had rather","Have skipp'd from sixteen years of age to sixty,","To have turn'd my leaping time into a crutch,","Than have seen this."],"id":733,"m":"The bird we loved so much is dead. I'd rather have jumped from sixteen to sixty and turned my leaping days into a crutch than have seen this."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["O sweetest, fairest lily!","My brother wears thee not the one half so well","As when thou grew'st thyself."],"id":734,"m":"Oh, sweetest, fairest lily! My brother doesn't wear you half as well as when you grew by yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["O melancholy!","Who ever yet could sound thy bottom? find","The ooze to show what coast thy sluggish crare","Might'st easiliest harbour in? Thou blessed thing!","Jove knows what man thou mightst have made; but I,","Thou diedst, a most rare boy, of melancholy.","How found you him?"],"id":735,"m":"Oh, melancholy! Who has ever sounded your depths, or found the seabed to show on what coast your sluggish boat could most easily harbour? You blessed thing! Jove knows what a man you might have made; but I know that you died, a most rare boy, of melancholy. How did you find him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Stark, as you see;","Thus smiling, as some fly had tickled slumber,","Not as death's dart, being laugh'd at; his right cheek","Reposing on a cushion."],"id":736,"m":"Stiff, as you see; smiling like this, as if a fly had tickled him in his sleep, not as if he'd laughed at death's arrow; his right cheek resting on a cushion."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Where?"],"id":737,"m":"Where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["O' th' floor;","His arms thus leagu'd. I thought he slept, and put","My clouted brogues from off my feet, whose rudeness","Answer'd my steps too loud."],"id":738,"m":"On the floor, his arms folded like this. I thought he was asleep and took off my hobnailed shoes, because their roughness made my steps too loud."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Why, he but sleeps.","If he be gone he'll make his grave a bed;","With female fairies will his tomb be haunted,","And worms will not come to thee."],"id":739,"m":"Why, he's only asleep. If he's gone, he'll make his grave a bed. His tomb will be visited by female fairies, and worms won't come near you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["With fairest flowers,","Whilst summer lasts and I live here, Fidele,","I'll sweeten thy sad grave. Thou shalt not lack","The flower that's like thy face, pale primrose; nor","The azur'd hare-bell, like thy veins; no, nor","The leaf of eglantine, whom not to slander,","Out-sweet'ned not thy breath. The ruddock would,","With charitable bill—O bill, sore shaming","Those rich-left heirs that let their fathers lie","Without a monument!—bring thee all this;","Yea, and furr'd moss besides, when flow'rs are none,","To winter-ground thy corse—"],"id":740,"m":"With the loveliest flowers, while summer lasts and I live here, Fidele, I'll sweeten your sad grave. You won't lack the flower that's like your face, the pale primrose, nor the blue harebell, like your veins; no, nor the sweet-briar leaf, which, to be fair to it, was no sweeter than your breath. The robin, with its charitable beak (oh, a beak that shames those rich heirs who leave their fathers without a monument!), would bring you all this; yes, and furry moss as well, when there are no flowers, to cover your body against the winter..."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Prithee have done,","And do not play in wench-like words with that","Which is so serious. Let us bury him,","And not protract with admiration what","Is now due debt. To th' grave."],"id":741,"m":"Please stop, and don't play with girlish words about something so serious. Let's bury him and not delay, with our admiring, what is now a debt we owe. To the grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Say, where shall's lay him?"],"id":742,"m":"Tell me, where shall we lay him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["By good Euriphile, our mother."],"id":743,"m":"Beside good Euriphile, our mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Be't so;","And let us, Polydore, though now our voices","Have got the mannish crack, sing him to th' ground,","As once to our mother; use like note and words,","Save that Euriphile must be Fidele."],"id":744,"m":"So be it. And, Polydore, though our voices have broken now like men's, let's sing him into the ground as we once did our mother, with the same tune and words, except that Euriphile must be Fidele."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Cadwal,","I cannot sing. I'll weep, and word it with thee;","For notes of sorrow out of tune are worse","Than priests and fanes that lie."],"id":745,"m":"Cadwal, I can't sing. I'll weep and say the words with you, because songs of sorrow sung out of tune are worse than lying priests and temples."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["We'll speak it, then."],"id":746,"m":"We'll speak it, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Great griefs, I see, med'cine the less, for Cloten","Is quite forgot. He was a queen's son, boys;","And though he came our enemy, remember","He was paid for that. Though mean and mighty rotting","Together have one dust, yet reverence—","That angel of the world—doth make distinction","Of place 'tween high and low. Our foe was princely;","And though you took his life, as being our foe,","Yet bury him as a prince."],"id":747,"m":"Great griefs, I see, cure smaller ones, because Cloten is quite forgotten. He was a queen's son, boys, and though he came as our enemy, remember he's paid for that. Though the low and the mighty rot into the same dust, respect, that angel of the world, still distinguishes between high and low rank. Our enemy was a prince, and though you took his life because he was our enemy, bury him as a prince."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Pray you fetch him hither.","Thersites' body is as good as Ajax',","When neither are alive."],"id":748,"m":"Please bring him here. Thersites' body is as good as Ajax's when neither is alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["If you'll go fetch him,","We'll say our song the whilst. Brother, begin."],"id":749,"m":"If you'll go and fetch him, we'll say our song meanwhile. Brother, begin."},{"t":"sd","id":750,"text":"Exit Belarius."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Nay, Cadwal, we must lay his head to th' East;","My father hath a reason for't."],"id":751,"m":"No, Cadwal, we must lay his head to the east. Our father has a reason for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["'Tis true."],"id":752,"m":"That's true."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Come on, then, and remove him."],"id":753,"m":"Come on, then, and move him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["So. Begin."],"id":754,"m":"There. Begin."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["~Fear no more the heat o' th' sun","~Nor the furious winter's rages;","~Thou thy worldly task hast done,","~Home art gone, and ta'en thy wages.","~Golden lads and girls all must,","~As chimney-sweepers, come to dust."],"id":755,"m":"(Song) Don't fear the heat of the sun any more, or the raging of fierce winter. You've done your work in the world, gone home and been paid your wages. Golden boys and girls, like chimney-sweeps, must all come to dust."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["~Fear no more the frown o' th' great;","~Thou art past the tyrant's stroke.","~Care no more to clothe and eat;","~To thee the reed is as the oak.","~The sceptre, learning, physic, must","~All follow this and come to dust."],"id":756,"m":"Don't fear the frown of the great any more; you're beyond the tyrant's blow. Don't worry any more about clothes and food; to you the reed is the same as the oak. Kings, scholars and doctors must all follow this way and come to dust."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["~Fear no more the lightning flash,"],"id":757,"m":"Don't fear the lightning flash any more,"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["~Nor th' all-dreaded thunder-stone;"],"id":758,"m":"nor the dreaded thunderbolt."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["~Fear not slander, censure rash;"],"id":759,"m":"Don't fear slander or hasty blame."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["~Thou hast finish'd joy and moan."],"id":760,"m":"You've finished with joy and grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS AND ARVIRAGUS","lines":["~All lovers young, all lovers must","~Consign to thee and come to dust."],"id":761,"m":"All young lovers, all lovers must go the way you've gone and come to dust."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["~No exorciser harm thee!"],"id":762,"m":"May no one raising spirits disturb you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["~Nor no witchcraft charm thee!"],"id":763,"m":"May no witchcraft bewitch you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["~Ghost unlaid forbear thee!"],"id":764,"m":"May no restless ghost trouble you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["~Nothing ill come near thee!"],"id":765,"m":"May nothing evil come near you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS AND ARVIRAGUS","lines":["~Quiet consummation have,","~And renowned be thy grave!"],"id":766,"m":"Have a quiet end, and may your grave be honoured!"},{"t":"sd","id":767,"text":"Re-enter Belarius with the body of Cloten."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["We have done our obsequies. Come, lay him down."],"id":768,"m":"We've done our funeral rites. Come, lay him down."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Here's a few flowers; but 'bout midnight, more.","The herbs that have on them cold dew o' th' night","Are strewings fit'st for graves. Upon their faces.","You were as flow'rs, now wither'd. Even so","These herblets shall which we upon you strew.","Come on, away. Apart upon our knees.","The ground that gave them first has them again.","Their pleasures here are past, so is their pain."],"id":769,"m":"Here are a few flowers, but I'll bring more around midnight. Herbs with the cold night dew on them are the best things to scatter on graves. (To the bodies) Upon your faces. You were like flowers, now withered; so will these little herbs we scatter on you. Come on, let's go. We'll kneel apart. The earth that gave them life has them back again. Their pleasures here are over, and so is their pain."},{"t":"sd","id":770,"text":"Exeunt all but Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["[Awaking] Yes, sir, to Milford Haven. Which is the way?","I thank you. By yond bush? Pray, how far thither?","'Ods pittikins! can it be six mile yet?","I have gone all night. Faith, I'll lie down and sleep.","But, soft! no bedfellow. O gods and goddesses!"],"id":771,"m":"(Waking) Yes, sir, to Milford Haven. Which way is it? Thank you. By that bush? Please, how far is it? Goodness, can it still be six miles? I've walked all night. Honestly, I'll lie down and sleep. But wait, no bedfellow! Oh, gods and goddesses!"},{"t":"sd","id":772,"text":"Seeing the body."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","cont":1,"lines":["These flow'rs are like the pleasures of the world;","This bloody man, the care on't. I hope I dream;","For so I thought I was a cave-keeper,","And cook to honest creatures. But 'tis not so;","'Twas but a bolt of nothing, shot at nothing,","Which the brain makes of fumes. Our very eyes","Are sometimes, like our judgments, blind. Good faith,","I tremble still with fear; but if there be","Yet left in heaven as small a drop of pity","As a wren's eye, fear'd gods, a part of it!","The dream's here still. Even when I wake it is","Without me, as within me; not imagin'd, felt.","A headless man? The garments of Posthumus?","I know the shape of's leg; this is his hand,","His foot Mercurial, his Martial thigh,","The brawns of Hercules; but his Jovial face—","Murder in heaven! How! 'Tis gone. Pisanio,","All curses madded Hecuba gave the Greeks,","And mine to boot, be darted on thee! Thou,","Conspir'd with that irregulous devil, Cloten,","Hath here cut off my lord. To write and read","Be henceforth treacherous! Damn'd Pisanio","Hath with his forged letters—damn'd Pisanio—","From this most bravest vessel of the world","Struck the main-top. O Posthumus! alas,","Where is thy head? Where's that? Ay me! where's that?","Pisanio might have kill'd thee at the heart,","And left this head on. How should this be? Pisanio?","'Tis he and Cloten; malice and lucre in them","Have laid this woe here. O, 'tis pregnant, pregnant!","The drug he gave me, which he said was precious","And cordial to me, have I not found it","Murd'rous to th' senses? That confirms it home.","This is Pisanio's deed, and Cloten. O!","Give colour to my pale cheek with thy blood,","That we the horrider may seem to those","Which chance to find us. O, my lord, my lord!"],"id":773,"m":"These flowers are like the pleasures of the world; this bloody man is like its troubles. I hope I'm dreaming, because I thought I was a cave-keeper and cook to honest people. But it's not so; it was just an arrow of nothing shot at nothing, the kind of thing the brain makes out of vapours. Our very eyes are sometimes blind, like our judgment. Truly, I'm still trembling with fear; but if there's still a drop of pity left in heaven, as small as a wren's eye, you feared gods, give me some of it! The dream is still here. Even now that I'm awake it's outside me as well as inside: not imagined, but felt. A headless man? Posthumus's clothes? I know the shape of his leg. This is his hand, his foot as swift as Mercury's, his thigh strong as Mars's, his Herculean muscles; but his face, like Jove's... Murder in heaven? What? It's gone! Pisanio, may all the curses mad Hecuba threw at the Greeks, and mine as well, strike you! You, conspiring with that lawless devil Cloten, have killed my lord here. From now on let writing and reading be treacherous! Damned Pisanio, with his forged letters (damned Pisanio!), has knocked the mainmast off the bravest ship in the world. Oh, Posthumus! Alas, where's your head? Where is it? Oh, where is it? Pisanio could have stabbed you in the heart and left this head on. How could this happen? Pisanio? It's him and Cloten: malice and greed in them have caused this sorrow here. Oh, it's clear, clear! The drug he gave me, which he said was precious and restoring, didn't I find it deadening to the senses? That proves it completely. This is Pisanio's work, and Cloten's. Oh! Let me colour my pale cheek with your blood, so we'll look the more horrible to whoever finds us. Oh, my lord, my lord!"},{"t":"sd","id":774,"text":"Falls fainting on the body."},{"t":"sd","id":775,"text":"Enter Lucius, Captains, and a Soothsayer."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["To them the legions garrison'd in Gallia,","After your will, have cross'd the sea, attending","You here at Milford Haven; with your ships,","They are in readiness."],"id":776,"m":"In addition to them, the legions stationed in France have crossed the sea as you ordered, and are waiting for you here at Milford Haven with your ships. They're ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["But what from Rome?"],"id":777,"m":"But what news from Rome?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["The Senate hath stirr'd up the confiners","And gentlemen of Italy, most willing spirits,","That promise noble service; and they come","Under the conduct of bold Iachimo,","Sienna's brother."],"id":778,"m":"The Senate has roused the frontier people and gentlemen of Italy, very willing men who promise noble service. They're coming under the command of brave Iachimo, the Duke of Siena's brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["When expect you them?"],"id":779,"m":"When do you expect them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["With the next benefit o' th' wind."],"id":780,"m":"With the next favourable wind."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["This forwardness","Makes our hopes fair. Command our present numbers","Be muster'd; bid the captains look to't. Now, sir,","What have you dream'd of late of this war's purpose?"],"id":781,"m":"This readiness makes our hopes look good. Order the troops we have now to be assembled; tell the captains to see to it. Now, sir, what have you dreamed lately about the outcome of this war?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["Last night the very gods show'd me a vision—","I fast and pray'd for their intelligence—thus:","I saw Jove's bird, the Roman eagle, wing'd","From the spongy south to this part of the west,","There vanish'd in the sunbeams; which portends,","Unless my sins abuse my divination,","Success to th' Roman host."],"id":782,"m":"Last night the gods themselves showed me a vision (I fasted and prayed for their knowledge). I saw Jove's bird, the Roman eagle, fly from the damp south to this part of the west, and vanish there in the sunbeams. That means, unless my sins have spoiled my prophecy, success for the Roman army."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Dream often so,","And never false. Soft, ho! what trunk is here","Without his top? The ruin speaks that sometime","It was a worthy building. How? a page?","Or dead or sleeping on him? But dead, rather;","For nature doth abhor to make his bed","With the defunct, or sleep upon the dead.","Let's see the boy's face."],"id":783,"m":"Dream like that often, and never falsely. Wait, what's this trunk without a top? The ruin shows it was once a fine building. What? A page? Dead, or asleep on him? Dead, more likely, because nature hates to make its bed with the dead or sleep on a corpse. Let's see the boy's face."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["He's alive, my lord."],"id":784,"m":"He's alive, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["He'll then instruct us of this body. Young one,","Inform us of thy fortunes; for it seems","They crave to be demanded. Who is this","Thou mak'st thy bloody pillow? Or who was he","That, otherwise than noble nature did,","Hath alter'd that good picture? What's thy interest","In this sad wreck? How came't? Who is't? What art thou?"],"id":785,"m":"Then he'll tell us about this body. Young one, tell us your story, for it seems to be begging to be asked. Who is this you're using as a bloody pillow? Or who was it that has spoiled this fine picture that noble nature made? What's your connection to this sad wreck? How did it happen? Who is it? Who are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I am nothing; or if not,","Nothing to be were better. This was my master,","A very valiant Briton and a good,","That here by mountaineers lies slain. Alas!","There is no more such masters. I may wander","From east to occident; cry out for service;","Try many, all good; serve truly; never","Find such another master."],"id":786,"m":"I'm nothing; or if I am something, it would be better to be nothing. This was my master, a very brave and good Briton, killed here by mountain men. Alas, there are no more masters like him. I may wander from east to west, beg for work, try many masters, all good, serve them faithfully, and never find another like him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["'Lack, good youth!","Thou mov'st no less with thy complaining than","Thy master in bleeding. Say his name, good friend."],"id":787,"m":"Alas, good boy! You move me as much with your lament as your master does by bleeding. Tell me his name, good friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Richard du Champ. [Aside] If I do lie, and do","No harm by it, though the gods hear, I hope","They'll pardon it.—Say you, sir?"],"id":788,"m":"Richard du Champ. (Aside) If I lie and do no harm by it, I hope the gods will pardon it, though they hear me. (Aloud) What did you say, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Thy name?"],"id":789,"m":"Your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Fidele, sir."],"id":790,"m":"Fidele, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Thou dost approve thyself the very same;","Thy name well fits thy faith, thy faith thy name.","Wilt take thy chance with me? I will not say","Thou shalt be so well master'd; but, be sure,","No less belov'd. The Roman Emperor's letters,","Sent by a consul to me, should not sooner","Than thine own worth prefer thee. Go with me."],"id":791,"m":"You prove yourself exactly that: your name fits your faithfulness, and your faithfulness your name. Will you take your chances with me? I won't say you'll have as good a master, but you can be sure you'll be just as loved. Letters from the Roman Emperor, sent to me by a consul, wouldn't recommend you sooner than your own worth does. Come with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I'll follow, sir. But first, an't please the gods,","I'll hide my master from the flies, as deep","As these poor pickaxes can dig; and when","With wild wood-leaves and weeds I ha' strew'd his grave,","And on it said a century of prayers,","Such as I can, twice o'er, I'll weep and sigh;","And leaving so his service, follow you,","So please you entertain me."],"id":792,"m":"I'll follow, sir. But first, if the gods allow, I'll hide my master from the flies, as deep as these poor fingers can dig; and when I've covered his grave with wild leaves and weeds and said a hundred prayers over it, such as I can, twice over, I'll weep and sigh; and leaving his service like that, I'll follow you, if you'll take me on."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Ay, good youth;","And rather father thee than master thee.","My friends,","The boy hath taught us manly duties; let us","Find out the prettiest daisied plot we can,","And make him with our pikes and partisans","A grave. Come, arm him. Boy, he is preferr'd","By thee to us; and he shall be interr'd","As soldiers can. Be cheerful; wipe thine eyes.","Some falls are means the happier to arise."],"id":793,"m":"Yes, good boy, and I'll be more your father than your master. My friends, the boy has taught us our duty as men. Let's find the prettiest daisy-covered patch we can and dig him a grave with our pikes and spears. Come, pick him up. Boy, your loyalty has recommended him to us, and he'll be buried as well as soldiers can manage. Cheer up; wipe your eyes. Some falls are the way to rise more happily."},{"t":"sd","id":794,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Britain. Cymbeline's palace.","id":795},{"t":"sd","id":796,"text":"Enter Cymbeline, Lords, Pisanio, and attendants."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Again! and bring me word how 'tis with her."],"id":797,"m":"Again! And bring me word how she is."},{"t":"sd","id":798,"text":"Exit an attendant."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","cont":1,"lines":["A fever with the absence of her son;","A madness, of which her life's in danger. Heavens,","How deeply you at once do touch me! Imogen,","The great part of my comfort, gone; my queen","Upon a desperate bed, and in a time","When fearful wars point at me; her son gone,","So needful for this present. It strikes me past","The hope of comfort. But for thee, fellow,","Who needs must know of her departure and","Dost seem so ignorant, we'll enforce it from thee","By a sharp torture."],"id":799,"m":"A fever from the absence of her son; a madness that endangers her life. Heavens, how deeply you strike me all at once! Imogen, the greater part of my comfort, gone; my queen on her deathbed, and at a time when terrible wars are aimed at me; her son gone, who's so needed right now. It strikes me beyond all hope of comfort. But you, fellow, who must know about her leaving and seem so ignorant: we'll force it out of you with sharp torture."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Sir, my life is yours;","I humbly set it at your will; but for my mistress,","I nothing know where she remains, why gone,","Nor when she purposes return. Beseech your Highness,","Hold me your loyal servant."],"id":800,"m":"Sir, my life is yours. I humbly put it at your disposal. But as for my mistress, I know nothing about where she is, why she went, or when she means to come back. I beg your Highness, think of me as your loyal servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Good my liege,","The day that she was missing he was here.","I dare be bound he's true and shall perform","All parts of his subjection loyally. For Cloten,","There wants no diligence in seeking him,","And will no doubt be found."],"id":801,"m":"My good king, the day she went missing he was here. I'd stake my word that he's loyal and will do every part of his duty faithfully. As for Cloten, no effort is being spared in searching for him, and he'll surely be found."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["The time is troublesome.","[To Pisanio] We'll slip you for a season; but our jealousy","Does yet depend."],"id":802,"m":"These are troubled times. (To Pisanio) We'll let you go for now, but our suspicion still hangs over you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["So please your Majesty,","The Roman legions, all from Gallia drawn,","Are landed on your coast, with a supply","Of Roman gentlemen by the Senate sent."],"id":803,"m":"If it please your Majesty, the Roman legions, all brought over from France, have landed on your coast, with reinforcements of Roman gentlemen sent by the Senate."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Now for the counsel of my son and queen!","I am amaz'd with matter."],"id":804,"m":"Now I need the advice of my son and queen! I'm overwhelmed with business."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Good my liege,","Your preparation can affront no less","Than what you hear of. Come more, for more you're ready.","The want is but to put those pow'rs in motion","That long to move."],"id":805,"m":"My good king, your forces can face no less than what you hear of. If more come, you're ready for more. All that's needed is to set in motion the forces that are longing to move."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["I thank you. Let's withdraw,","And meet the time as it seeks us. We fear not","What can from Italy annoy us; but","We grieve at chances here. Away!"],"id":806,"m":"Thank you. Let's withdraw and meet events as they come for us. We're not afraid of anything Italy can do to harm us; but we grieve at what's happening here. Let's go!"},{"t":"sd","id":807,"text":"Exeunt all but Pisanio."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["I heard no letter from my master since","I wrote him Imogen was slain. 'Tis strange.","Nor hear I from my mistress, who did promise","To yield me often tidings. Neither know","What is betid to Cloten, but remain","Perplex'd in all. The heavens still must work.","Wherein I am false I am honest; not true, to be true.","These present wars shall find I love my country,","Even to the note o' th' King, or I'll fall in them.","All other doubts, by time let them be clear'd:","Fortune brings in some boats that are not steer'd."],"id":808,"m":"I've had no letter from my master since I wrote to him that Imogen was killed. It's strange. And I don't hear from my mistress either, who promised to send me news often. Nor do I know what's happened to Cloten. I'm puzzled by everything. The heavens must still be at work. Where I'm false I'm honest; I'm untrue in order to be true. These wars will find that I love my country, even so the King notices, or I'll die in them. All my other doubts, let time clear them up: fortune brings some boats into harbour that nobody steers."},{"t":"sd","id":809,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Wales. Before the cave of Belarius.","id":810},{"t":"sd","id":811,"text":"Enter Belarius, Guiderius, and Arviragus."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["The noise is round about us."],"id":812,"m":"The noise is all around us."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Let us from it."],"id":813,"m":"Let's get away from it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["What pleasure, sir, find we in life, to lock it","From action and adventure?"],"id":814,"m":"What pleasure do we get from life, sir, if we lock it away from action and adventure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Nay, what hope","Have we in hiding us? This way the Romans","Must or for Britons slay us, or receive us","For barbarous and unnatural revolts","During their use, and slay us after."],"id":815,"m":"No, what hope do we have by hiding? This way the Romans will either kill us as Britons, or take us on as savage, unnatural rebels while they need us, and kill us afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Sons,","We'll higher to the mountains; there secure us.","To the King's party there's no going. Newness","Of Cloten's death—we being not known, not muster'd","Among the bands-may drive us to a render","Where we have liv'd, and so extort from's that","Which we have done, whose answer would be death,","Drawn on with torture."],"id":816,"m":"Sons, we'll go higher into the mountains and keep safe there. We can't go to the King's side. The news of Cloten's death, since we're not known or enlisted in the army, might force us to account for where we've lived, and so squeeze out of us what we've done, and the answer to that would be death, with torture first."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["This is, sir, a doubt","In such a time nothing becoming you","Nor satisfying us."],"id":817,"m":"That's a fear, sir, that doesn't suit you at all at a time like this, and doesn't convince us."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["It is not likely","That when they hear the Roman horses neigh,","Behold their quarter'd fires, have both their eyes","And ears so cloy'd importantly as now,","That they will waste their time upon our note,","To know from whence we are."],"id":818,"m":"It's not likely that when they hear the Roman horses neighing, see their campfires, and have their eyes and ears so fully taken up with important things, they'll waste their time noticing us and asking where we come from."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["O, I am known","Of many in the army. Many years,","Though Cloten then but young, you see, not wore him","From my remembrance. And, besides, the King","Hath not deserv'd my service nor your loves,","Who find in my exile the want of breeding,","The certainty of this hard life; aye hopeless","To have the courtesy your cradle promis'd,","But to be still hot summer's tanlings and","The shrinking slaves of winter."],"id":819,"m":"Oh, many people in the army know me. Many years, as you saw, didn't wear Cloten out of my memory, though he was only young then. And besides, the King hasn't deserved my service or your love, since my exile has left you without education and certain of this hard life, forever without hope of the courtly manners your birth promised, and always the sunburnt children of hot summer and the shivering slaves of winter."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Than be so,","Better to cease to be. Pray, sir, to th' army.","I and my brother are not known; yourself","So out of thought, and thereto so o'ergrown,","Cannot be questioned."],"id":820,"m":"Better to stop living than live like that. Please, sir, let's go to the army. My brother and I aren't known; and you're so long forgotten and so overgrown with beard that nobody can question you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["By this sun that shines,","I'll thither. What thing is't that I never","Did see man die! scarce ever look'd on blood","But that of coward hares, hot goats, and venison!","Never bestrid a horse, save one that had","A rider like myself, who ne'er wore rowel","Nor iron on his heel! I am asham'd","To look upon the holy sun, to have","The benefit of his blest beams, remaining","So long a poor unknown."],"id":821,"m":"By this shining sun, I'll go there. What kind of man am I, never to have seen a man die! I've hardly ever seen blood except from cowardly hares, lusty goats and deer! I've never ridden a horse, except one whose rider was like me and never wore spurs or iron on his heel! I'm ashamed to look at the holy sun and enjoy its blessed rays, while staying so long a poor nobody."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["By heavens, I'll go!","If you will bless me, sir, and give me leave,","I'll take the better care; but if you will not,","The hazard therefore due fall on me by","The hands of Romans!"],"id":822,"m":"By heaven, I'll go! If you'll bless me, sir, and give me permission, I'll take better care of myself; but if you won't, let the danger that brings fall on me by Roman hands!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["So say I. Amen."],"id":823,"m":"I say the same. Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["No reason I, since of your lives you set","So slight a valuation, should reserve","My crack'd one to more care. Have with you, boys!","If in your country wars you chance to die,","That is my bed too, lads, and there I'll lie.","Lead, lead. [Aside] The time seems long; their blood thinks scorn","Till it fly out and show them princes born."],"id":824,"m":"Since you value your lives so cheaply, I've no reason to take more care of my cracked old one. I'm with you, boys! If you happen to die in your country's wars, that's my bed too, lads, and I'll lie there. Lead on, lead on. (Aside) Time seems slow to them. Their blood is impatient until it flies out and shows they were born princes."},{"t":"sd","id":825,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Britain. The Roman camp.","id":826},{"t":"sd","id":827,"text":"Enter Posthumus alone, with a bloody handkerchief."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Yea, bloody cloth, I'll keep thee; for I wish'd","Thou shouldst be colour'd thus. You married ones,","If each of you should take this course, how many","Must murder wives much better than themselves","For wrying but a little! O Pisanio!","Every good servant does not all commands;","No bond but to do just ones. Gods! if you","Should have ta'en vengeance on my faults, I never","Had liv'd to put on this; so had you saved","The noble Imogen to repent, and struck","Me, wretch more worth your vengeance. But alack,","You snatch some hence for little faults; that's love,","To have them fall no more. You some permit","To second ills with ills, each elder worse,","And make them dread it, to the doer's thrift.","But Imogen is your own. Do your best wills,","And make me blest to obey. I am brought hither","Among th' Italian gentry, and to fight","Against my lady's kingdom. 'Tis enough","That, Britain, I have kill'd thy mistress; peace!","I'll give no wound to thee. Therefore, good heavens,","Hear patiently my purpose. I'll disrobe me","Of these Italian weeds, and suit myself","As does a Britain peasant. So I'll fight","Against the part I come with; so I'll die","For thee, O Imogen, even for whom my life","Is every breath a death. And thus unknown,","Pitied nor hated, to the face of peril","Myself I'll dedicate. Let me make men know","More valour in me than my habits show.","Gods, put the strength o' th' Leonati in me!","To shame the guise o' th' world, I will begin","The fashion—less without and more within."],"id":828,"m":"Yes, bloody cloth, I'll keep you, because I wished you to be coloured like this. You married men, if each of you followed my course, how many would murder wives much better than themselves for straying just a little! Oh, Pisanio! A good servant doesn't obey every order; he's only bound to obey just ones. Gods! If you'd taken vengeance on my faults, I'd never have lived to do this. You'd have saved the noble Imogen to repent, and struck me, the wretch who deserved your vengeance more. But alas, you take some people away for small faults; that's love, so they don't sin again. Others you allow to pile sin on sin, each worse than the last, and make them dread it, for their own good. But Imogen is yours now. Do what you will, and make me blessed by obeying. I've been brought here among the Italian gentlemen to fight against my lady's kingdom. It's enough, Britain, that I've killed your princess. Peace! I won't wound you. So, good heavens, listen patiently to my plan. I'll strip off these Italian clothes and dress myself like a British peasant. That way I'll fight against the side I came with; that way I'll die for you, Imogen, for whom every breath of my life is a death. And so, unknown, neither pitied nor hated, I'll throw myself in the face of danger. Let me show men more courage in me than my clothes suggest. Gods, give me the strength of the Leonati! To shame the fashion of the world, I'll start a new one: less on the outside and more within."},{"t":"sd","id":829,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Britain. A field of battle between the British and Roman camps.","id":830},{"t":"sd","id":831,"text":"Enter Lucius, Iachimo, and the Roman army at one door, and the British army at another, Leonatus Posthumus following like a poor soldier. They march over and go out. Alarums. Then enter again, in skirmish, Iachimo and Posthumus. He vanquisheth and disarmeth Iachimo, and then leaves him."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["The heaviness and guilt within my bosom","Takes off my manhood. I have belied a lady,","The Princess of this country, and the air on't","Revengingly enfeebles me; or could this carl,","A very drudge of nature's, have subdu'd me","In my profession? Knighthoods and honours borne","As I wear mine are titles but of scorn.","If that thy gentry, Britain, go before","This lout as he exceeds our lords, the odds","Is that we scarce are men, and you are gods."],"id":832,"m":"The weight of guilt in my heart takes away my manhood. I've slandered a lady, the princess of this country, and its very air weakens me in revenge. Otherwise, could this peasant, one of nature's drudges, have beaten me at my own profession? Knighthoods and honours worn as I wear mine are only titles of scorn. If your gentlemen, Britain, are as much better than this lout as he is better than our lords, then the difference is that we're hardly men and you are gods."},{"t":"sd","id":833,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sd","id":834,"text":"The battle continues; the Britons fly; Cymbeline is taken. Then enter to his rescue Belarius, Guiderius, and Arviragus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Stand, stand! We have th' advantage of the ground;","The lane is guarded; nothing routs us but","The villainy of our fears."],"id":835,"m":"Stand, stand! We have the advantage of the ground. The lane is guarded. Nothing can rout us but our own cowardly fears."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS AND ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Stand, stand, and fight!"],"id":836,"m":"Stand, stand, and fight!"},{"t":"sd","id":837,"text":"Re-enter Posthumus, and seconds the Britons; they rescue Cymbeline, and exeunt. Then re-enter Lucius and Iachimo, with Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Away, boy, from the troops, and save thyself;","For friends kill friends, and the disorder's such","As war were hoodwink'd."],"id":838,"m":"Get away from the troops, boy, and save yourself. Friends are killing friends, and the chaos is such that war seems blindfolded."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["'Tis their fresh supplies."],"id":839,"m":"It's their fresh reinforcements."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["It is a day turn'd strangely. Or betimes","Let's reinforce or fly."],"id":840,"m":"It's a day that has turned strangely. We must quickly either reinforce or run."},{"t":"sd","id":841,"text":"Exeunt."},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Britain. Another part of the field.","id":842},{"t":"sd","id":843,"text":"Enter Posthumus and a Britain Lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Cam'st thou from where they made the stand?"],"id":844,"m":"Did you come from where they made their stand?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I did:","Though you, it seems, come from the fliers."],"id":845,"m":"I did, though you, it seems, come from the ones who ran."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["I did."],"id":846,"m":"I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["No blame be to you, sir, for all was lost,","But that the heavens fought. The King himself","Of his wings destitute, the army broken,","And but the backs of Britons seen, an flying,","Through a strait lane—the enemy, full-hearted,","Lolling the tongue with slaught'ring, having work","More plentiful than tools to do't, struck down","Some mortally, some slightly touch'd, some falling","Merely through fear, that the strait pass was damm'd","With dead men hurt behind, and cowards living","To die with length'ned shame."],"id":847,"m":"No blame to you, sir, because all was lost if the heavens hadn't fought for us. The King himself was cut off from his wings, the army broken, and nothing to be seen but the backs of Britons, all fleeing through a narrow lane. The enemy, full of spirit, tongues hanging out from slaughter, with more work than tools to do it, struck some down fatally, some lightly wounded, some fell from pure fear, so that the narrow way was blocked with dead men wounded in the back and cowards who lived only to die in long shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Where was this lane?"],"id":848,"m":"Where was this lane?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Close by the battle, ditch'd, and wall'd with turf,","Which gave advantage to an ancient soldier—","An honest one, I warrant, who deserv'd","So long a breeding as his white beard came to,","In doing this for's country. Athwart the lane","He, with two striplings—lads more like to run","The country base than to commit such slaughter;","With faces fit for masks, or rather fairer","Than those for preservation cas'd or shame—","Made good the passage, cried to those that fled","'Our Britain's harts die flying, not our men.","To darkness fleet souls that fly backwards! Stand;","Or we are Romans and will give you that,","Like beasts, which you shun beastly, and may save","But to look back in frown. Stand, stand!' These three,","Three thousand confident, in act as many—","For three performers are the file when all","The rest do nothing—with this word 'Stand, stand!'","Accommodated by the place, more charming","With their own nobleness, which could have turn'd","A distaff to a lance, gilded pale looks,","Part shame, part spirit renew'd; that some turn'd coward","But by example—O, a sin in war","Damn'd in the first beginners!—gan to look","The way that they did and to grin like lions","Upon the pikes o' th' hunters. Then began","A stop i' th' chaser, a retire; anon","A rout, confusion thick. Forthwith they fly,","Chickens, the way which they stoop'd eagles; slaves,","The strides they victors made; and now our cowards,","Like fragments in hard voyages, became","The life o' th' need. Having found the back-door open","Of the unguarded hearts, heavens, how they wound!","Some slain before, some dying, some their friends","O'erborne i' th' former wave. Ten chas'd by one","Are now each one the slaughterman of twenty.","Those that would die or ere resist are grown","The mortal bugs o' th' field."],"id":849,"m":"Right by the battlefield, with a ditch and turf walls. It gave the advantage to an old soldier, an honest one, I'd swear, who deserved the long life his white beard showed by doing this for his country. Across the lane he, with two youngsters (lads who looked more fit to play country games than to do such slaughter, with faces fit for masks, or fairer than faces covered to keep them pale or hide shame), held the passage and shouted to those running away: \"Our Britain's deer die running, not our men. Souls that run backwards fly to darkness! Stand, or we'll act like Romans and give you the death like beasts that you're running from like beasts, and which you can avoid only by turning round to face them. Stand, stand!\" These three, with the confidence of three thousand, and as good as that many in action (because three who act are a whole army when all the rest do nothing), with this cry of \"Stand, stand!\", helped by the place, and even more inspiring by their own nobility, which could have turned a spinning stick into a lance, brought colour back to pale faces, partly from shame, partly from renewed spirit. Some who had become cowards only by example (oh, a sin in war, damnable in those who start it) began to look the way the three did and to snarl like lions at the hunters' spears. Then the pursuers paused, then retreated; soon there was a rout, thick confusion. At once they ran like chickens the way they had swooped like eagles; they ran back like slaves over the ground they'd crossed as victors. And now our cowards, like scraps of food saved on hard voyages, became the lifesavers in the crisis. Finding the back door of the unguarded hearts open, heavens, how they struck! Some at those already dead, some at the dying, some at their own friends knocked down in the first wave. Ten who'd been chased by one now each became the butcher of twenty. Those who'd have died rather than resist became the deadly terrors of the field."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["This was strange chance:","A narrow lane, an old man, and two boys."],"id":850,"m":"That was a strange event: a narrow lane, an old man, and two boys."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Nay, do not wonder at it; you are made","Rather to wonder at the things you hear","Than to work any. Will you rhyme upon't,","And vent it for a mock'ry? Here is one:","'Two boys, an old man (twice a boy), a lane,","Preserv'd the Britons, was the Romans' bane.'"],"id":851,"m":"No, don't wonder at it. You're made to wonder at things you hear, not to do any. Will you make a rhyme about it and pass it round as a joke? Here's one: \"Two boys, an old man (twice a boy), a lane, saved the Britons and were the Romans' bane.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Nay, be not angry, sir."],"id":852,"m":"No, don't be angry, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["'Lack, to what end?","Who dares not stand his foe I'll be his friend;","For if he'll do as he is made to do,","I know he'll quickly fly my friendship too.","You have put me into rhyme."],"id":853,"m":"Alas, what for? If a man won't stand and face his enemy, I'll be his friend; because if he does what's natural to him, I know he'll quickly run from my friendship too. You've set me rhyming."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Farewell; you're angry."],"id":854,"m":"Goodbye. You're angry."},{"t":"sd","id":855,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Still going? This is a lord! O noble misery,","To be i' th' field and ask 'What news?' of me!","To-day how many would have given their honours","To have sav'd their carcasses! took heel to do't,","And yet died too! I, in mine own woe charm'd,","Could not find death where I did hear him groan,","Nor feel him where he struck. Being an ugly monster,","'Tis strange he hides him in fresh cups, soft beds,","Sweet words; or hath moe ministers than we","That draw his knives i' th' war. Well, I will find him;","For being now a favourer to the Briton,","No more a Briton, I have resum'd again","The part I came in. Fight I will no more,","But yield me to the veriest hind that shall","Once touch my shoulder. Great the slaughter is","Here made by th' Roman; great the answer be","Britons must take. For me, my ransom's death;","On either side I come to spend my breath,","Which neither here I'll keep nor bear again,","But end it by some means for Imogen."],"id":856,"m":"Still running? That's a lord! Oh, noble wretch, to be on the battlefield and ask me \"What's the news?\" Today how many would have given their honour to save their skins, ran for it, and still died! I, protected by my own misery, couldn't find death where I heard him groaning, or feel him where he struck. Being such an ugly monster, it's strange he hides himself in fresh cups of wine, soft beds, sweet words, or has more servants there than we who draw his knives in war. Well, I'll find him. Since I've been fighting for the Britons, I'll be a Briton no more; I'll take up again the part I came in. I won't fight any more, but surrender to the lowest peasant who touches my shoulder. Great is the slaughter the Romans have made here; great must be the revenge the Britons take. For me, death will be my ransom. I came to spend my life on either side, and I won't keep it here or take it home again, but end it somehow for Imogen."},{"t":"sd","id":857,"text":"Enter two British Captains and soldiers."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CAPTAIN","lines":["Great Jupiter be prais'd! Lucius is taken.","'Tis thought the old man and his sons were angels."],"id":858,"m":"Great Jupiter be praised! Lucius is captured. People think the old man and his sons were angels."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CAPTAIN","lines":["There was a fourth man, in a silly habit,","That gave th' affront with them."],"id":859,"m":"There was a fourth man in shabby clothes who fought the enemy with them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CAPTAIN","lines":["So 'tis reported;","But none of 'em can be found. Stand! who's there?"],"id":860,"m":"So it's said, but none of them can be found. Halt! Who's there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["A Roman,","Who had not now been drooping here if seconds","Had answer'd him."],"id":861,"m":"A Roman, who wouldn't be hanging his head here now if his comrades had backed him up."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CAPTAIN","lines":["Lay hands on him; a dog!","A leg of Rome shall not return to tell","What crows have peck'd them here. He brags his service,","As if he were of note. Bring him to th' King."],"id":862,"m":"Seize him, the dog! Not one Roman leg will go home to tell what crows have pecked them here. He boasts of his service as if he were someone important. Take him to the King."},{"t":"sd","id":863,"text":"Enter Cymbeline, Belarius, Guiderius, Arviragus, Pisanio, and Roman captives. The Captains present Posthumus to Cymbeline, who delivers him over to a gaoler. Exeunt omnes."},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Britain. A prison.","id":864},{"t":"sd","id":865,"text":"Enter Posthumus and two Gaolers."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GAOLER","lines":["You shall not now be stol'n, you have locks upon you;","So graze as you find pasture."],"id":866,"m":"You won't be stolen now; you've got locks on you. So graze wherever you find pasture."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GAOLER","lines":["Ay, or a stomach."],"id":867,"m":"Yes, or an appetite."},{"t":"sd","id":868,"text":"Exeunt Gaolers."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Most welcome, bondage! for thou art a way,","I think, to liberty. Yet am I better","Than one that's sick o' th' gout, since he had rather","Groan so in perpetuity than be cur'd","By th' sure physician death, who is the key","T' unbar these locks. My conscience, thou art fetter'd","More than my shanks and wrists; you good gods, give me","The penitent instrument to pick that bolt,","Then, free for ever! Is't enough I am sorry?","So children temporal fathers do appease;","Gods are more full of mercy. Must I repent,","I cannot do it better than in gyves,","Desir'd more than constrain'd. To satisfy,","If of my freedom 'tis the main part, take","No stricter render of me than my all.","I know you are more clement than vile men,","Who of their broken debtors take a third,","A sixth, a tenth, letting them thrive again","On their abatement; that's not my desire.","For Imogen's dear life take mine; and though","'Tis not so dear, yet 'tis a life; you coin'd it.","'Tween man and man they weigh not every stamp;","Though light, take pieces for the figure's sake;","You rather mine, being yours. And so, great pow'rs,","If you will take this audit, take this life,","And cancel these cold bonds. O Imogen!","I'll speak to thee in silence. [Sleeps]"],"id":869,"m":"Welcome, imprisonment! Because I think you're a way to freedom. Still, I'm better off than a man sick with gout, since he'd rather groan like that forever than be cured by the sure doctor, death, who holds the key to unlock these locks. My conscience, you're chained more tightly than my ankles and wrists. Good gods, give me the tool of penitence to pick that lock, and then I'm free for ever! Is it enough that I'm sorry? That's how children calm their earthly fathers; gods are more full of mercy. If I must repent, I can't do it better than in chains I want rather than chains forced on me. If making amends is the main part of my freedom, take nothing less from me than my all. I know you're more merciful than vile men, who take a third, a sixth, a tenth from their bankrupt debtors and let them recover on what's left. That's not what I want. For Imogen's precious life take mine; and though it's not as precious, still it's a life: you minted it. Between man and man they don't weigh every coin; even if they're light, they accept them for the image stamped on them. Take mine all the more because it's yours. And so, great powers, if you'll accept this account, take this life and cancel these cold chains. Oh, Imogen! I'll speak to you in silence. (He sleeps.)"},{"t":"sd","id":870,"text":"Solemn music. Enter, as in an apparition, Sicilius Leonatus, father to Posthumus, an old man attired like a warrior; leading in his hand an ancient matron, his Wife, and mother to Posthumus, with music before them. Then, after other music, follows the two young Leonati, brothers to Posthumus, with wounds, as they died in the wars. They circle Posthumus round as he lies sleeping."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICILIUS","lines":["No more, thou thunder-master, show","Thy spite on mortal flies.","With Mars fall out, with Juno chide,","That thy adulteries","Rates and revenges.","Hath my poor boy done aught but well,","Whose face I never saw?","I died whilst in the womb he stay'd","Attending nature's law;","Whose father then, as men report","Thou orphans' father art,","Thou shouldst have been, and shielded him","From this earth-vexing smart."],"id":871,"m":"Thunder-master, stop showing your spite to mortal flies. Quarrel with Mars, argue with Juno, who scolds you for your affairs and pays you back for them. Has my poor boy, whose face I never saw, done anything but good? I died while he was still in the womb waiting to be born. You, who are said to be the father of orphans, should have been his father then and shielded him from this earthly pain."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTHER","lines":["Lucina lent not me her aid,","But took me in my throes,","That from me was Posthumus ripp'd,","Came crying 'mongst his foes,","A thing of pity."],"id":872,"m":"The goddess of childbirth didn't help me, but took me in my labour pains, so that Posthumus was ripped from me and came crying into the world among his enemies, a thing to be pitied."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICILIUS","lines":["Great Nature like his ancestry","Moulded the stuff so fair","That he deserv'd the praise o' th' world","As great Sicilius' heir."],"id":873,"m":"Great Nature, like his ancestors, moulded him so finely that he deserved the world's praise as great Sicilius's heir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BROTHER","lines":["When once he was mature for man,","In Britain where was he","That could stand up his parallel,","Or fruitful object be","In eye of Imogen, that best","Could deem his dignity?"],"id":874,"m":"When he grew to manhood, where in Britain was there anyone who could stand up as his equal, or win Imogen's eye, who could best judge his worth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTHER","lines":["With marriage wherefore was he mock'd,","To be exil'd and thrown","From Leonati seat and cast","From her his dearest one,","Sweet Imogen?"],"id":875,"m":"Why was he mocked with marriage, only to be exiled and thrown out of the Leonati's home, cast away from his dearest one, sweet Imogen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICILIUS","lines":["Why did you suffer Iachimo,","Slight thing of Italy,","To taint his nobler heart and brain","With needless jealousy,","And to become the geck and scorn","O' th' other's villainy?"],"id":876,"m":"Why did you let Iachimo, a worthless Italian, poison his nobler heart and mind with groundless jealousy, and make him the dupe and laughing-stock of the other's villainy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND BROTHER","lines":["For this from stiller seats we came,","Our parents and us twain,","That, striking in our country's cause,","Fell bravely and were slain,","Our fealty and Tenantius' right","With honour to maintain."],"id":877,"m":"For this we came from the quiet fields of the blessed, our parents and the two of us, who fought for our country and fell bravely and were killed, defending with honour our loyalty and Tenantius's right."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BROTHER","lines":["Like hardiment Posthumus hath","To Cymbeline perform'd.","Then, Jupiter, thou king of gods,","Why hast thou thus adjourn'd","The graces for his merits due,","Being all to dolours turn'd?"],"id":878,"m":"Posthumus has done just as bravely for Cymbeline. So, Jupiter, king of the gods, why have you held back the rewards his merits deserve and turned them all to sorrows?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICILIUS","lines":["Thy crystal window ope; look out;","No longer exercise","Upon a valiant race thy harsh","And potent injuries."],"id":879,"m":"Open your crystal window and look out. Stop inflicting your harsh and mighty injuries on a brave family."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOTHER","lines":["Since, Jupiter, our son is good,","Take off his miseries."],"id":880,"m":"Since our son is good, Jupiter, take away his miseries."},{"t":"sp","who":"SICILIUS","lines":["Peep through thy marble mansion. Help!","Or we poor ghosts will cry","To th' shining synod of the rest","Against thy deity."],"id":881,"m":"Look out of your marble palace. Help! Or we poor ghosts will appeal to the shining council of the other gods against your godhead."},{"t":"sp","who":"BROTHERS","lines":["Help, Jupiter! or we appeal,","And from thy justice fly."],"id":882,"m":"Help, Jupiter, or we'll appeal and fly from your justice!"},{"t":"sd","id":883,"text":"Jupiter descends in thunder and lightning, sitting upon an eagle. He throws a thunderbolt. The Ghosts fall on their knees."},{"t":"sp","who":"JUPITER","lines":["No more, you petty spirits of region low,","Offend our hearing; hush! How dare you ghosts","Accuse the Thunderer whose bolt, you know,","Sky-planted, batters all rebelling coasts?","Poor shadows of Elysium, hence and rest","Upon your never-withering banks of flow'rs.","Be not with mortal accidents opprest:","No care of yours it is; you know 'tis ours.","Whom best I love I cross; to make my gift,","The more delay'd, delighted. Be content;","Your low-laid son our godhead will uplift;","His comforts thrive, his trials well are spent.","Our Jovial star reign'd at his birth, and in","Our temple was he married. Rise and fade!","He shall be lord of Lady Imogen,","And happier much by his affliction made.","This tablet lay upon his breast, wherein","Our pleasure his full fortune doth confine;","And so, away; no farther with your din","Express impatience, lest you stir up mine.","Mount, eagle, to my palace crystalline. [Ascends]"],"id":884,"m":"No more, you petty spirits from the lower world; stop offending my ears. Quiet! How dare you ghosts accuse the Thunderer, whose bolt, as you know, hurled from the sky, smashes every rebellious coast? Poor shadows from Elysium, go back and rest on your banks of flowers that never fade. Don't trouble yourselves with mortal matters: they're not your concern; you know they're ours. I test those I love best, to make my gift more delightful for being delayed. Be content. My godhead will raise up your fallen son. His comforts will grow; his trials are nearly over. My star, Jupiter, ruled at his birth, and he was married in my temple. Rise and vanish! He'll be husband of Lady Imogen, and much happier for his suffering. Lay this tablet on his breast; in it I've set out his whole fortune. And so, go; don't show your impatience with any more noise, or you'll stir up mine. Rise, eagle, to my crystal palace. (He ascends.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICILIUS","lines":["He came in thunder; his celestial breath","Was sulpherous to smell; the holy eagle","Stoop'd as to foot us. His ascension is","More sweet than our blest fields. His royal bird","Prunes the immortal wing, and cloys his beak,","As when his god is pleas'd."],"id":885,"m":"He came in thunder; his heavenly breath smelled of sulphur. The holy eagle swooped as if to seize us. His going up is sweeter than our blessed fields. His royal bird preens its immortal wing and cleans its beak, as it does when its god is pleased."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Thanks, Jupiter!"],"id":886,"m":"Thanks, Jupiter!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SICILIUS","lines":["The marble pavement closes, he is enter'd","His radiant roof. Away! and, to be blest,","Let us with care perform his great behest. [Ghosts vanish]"],"id":887,"m":"The marble floor of heaven closes; he's gone into his shining palace. Let's go, and to be blessed, let's carry out his great command with care. (The ghosts vanish.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["[Waking] Sleep, thou has been a grandsire and begot","A father to me; and thou hast created","A mother and two brothers. But, O scorn,","Gone! They went hence so soon as they were born.","And so I am awake. Poor wretches, that depend","On greatness' favour, dream as I have done;","Wake and find nothing. But, alas, I swerve;","Many dream not to find, neither deserve,","And yet are steep'd in favours; so am I,","That have this golden chance, and know not why.","What fairies haunt this ground? A book? O rare one!","Be not, as is our fangled world, a garment","Nobler than that it covers. Let thy effects","So follow to be most unlike our courtiers,","As good as promise.","[Reads] 'When as a lion's whelp shall, to himself unknown,","without seeking find, and be embrac'd by a piece of tender air;","and when from a stately cedar shall be lopp'd branches which,","being dead many years, shall after revive, be jointed to the old","stock, and freshly grow; then shall Posthumus end his miseries,","Britain be fortunate and flourish in peace and plenty.'","'Tis still a dream, or else such stuff as madmen","Tongue, and brain not; either both or nothing,","Or senseless speaking, or a speaking such","As sense cannot untie. Be what it is,","The action of my life is like it, which","I'll keep, if but for sympathy."],"id":888,"m":"(Waking) Sleep, you've been a grandfather and given me a father, and you've created a mother and two brothers. But oh, the mockery: gone! They went as soon as they were born. And so I'm awake. Poor wretches who depend on great men's favour dream as I have done, and wake to find nothing. But alas, I'm going wrong: many don't dream of finding it, or deserve it, and are still soaked in favours. So am I, who have this golden chance and don't know why. What fairies haunt this place? A book? Oh, a fine one! Don't be, like our showy world, a garment nobler than what it covers. Let what you do be as good as what you promise, quite unlike our courtiers. (Reading) \"When a lion's cub, unknown to himself, shall find without searching, and be embraced by a piece of tender air; and when from a stately cedar branches are cut which, after being dead for many years, revive, are joined to the old trunk, and grow fresh again; then Posthumus will end his miseries, and Britain will be fortunate and flourish in peace and plenty.\" It's still a dream, or else the kind of thing madmen say without thinking; either both or nothing; either senseless talk or talk that sense can't untangle. Whatever it is, the course of my life is like it, so I'll keep it, if only because it matches me."},{"t":"sd","id":889,"text":"Re-enter Gaoler."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["Come, sir, are you ready for death?"],"id":890,"m":"Come, sir, are you ready to die?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Over-roasted rather; ready long ago."],"id":891,"m":"Over-cooked, rather; I've been ready a long time."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["Hanging is the word, sir; if you be ready for that, you are","well cook'd."],"id":892,"m":"Hanging's the word, sir. If you're ready for that, you're well cooked."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["So, if I prove a good repast to the spectators, the dish","pays the shot."],"id":893,"m":"Then if I make a good meal for the spectators, the dish pays the bill."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["A heavy reckoning for you, sir. But the comfort is, you","shall be called to no more payments, fear no more tavern bills,","which are often the sadness of parting, as the procuring of mirth.","You come in faint for want of meat, depart reeling with too much","drink; sorry that you have paid too much, and sorry that you are","paid too much; purse and brain both empty; the brain the heavier","for being too light, the purse too light, being drawn of","heaviness. O, of this contradiction you shall now be quit. O, the","charity of a penny cord! It sums up thousands in a trice. You","have no true debitor and creditor but it; of what's past, is, and","to come, the discharge. Your neck, sir, is pen, book, and","counters; so the acquittance follows."],"id":894,"m":"A heavy bill for you, sir. But the comfort is, you'll be asked to pay nothing more, and needn't fear any more tavern bills, which are often as sad at parting time as they were cheerful at the start. You come in faint with hunger and leave reeling with too much drink, sorry you've paid too much and sorry you've been paid out too much; purse and brain both empty, the brain heavier for being too light, the purse too light for being emptied of its weight. Oh, you'll be free of this contradiction now. Oh, the charity of a penny rope! It settles thousands in an instant. It's your only true accountant, settling everything past, present and to come. Your neck, sir, is pen, ledger and counters; so the receipt follows."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I am merrier to die than thou art to live."],"id":895,"m":"I'm merrier to die than you are to live."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["Indeed, sir, he that sleeps feels not the toothache. But a","man that were to sleep your sleep, and a hangman to help him to","bed, I think he would change places with his officer; for look","you, sir, you know not which way you shall go."],"id":896,"m":"Indeed, sir, a man who's asleep doesn't feel toothache. But a man about to sleep your sleep, with a hangman to help him to bed, would gladly change places with his officer, I think; because, you see, sir, you don't know which way you're going."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Yes indeed do I, fellow."],"id":897,"m":"Yes, I do, fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["Your death has eyes in's head, then; I have not seen him so","pictur'd. You must either be directed by some that take upon them","to know, or to take upon yourself that which I am sure you do not","know, or jump the after-inquiry on your own peril. And how you","shall speed in your journey's end, I think you'll never return to","tell one."],"id":898,"m":"Then your death has eyes in his head; I've never seen him painted that way. You must either be guided by people who claim to know, or claim to know yourself what I'm sure you don't, or risk finding out afterwards on your own head. And how you'll fare at your journey's end, I don't think you'll ever come back to tell anyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I tell thee, fellow, there are none want eyes to direct","them the way I am going, but such as wink and will not use them."],"id":899,"m":"I tell you, fellow, nobody lacks eyes to guide them the way I'm going, except those who shut them and won't use them."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["What an infinite mock is this, that a man should have the","best use of eyes to see the way of blindness! I am sure hanging's","the way of winking."],"id":900,"m":"What an endless joke this is, that a man should have the best use of his eyes to see the way to blindness! I'm sure hanging is the way to shut your eyes."},{"t":"sd","id":901,"text":"Enter a Messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Knock off his manacles; bring your prisoner to the King."],"id":902,"m":"Knock off his handcuffs and bring your prisoner to the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Thou bring'st good news: I am call'd to be made free."],"id":903,"m":"You bring good news: I'm called to be set free."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["I'll be hang'd then."],"id":904,"m":"I'll be hanged, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Thou shalt be then freer than a gaoler; no bolts for the","dead."],"id":905,"m":"Then you'll be freer than a jailer: the dead have no locks."},{"t":"sd","id":906,"text":"Exeunt Posthumus and Messenger."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAOLER","lines":["Unless a man would marry a gallows and beget young gibbets,","I never saw one so prone. Yet, on my conscience, there are verier","knaves desire to live, for all he be a Roman; and there be some","of them too that die against their wills; so should I, if I were","one. I would we were all of one mind, and one mind good. O, there","were desolation of gaolers and gallowses! I speak against my","present profit, but my wish hath a preferment in't."],"id":907,"m":"Unless a man wanted to marry a gallows and father little gallows, I never saw anyone so eager. And yet, I swear, there are bigger rogues who want to live, even though he's a Roman; and some of them die against their will too; so would I, if I were one. I wish we were all of one mind, and a good mind. Oh, then there'd be an end of jailers and gallows! I'm speaking against my own present profit, but my wish has a promotion in it."},{"t":"sd","id":908,"text":"Exit."},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Britain. Cymbeline's tent.","id":909},{"t":"sd","id":910,"text":"Enter Cymbeline, Belarius, Guiderius, Arviragus, Pisanio, Lords, Officers, and attendants."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Stand by my side, you whom the gods have made","Preservers of my throne. Woe is my heart","That the poor soldier that so richly fought,","Whose rags sham'd gilded arms, whose naked breast","Stepp'd before targes of proof, cannot be found.","He shall be happy that can find him, if","Our grace can make him so."],"id":911,"m":"Stand beside me, you whom the gods have made the saviours of my throne. My heart grieves that the poor soldier who fought so splendidly, whose rags shamed gilded armour, whose bare chest stepped in front of tested shields, can't be found. Whoever can find him will be made happy, if my favour can do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["I never saw","Such noble fury in so poor a thing;","Such precious deeds in one that promis'd nought","But beggary and poor looks."],"id":912,"m":"I never saw such noble fury in so poor a figure, such precious deeds from someone who looked like nothing but a beggar."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["No tidings of him?"],"id":913,"m":"No news of him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["He hath been search'd among the dead and living,","But no trace of him."],"id":914,"m":"He's been searched for among the dead and the living, but there's no trace of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["To my grief, I am","The heir of his reward; [To Belarius, Guiderius, and Arviragus]","which I will add","To you, the liver, heart, and brain, of Britain,","By whom I grant she lives. 'Tis now the time","To ask of whence you are. Report it."],"id":915,"m":"To my sorrow, I inherit his reward, (to Belarius, Guiderius and Arviragus) which I'll add to yours, the liver, heart and brain of Britain, by whom I admit she lives. Now it's time to ask where you come from. Tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Sir,","In Cambria are we born, and gentlemen;","Further to boast were neither true nor modest,","Unless I add we are honest."],"id":916,"m":"Sir, we were born in Wales, and we're gentlemen. To boast further would be neither true nor modest, unless I add that we're honest."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Bow your knees.","Arise my knights o' th' battle; I create you","Companions to our person, and will fit you","With dignities becoming your estates."],"id":917,"m":"Kneel. Rise, my knights of the battle. I make you companions of my person, and I'll give you titles to match your new rank."},{"t":"sd","id":918,"text":"Enter Cornelius and Ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","cont":1,"lines":["There's business in these faces. Why so sadly","Greet you our victory? You look like Romans,","And not o' th' court of Britain."],"id":919,"m":"There's trouble in these faces. Why do you greet our victory so sadly? You look like Romans, not members of the British court."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["Hail, great King!","To sour your happiness I must report","The Queen is dead."],"id":920,"m":"Hail, great King! To sour your happiness I have to tell you the Queen is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Who worse than a physician","Would this report become? But I consider","By med'cine'life may be prolong'd, yet death","Will seize the doctor too. How ended she?"],"id":921,"m":"Who'd be a worse bearer of this news than a doctor? But I remember that medicine may lengthen life, yet death will seize the doctor too. How did she die?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["With horror, madly dying, like her life;","Which, being cruel to the world, concluded","Most cruel to herself. What she confess'd","I will report, so please you; these her women","Can trip me if I err, who with wet cheeks","Were present when she finish'd."],"id":922,"m":"In horror, dying madly, as she lived. Her life, cruel to the world, ended most cruelly to herself. I'll report what she confessed, if you please. These women of hers, who were there in tears when she died, can correct me if I'm wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Prithee say."],"id":923,"m":"Please tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["First, she confess'd she never lov'd you; only","Affected greatness got by you, not you;","Married your royalty, was wife to your place;","Abhorr'd your person."],"id":924,"m":"First, she confessed she never loved you. She only loved the greatness she got through you, not you. She married your royalty, was a wife to your position, and loathed your person."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["She alone knew this;","And but she spoke it dying, I would not","Believe her lips in opening it. Proceed."],"id":925,"m":"Only she knew this; and if she hadn't said it dying, I wouldn't believe her lips if they told me. Go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["Your daughter, whom she bore in hand to love","With such integrity, she did confess","Was as a scorpion to her sight; whose life,","But that her flight prevented it, she had","Ta'en off by poison."],"id":926,"m":"Your daughter, whom she pretended to love so sincerely, she confessed was like a scorpion to her eyes, and she would have killed her with poison if her running away hadn't prevented it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["O most delicate fiend!","Who is't can read a woman? Is there more?"],"id":927,"m":"Oh, most exquisite devil! Who can read a woman? Is there more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["More, sir, and worse. She did confess she had","For you a mortal mineral, which, being took,","Should by the minute feed on life, and ling'ring,","By inches waste you. In which time she purpos'd,","By watching, weeping, tendance, kissing, to","O'ercome you with her show; and in time,","When she had fitted you with her craft, to work","Her son into th' adoption of the crown;","But failing of her end by his strange absence,","Grew shameless-desperate, open'd, in despite","Of heaven and men, her purposes, repented","The evils she hatch'd were not effected; so,","Despairing, died."],"id":928,"m":"More, sir, and worse. She confessed she had a deadly mineral poison for you which, once taken, would feed on your life minute by minute and waste you away by inches. Meanwhile she planned, by sitting up with you, weeping, nursing and kissing, to win you over with her show, and in time, when she'd worked on you with her cunning, to get her son named heir to the crown. But when her plan failed because of his strange absence, she grew shamelessly desperate, revealed her plans in defiance of heaven and men, regretted that the evils she'd hatched hadn't been carried out, and so died in despair."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Heard you all this, her women?"],"id":929,"m":"Did you hear all this, her women?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["We did, so please your Highness."],"id":930,"m":"We did, if it please your Highness."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Mine eyes","Were not in fault, for she was beautiful;","Mine ears, that heard her flattery; nor my heart","That thought her like her seeming. It had been vicious","To have mistrusted her; yet, O my daughter!","That it was folly in me thou mayst say,","And prove it in thy feeling. Heaven mend all!"],"id":931,"m":"My eyes weren't to blame, for she was beautiful; nor my ears, which heard her flattery; nor my heart, which thought she was what she seemed. It would have been wicked to mistrust her. And yet, oh, my daughter! You can say it was folly in me, and prove it by what you've suffered. Heaven put it all right!"},{"t":"sd","id":932,"text":"Enter Lucius, Iachimo, the Soothsayer, and other Roman prisoners, guarded; Posthumus behind, and Imogen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","cont":1,"lines":["Thou com'st not, Caius, now for tribute; that","The Britons have raz'd out, though with the loss","Of many a bold one, whose kinsmen have made suit","That their good souls may be appeas'd with slaughter","Of you their captives, which ourself have granted;","So think of your estate."],"id":933,"m":"You don't come for tribute now, Caius. The Britons have wiped that out, though with the loss of many brave men, whose relatives have asked that their good souls be appeased by the slaughter of you, their prisoners, which I have granted. So think about your situation."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Consider, sir, the chance of war. The day","Was yours by accident; had it gone with us,","We should not, when the blood was cool, have threaten'd","Our prisoners with the sword. But since the gods","Will have it thus, that nothing but our lives","May be call'd ransom, let it come. Sufficeth","A Roman with a Roman's heart can suffer.","Augustus lives to think on't; and so much","For my peculiar care. This one thing only","I will entreat: my boy, a Briton born,","Let him be ransom'd. Never master had","A page so kind, so duteous, diligent,","So tender over his occasions, true,","So feat, so nurse-like; let his virtue join","With my request, which I'll make bold your Highness","Cannot deny; he hath done no Briton harm","Though he have serv'd a Roman. Save him, sir,","And spare no blood beside."],"id":934,"m":"Consider, sir, the fortunes of war. The day was yours by chance. If it had gone our way, we wouldn't have threatened our prisoners with the sword once our blood had cooled. But since the gods want it this way, that nothing but our lives will pay our ransom, let it come. It's enough that a Roman can suffer with a Roman's heart. Augustus lives to think about it; and that's enough about my own concerns. There's just one thing I'll beg: my boy, a Briton by birth, let him be ransomed. No master ever had a page so kind, so dutiful, hard-working, so attentive to his needs, so loyal, so skilful, so like a nurse. Let his virtue join with my request, which I'm bold enough to think your Highness can't refuse. He's done no Briton any harm, though he's served a Roman. Save him, sir, and spare no other blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["I have surely seen him;","His favour is familiar to me. Boy,","Thou hast look'd thyself into my grace,","And art mine own. I know not why, wherefore","To say 'Live, boy.' Ne'er thank thy master. Live;","And ask of Cymbeline what boon thou wilt,","Fitting my bounty and thy state, I'll give it;","Yea, though thou do demand a prisoner,","The noblest ta'en."],"id":935,"m":"I've certainly seen him; his face is familiar to me. Boy, your looks have won my favour, and you're mine. I don't know why or for what reason I say, \"Live, boy.\" Don't thank your master. Live, and ask Cymbeline for any favour you like that suits my generosity and your rank, and I'll grant it; yes, even if you ask for a prisoner, the noblest captured."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I humbly thank your Highness."],"id":936,"m":"I humbly thank your Highness."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I do not bid thee beg my life, good lad,","And yet I know thou wilt."],"id":937,"m":"I don't tell you to beg for my life, good lad, but I know you will."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["No, no! Alack,","There's other work in hand. I see a thing","Bitter to me as death; your life, good master,","Must shuffle for itself."],"id":938,"m":"No, no! Alas, there's other work to do. I see something as bitter to me as death. Your life, good master, will have to look after itself."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["The boy disdains me,","He leaves me, scorns me. Briefly die their joys","That place them on the truth of girls and boys.","Why stands he so perplex'd?"],"id":939,"m":"The boy scorns me; he leaves me, despises me. Their joys die quickly who rest them on the loyalty of girls and boys. Why does he stand there so troubled?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["What wouldst thou, boy?","I love thee more and more; think more and more","What's best to ask. Know'st him thou look'st on? Speak,","Wilt have him live? Is he thy kin? thy friend?"],"id":940,"m":"What do you want, boy? I love you more and more; think more and more about what's best to ask. Do you know the man you're looking at? Speak, do you want him to live? Is he your relative? Your friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["He is a Roman, no more kin to me","Than I to your Highness; who, being born your vassal,","Am something nearer."],"id":941,"m":"He's a Roman, no more related to me than I am to your Highness; and being born your subject, I'm rather closer."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Wherefore ey'st him so?"],"id":942,"m":"Why do you stare at him like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I'll tell you, sir, in private, if you please","To give me hearing."],"id":943,"m":"I'll tell you in private, sir, if you'll hear me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Ay, with all my heart,","And lend my best attention. What's thy name?"],"id":944,"m":"Yes, with all my heart, and I'll give you my full attention. What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Fidele, sir."],"id":945,"m":"Fidele, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Thou'rt my good youth, my page;","I'll be thy master. Walk with me; speak freely."],"id":946,"m":"You're my good boy, my page. I'll be your master. Walk with me; speak freely."},{"t":"sd","id":947,"text":"Cymbeline and Imogen converse apart."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Is not this boy reviv'd from death?"],"id":948,"m":"Isn't this boy come back from the dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["One sand another","Not more resembles—that sweet rosy lad","Who died and was Fidele. What think you?"],"id":949,"m":"One grain of sand isn't more like another than he is like that sweet rosy boy who died and was Fidele. What do you think?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["The same dead thing alive."],"id":950,"m":"The same dead thing, alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Peace, peace! see further. He eyes us not; forbear.","Creatures may be alike; were't he, I am sure","He would have spoke to us."],"id":951,"m":"Quiet, quiet! Let's see more. He's not looking at us; wait. People can look alike. If it were him, I'm sure he'd have spoken to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["But we saw him dead."],"id":952,"m":"But we saw him dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Be silent; let's see further."],"id":953,"m":"Be quiet; let's see more."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["[Aside] It is my mistress.","Since she is living, let the time run on","To good or bad. [Cymbeline and Imogen advance]"],"id":954,"m":"(Aside) It's my mistress. Since she's alive, let time run on to good or bad."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Come, stand thou by our side;","Make thy demand aloud. [To Iachimo] Sir, step you forth;","Give answer to this boy, and do it freely,","Or, by our greatness and the grace of it,","Which is our honour, bitter torture shall","Winnow the truth from falsehood. On, speak to him."],"id":955,"m":"Come, stand by our side and make your request aloud. (To Iachimo) Sir, step forward. Answer this boy, and do it frankly, or, by our greatness and the honour that goes with it, bitter torture will sift the truth from the lies. Go on, speak to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["My boon is that this gentleman may render","Of whom he had this ring."],"id":956,"m":"My request is that this gentleman tell us from whom he got this ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["[Aside] What's that to him?"],"id":957,"m":"(Aside) What's that to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["That diamond upon your finger, say","How came it yours?"],"id":958,"m":"That diamond on your finger: tell us how it came to be yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Thou'lt torture me to leave unspoken that","Which to be spoke would torture thee."],"id":959,"m":"You'll torture me for leaving unspoken what would torture you if it were spoken."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["How? me?"],"id":960,"m":"What? Me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["I am glad to be constrain'd to utter that","Which torments me to conceal. By villainy","I got this ring; 'twas Leonatus' jewel,","Whom thou didst banish; and—which more may grieve thee,","As it doth me—a nobler sir ne'er liv'd","'Twixt sky and ground. Wilt thou hear more, my lord?"],"id":961,"m":"I'm glad to be forced to say what it torments me to hide. I got this ring by villainy. It was the jewel of Leonatus, whom you banished; and (which may grieve you more, as it does me) a nobler gentleman never lived between sky and earth. Will you hear more, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["All that belongs to this."],"id":962,"m":"Everything to do with this."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["That paragon, thy daughter,","For whom my heart drops blood and my false spirits","Quail to remember—Give me leave, I faint."],"id":963,"m":"That model of perfection, your daughter, for whom my heart bleeds and my false spirit shrinks to remember... Give me a moment; I feel faint."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["My daughter? What of her? Renew thy strength;","I had rather thou shouldst live while nature will","Than die ere I hear more. Strive, man, and speak."],"id":964,"m":"My daughter? What about her? Pull yourself together. I'd rather you lived as long as nature allows than die before I hear more. Make an effort, man, and speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Upon a time—unhappy was the clock","That struck the hour!—was in Rome—accurs'd","The mansion where!—'twas at a feast—O, would","Our viands had been poison'd, or at least","Those which I heav'd to head!—the good Posthumus—","What should I say? he was too good to be","Where ill men were, and was the best of all","Amongst the rar'st of good ones—sitting sadly","Hearing us praise our loves of Italy","For beauty that made barren the swell'd boast","Of him that best could speak; for feature, laming","The shrine of Venus or straight-pight Minerva,","Postures beyond brief nature; for condition,","A shop of all the qualities that man","Loves woman for; besides that hook of wiving,","Fairness which strikes the eye—"],"id":965,"m":"Once (unlucky was the clock that struck that hour!) in Rome (cursed be the house where it happened!) at a feast (oh, I wish our food had been poisoned, or at least what I lifted to my mouth!), the good Posthumus (what can I say? He was too good to be among bad men, and the best of all among the rarest good ones) was sitting sadly, listening to us praise our Italian sweethearts: for beauty that left the boldest boasts of the best speaker empty; for figure, that put to shame the statue of Venus or upright Minerva, shapes beyond what short-lived nature can make; for character, a store of every quality men love women for; and besides that, the hook that catches husbands, beauty that strikes the eye..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["I stand on fire.","Come to the matter."],"id":966,"m":"I'm on fire. Get to the point."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["All too soon I shall,","Unless thou wouldst grieve quickly. This Posthumus,","Most like a noble lord in love and one","That had a royal lover, took his hint;","And not dispraising whom we prais'd—therein","He was as calm as virtue—he began","His mistress' picture; which by his tongue being made,","And then a mind put in't, either our brags","Were crack'd of kitchen trulls, or his description","Prov'd us unspeaking sots."],"id":967,"m":"I'll get there all too soon, unless you want to grieve quickly. This Posthumus, like a noble lord in love and one who had a royal sweetheart, took his cue, and without criticising those we praised (in that he was as calm as virtue itself), he began to paint a picture of his lady. Once his words had drawn it and given it a mind, either our boasts were about kitchen sluts, or his description proved us tongue-tied fools."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Nay, nay, to th' purpose."],"id":968,"m":"No, no, get to the point."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["Your daughter's chastity—there it begins.","He spake of her as Dian had hot dreams","And she alone were cold; whereat I, wretch,","Made scruple of his praise, and wager'd with him","Pieces of gold 'gainst this which then he wore","Upon his honour'd finger, to attain","In suit the place of's bed, and win this ring","By hers and mine adultery. He, true knight,","No lesser of her honour confident","Than I did truly find her, stakes this ring;","And would so, had it been a carbuncle","Of Phoebus' wheel; and might so safely, had it","Been all the worth of's car. Away to Britain","Post I in this design. Well may you, sir,","Remember me at court, where I was taught","Of your chaste daughter the wide difference","'Twixt amorous and villainous. Being thus quench'd","Of hope, not longing, mine Italian brain","Gan in your duller Britain operate","Most vilely; for my vantage, excellent;","And, to be brief, my practice so prevail'd","That I return'd with simular proof enough","To make the noble Leonatus mad,","By wounding his belief in her renown","With tokens thus and thus; averring notes","Of chamber-hanging, pictures, this her bracelet—","O cunning, how I got it!—nay, some marks","Of secret on her person, that he could not","But think her bond of chastity quite crack'd,","I having ta'en the forfeit. Whereupon—","Methinks I see him now—"],"id":969,"m":"Your daughter's chastity: that's where it begins. He spoke of her as if Diana had hot dreams and she alone were cold. At that I, wretch that I am, doubted his praise, and bet him gold against this ring, which he wore then on his honoured finger, that I would win my way into her bed and win this ring through her adultery and mine. He, a true knight, no less confident of her honour than I truly found her, staked this ring; and he would have done so if it had been a jewel from the sun god's chariot wheel, and could have done so safely if it had been worth the whole chariot. Off to Britain I hurried with this plan. You may well remember me at court, sir, where your chaste daughter taught me the wide difference between a lover and a villain. My hope put out, though not my desire, my Italian brain began to work in your duller Britain, most vilely, but to my great advantage. To be brief, my scheme succeeded so well that I came back with enough fake proof to drive the noble Leonatus mad, wounding his faith in her good name with such and such tokens: a detailed description of the hangings and pictures in her room, this bracelet of hers (oh, the trick by which I got it!), yes, even secret marks on her body, so that he couldn't help thinking her bond of chastity quite broken and that I'd taken the forfeit. Whereupon (I think I see him now)..."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["[Coming forward] Ay, so thou dost,","Italian fiend! Ay me, most credulous fool,","Egregious murderer, thief, anything","That's due to all the villains past, in being,","To come! O, give me cord, or knife, or poison,","Some upright justicer! Thou, King, send out","For torturers ingenious. It is I","That all th' abhorred things o' th' earth amend","By being worse than they. I am Posthumus,","That kill'd thy daughter; villain-like, I lie—","That caus'd a lesser villain than myself,","A sacrilegious thief, to do't. The temple","Of virtue was she; yea, and she herself.","Spit, and throw stones, cast mire upon me, set","The dogs o' th' street to bay me. Every villain","Be call'd Posthumus Leonatus, and","Be villainy less than 'twas! O Imogen!","My queen, my life, my wife! O Imogen,","Imogen, Imogen!"],"id":970,"m":"(Stepping forward) Yes, so you do, Italian devil! Oh, I'm the most gullible fool, a monstrous murderer, thief, anything that's due to all villains past, present and to come! Oh, give me a rope, or a knife, or poison, some honest judge! You, King, send for ingenious torturers. I'm the one who makes all the hateful things on earth look better by being worse than they are. I am Posthumus, who killed your daughter. No, I lie like a villain: who made a lesser villain than myself, a sacrilegious thief, do it. She was the temple of virtue; yes, and virtue herself. Spit on me, throw stones, throw mud at me, set the street dogs barking at me. Let every villain be called Posthumus Leonatus, and let villainy be less than it was! Oh, Imogen! My queen, my life, my wife! Oh, Imogen, Imogen, Imogen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Peace, my lord. Hear, hear!"],"id":971,"m":"Quiet, my lord. Listen, listen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Shall's have a play of this? Thou scornful page,","There lies thy part. [Strikes her. She falls]"],"id":972,"m":"Are we to have a comedy about this? You scornful page, there's your part. (He strikes her and she falls.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["O gentlemen, help!","Mine and your mistress! O, my lord Posthumus!","You ne'er kill'd Imogen till now. Help, help!","Mine honour'd lady!"],"id":973,"m":"Oh, gentlemen, help! My mistress and yours! Oh, my lord Posthumus! You never killed Imogen until now. Help, help! My honoured lady!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Does the world go round?"],"id":974,"m":"Is the world spinning?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["How comes these staggers on me?"],"id":975,"m":"Why am I reeling like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Wake, my mistress!"],"id":976,"m":"Wake up, my mistress!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["If this be so, the gods do mean to strike me","To death with mortal joy."],"id":977,"m":"If this is so, the gods mean to strike me dead with deadly joy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["How fares my mistress?"],"id":978,"m":"How is my mistress?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["O, get thee from my sight;","Thou gav'st me poison. Dangerous fellow, hence!","Breathe not where princes are."],"id":979,"m":"Oh, get out of my sight. You gave me poison. Dangerous man, go! Don't breathe where princes are."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["The tune of Imogen!"],"id":980,"m":"The voice of Imogen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["Lady,","The gods throw stones of sulphur on me, if","That box I gave you was not thought by me","A precious thing! I had it from the Queen."],"id":981,"m":"Lady, may the gods throw burning sulphur stones at me if I didn't think that box I gave you was a precious thing! I got it from the Queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["New matter still?"],"id":982,"m":"Still more news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["It poison'd me."],"id":983,"m":"It poisoned me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["O gods!","I left out one thing which the Queen confess'd,","Which must approve thee honest. 'If Pisanio","Have' said she 'given his mistress that confection","Which I gave him for cordial, she is serv'd","As I would serve a rat.'"],"id":984,"m":"Oh, gods! I left out one thing the Queen confessed, which must prove you honest. \"If Pisanio,\" she said, \"has given his mistress that mixture which I gave him as a restorative, she's been treated as I'd treat a rat.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["What's this, Cornelius?"],"id":985,"m":"What's this, Cornelius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["The Queen, sir, very oft importun'd me","To temper poisons for her; still pretending","The satisfaction of her knowledge only","In killing creatures vile, as cats and dogs,","Of no esteem. I, dreading that her purpose","Was of more danger, did compound for her","A certain stuff, which, being ta'en would cease","The present pow'r of life, but in short time","All offices of nature should again","Do their due functions. Have you ta'en of it?"],"id":986,"m":"The Queen, sir, very often pestered me to mix poisons for her, always claiming she only wanted to increase her knowledge by killing worthless creatures like cats and dogs. I was afraid her purpose was more dangerous, so I made her a certain substance which, when taken, would stop the powers of life for a while, but in a short time all the body's functions would work again as normal. Did you take any of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Most like I did, for I was dead."],"id":987,"m":"Most likely I did, because I was dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["My boys,","There was our error."],"id":988,"m":"My boys, that's where we went wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["This is sure Fidele."],"id":989,"m":"This is certainly Fidele."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["Why did you throw your wedded lady from you?","Think that you are upon a rock, and now","Throw me again. [Embracing him]"],"id":990,"m":"Why did you throw your wife away from you? Imagine you're on a cliff, and now throw me again. (She embraces him.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Hang there like fruit, my soul,","Till the tree die!"],"id":991,"m":"Hang there like fruit, my soul, until the tree dies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["How now, my flesh? my child?","What, mak'st thou me a dullard in this act?","Wilt thou not speak to me?"],"id":992,"m":"What's this, my flesh, my child? What, are you making me a fool in this scene? Won't you speak to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["[Kneeling] Your blessing, sir."],"id":993,"m":"(Kneeling) Your blessing, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["[To Guiderius and Arviragus] Though you did love this","youth, I blame ye not;","You had a motive for't."],"id":994,"m":"(To Guiderius and Arviragus) Though you loved this boy, I don't blame you; you had a reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["My tears that fall","Prove holy water on thee! Imogen,","Thy mother's dead."],"id":995,"m":"May my falling tears be holy water on you! Imogen, your stepmother's dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["I am sorry for't, my lord."],"id":996,"m":"I'm sorry for it, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["O, she was naught, and long of her it was","That we meet here so strangely; but her son","Is gone, we know not how nor where."],"id":997,"m":"Oh, she was wicked, and it's because of her that we meet here so strangely. But her son is gone, we don't know how or where."},{"t":"sp","who":"PISANIO","lines":["My lord,","Now fear is from me, I'll speak troth. Lord Cloten,","Upon my lady's missing, came to me","With his sword drawn, foam'd at the mouth, and swore,","If I discover'd not which way she was gone,","It was my instant death. By accident","I had a feigned letter of my master's","Then in my pocket, which directed him","To seek her on the mountains near to Milford;","Where, in a frenzy, in my master's garments,","Which he enforc'd from me, away he posts","With unchaste purpose, and with oath to violate","My lady's honour. What became of him","I further know not."],"id":998,"m":"My lord, now that I've no fear, I'll tell the truth. When my lady went missing, Lord Cloten came to me with his sword drawn, foaming at the mouth, and swore that if I didn't reveal which way she'd gone, I'd die on the spot. By chance I had in my pocket a false letter from my master, which sent him to look for her in the mountains near Milford. And there he rushed off in a frenzy, wearing my master's clothes, which he forced me to give him, with a lustful purpose and an oath to rape my lady. What happened to him after that I don't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["Let me end the story:","I slew him there."],"id":999,"m":"Let me finish the story: I killed him there."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Marry, the gods forfend!","I would not thy good deeds should from my lips","Pluck a hard sentence. Prithee, valiant youth,","Deny't again."],"id":1000,"m":"The gods forbid! I wouldn't want your good deeds to drag a harsh sentence from my lips. Please, brave young man, deny it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["I have spoke it, and I did it."],"id":1001,"m":"I've said it, and I did it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["He was a prince."],"id":1002,"m":"He was a prince."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["A most incivil one. The wrongs he did me","Were nothing prince-like; for he did provoke me","With language that would make me spurn the sea,","If it could so roar to me. I cut off's head,","And am right glad he is not standing here","To tell this tale of mine."],"id":1003,"m":"A very uncivil one. The wrongs he did me were nothing like a prince's. He provoked me with language that would have made me kick the sea, if it roared at me like that. I cut off his head, and I'm very glad he isn't standing here to tell this story about me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["I am sorry for thee.","By thine own tongue thou art condemn'd, and must","Endure our law. Thou'rt dead."],"id":1004,"m":"I'm sorry for you. You're condemned by your own tongue and must suffer our law. You're a dead man."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["That headless man","I thought had been my lord."],"id":1005,"m":"That headless man I thought was my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Bind the offender,","And take him from our presence."],"id":1006,"m":"Tie up the offender and take him out of our sight."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Stay, sir King.","This man is better than the man he slew,","As well descended as thyself, and hath","More of thee merited than a band of Clotens","Had ever scar for. [To the guard] Let his arms alone;","They were not born for bondage."],"id":1007,"m":"Wait, King. This man is better than the man he killed, as well born as you, and has deserved more from you than a troop of Clotens ever earned by their scars. (To the guards) Leave his arms alone; they weren't born to be tied."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Why, old soldier,","Wilt thou undo the worth thou art unpaid for","By tasting of our wrath? How of descent","As good as we?"],"id":1008,"m":"Why, old soldier, will you undo the reward you haven't yet been paid by bringing on our anger? How can he be as well born as we are?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["In that he spake too far."],"id":1009,"m":"In that he went too far."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["And thou shalt die for't."],"id":1010,"m":"And you'll die for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["We will die all three;","But I will prove that two on's are as good","As I have given out him. My sons, I must","For mine own part unfold a dangerous speech,","Though haply well for you."],"id":1011,"m":"We'll all three die; but I'll prove that two of us are as good as I said he was. My sons, for my own part I must tell a dangerous story, though perhaps it will be good for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Your danger's ours."],"id":1012,"m":"Your danger is ours."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["And our good his."],"id":1013,"m":"And our good is his."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Have at it then by leave!","Thou hadst, great King, a subject who","Was call'd Belarius."],"id":1014,"m":"Here goes, then, with your permission! Great King, you once had a subject called Belarius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["What of him? He is","A banish'd traitor."],"id":1015,"m":"What of him? He's a banished traitor."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["He it is that hath","Assum'd this age; indeed a banish'd man;","I know not how a traitor."],"id":1016,"m":"He's the one who has reached this old age: a banished man, certainly; how a traitor, I don't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Take him hence,","The whole world shall not save him."],"id":1017,"m":"Take him away. The whole world won't save him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Not too hot.","First pay me for the nursing of thy sons,","And let it be confiscate all, so soon","As I have receiv'd it."],"id":1018,"m":"Not so fast. First pay me for bringing up your sons, and then confiscate it all as soon as I've received it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Nursing of my sons?"],"id":1019,"m":"Bringing up my sons?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["I am too blunt and saucy: here's my knee.","Ere I arise I will prefer my sons;","Then spare not the old father. Mighty sir,","These two young gentlemen that call me father,","And think they are my sons, are none of mine;","They are the issue of your loins, my liege,","And blood of your begetting."],"id":1020,"m":"I'm too blunt and rude. Here, I kneel. Before I get up I'll present my sons, and then don't spare the old father. Mighty sir, these two young gentlemen who call me father and think they're my sons are not mine. They are the children of your body, my king, and of your blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["How? my issue?"],"id":1021,"m":"What? My children?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["So sure as you your father's. I, old Morgan,","Am that Belarius whom you sometime banish'd.","Your pleasure was my mere offence, my punishment","Itself, and all my treason; that I suffer'd","Was all the harm I did. These gentle princes—","For such and so they are—these twenty years","Have I train'd up; those arts they have as","Could put into them. My breeding was, sir, as","Your Highness knows. Their nurse, Euriphile,","Whom for the theft I wedded, stole these children","Upon my banishment; I mov'd her to't,","Having receiv'd the punishment before","For that which I did then. Beaten for loyalty","Excited me to treason. Their dear loss,","The more of you 'twas felt, the more it shap'd","Unto my end of stealing them. But, gracious sir,","Here are your sons again, and I must lose","Two of the sweet'st companions in the world.","The benediction of these covering heavens","Fall on their heads like dew! for they are worthy","To inlay heaven with stars."],"id":1022,"m":"As surely as you are your father's. I, old Morgan, am that Belarius you once banished. Your whim was my only offence, my punishment, and all my treason; what I suffered was all the harm I did. These gentle princes (for that's what they are) I've brought up for twenty years. They have all the skills I could teach them, and you know, sir, how I was brought up myself. Their nurse, Euriphile, whom I married for the theft, stole these children when I was banished. I put her up to it, having already been punished for what I then did. Being beaten for loyalty drove me to treason. The more you felt their loss, the better it served my purpose in stealing them. But, gracious sir, here are your sons again, and I must lose two of the sweetest companions in the world. May the blessing of the heavens above fall on their heads like dew, because they're worthy to set the sky with stars."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Thou weep'st and speak'st.","The service that you three have done is more","Unlike than this thou tell'st. I lost my children.","If these be they, I know not how to wish","A pair of worthier sons."],"id":1023,"m":"You weep as you speak. The service you three have done is more incredible than what you're telling me. I lost my children. If these are they, I couldn't wish for a worthier pair of sons."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["Be pleas'd awhile.","This gentleman, whom I call Polydore,","Most worthy prince, as yours, is true Guiderius;","This gentleman, my Cadwal, Arviragus,","Your younger princely son; he, sir, was lapp'd","In a most curious mantle, wrought by th' hand","Of his queen mother, which for more probation","I can with ease produce."],"id":1024,"m":"Bear with me a little. This gentleman, whom I call Polydore, most worthy prince, is your true Guiderius. This gentleman, my Cadwal, is Arviragus, your younger royal son. He, sir, was wrapped in a beautifully made cloak, worked by the hand of his mother the queen, which I can easily produce for further proof."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Guiderius had","Upon his neck a mole, a sanguine star;","It was a mark of wonder."],"id":1025,"m":"Guiderius had a mole on his neck, a red star. It was a remarkable mark."},{"t":"sp","who":"BELARIUS","lines":["This is he,","Who hath upon him still that natural stamp.","It was wise nature's end in the donation,","To be his evidence now."],"id":1026,"m":"This is he, who still has that natural stamp on him. It was wise nature's purpose in giving it to be his proof now."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["O, what am I?","A mother to the birth of three? Ne'er mother","Rejoic'd deliverance more. Blest pray you be,","That, after this strange starting from your orbs,","You may reign in them now! O Imogen,","Thou hast lost by this a kingdom."],"id":1027,"m":"Oh, what am I? A mother who's given birth to three? No mother ever rejoiced more in a delivery. Be blessed, I pray, so that after this strange wandering from your spheres you may reign in them now! Oh, Imogen, you've lost a kingdom by this."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["No, my lord;","I have got two worlds by't. O my gentle brothers,","Have we thus met? O, never say hereafter","But I am truest speaker! You call'd me brother,","When I was but your sister: I you brothers,","When we were so indeed."],"id":1028,"m":"No, my lord, I've gained two worlds by it. Oh, my gentle brothers, have we met like this? Oh, never say after this that I'm not the truest speaker! You called me brother when I was only your sister; I called you brothers when you really were."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Did you e'er meet?"],"id":1029,"m":"Have you met before?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["Ay, my good lord."],"id":1030,"m":"Yes, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"GUIDERIUS","lines":["And at first meeting lov'd,","Continu'd so until we thought he died."],"id":1031,"m":"And loved at first meeting, and went on loving until we thought he died."},{"t":"sp","who":"CORNELIUS","lines":["By the Queen's dram she swallow'd."],"id":1032,"m":"From the Queen's drug, which she swallowed."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["O rare instinct!","When shall I hear all through? This fierce abridgment","Hath to it circumstantial branches, which","Distinction should be rich in. Where? how liv'd you?","And when came you to serve our Roman captive?","How parted with your brothers? how first met them?","Why fled you from the court? and whither? These,","And your three motives to the battle, with","I know not how much more, should be demanded,","And all the other by-dependences,","From chance to chance; but nor the time nor place","Will serve our long interrogatories. See,","Posthumus anchors upon Imogen;","And she, like harmless lightning, throws her eye","On him, her brothers, me, her master, hitting","Each object with a joy; the counterchange","Is severally in all. Let's quit this ground,","And smoke the temple with our sacrifices.","[To Belarius] Thou art my brother; so we'll hold thee ever."],"id":1033,"m":"Oh, wonderful instinct! When will I hear it all? This rapid summary has branching details that should be told fully. Where? How did you live? And when did you come to serve our Roman prisoner? How did you part from your brothers? How did you first meet them? Why did you run away from court? And where to? These, and the reasons all three of you went to the battle, and I don't know how much more, should be asked, and all the other connected events, one chance after another. But neither the time nor the place suits our long questioning. Look, Posthumus anchors himself to Imogen, and she, like harmless lightning, throws her eye at him, her brothers, me, her master, striking each with joy, and each returns it. Let's leave this ground and fill the temple with the smoke of our sacrifices. (To Belarius) You're my brother; we'll always think of you so."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["You are my father too, and did relieve me","To see this gracious season."],"id":1034,"m":"You're my father too, and you helped me live to see this happy time."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["All o'erjoy'd","Save these in bonds. Let them be joyful too,","For they shall taste our comfort."],"id":1035,"m":"Everyone overjoyed except these prisoners. Let them be joyful too, because they'll share our happiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"IMOGEN","lines":["My good master,","I will yet do you service."],"id":1036,"m":"My good master, I'll still serve you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Happy be you!"],"id":1037,"m":"May you be happy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["The forlorn soldier, that so nobly fought,","He would have well becom'd this place and grac'd","The thankings of a king."],"id":1038,"m":"The lost soldier who fought so nobly would have suited this place well and honoured a king's thanks."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["I am, sir,","The soldier that did company these three","In poor beseeming; 'twas a fitment for","The purpose I then follow'd. That I was he,","Speak, Iachimo. I had you down, and might","Have made you finish."],"id":1039,"m":"I am, sir, the soldier who fought with these three, dressed poorly, as suited the purpose I had then. Say that it was me, Iachimo. I had you down and could have finished you."},{"t":"sp","who":"IACHIMO","lines":["[Kneeling] I am down again;","But now my heavy conscience sinks my knee,","As then your force did. Take that life, beseech you,","Which I so often owe; but your ring first,","And here the bracelet of the truest princess","That ever swore her faith."],"id":1040,"m":"(Kneeling) I'm down again; but now my heavy conscience bends my knee, as your strength did then. Take my life, I beg you, which I owe you so many times over; but first your ring, and here's the bracelet of the most faithful princess who ever swore her loyalty."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Kneel not to me.","The pow'r that I have on you is to spare you;","The malice towards you to forgive you. Live,","And deal with others better."],"id":1041,"m":"Don't kneel to me. The power I have over you is to spare you; my spite against you is to forgive you. Live, and treat others better."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Nobly doom'd!","We'll learn our freeness of a son-in-law;","Pardon's the word to all."],"id":1042,"m":"A noble judgment! We'll learn generosity from a son-in-law. Pardon is the word for all."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARVIRAGUS","lines":["You holp us, sir,","As you did mean indeed to be our brother;","Joy'd are we that you are."],"id":1043,"m":"You helped us, sir, as if you really meant to be our brother. We're glad that you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"POSTHUMUS","lines":["Your servant, Princes. Good my lord of Rome,","Call forth your soothsayer. As I slept, methought","Great Jupiter, upon his eagle back'd,","Appear'd to me, with other spritely shows","Of mine own kindred. When I wak'd, I found","This label on my bosom; whose containing","Is so from sense in hardness that I can","Make no collection of it. Let him show","His skill in the construction."],"id":1044,"m":"Your servant, princes. My good lord of Rome, call your soothsayer. As I slept I thought great Jupiter, riding on his eagle, appeared to me, along with other ghostly visions of my own family. When I woke I found this tablet on my chest. What it says is so hard to make sense of that I can't understand it. Let him show his skill in interpreting it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Philarmonus!"],"id":1045,"m":"Philarmonus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["Here, my good lord."],"id":1046,"m":"Here, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Read, and declare the meaning."],"id":1047,"m":"Read it and tell us what it means."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["[Reads] 'When as a lion's whelp shall, to himself","unknown, without seeking find, and be embrac'd by","a piece of tender air; and when from a stately cedar shall","be lopp'd branches which, being dead many years, shall","after revive, be jointed to the old stock, and freshly grow;","then shall Posthumus end his miseries, Britain be fortunate","and flourish in peace and plenty.'","Thou, Leonatus, art the lion's whelp;","The fit and apt construction of thy name,","Being Leo-natus, doth import so much.","[To Cymbeline] The piece of tender air, thy virtuous daughter,","Which we call 'mollis aer,' and 'mollis aer'","We term it 'mulier'; which 'mulier' I divine","Is this most constant wife, who even now","Answering the letter of the oracle,","Unknown to you, unsought, were clipp'd about","With this most tender air."],"id":1048,"m":"(Reading) \"When a lion's cub, unknown to himself, shall find without searching, and be embraced by a piece of tender air; and when from a stately cedar branches are cut which, after being dead for many years, revive, are joined to the old trunk, and grow fresh again; then Posthumus will end his miseries, and Britain will be fortunate and flourish in peace and plenty.\" You, Leonatus, are the lion's cub. The proper meaning of your name, Leo-natus, \"lion-born\", says as much. (To Cymbeline) The piece of tender air is your virtuous daughter. In Latin we call tender air \"mollis aer\", and from \"mollis aer\" we get \"mulier\", woman. That woman, I believe, is this most faithful wife, who just now, fulfilling the words of the oracle, embraced him, unknown to you and unsought, as tender air."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["This hath some seeming."],"id":1049,"m":"That seems likely."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["The lofty cedar, royal Cymbeline,","Personates thee; and thy lopp'd branches point","Thy two sons forth, who, by Belarius stol'n,","For many years thought dead, are now reviv'd,","To the majestic cedar join'd, whose issue","Promises Britain peace and plenty."],"id":1050,"m":"The tall cedar, royal Cymbeline, stands for you, and your lopped branches point to your two sons, who were stolen by Belarius and thought dead for many years, and are now revived and joined to the majestic cedar, whose children promise Britain peace and plenty."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Well,","My peace we will begin. And, Caius Lucius,","Although the victor, we submit to Caesar","And to the Roman empire, promising","To pay our wonted tribute, from the which","We were dissuaded by our wicked queen,","Whom heavens in justice, both on her and hers,","Have laid most heavy hand."],"id":1051,"m":"Well, I'll begin my peace. And, Caius Lucius, although we're the victors, we submit to Caesar and the Roman Empire, and promise to pay our usual tribute, which we were talked out of by our wicked queen, on whom, and on whose son, the heavens have justly laid a very heavy hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["The fingers of the pow'rs above do tune","The harmony of this peace. The vision","Which I made known to Lucius ere the stroke","Of yet this scarce-cold battle, at this instant","Is full accomplish'd; for the Roman eagle,","From south to west on wing soaring aloft,","Lessen'd herself and in the beams o' th' sun","So vanish'd; which foreshow'd our princely eagle,","Th'imperial Caesar, Caesar, should again unite","His favour with the radiant Cymbeline,","Which shines here in the west."],"id":1052,"m":"The fingers of the powers above are tuning the harmony of this peace. The vision I told Lucius before this barely cooled battle began is fulfilled at this moment. For the Roman eagle, soaring high on the wing from south to west, grew smaller and vanished in the sun's beams; which foretold that our princely eagle, imperial Caesar, would unite his favour again with the radiant Cymbeline, who shines here in the west."},{"t":"sp","who":"CYMBELINE","lines":["Laud we the gods;","And let our crooked smokes climb to their nostrils","From our bless'd altars. Publish we this peace","To all our subjects. Set we forward; let","A Roman and a British ensign wave","Friendly together. So through Lud's Town march;","And in the temple of great Jupiter","Our peace we'll ratify; seal it with feasts.","Set on there! Never was a war did cease,","Ere bloody hands were wash'd, with such a peace."],"id":1053,"m":"Let's praise the gods, and let the curling smoke from our blessed altars climb to their nostrils. Let's announce this peace to all our subjects. Let's set out, with a Roman and a British flag waving together in friendship. So we'll march through London, and in the temple of great Jupiter we'll confirm our peace and seal it with feasts. Set off! Never did a war end, before the bloody hands were even washed, with such a peace."},{"t":"sd","id":1054,"text":"Exeunt."}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Imogen","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"Cymbeline's daughter and, while her brothers are lost, heir to Britain. She has secretly married Posthumus, the orphan brought up at court, instead of the Queen's son Cloten, and is kept a prisoner for it. She sees through the Queen, scorns Cloten to his face and slaps down Iachimo's attempt at seduction. Ordered killed by her husband, she disguises herself as the boy 'Fidele', is taken in by three Welsh mountaineers who turn out to be her brothers, wakes from a drugged sleep beside a headless body she takes for Posthumus, and becomes page to the Roman general Lucius. In the last scene she unravels the plot by asking Iachimo where he got his ring."},{"name":"Posthumus Leonatus","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"A gentleman born after his father's death (hence 'Posthumus'), orphaned at birth and raised by Cymbeline as a royal ward. Everyone praises him, yet in Rome he lets Iachimo draw him into a bet on his wife's chastity and believes the faked proof almost at once. He orders Pisanio to kill Imogen, then, given a bloody cloth as proof of her death, repents. He changes into a British peasant's clothes, fights for Britain beside Belarius and the princes, gives himself up as a Roman to die, and in prison is visited by the ghosts of his family and by Jupiter. He ends by forgiving Iachimo."},{"name":"Iachimo","group":"Italy","desc":"An Italian gentleman in Philario's circle. He mocks Posthumus's praise of Imogen and bets ten thousand ducats against his ring that he can seduce her. When she rejects him he has himself carried into her bedroom in a trunk, notes the room and a mole on her breast while she sleeps, steals her bracelet, and returns to Rome to 'prove' he has won. He comes back to Britain with the Roman army, is beaten in battle by the disguised Posthumus, and finally confesses everything in a long, elaborate speech."},{"name":"Cymbeline","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"King of Britain. Ruled by his second wife, the Queen, he banishes Posthumus and imprisons his daughter for marrying him. Twenty years earlier he banished his loyal soldier Belarius on a false charge, and lost his two infant sons. Urged by the Queen and Cloten, he refuses to pay Rome's tribute and goes to war, is captured in the battle and rescued. In the last scene he hears the Queen's confession, recovers his daughter and sons, pardons everyone and chooses to pay the tribute after all."},{"name":"The Queen","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"Cymbeline's second wife, a widow, and Cloten's mother. She pretends kindness to Imogen while plotting to marry her to Cloten and to make him king. She asks the doctor Cornelius for poisons, gives Pisanio a box she believes is deadly, and stiffens Cymbeline against Rome with a proud speech on Britain's island defences. Sick with grief after Cloten disappears, she dies off stage, confessing that she hated the King and meant to poison him and Imogen."},{"name":"Cloten","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"The Queen's son, Imogen's would-be husband. He is boastful, quarrelsome, stupid and cruel, mocked behind his back by his own attendant lord. Rejected by Imogen, who says Posthumus's meanest garment is worth more than he is, he forces Pisanio to give him Posthumus's clothes and goes to Milford Haven meaning to kill Posthumus and rape Imogen. In Wales he insults Guiderius, who cuts off his head."},{"name":"Pisanio","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"Posthumus's servant, left behind to serve Imogen. Ordered by his master to murder her, he refuses, believes she has been slandered, and instead disguises her as a boy and sends Posthumus a bloody cloth as false proof of her death. He gives her the Queen's box, thinking it medicine. Loyal to both his master and his mistress, he says that he is 'false' only to be true."},{"name":"Belarius","group":"Wales","desc":"A soldier once loved by Cymbeline, banished twenty years ago on the false word of two villains. In revenge he stole the King's infant sons and raised them in a Welsh cave under the name Morgan, calling them Polydore and Cadwal. He praises the simple mountain life over the court, but watches the princes' royal nature break through. He leads them into battle, holds a narrow lane against the Romans, and in the end reveals who the boys are."},{"name":"Guiderius (Polydore)","group":"Wales","desc":"Cymbeline's elder son and heir, stolen at three and raised as Belarius's son Polydore. Bold and impatient, he longs to see the world beyond the cave. He kills Cloten and throws his head into the stream, and in the last scene admits it openly, which nearly gets him executed. A mole on his neck proves his identity."},{"name":"Arviragus (Cadwal)","group":"Wales","desc":"Cymbeline's younger son, stolen as a baby and raised as Cadwal. Gentler and more imaginative than his brother, he loves 'Fidele' at once, carries the apparently dead boy out of the cave and speaks a speech of flowers over the grave before the brothers say the dirge 'Fear no more the heat o' th' sun'."},{"name":"Caius Lucius","group":"Rome","desc":"The Roman ambassador who demands the overdue tribute and declares war when Cymbeline refuses. Later he commands the Roman army in Britain. He finds the fainting 'Fidele' beside the headless body, takes the boy on as his page, and when captured asks only that the boy be spared. He is a model of Roman honour."},{"name":"Philario","group":"Italy","desc":"An Italian, friend of Posthumus's father, who gives Posthumus a home in Rome. He tries to stop the wager and later urges Posthumus not to believe Iachimo's evidence."},{"name":"Cornelius","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"The court doctor. Suspecting the Queen, he gives her a sleeping drug instead of the poison she asks for. In the last scene he reports her confession and explains that the 'poison' only imitates death."},{"name":"Sicilius Leonatus and family","group":"The vision","desc":"The ghosts of Posthumus's father, mother and two brothers, who appear around him as he sleeps in prison and appeal to Jupiter on his behalf."},{"name":"Jupiter","group":"The vision","desc":"King of the gods. He descends on an eagle, rebukes the ghosts, promises that Posthumus will be happy with Imogen, and leaves a written prophecy on his chest."},{"name":"Soothsayer (Philarmonus)","group":"Rome","desc":"Lucius's prophet. He interprets a vision of the Roman eagle before the battle and the riddle of Jupiter's tablet after it."},{"name":"Cloten's two lords","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"Courtiers who follow Cloten. The First flatters him; the Second mocks him in asides and prays for Imogen."},{"name":"Helen and the Queen's ladies","group":"Cymbeline's court","desc":"Imogen's attendant Helen, who folds down her page before she sleeps, and the ladies who witness the Queen's deathbed confession."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"Two gentlemen explain the court's gloom: Princess Imogen has secretly married Posthumus, an orphan raised by the King, instead of the Queen's son Cloten. Posthumus is banished and Imogen confined. The King's two sons were stolen twenty years ago. The Queen pretends to help the lovers. They exchange tokens: Imogen gives Posthumus her mother's diamond ring, he puts a bracelet on her arm. Cymbeline banishes him in a rage and turns on Imogen, and Pisanio reports that Cloten drew his sword on Posthumus as he left.","key":["The opening exposition sets up three plots: the forbidden marriage, the lost princes and the Queen's ambition.","The ring and the bracelet, 'a manacle of love', become the tokens the whole wager plot turns on.","Imogen defends her choice: 'I chose an eagle, And did avoid a puttock.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Posthumus banished?","o":["He fought a duel with Cloten","He married Imogen without the King's consent","He is suspected of helping Rome","He insulted the Queen"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Imogen give Posthumus?","o":["A bracelet","A letter","Her mother's diamond ring","A miniature portrait"],"a":2},{"q":"What happened to the King's two sons?","o":["They died in the war with Rome","They were stolen from their nursery twenty years ago","They were sent to Rome as hostages","They were banished with Belarius's consent"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Cloten, sweaty from his brief fight with Posthumus, boasts with his two lords. The First Lord flatters him; the Second mocks him in asides, saying Posthumus only played with him, and that if choosing well is a sin, Imogen is damned.","key":["The Second Lord's asides let the audience laugh at Cloten from the start.","Cloten's vanity and cowardice are shown before his danger is."],"quiz":[{"q":"According to the Second Lord, how did Posthumus fight Cloten?","o":["Fiercely, nearly killing him","He played rather than fought","He ran away at once","He refused to draw"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Second Lord say about Imogen's choice?","o":["It was foolish","If choosing well is a sin, she is damned","She will regret it","The King was right to stop it"],"a":1},{"q":"Who flatters Cloten?","o":["The First Lord","The Second Lord","Pisanio","The Queen"],"a":0}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"Pisanio tells Imogen how Posthumus waved from the deck until his ship was out of sight. She wishes she had watched him shrink to a gnat and vanished into air, and regrets the things she never had time to say before her father interrupted their parting.","key":["Imogen's speech of watching the ship is one of the play's most tender images of love.","She had planned to make him swear the 'shes of Italy' would not tempt him: dramatic irony for what follows in Rome."],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Posthumus wave from the ship?","o":["His sword","A glove, hat or handkerchief","Imogen's bracelet","A letter"],"a":1},{"q":"How small does Imogen say she would have watched him grow?","o":["As small as a crow, then a gnat, then air","As small as a ship's sail","As small as a coin","As small as a star"],"a":0},{"q":"Who interrupted the lovers' parting?","o":["The Queen","Cloten","Cymbeline","Pisanio"],"a":2}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"In Philario's house in Rome, Iachimo and a Frenchman discuss Posthumus. The Frenchman recalls an old quarrel in which Posthumus praised his lady above the ladies of France. Iachimo provokes him again and they make a wager: ten thousand ducats against Posthumus's diamond that Iachimo can seduce Imogen. If he fails, Posthumus will fight him. Posthumus gives him a letter of introduction.","key":["Posthumus treats Imogen and the ring as two treasures; Iachimo says both can be stolen.","The wager turns a wife's honour into a bet between men.","Posthumus agrees too easily, and even gives Iachimo the introduction he needs."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Iachimo stake against Posthumus's ring?","o":["Half his estate","Ten thousand ducats","His own sword","A house in Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"Who tries to stop the wager?","o":["The Frenchman","Philario","Iachimo","Lucius"],"a":1},{"q":"What will happen if Iachimo fails?","o":["He pays the ducats and must fight Posthumus","He is banished from Rome","He must marry Imogen's lady","Nothing more"],"a":0}]},"a1s5":{"sum":"The Queen collects flowers and asks the doctor Cornelius for poisons, claiming she wants to test them on animals. Cornelius, suspicious, tells the audience he has given her a drug that only imitates death. The Queen offers Pisanio the box as a precious cordial and tries to win him to Cloten's side. He refuses in private.","key":["Cornelius's aside lets the audience know the 'poison' is harmless, which shapes how we watch Act 4.","The Queen is a fairy-tale wicked stepmother with poisons and flowers.","Pisanio's loyalty is tested and holds."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Queen say she wants the poisons for?","o":["To kill Posthumus","To test them on animals","To protect the King","To cure the King's fever"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Cornelius really given her?","o":["A strong poison","A drug that causes a deathlike sleep","A love potion","Plain water"],"a":1},{"q":"How does the Queen describe the box to Pisanio?","o":["A poison for his enemies","A cordial that has saved the King five times","A gift from Imogen","A love token"],"a":1}]},"a1s6":{"sum":"Iachimo arrives with Posthumus's letter. He tells Imogen that Posthumus mocks lovers and spends her money on prostitutes in Rome, and offers himself as her revenge. She calls for Pisanio and threatens to tell the King. Iachimo instantly says he was only testing her, praises Posthumus, and asks her to keep a trunk of valuables in her bedroom overnight. She agrees.","key":["Iachimo's first plan fails because Imogen sees through the slander at once.","He recovers smoothly: a master of improvisation.","The trunk is the Trojan horse of the play."],"quiz":[{"q":"What lie does Iachimo tell Imogen about Posthumus?","o":["That he is dead","That he consorts with prostitutes in Rome","That he has married again","That he plans to invade Britain"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Imogen react to Iachimo's offer?","o":["She is tempted","She calls for Pisanio and threatens to tell the King","She faints","She agrees to meet him later"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Iachimo ask her to keep?","o":["A letter","A trunk of plate and jewels","A ring","A horse"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Cloten has lost at bowls and broken a man's head for rebuking his swearing. Hearing that an Italian friend of Posthumus has arrived, he decides to go and win money from him. Alone, the Second Lord pities Imogen, caught between a father ruled by her stepmother, a plotting stepmother and a hateful suitor, and prays her honour will hold.","key":["Cloten's world is gambling, swearing and rank.","The Second Lord's soliloquy reminds us of Imogen's danger while Iachimo is in the palace."],"quiz":[{"q":"What game has Cloten been playing?","o":["Dice","Bowls","Cards","Tennis"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Second Lord pray for?","o":["Cloten's success","That Imogen's honour stays unshaken","Posthumus's death","Peace with Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Cloten want to meet the Italian?","o":["To fight him","To win money from him","To send a letter to Posthumus","To buy jewels"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Imogen has read for three hours and goes to sleep, asking the gods to guard her. Iachimo climbs out of the trunk. He notes the room, the tapestry and the pictures, slips the bracelet from her arm, and sees a mole on her left breast. She had been reading the story of Tereus and Philomel. The clock strikes three and he returns to the trunk.","key":["Iachimo compares himself to Tarquin, the rapist of Lucrece, though he only looks.","The book open at Philomel's rape warns of what could happen.","Everything he gathers is evidence of being in the room, not of adultery."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Iachimo take from Imogen?","o":["Her ring","Her bracelet","A letter","A lock of hair"],"a":1},{"q":"What mark does he see on her body?","o":["A scar on her hand","A five-spotted mole on her left breast","A birthmark on her neck","A tattoo"],"a":1},{"q":"What story was Imogen reading?","o":["Dido and Aeneas","Tereus and Philomel","Venus and Adonis","Pyramus and Thisbe"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Cloten hires musicians to wake Imogen with a song, 'Hark, hark! the lark'. Cymbeline and the Queen encourage his suit. Imogen rejects him bluntly and says Posthumus's meanest garment is dearer to her than Cloten. She sends Pisanio to look for her lost bracelet. Cloten swears revenge, repeating 'His meanest garment!'","key":["The beautiful aubade is used by a crude suitor.","'His meanest garment' plants the idea for Cloten's later plan.","Imogen notices the bracelet is gone, but has no idea how."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Cloten try to woo Imogen?","o":["With a letter","With morning music","With jewels","With a poem"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Imogen say is worth more than Cloten?","o":["Posthumus's sword","Posthumus's meanest garment","Her father's crown","Her bracelet"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Imogen send Pisanio to find?","o":["Her bracelet","Her ring","Her book","A horse"],"a":0}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"In Rome, Iachimo returns. He describes Imogen's bedroom in detail; Posthumus says he could have heard of it. Then Iachimo shows the bracelet, and Posthumus breaks. Philario suggests it may have been stolen, but Iachimo describes the mole under her breast, and Posthumus gives up the ring, rages and leaves.","key":["Posthumus moves from confidence to despair in a few lines.","Philario offers reasonable doubts; Posthumus ignores them.","The mole is the 'proof' that convinces him."],"quiz":[{"q":"What finally convinces Posthumus?","o":["The description of the room","The bracelet and the mole","A letter from Imogen","Pisanio's report"],"a":1},{"q":"What story is on the tapestry Iachimo describes?","o":["Cleopatra meeting Antony on the Cydnus","Diana bathing","The fall of Troy","Jupiter and Europa"],"a":0},{"q":"Who urges patience and doubt?","o":["Philario","Iachimo","Lucius","The Frenchman"],"a":0}]},"a2s5":{"sum":"Alone, Posthumus rages that all men are bastards because women are false, imagines Iachimo and Imogen together, and condemns every vice as 'the woman's part'. He vows to write against women.","key":["The soliloquy turns one supposed betrayal into hatred of all women.","Posthumus wishes he could tear 'the woman's part' out of himself."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Posthumus say every vice is?","o":["The devil's work","The woman's part","Rome's fault","Fortune's doing"],"a":1},{"q":"How does he picture Iachimo?","o":["As a German boar","As a fox","As a serpent","As a wolf"],"a":0},{"q":"What does he decide to do?","o":["Return to Britain","Write against women","Kill Iachimo","Enter a monastery"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Caius Lucius demands the tribute of three thousand pounds a year that Cassibelan agreed to pay Julius Caesar. The Queen and Cloten boast of Britain's island defences and refuse; Cymbeline agrees, citing Britain's ancient laws. Lucius declares war in Augustus's name, though the King welcomes him personally.","key":["The Queen's patriotic speech is stirring, but she is the play's villain.","The Roman tribute plot will meet the family plot in Act 5.","Cymbeline was knighted by Caesar and spent his youth in Rome."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Rome demand?","o":["Hostages","A yearly tribute","Imogen's hand","Wales"],"a":1},{"q":"Who speaks of Britain as 'Neptune's park'?","o":["Cloten","The Queen","Cymbeline","Lucius"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the result of the meeting?","o":["Peace","War between Britain and Rome","A marriage alliance","A truce for one year"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Pisanio reads Posthumus's letter ordering him to kill Imogen, and refuses to believe she is false. A second letter tells Imogen that Posthumus is at Milford Haven in Wales. Overjoyed, she plans to ride there at once, disguised in plain clothes.","key":["Posthumus uses Imogen's love as the bait for her murder.","Imogen's breathless joy is painful because we know the truth."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Posthumus's letter order Pisanio to do?","o":["Bring Imogen to Rome","Kill Imogen","Watch Cloten","Steal the bracelet back"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does the letter to Imogen say Posthumus is?","o":["Rome","Milford Haven","London","Gaul"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Imogen respond?","o":["She suspects a trap","She wishes for a horse with wings","She tells her father","She refuses to go"],"a":1}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"In Wales, Belarius and the two young men he calls Polydore and Cadwal greet the morning. Belarius praises their simple life over the court; the boys complain they have seen nothing of the world. Alone, Belarius reveals that they are Cymbeline's stolen sons, Guiderius and Arviragus, and that he took them in revenge for his unjust banishment.","key":["The pastoral world of Wales contrasts with the court.","The audience learns the princes' identity long before anyone on stage.","Royal blood shows through, Belarius says, in spite of upbringing."],"quiz":[{"q":"What name does Belarius use?","o":["Morgan","Polydore","Cadwal","Fidele"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did Belarius steal the princes?","o":["To ransom them","In revenge for his unjust banishment","To save them from the Queen","On Rome's orders"],"a":1},{"q":"Who was their nurse?","o":["Helen","Euriphile","Dorothy","Juno"],"a":1}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"Near Milford Haven, Pisanio shows Imogen her husband's letter. She is devastated, denies the charge, and bares her breast for the sword. Pisanio refuses to kill her. He suggests she dress as a boy and join Lucius, who will soon be at Milford, so she can be near Posthumus. He will send Posthumus false news of her death. He gives her the Queen's box as medicine.","key":["Imogen's grief turns to anger at men's false vows, but she still blames herself.","Pisanio's plan creates 'Fidele'.","The Queen's box is passed on in good faith."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Imogen find in her bodice?","o":["The bracelet","Posthumus's letters","A dagger","The box"],"a":1},{"q":"What disguise does Pisanio propose?","o":["A nun","A boy","A soldier","A shepherdess"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom should she try to serve?","o":["Belarius","Caius Lucius","Philario","Cloten"],"a":1}]},"a3s5":{"sum":"Lucius leaves the court. Imogen's absence is discovered; the Queen hopes she has died or fled to dishonour. Cloten threatens Pisanio, who gives him the false letter sending him to Milford. Cloten takes Posthumus's clothes, planning to kill Posthumus and rape Imogen while wearing them.","key":["Cloten's plan is grotesque revenge for 'his meanest garment'.","Pisanio sends him to Milford, trusting Imogen is far away.","The Queen's ambition is plain: with Imogen gone, she controls the crown."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Cloten take from Pisanio?","o":["The box","Posthumus's clothes","Imogen's ring","A horse"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Cloten plan to do at Milford?","o":["Bring Imogen home","Kill Posthumus and rape Imogen","Join the Romans","Find the lost princes"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Queen hope?","o":["Imogen is safe","Imogen is dead or dishonoured","Posthumus returns","Peace with Rome"],"a":1}]},"a3s6":{"sum":"Imogen, as Fidele, lost and starving, enters the empty cave and eats. Belarius and the boys return and find her. She offers money, which they scorn, and they welcome her warmly. Guiderius and Arviragus love the boy at once; Imogen wishes they were her brothers.","key":["Imogen does not know these are her brothers, though she wishes it.","The bond between the siblings is instinctive.","The disguise both protects her and keeps her from the truth."],"quiz":[{"q":"What name does Imogen give?","o":["Fidele","Polydore","Cadwal","Richard du Champ"],"a":0},{"q":"How do the brothers react to her?","o":["They suspect a spy","They love the boy at once","They send him away","They ask for payment"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Imogen wish?","o":["That she had stayed at court","That they were her father's sons","That she was a man","That Posthumus were dead"],"a":1}]},"a3s7":{"sum":"In Rome, a senator tells the tribunes that Lucius has been made proconsul, and that the gentry must be levied to reinforce the legions in Gaul for the war against Britain.","key":["A brief scene moving the war forward.","Rome's army is stretched by wars elsewhere."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is made proconsul?","o":["Iachimo","Lucius","Philario","Augustus"],"a":1},{"q":"Who must be levied?","o":["The common soldiers","The gentry","The Gauls","The Britons in Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"Where are the legions waiting?","o":["Gaul","Spain","Italy","Wales"],"a":0}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Cloten, in Posthumus's clothes, arrives near the cave. He boasts that he is better than Posthumus in every way and plans to kill him, rape Imogen in front of him and kick her home to her father.","key":["The clothes fit; Cloten believes he could replace Posthumus completely.","His plan is the play's ugliest threat."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose clothes is Cloten wearing?","o":["Pisanio's","Posthumus's","Belarius's","The King's"],"a":1},{"q":"What does he plan to do with Imogen afterwards?","o":["Marry her","Send her home to her father","Take her to Rome","Imprison her in Wales"],"a":1},{"q":"How does he compare himself to Posthumus?","o":["As worse","As better in birth, strength and youth","As exactly equal","As less handsome"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Imogen, unwell, stays in the cave and takes the Queen's drug. Cloten meets Guiderius, insults him and is beheaded. Arviragus finds Fidele apparently dead. The brothers say the dirge 'Fear no more the heat o' th' sun' and lay Fidele beside Cloten's body. Imogen wakes, takes the headless body in Posthumus's clothes for her husband, blames Pisanio, and faints on it. Lucius finds her, she calls herself Fidele, servant to 'Richard du Champ', and becomes his page.","key":["Comedy and tragedy collide: the dirge, the mistaken body, Imogen's grief.","The audience knows the body is Cloten, which makes the scene both painful and grotesque.","Lucius's kindness carries Imogen into the last act."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who kills Cloten?","o":["Belarius","Guiderius","Arviragus","Posthumus"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Imogen think the headless body is?","o":["Cloten","Posthumus","Pisanio","Lucius"],"a":1},{"q":"What name does she give her dead 'master'?","o":["Richard du Champ","Leonatus","Morgan","Philarmonus"],"a":0}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"At court, the Queen is ill with grief for Cloten, and Imogen is gone. Cymbeline threatens Pisanio with torture. News comes that the Roman legions have landed. Alone, Pisanio is puzzled to have heard nothing from anyone and resolves to fight for Britain.","key":["Cymbeline's world is falling apart before the battle.","Pisanio: 'Wherein I am false I am honest.'"],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the Queen ill?","o":["Poison","Grief at Cloten's absence","Fear of Rome","Old age"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Cymbeline threaten Pisanio?","o":["With banishment","With torture","With death at once","With prison"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Pisanio not received?","o":["Any letter from Posthumus","His wages","A pardon","A sword"],"a":0}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"With the battle noise around them, Belarius wants to hide higher in the mountains. The princes insist on joining the British army, ashamed never to have seen a battle. Belarius gives in and goes with them.","key":["The princes' 'royal blood' drives them to fight for a father they do not know.","Belarius's reluctance comes from fear of being recognised."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Belarius first suggest?","o":["Joining Rome","Hiding higher in the mountains","Going to court","Fleeing to Gaul"],"a":1},{"q":"Why do the princes want to fight?","o":["For money","They are ashamed to have done nothing","To kill Cloten","To find Fidele"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Belarius finally do?","o":["Stays behind","Goes with them","Informs the King","Joins the Romans"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Posthumus, with the Roman army and holding the bloody cloth, repents ordering Imogen's death. He decides to change into a British peasant's clothes and die fighting for her country.","key":["He repents believing she is guilty; he forgives before he knows the truth.","The change of clothes mirrors Cloten's, but in the opposite spirit."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Posthumus carry?","o":["A bloody handkerchief","Imogen's ring","The bracelet","A letter"],"a":0},{"q":"Which side does he decide to fight for?","o":["Rome","Britain","Neither","Both"],"a":1},{"q":"How will he disguise himself?","o":["As a Roman officer","As a British peasant","As a priest","As a woman"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"In the battle, Posthumus, disguised, beats and disarms Iachimo, whose guilt weakens him. The Britons flee and Cymbeline is captured, but Belarius, the princes and Posthumus rescue him. Lucius tells Fidele to save himself.","key":["Iachimo is defeated by the man he wronged without knowing it.","The battle turns on a few individuals."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who defeats Iachimo?","o":["Guiderius","Posthumus","Belarius","Cymbeline"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Iachimo say he is weak?","o":["He is wounded","Guilt over slandering the princess","He is old","He is hungry"],"a":1},{"q":"Who rescues the King?","o":["Lucius","Belarius, the princes and Posthumus","Pisanio","Cloten"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Posthumus tells a fleeing lord how an old man and two boys held a narrow lane and turned the battle. Disgusted by cowardice and still seeking death, he resumes his Roman identity and lets two British captains arrest him.","key":["The story of the lane is the British victory told in miniature.","Posthumus wants death as payment for Imogen's."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who held the lane?","o":["Three Roman soldiers","An old man and two boys","Cymbeline and his lords","Posthumus alone"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Posthumus get captured?","o":["He is wounded","He claims to be a Roman","He is betrayed by Iachimo","He surrenders to Cymbeline"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the lord do?","o":["Fights bravely","Fled from the battle","Captures Lucius","Writes a rhyme"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"In prison, Posthumus offers his life to the gods for Imogen's and sleeps. The ghosts of his father, mother and brothers appear and appeal to Jupiter, who descends on an eagle, promises Posthumus happiness and leaves a riddling tablet. The gaoler jokes about hanging, then Posthumus is called before the King.","key":["The masque-like vision brings the gods into the play.","Jupiter: 'Whom best I love I cross.'","The prophecy is a puzzle solved in the final scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who appears to Posthumus?","o":["Imogen's ghost","His dead family","Cloten's ghost","The Queen"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Jupiter appear?","o":["On a chariot","On an eagle, in thunder","Disguised as a beggar","In a dream of Rome"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Jupiter leave?","o":["A ring","A tablet with a prophecy","A sword","A crown"],"a":1}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"Cymbeline knights Belarius and the princes. Cornelius reports the Queen's death and confession. The Roman prisoners are brought in; Imogen, as Fidele, asks Iachimo where he got his ring, and he confesses the whole deception. Posthumus reveals himself, strikes the 'page', and Imogen is recognised. Pisanio and Cornelius explain the drug. Guiderius admits killing Cloten; Belarius reveals the princes. Posthumus forgives Iachimo, the soothsayer interprets the prophecy, and Cymbeline makes peace and agrees to pay the tribute.","key":["A chain of over twenty revelations, each releasing another.","Forgiveness replaces revenge: Posthumus pardons Iachimo, Cymbeline pardons all.","The victor chooses to pay Rome's tribute: peace on Rome's terms."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Imogen ask as her boon?","o":["Lucius's life","That Iachimo say where he got the ring","Her brothers' freedom","Posthumus's pardon"],"a":1},{"q":"How is Guiderius recognised?","o":["By his cloak","By a mole like a red star on his neck","By his voice","By a ring"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Cymbeline decide about the tribute?","o":["Never to pay it","To pay it as before","To demand tribute from Rome","To let Wales pay it"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"1":"The play opens with two courtiers explaining the situation, a common Shakespearean device. Their point is that the whole court must copy the King's frown while secretly approving of Imogen's choice.","3":"'Referr'd herself' means she has given herself in marriage. The three facts arrive in a rush: married, banished, imprisoned.","11":"Posthumus's name means 'born after his father's death'; Leonatus means 'lion-born', which matters in the prophecy of 5.4 and 5.5.","13":"The stolen princes are mentioned in passing in the first scene, a plot the play does not take up again until 3.3.","22":"The Queen's promise not to be a typical stepmother is exactly what a wicked stepmother would say: Imogen calls it 'dissembling courtesy'.","35":"The bracelet is 'a manacle of love'. Iachimo will steal it as false proof that Imogen gave herself away.","48":"An eagle against a puttock (a kite or buzzard, a bird of prey that scavenges): Imogen ranks Posthumus above Cloten by nature, not birth.","77":"The Second Lord's asides form a running commentary that turns Cloten's boasts into jokes for the audience.","107":"Imogen's 'eyestrings' were believed to break at the moment of death or extreme grief.","109":"The 'shes of Italy' sets up the Italian scenes: Imogen fears Italian women, but the danger is an Italian man.","132":"The earlier quarrel in France shows that boasting about his wife is a habit of Posthumus's, not a single slip.","145":"'Strange fowl light upon neighbouring ponds': other men's wives can be visited. Iachimo treats both the ring and the wife as property that can be stolen.","163":"Posthumus accepts the wager, a decision many readers and audiences find hard to forgive.","183":"The Queen's claim to test poisons on animals is a thin cover; Cornelius sees through it at once.","190":"Cornelius's aside is crucial: the audience knows from now on that the 'poison' only causes a deathlike sleep.","196":"The Queen gives Pisanio the box, telling him it is a cordial that has saved the King's life. In 3.4 Pisanio gives it to Imogen in good faith.","206":"'Most miserable Is the desire that's glorious': aspiring to high things brings misery. Imogen envies people of low birth who can marry whom they like.","211":"The 'Arabian bird' is the phoenix, of which there was only one in the world at a time.","239":"Iachimo's speech is deliberately broken and suggestive: he hints at Posthumus's unfaithfulness without stating anything.","249":"Iachimo's sudden praise of Posthumus is a quick recovery, and it is also true: the play keeps insisting on Posthumus's reputation.","255":"The 'present for the Emperor' is the excuse for the trunk.","266":"To 'kiss the jack' in bowls is to roll your bowl right up against the small target ball.","293":"The Second Lord's soliloquy gives the audience a reminder of Imogen's danger at the moment Iachimo is hidden in her room.","305":"Tarquin raped Lucrece; Iachimo casts himself in that role but uses his eyes, not force. Cytherea is Venus.","307":"Tereus raped his sister-in-law Philomel and cut out her tongue (Ovid's Metamorphoses). The leaf turned down at that story is a sinister image of what might have happened.","318":"Cloten's lines are full of crude double meanings ('penetrate', 'fingering').","319":"The aubade 'Hark, hark! the lark' (a dawn song) is one of Shakespeare's best-known lyrics, here commissioned by the play's crudest character.","357":"Cloten argues that a princess cannot make a private marriage contract the way commoners can, because the crown is at stake.","360":"'His mean'st garment' becomes Cloten's obsession and leads him to dress in Posthumus's clothes.","382":"Posthumus, though banished, predicts that Britain will fight and win: a patriot in exile.","404":"The tapestry shows Cleopatra meeting Antony on the river Cydnus, the subject of a famous speech in Antony and Cleopatra.","426":"The mole was the one detail Iachimo could not have learned from gossip; it convinces Posthumus.","440":"Posthumus's speech against women is one of the most violent misogynist outbursts in Shakespeare. The play does not endorse it: he repents in 5.1.","445":"The tribute is historical legend: Holinshed records Cassibelan agreeing to pay Julius Caesar.","448":"The Queen's speech on Britain as 'Neptune's park' anticipates John of Gaunt's patriotic praise of England in Richard II.","454":"Mulmutius was a legendary early king said to have made Britain's first laws.","464":"Pisanio's loyalty is to the 'true' Posthumus, not to the deceived one who gives this order.","471":"Cambria is Wales. Milford Haven, a harbour in west Wales, was where Henry Tudor landed in 1485: an important place in the Tudor story.","479":"Belarius turns the low cave entrance into a lesson: it teaches them to bow to heaven.","489":"Belarius's soliloquy reveals the princes' identity to the audience only. He uses three names: Polydore/Guiderius and Cadwal/Arviragus; he himself is Morgan.","501":"Aeneas abandoned Dido; Sinon tricked the Trojans with false tears into accepting the wooden horse. Imogen says Posthumus's falseness will make all good men look false.","503":"Imogen finds Posthumus's letters in her bodice ('stomacher') between her heart and the sword and throws them away.","519":"'In a great pool a swan's nest': Britain seen as part of the world but separate from it.","522":"Pisanio tells Imogen to give up a woman's modesty and become cheeky and quick like a boy.","589":"Cloten's plan to rape Imogen in Posthumus's clothes is his twisted revenge for the 'meanest garment' insult.","601":"Imogen's soliloquy in male clothing: hungry, tired and still resolute.","646":"Cloten's soliloquy compares himself to Posthumus point by point; he even notes that the clothes fit him.","662":"Imogen drinks the Queen's drug believing it to be medicine. The audience knows from Cornelius it is not deadly.","711":"Guiderius kills a prince and is unafraid. He does not know that Cloten is his stepbrother.","724":"Belarius sees nature, not education, in the princes' behaviour: an argument that runs through the play.","740":"Arviragus's flower speech echoes the pastoral elegies of the period.","755":"The dirge 'Fear no more the heat o' th' sun' is spoken, not sung (Guiderius says he cannot sing). 'Chimney-sweepers' may also be a country name for dandelion seed heads.","773":"Imogen's grief over the wrong body is one of the strangest moments in Shakespeare: an audience may weep and laugh at once.","788":"'Richard du Champ' is an invented French name. Imogen admits the lie in an aside.","799":"Cymbeline is losing his whole family at once: daughter gone, wife dying, stepson missing.","828":"Posthumus forgives Imogen before he knows she is innocent, which is part of what makes his repentance remarkable.","849":"The defence of the narrow lane is based on a story in Holinshed about the Scottish farmer Hay and his sons at the battle of Luncarty.","869":"Posthumus speaks of his life as a coin stamped by the gods, which he offers in payment for Imogen's.","884":"Jupiter speaks in rhymed pentameter, raising the language above the rest of the play.","894":"The gaoler's jokes about death paying all debts lighten the scene before the reversal.","928":"The Queen's confession explains her whole plot, including the plan to poison Cymbeline.","969":"Iachimo's confession is long and full of digressions, partly to delay the worst and partly because he enjoys telling a story.","972":"Posthumus strikes Imogen without recognising her: one last blow before the reunion.","991":"'Hang there like fruit, my soul, Till the tree die!': one of the play's most admired lines.","1022":"Belarius explains why he stole the princes: he had already been punished as a traitor, so he made himself one.","1033":"Cymbeline admits the story is too complicated to tell in full here, a joke about the play's own plotting.","1048":"The soothsayer's Latin pun, 'mollis aer' (tender air) giving 'mulier' (woman), was an old false etymology.","1051":"The victor agrees to pay tribute to the defeated: peace with Rome matters more than pride."},"gloss":{"bloods":"moods, temperaments","purpos'd":"intended","referr'd":"given","outward":"in appearance","bent":"inclination","sur-addition":"extra name, title","fond of issue":"loving his children","feated":"shaped, set an example to","dotards":"foolish old men","election":"choice","swathing clothes":"swaddling clothes","convey'd":"stolen away","slackly":"carelessly","forbear":"stop, keep quiet","advocate":"supporter","marry":"indeed","lean'd unto":"accepted","dissembling":"pretended","abide":"endure","plight troth":"pledge faith","gall":"bitter liquid; poison","sear up":"seal up","manacle":"handcuff","avoid":"go away","fraught":"burden","repair":"renew","puttock":"kite, a scavenging bird of prey","neat-herd":"cowherd","languish":"waste away","Afric":"Africa","goer-back":"one who retreats","haven":"harbour","reek":"steam, smell","passable":"able to be passed through","throughfare":"thoroughfare, passage","eyestrings":"muscles of the eye","diminution":"lessening","orisons":"prayers","tyrannous":"cruel","crescent":"growing","tabled":"listed","approbation":"approval","atone":"reconcile","arbitrement":"decision","attemptable":"open to seduction","outlustres":"outshines","unparagon'd":"matchless","casual":"subject to accident","convince":"overcome","moiety":"half","ducats":"gold coins","covenants":"agreements","articles":"terms of agreement","confections":"mixtures, medicines","languishing":"slowly wasting","cordial":"restoring the heart","earnest":"down payment","remembrancer":"one who reminds","hand-fast":"marriage contract","leigers":"resident ambassadors","step-dame":"stepmother","Arabian bird":"phoenix","Parthian":"of the Parthians, who shot arrows while retreating","vaulted arch":"the sky","twinn'd":"identical","mows":"grimaces","cloyed":"overfed","ravening":"devouring greedily","peevish":"silly, foolish","Gallian":"French","bondage":"slavery","solace":"take comfort","snuff":"candle stub","slaver":"slobber","tallow":"animal fat used for candles","empery":"empire","tomboys":"loose women","runagate":"runaway, vagabond","Romish stew":"Roman brothel","saucy":"insolent","affiance":"faith","chaffless":"without chaff, pure","plate":"silver or gold dishes","factor":"agent","jack":"small target ball in bowls","pate":"head","jackanapes":"monkey; conceited fellow","capon":"castrated cock; fool","derogation":"loss of dignity","rushes":"floor coverings of dried reeds","Cytherea":"Venus","arras":"tapestry","cinque-spotted":"with five spots","cowslip":"yellow wild flower","voucher":"proof","Gordian knot":"knot no one could untie","Philomel":"Philomela, raped by Tereus","Phoebus":"the sun god","Mary-buds":"marigolds","bin":"is, are","horsehairs":"bow strings","calves' guts":"instrument strings","minion":"darling","dismission":"being sent away","Diana's rangers":"gamekeepers of the goddess of chastity","stand":"hunter's hiding place","foster'd":"fed","pantler":"pantry servant","hilding":"good-for-nothing","sprited":"haunted","shrew me":"curse me","statist":"statesman","Gallia":"Gaul, France","harts":"male deer","basilisk":"mythical serpent that killed with a look","colted":"ridden like a colt; possessed","limb-meal":"limb from limb","cognizance":"badge, token","pudency":"modesty","unsunn'd":"untouched by the sun","German":"German boars were proverbially fierce","mutability":"changeableness","Cassibelan":"Cassivellaunus, British king who fought Julius Caesar","Lud's Town":"London","giglot":"wanton, fickle","Mulmutius":"legendary early British king and lawgiver","Pannonians":"people of modern Hungary","Dalmatians":"people of the Adriatic coast","fedary":"accomplice","Cambria":"Wales","franklin":"farmer who owns his land","huswife":"housewife","prince it":"behave like a prince","sharded":"with wing cases","check":"rebuke","tanlings":"sunburnt creatures","curtsy":"bow","Euriphile":"the princes' nurse","Aeneas":"Trojan hero who abandoned Dido","Sinon":"Greek who tricked Troy with false tears","stomachers":"pieces worn over the breast","tent":"probe a wound","bauble":"trinket","jay":"flashy bird; loose woman","Titan":"the sun","carriage":"carrying off","conduct":"escort","runagates":"runaways","clotpoll":"blockhead","ingenious":"skilful","crare":"small trading ship","clouted brogues":"hobnailed shoes","ruddock":"robin","eglantine":"sweet-briar","Thersites":"railing, cowardly Greek in the Iliad","Ajax":"great Greek warrior","obsequies":"funeral rites","exorciser":"one who raises spirits","consummation":"end","'ods pittikins":"God's pity","Hecuba":"Queen of Troy, maddened by grief","irregulous":"lawless","pregnant":"obvious","soothsayer":"prophet","partisans":"long-handled spears","preferr'd":"recommended","proconsul":"governor of a province","levy":"raising of troops","render":"account","rowel":"spur","wrying":"going astray","weeds":"clothes","carl":"peasant","hoodwink'd":"blindfolded","targes":"shields","strait":"narrow","bugs":"terrors","bane":"ruin","gyves":"chains","Lucina":"goddess of childbirth","geck":"dupe","synod":"assembly","Elysium":"the home of the blessed dead","cloys":"claws","prunes":"preens","label":"tablet, slip","whelp":"cub","cedar":"tree; emblem of kingship","shot":"bill","penny cord":"cheap rope (for hanging)","debitor":"debt account","mortal":"deadly","scorpion":"deadly creature","importun'd":"pestered","sanguine":"blood-red","mollis aer":"tender air (Latin)","mulier":"woman (Latin)","ensign":"flag","Leo-natus":"lion-born (Latin)","Philarmonus":"lover of harmony","Dian":"Diana, goddess of chastity","Jove":"Jupiter, king of the gods","Milford Haven":"harbour in south-west Wales"}};
const PARTS={"11":[[0,12,"I can't trace his roots far. His father was Sicilius, who fought for honour alongside Cassibelan against the Romans but got his titles from Tenantius, whom he served with glory and great success, and so earned the extra name Leonatus. Besides this gentleman, he had two other sons, who died sword in hand in the wars of the time. Their father, old and devoted to his children, grieved so much that he died, and his gentle wife, pregnant with this gentleman, died as he was born."],[13,22,"The King took the baby under his protection, named him Posthumus Leonatus, brought him up, made him a gentleman of his bedchamber, and gave him every kind of learning his age could offer. He took it in as fast as we breathe air and was ripe for harvest in his youth. He lived at court, which is rare, and was praised and loved by all: a model to the young, a mirror for the mature to dress themselves by, and to the old, a child who guided their foolish age."],[23,26,"As for his lady, for whom he is now banished, her own worth shows how highly she valued him and his virtue. You can read what kind of man he is from her choice."]],"22":[[0,3,"No, daughter, you can be sure you won't find me treating you with the evil eye that stepmothers are famous for. You're my prisoner, but your jailer will hand you the keys that lock you up."],[4,9,"As for you, Posthumus, as soon as I can soften the angry King, I'll be known as your champion. But right now he's burning with rage, and you'd do well to accept his sentence with as much patience as your good sense allows."]],"27":[[0,4,"My queen, my lady! Don't cry any more, or I'll show more tenderness than a man should. I'll stay the most loyal husband who ever made a vow."],[5,9,"I'll be living in Rome at the house of a man called Philario, a friend of my father's whom I know only by letter. Write to me there, my queen, and I'll drink in your words with my eyes, even if the ink is bitter gall."]],"109":[[0,4,"I didn't say goodbye to him properly, and I had such pretty things to say. Before I could tell him how I'd think of him at certain hours, with such and such thoughts; or make him swear the women of Italy wouldn't betray my rights and his honour;"],[5,12,"or tell him to meet me in prayer at six in the morning, at noon and at midnight, because then I'm in heaven for him; or before I could give him the parting kiss I had placed between two magic words, in comes my father and, like the cruel north wind, shakes all our buds before they can grow."]],"183":[[0,4,"I'm surprised you ask me that, Doctor. Haven't I been your pupil for a long time? Haven't you taught me to make perfumes, to distil, to preserve, so well that the great King himself often begs me for my concoctions?"],[5,13,"Having come this far (unless you think I'm a devil), isn't it right that I should widen my knowledge into other experiments? I'll test the strength of your mixtures on creatures we don't think worth hanging, but no humans, to see how powerful they are, try out remedies against them, and so learn their various properties and effects."]],"190":[[0,5,"(Aside) I don't like her. She thinks she has strange slow poisons. I know her nature and won't trust anyone so spiteful with a drug that deadly. What I've given her will stun and deaden the senses for a while. She'll probably test it first on cats and dogs, then on higher creatures."],[6,11,"But there's no danger in the appearance of death it causes, beyond shutting down the vital spirits for a time, so they revive all the fresher. She's fooled by a completely false effect, and I'm all the more honest for being false to her."]],"194":[[0,7,"Is she still crying, you say? Don't you think that in time she'll cool off and let good advice in where foolishness rules now? Work on her. When you bring me word that she loves my son, I'll tell you on the spot that you're as great as your master; greater, because his fortunes are struck dumb and his name is at its last gasp."],[8,14,"He can't come back, and he can't stay where he is. To move would be to swap one misery for another, and every new day only undoes another day's work in him. What can you hope for by depending on a falling thing that can't be rebuilt and hasn't a friend to prop it up?"]],"196":[[0,7,"You're picking up you don't know what, but keep it for your trouble. It's something I made that has saved the King from death five times. I don't know a better restorative. No, please take it. It's a down payment on more good things I mean to do for you. Tell your mistress how things stand for her; say it as if it came from you."],[8,15,"Think what a chance you're getting to change sides; and think that you'll still have your mistress, and my son into the bargain, who'll take notice of you. I'll persuade the King to promote you any way you like, and I myself, who set you on to earn it, will be bound to reward you richly. Call my women. Think about what I've said."]],"227":[[0,4,"I never saw him sad. He has a companion there, a Frenchman, a distinguished gentleman who apparently loves a French girl back home very much. He pumps out heavy sighs like a furnace,"],[5,10,"while the jolly Briton (your lord, I mean) laughs from the bottom of his lungs and cries, \"Oh, my sides will split, to think that a man who knows from history, gossip or his own experience what woman is, yes, what she can't help being, would waste his free hours pining for certain slavery!\""]],"239":[[0,5,"If I had this cheek to bathe my lips on, this hand whose every touch would force the toucher's soul to swear loyalty, this face that captures my wandering eye and fixes it here alone,"],[6,13,"would I then, damned for it, slobber on lips as common as the steps up to the Capitol, clasp hands made hard by hourly lying (lying as if it were hard work), then peep sideways into an eye as low and as bright as a smoky candle fed on stinking fat? It would be right for all the plagues of hell to strike such betrayal at once."]],"243":[[0,7,"Oh dearest soul, your situation strikes my heart with a pity that makes me sick! A lady so beautiful, and heir to an empire that would double the greatest king, to be put on a level with whores hired with the very allowance your own treasury pays him! With diseased prostitutes who gamble for gold with every sickness rottenness can give a body!"],[8,10,"Such stewed filth that it could poison poison! Get revenge, or the woman who gave birth to you was no queen, and you fall short of your great family."]],"248":[[0,7,"Get away! I blame my ears for listening to you so long. If you were honourable, you'd have told this story to serve virtue, not for the purpose you're after, as low as it is strange. You slander a gentleman who is as far from your report as you are from honour, and you try to seduce a lady who despises you and the devil alike. Pisanio!"],[8,14,"The King my father will be told of your attack. If he thinks it right for an insolent stranger to bargain in his court as if it were a Roman brothel, and to lay out his beastly mind to us, then he has a court he cares little for, and a daughter he doesn't respect at all. Pisanio!"]],"249":[[0,6,"Oh, lucky Leonatus! I can say your lady's faith in you deserves your trust, and your perfect goodness deserves her certain faith. Long may you both live happily, a lady married to the worthiest gentleman any country ever called its own, and you his lady, fit only for the worthiest! Forgive me."],[7,12,"I said all that to find out whether your faith was deeply rooted, and I'll make your lord what he already is all over again. He's the most truly well-bred of men, such a holy enchanter that he charms company to him; half of every man's heart is his."]],"251":[[0,6,"He sits among men like a god come down to earth. He has a kind of honour that makes him look more than mortal. Don't be angry, most mighty Princess, that I dared to test how you'd take a false report, which has proved again your great judgment in choosing so rare a gentleman,"],[7,9,"a judgment you know can't go wrong. My love for him made me winnow you like this, but the gods made you, unlike everyone else, without chaff. Please forgive me."]],"293":[[0,4,"That such a crafty devil as his mother should give the world this ass! A woman who overpowers everything with her brain, and this son of hers can't take two from twenty and leave eighteen to save his life."],[5,13,"Alas, poor princess, divine Imogen, what you endure: between a father ruled by your stepmother, a stepmother hatching plots every hour, and a suitor more hateful than the cruel banishment of your dear husband or the horrible divorce he'd make! May the heavens keep the walls of your honour strong and that temple, your pure mind, unshaken, so that you may live to enjoy your banished lord and this great country!"]],"305":[[0,5,"The crickets are singing, and man's overworked senses are restoring themselves by rest. This is how our Tarquin stepped softly on the rushes before he woke the chastity he wounded. Venus, how beautifully you suit your bed! Fresh lily, whiter than the sheets! If only I could touch!"],[6,12,"Just kiss; one kiss! Matchless rubies, how sweetly they press together! It's her breath that perfumes the room like this. The candle flame bends toward her and wants to peep under her eyelids to see the lights shut in there, now curtained under these white and blue lids, laced with veins the colour of the sky."],[13,19,"But my plan is to note the room. I'll write it all down: such and such pictures; the window there; her bed decorated like this; the tapestry, its figures, so and so, and what story it tells. Ah, but some natural marks on her body would be better evidence than ten thousand lesser furnishings, and would enrich my list."],[20,22,"Oh sleep, you imitation of death, lie heavy on her, and let her senses be like a stone effigy lying in a chapel! Come off, come off;"]],"307":[[0,8,"as slippery as the Gordian knot was tight! It's mine, and this will be outward proof, as strong as his conscience is within, to drive her lord mad. On her left breast there's a mole with five spots, like the red drops at the bottom of a cowslip. Here's proof stronger than any law could produce. This secret will force him to think I've picked the lock and taken the treasure of her honour."],[9,13,"No more. What for? Why write down what's riveted and screwed into my memory? She's been reading late: the story of Tereus. Here's the page turned down where Philomel gave in. I have enough. Back into the trunk, and shut its spring."],[14,17,"Hurry, hurry, you dragons that pull the night, so that dawn may open the raven's eye! I'm lodged in fear. Though this is a heavenly angel, hell is here. (A clock strikes.) One, two, three. Time, time!"]],"328":[[0,4,"You owe a great deal to the King, who lets no chance go by to recommend you to his daughter. Make yourself a proper suitor and choose your moments well."],[5,9,"Let every refusal make you serve her more. Seem as if you were inspired to do the services you offer her; obey her in everything, except when she orders you to leave, and then act as if you hadn't heard."]],"334":[[0,6,"If she's up, I'll speak to her; if not, let her lie and dream. Excuse me, hello! (He knocks.) I know her women are with her. What if I tip one of them? It's gold that buys entry; it often does, yes, and makes Diana's own gamekeepers betray her, and drive their deer to the poacher's stand."],[7,12,"And it's gold that gets the honest man killed and saves the thief; no, sometimes hangs both thief and honest man. What can't it do and undo? I'll make one of her women my lawyer, because I don't understand the case myself yet. Excuse me. (He knocks.)"]],"356":[[0,4,"Since I'm mad, yes. If you'll be patient, I'll stop being mad; that cures us both. I'm very sorry, sir, that you make me forget a lady's manners by speaking so plainly,"],[5,9,"but learn once and for all that I, who know my own heart, declare here, by its very truth, that I don't care for you, and I'm so close to lacking charity that I'd accuse myself of hating you, which I'd rather you felt than heard me boast of."]],"357":[[0,4,"You sin against the obedience you owe your father. As for the marriage you claim with that low wretch, brought up on charity and fed on cold leftovers, scraps from the court: it's no marriage, none."],[5,13,"And though lower people (but who's lower than he?) are allowed to tie the knot themselves, when all they have to depend on is brats and poverty, you are held back from that freedom by the importance of the crown, and mustn't stain its precious name with a low slave, a lackey fit for a servant's uniform, a squire's cast-off, a pantry boy, and not even that grand!"]],"365":[[0,5,"I'm haunted by a fool, frightened and worse, angered. Go and tell my maid to look for a jewel that has slipped off my arm by accident. It was your master's. Curse me if I'd lose it for the income of any king in Europe!"],[6,9,"I think I saw it this morning; I'm certain it was on my arm last night: I kissed it. I hope it hasn't gone to tell my lord that I kiss anything but him."]],"382":[[0,5,"I believe, though I'm no statesman and never will be, that this will turn into a war, and that you'll hear of the legions now in France landing in our unafraid Britain before you hear of a penny of tribute paid."],[6,11,"Our countrymen are better organised than when Julius Caesar smiled at their lack of skill but found their courage worth frowning at. Their discipline, now joined to their courage, will show anyone who tests them that they're a people who improve with time."]],"404":[[0,6,"First, her bedroom (where, I admit, I didn't sleep, but I declare I had what was well worth staying awake for). It was hung with tapestry of silk and silver showing proud Cleopatra meeting her Roman, and the river Cydnus swelling over its banks, either from the crowd of boats or from pride."],[7,10,"A piece of work so finely and richly done that its craftsmanship competed with its value. I wondered how it could be made so rarely and exactly, since the true life of it was..."]],"440":[[0,7,"Is there no way for men to be born without women doing half the work? We're all bastards, and that most respected man I called my father was who knows where when I was minted. Some forger with his tools made me a fake coin; and yet my mother seemed the Diana of her day, as my wife seems the one and only of this one. Oh, revenge, revenge!"],[8,12,"She held me back from my lawful pleasure and often begged me to be patient, and did it with such rosy modesty that the sweet sight of it could have warmed old Saturn, so that I thought her as chaste as snow the sun has never touched. Oh, all the devils!"],[13,18,"This sallow Iachimo, in an hour, wasn't it? Or less, right from the start? Maybe he didn't even speak but, like a German boar stuffed with acorns, cried \"Oh!\" and mounted, and found no resistance except what he expected to meet, the part she should have guarded."],[19,27,"If only I could find the woman's part in me! For there's no impulse toward vice in a man that isn't, I swear, the woman's part. If it's lying, mark it, it's the woman's; flattering, hers; deceiving, hers; lust and filthy thoughts, hers, hers; revenge, hers; ambition, greed, changing vanities, scorn, fussy cravings, slander, fickleness, every fault a man can name, no, that hell knows: hers, in part or all; but rather all."],[28,34,"Because they aren't even constant in their vices, but keep swapping one that's a minute old for one not half as old. I'll write against them, hate them, curse them. And yet the cleverest true hatred is to pray they get what they want: the devils themselves couldn't punish them better."]],"448":[[0,8,"That chance they had then to take it from us, we now have to take it back. Remember, sir, my king, the kings your ancestors, and the natural strength of your island, which stands like Neptune's park, ribbed and fenced with rocks that can't be climbed and roaring waters, with sands that won't hold up your enemies' boats but suck them down to the top of the mast."],[9,15,"Caesar made a sort of conquest here, but he didn't make his boast of \"I came, I saw, I conquered\" here. With shame, the first he ever felt, he was carried off our coast, beaten twice; and his ships, poor ignorant toys, were tossed on our terrible seas like eggshells on the waves and cracked just as easily against our rocks."],[16,19,"To celebrate, the famous Cassibelan, who was once on the point (oh, fickle fortune!) of mastering Caesar's sword, lit up London with bonfires and made Britons strut with courage."]],"464":[[0,5,"What? Adultery? Why don't you write what monsters accuse her? Leonatus! Oh, master, what strange infection has got into your ear! What false Italian, as poisonous of tongue as of hand, has worked on your too-willing hearing? Disloyal? No."],[6,12,"She's punished for her faithfulness and endures, more like a goddess than a wife, attacks that would conquer most virtue. Oh, my master! Your opinion of her is now as low as your fortunes were. What? I should murder her? Because of the love and loyalty and vows I've given to serve you? I, kill her? Shed her blood?"],[13,21,"If that's good service, never call me a good servant. How do I look, that I should seem to lack humanity as much as this deed requires? (Reading) \"Do it. The letter I've sent her will, by her own wish, give you the chance.\" Oh, damned paper, as black as the ink on you! Senseless thing, are you an accomplice in this act, and look so virgin-white outside? Look, here she comes."]],"469":[[0,3,"From whom? Your lord? That's my lord, Leonatus? Oh, that astronomer would be learned indeed who knew the stars as well as I know his handwriting: he'd lay the future open."],[4,8,"Kind gods, let what's in here taste of love, of my lord's health, of his happiness; but not happiness that we're apart; let that grieve him! Some griefs are healing, and that's one, because it keeps love healthy. Of his happiness in everything but that."],[9,13,"Good wax, excuse me. Blessed be you bees who make these seals for secrets! Lovers and men in dangerous debt don't pray alike: though you throw debtors in prison by sealing their bonds, you also seal young Cupid's love letters. Good news, gods!"]],"471":[[0,6,"(Reading) \"Justice and your father's anger, if he caught me in his kingdom, could not be so cruel to me that you, dearest of creatures, could not restore me with your eyes. Know that I am in Wales, at Milford Haven. Follow whatever your own love advises you from this. So wishes you all happiness he who remains loyal to his vow, and grows in love for you, Leonatus Posthumus.\""],[7,17,"Oh, for a horse with wings! Do you hear, Pisanio? He's at Milford Haven. Read it, and tell me how far it is there. If someone on ordinary business can plod it in a week, why can't I glide there in a day? So, faithful Pisanio, who longs like me to see your lord, who longs (oh, let me take that back) not like me, but still longs, in a fainter way (oh, not like me, because mine is beyond beyond!), tell me, and speak fast (love's adviser should cram the ears till they choke), how far it is to this blessed Milford."],[18,27,"And while you're at it, tell me how Wales was lucky enough to own such a harbour. But first of all, how we can slip away from here, and how to excuse the gap our absence will leave between going and coming back. But first, how to get away. Why invent an excuse before there's anything to excuse? We'll talk about that later. Please tell me how many miles we can ride in an hour."]],"482":[[0,5,"Now for our mountain hunting. Up that hill: your legs are young. I'll walk these flat lands. When you look down and see me as small as a crow, remember that it's position that makes things look small or great, and then you can think over the stories I've told you about courts, princes, and the tricks of war."],[6,11,"Service isn't valued for being done, but for being recognised. Thinking like this, we can learn from everything we see, and we'll often find, to our comfort, that the beetle in its shell is safer than the eagle with its full wings."],[12,16,"Oh, this life is nobler than waiting on a great man for a scolding, richer than doing nothing for a bribe, prouder than rustling in unpaid-for silk: such a man gets a bow from the tailor who dresses him, but never pays his bill. There's no life like ours!"]],"484":[[0,4,"What will we talk about when we're as old as you? When we hear rain and wind beating against dark December, how will we pass the freezing hours talking in this cramped cave?"],[5,9,"We've seen nothing. We're like animals: cunning as the fox for prey, fierce as the wolf for what we eat. Our courage is to chase what runs away. We make our cage a choir, like a caged bird, and sing freely about our captivity."]],"485":[[0,11,"How you talk! If you only knew the city's money-lending and had felt it yourself; the skill of the court, as hard to leave as to keep, where climbing to the top means certain falling, or the top's so slippery that the fear is as bad as the fall; the hard work of war, a pain that seems only to look for danger in the name of fame and honour, which dies in the search and as often gets a slanderous epitaph as a record of good deeds; no, often gets punished for doing well; and what's worse, has to bow to the judgment."],[12,20,"Oh, boys, the world can read this story in me. My body is marked by Roman swords, and my reputation was once among the very best. Cymbeline loved me, and whenever soldiers were talked of, my name wasn't far off. Then I was like a tree whose branches bent with fruit; but in one night a storm, or a robbery, call it what you like, shook down my ripe fruit, no, my leaves too, and left me bare to the weather."]],"487":[[0,8,"My only fault, as I've often told you, was that two villains, whose lying oaths beat my perfect honour, swore to Cymbeline that I was in league with the Romans. So I was banished, and for twenty years this rock and these lands have been my world. Here I've lived in honest freedom and paid more devout debts to heaven than in all the earlier part of my life."],[9,13,"But up to the mountains! This isn't hunters' talk. Whoever kills the deer first will be lord of the feast, and the other two will serve him. And we won't fear poison, which waits in places of greater state. I'll meet you in the valleys."]],"489":[[0,7,"How hard it is to hide the sparks of nature! These boys have no idea they're the King's sons, and Cymbeline doesn't dream they're alive. They think they're mine; and though they've been brought up so humbly in this cave where they have to stoop, their thoughts hit the roofs of palaces, and nature prompts them, even in simple, humble things, to act far more like princes than others do."],[8,19,"This Polydore, the heir of Cymbeline and Britain, whom the King his father named Guiderius: by Jove! When I sit on my three-legged stool and tell of my brave deeds in war, his spirit flies into my story. When I say, \"So my enemy fell, and so I put my foot on his neck,\" at once the royal blood flows into his cheek; he sweats, strains his young muscles, and takes up a stance that acts out my words. The younger brother, Cadwal, once Arviragus, brings my story to life in the same way, and shows even more of his own imagination. Listen, the game is up!"],[20,28,"Oh, Cymbeline, heaven and my conscience know you banished me unjustly! And so I stole these babies at three and two years old, meaning to rob you of heirs as you robbed me of my lands. Euriphile, you were their nurse; they thought you were their mother, and they honour her grave every day. Me, Belarius, now called Morgan, they think their real father. The game's up."]],"493":[[0,9,"You told me, when we got off the horses, that the place was close by. My mother never longed to see me for the first time as much as I long now. Pisanio! Man! Where is Posthumus? What's on your mind that makes you stare like that? Why does that sigh break from deep inside you? Someone painted looking like that would be taken for a person confused beyond explaining himself. Look less frightened, before wild fear overcomes my calmer senses. What's the matter?"],[10,17,"Why do you hand me that paper with such an unkind look? If it's summer news, smile first; if it's wintry, you only need to keep that face. My husband's handwriting? That poison-cursed Italy has outwitted him, and he's in some terrible trouble. Speak, man. Your tongue may soften some disaster that would kill me to read."]],"499":[[0,4,"I, false? Your conscience is my witness! Iachimo, when you accused him of lust, you looked like a villain; now I think your face looks honest enough. Some flashy Italian bird, whose mother was her makeup, has seduced him."],[5,11,"Poor me, I'm stale, a dress gone out of fashion, and since I'm too rich to hang on the wall, I must be ripped up. Tear me to pieces! Oh, men's vows betray women! Because of your betrayal, husband, every good appearance will be thought to be put on for wickedness; not natural, but worn as bait for ladies."]],"501":[[0,6,"Truly honest men, because of what was said about false Aeneas, were thought false in his time, and Sinon's weeping brought shame on many holy tears and took pity away from real misery. So you, Posthumus, will taint all good men: the handsome and the gallant will be thought false and lying because of your great failure."],[7,15,"Come on, fellow, be honest: do what your master orders. When you see him, testify a little to my obedience. Look, I'll draw the sword myself. Take it, and strike the innocent house of my love, my heart. Don't be afraid; it's empty of everything but grief. Your master isn't there, who used to be its whole treasure. Do as he says; strike. You may be brave in a better cause, but right now you look like a coward."]],"503":[[0,10,"Why, I must die, and if it isn't by your hand, you're no servant of your master's. There's a law against suicide so holy that it makes my weak hand a coward. Come, here's my heart. Something's in front of it. Wait, wait! We'll have no protection, obedient as a scabbard. What's this? The scriptures of the loyal Leonatus, all turned to heresy? Away, away, corrupters of my faith! You'll be no armour for my heart any more."],[11,21,"That's how poor fools believe false teachers; and though the betrayed feel the treason sharply, the traitor is in a worse state of misery. And you, Posthumus, who made me disobey the King my father and scorn the proposals of princes, will find later that it was no ordinary act but a rare one; and it grieves me to think how, when you've grown tired of the woman you now feed on, your memory will be tormented by me."],[22,24,"Please, get it over with. The lamb begs the butcher. Where's your knife? You're too slow to do your master's bidding, when I want it too."]],"520":[[0,5,"I'm very glad you're thinking of somewhere else. The ambassador, Lucius the Roman, comes to Milford Haven tomorrow. Now, if you could make your mind as dark as your fortune, and disguise what you are, which can't show itself yet without danger to you,"],[6,11,"you could take a path that's promising and full of prospects; yes, perhaps near where Posthumus is living; so close, at least, that even if you couldn't see what he does, news would bring him to your ear every hour as truly as he moves."]],"522":[[0,6,"Well then, here's the point. You must forget you're a woman: change commanding into obeying, and fear and delicacy, the servants of all women, or more truly, woman's own pretty self, into a cheeky courage; quick with jokes, quick with answers, saucy, and as quarrelsome as a weasel."],[7,12,"No, you must forget that rarest treasure, your complexion, and expose it (oh, how hard-hearted! but there's no help for it) to the greedy touch of the sun, who kisses everyone, and forget the elaborate, dainty clothes that made great Juno jealous."]],"524":[[0,5,"First, just make yourself look like one. Foreseeing this, I've already got ready (it's in my cloak-bag) a doublet, hat, breeches, everything that goes with them. If you'd dress in them and, with whatever imitation you can borrow from a boy of that age,"],[6,12,"present yourself to noble Lucius, ask to serve him, tell him what you're good at (and he'll find that out if he has any ear for music), he'll surely take you on gladly, because he's honourable and, more than that, very religious. As for your money while abroad, you have me, who has plenty, and I'll never fail to provide what you need now and later."]],"558":[[0,4,"Pisanio, you who stand up so for Posthumus! He has a drug of mine. I pray his absence is because he swallowed it, since he thinks it's very precious. But as for her, where has she gone?"],[5,9,"Maybe despair has taken hold of her, or, carried on the wings of her passionate love, she's flown to her beloved Posthumus. She's gone to death or to dishonour, and my purposes can make good use of either. With her out of the way, the British crown is mine to place."]],"564":[[0,4,"I love her and hate her. I love her because she's beautiful and royal, and has every courtly grace more exquisitely than any lady, all ladies, or womankind. She has the best of each of them and, made up of them all, outshines them all. That's why I love her."],[5,10,"But scorning me and throwing her favours on that low Posthumus so disgraces her judgment that her other rare qualities are choked. On that point I've decided to hate her; no, in fact, to get revenge on her. For when fools shall..."]],"589":[[0,5,"Meet him at Milford Haven! I forgot to ask him one thing; I'll remember it in a moment. Right there, you villain Posthumus, I'll kill you. I wish those clothes would come. She once said (I belch the bitterness of it up from my heart now) that she valued Posthumus's mere clothes more than my noble person and all my fine qualities."],[6,13,"With that suit on my back I'll rape her: but first kill him, in front of her. There she'll see my courage, which will then torment her scorn. With him on the ground and my insults spoken over his dead body, and when my lust has dined (which, as I say, I'll do in the clothes she praised so much, to spite her), I'll beat her back to court and kick her home. She's taken pleasure in scorning me, and I'll be merry in my revenge."]],"601":[[0,7,"I can see a man's life is a hard one. I've tired myself out, and for two nights running I've slept on the ground. I'd be ill, except my determination keeps me going. Milford, when Pisanio showed you to me from the mountain top, you were in sight. Oh, Jove! I think places of refuge run away from the wretched; I mean the places where they should get help."],[8,13,"Two beggars told me I couldn't miss my way. Would poor people lie, when they're suffering and know it's a punishment or a test? Yes; no wonder, when rich people hardly tell the truth. To sin when you have plenty is worse than lying out of need, and lying is worse in kings than in beggars."],[14,21,"My dear lord! You're one of the false ones. Now that I think of you my hunger's gone, though just before I was about to collapse for lack of food. But what's this? Here's a path to it; it's some wild man's den. I'd better not call; I don't dare call. But hunger, before it overthrows nature completely, makes it brave. Plenty and peace breed cowards; hardship is always the mother of hardiness."],[22,26,"Hello! Who's here? If you're anything civilised, speak; if you're savage, take from me or lend to me. Hello! No answer? Then I'll go in. I'd better draw my sword; and if my enemy fears a sword as much as I do, he'll hardly dare look at it. Give me such an enemy, good heavens!"]],"637":[[0,5,"This is the gist of the Emperor's order: since the common soldiers are now fighting the Pannonians and Dalmatians, and the legions now in France are too weak to take on our war against the rebel Britons,"],[6,9,"we are to call on the gentlemen to take up this business. He makes Lucius proconsul, and to you tribunes he gives full authority for this immediate call-up. Long live Caesar!"]],"646":[[0,10,"I'm near the place where they're supposed to meet, if Pisanio's map is accurate. How well his clothes fit me! Why shouldn't his lady, who was made by the same God who made the tailor, fit me too? All the more since (pardon the expression) they say a woman's fitness comes in fits. I'll have to work at that. I can say it to myself, because it isn't vanity for a man to consult his mirror in his own room: I mean, my body is as well drawn as his: no less young, stronger, not below him in fortune, ahead of him in present favour, above him in birth, just as experienced in battle, and more outstanding in single combat."],[11,20,"And yet this undiscerning thing loves him to spite me. What a thing human life is! Posthumus, your head, which is now growing on your shoulders, will be off within the hour; your lady raped; your clothes cut to pieces in front of her; and when all that's done, I'll kick her home to her father, who may be a little angry at my rough treatment; but my mother, who can control his temper, will turn it all into praise of me. My horse is tied up safely. Out, sword, to a cruel purpose! Fortune, put them into my hands. This is exactly the place described for their meeting, and the fellow wouldn't dare deceive me."]],"654":[[0,4,"I'm not so very ill, yet I'm not well; but I'm not such a spoilt city boy as to act as if I'm dying before I'm sick. Please, leave me; keep to your daily routine. Breaking a habit breaks everything."],[5,9,"I'm ill, but your being with me can't cure me. Company is no comfort to someone not in the mood for it. I'm not very ill, since I can reason about it. Please trust me here. I'll rob no one but myself, and let me die if I steal that poorly."]],"714":[[0,6,"We can't see a single soul. But common sense says he must have some attendants. Though his mood was nothing but changes, and those from one bad thing to worse, not even frenzy or total madness could have driven him to come here alone."],[7,15,"Perhaps it's been heard at court that people like us live in caves here, hunt here, are outlaws, and might one day gather a stronger force; and hearing that, he might, as is just like him, burst out swearing he'd bring us in. But it's not likely he'd come alone, either that he'd undertake it or that they'd allow it. So we have good reason to fear that this body has a tail more dangerous than its head."]],"724":[[0,7,"Oh, goddess, divine Nature, how you display yourself in these two princely boys! They're as gentle as the breeze blowing under the violet without even stirring its sweet head; and yet, when their royal blood is heated, as rough as the wildest wind that seizes the mountain pine by its top and bends it to the valley."],[8,14,"It's a wonder that an invisible instinct should shape them to royalty without learning it, honour without teaching, courtesy they've never seen in others, and courage that grows wild in them but yields a crop as if it had been sown. Still, it's strange what Cloten's coming here means for us, or what his death will bring us."]],"740":[[0,6,"With the loveliest flowers, while summer lasts and I live here, Fidele, I'll sweeten your sad grave. You won't lack the flower that's like your face, the pale primrose, nor the blue harebell, like your veins; no, nor the sweet-briar leaf, which, to be fair to it, was no sweeter than your breath."],[7,11,"The robin, with its charitable beak (oh, a beak that shames those rich heirs who leave their fathers without a monument!), would bring you all this; yes, and furry moss as well, when there are no flowers, to cover your body against the winter..."]],"773":[[0,9,"These flowers are like the pleasures of the world; this bloody man is like its troubles. I hope I'm dreaming, because I thought I was a cave-keeper and cook to honest people. But it's not so; it was just an arrow of nothing shot at nothing, the kind of thing the brain makes out of vapours. Our very eyes are sometimes blind, like our judgment. Truly, I'm still trembling with fear; but if there's still a drop of pity left in heaven, as small as a wren's eye, you feared gods, give me some of it!"],[10,16,"The dream is still here. Even now that I'm awake it's outside me as well as inside: not imagined, but felt. A headless man? Posthumus's clothes? I know the shape of his leg. This is his hand, his foot as swift as Mercury's, his thigh strong as Mars's, his Herculean muscles; but his face, like Jove's... Murder in heaven? What? It's gone!"],[17,24,"Pisanio, may all the curses mad Hecuba threw at the Greeks, and mine as well, strike you! You, conspiring with that lawless devil Cloten, have killed my lord here. From now on let writing and reading be treacherous! Damned Pisanio, with his forged letters (damned Pisanio!), has knocked the mainmast off the bravest ship in the world."],[25,29,"Oh, Posthumus! Alas, where's your head? Where is it? Oh, where is it? Pisanio could have stabbed you in the heart and left this head on. How could this happen? Pisanio? It's him and Cloten: malice and greed in them have caused this sorrow here. Oh, it's clear, clear!"],[30,36,"The drug he gave me, which he said was precious and restoring, didn't I find it deadening to the senses? That proves it completely. This is Pisanio's work, and Cloten's. Oh! Let me colour my pale cheek with your blood, so we'll look the more horrible to whoever finds us. Oh, my lord, my lord!"]],"793":[[0,2,"Yes, good boy, and I'll be more your father than your master. My friends,"],[3,9,"the boy has taught us our duty as men. Let's find the prettiest daisy-covered patch we can and dig him a grave with our pikes and spears. Come, pick him up. Boy, your loyalty has recommended him to us, and he'll be buried as well as soldiers can manage. Cheer up; wipe your eyes. Some falls are the way to rise more happily."]],"799":[[0,6,"A fever from the absence of her son; a madness that endangers her life. Heavens, how deeply you strike me all at once! Imogen, the greater part of my comfort, gone; my queen on her deathbed, and at a time when terrible wars are aimed at me; her son gone, who's so needed right now."],[7,10,"It strikes me beyond all hope of comfort. But you, fellow, who must know about her leaving and seem so ignorant: we'll force it out of you with sharp torture."]],"808":[[0,5,"I've had no letter from my master since I wrote to him that Imogen was killed. It's strange. And I don't hear from my mistress either, who promised to send me news often. Nor do I know what's happened to Cloten. I'm puzzled by everything. The heavens must still be at work."],[6,10,"Where I'm false I'm honest; I'm untrue in order to be true. These wars will find that I love my country, even so the King notices, or I'll die in them. All my other doubts, let time clear them up: fortune brings some boats into harbour that nobody steers."]],"819":[[0,3,"Oh, many people in the army know me. Many years, as you saw, didn't wear Cloten out of my memory, though he was only young then."],[4,9,"And besides, the King hasn't deserved my service or your love, since my exile has left you without education and certain of this hard life, forever without hope of the courtly manners your birth promised, and always the sunburnt children of hot summer and the shivering slaves of winter."]],"821":[[0,6,"By this shining sun, I'll go there. What kind of man am I, never to have seen a man die! I've hardly ever seen blood except from cowardly hares, lusty goats and deer! I've never ridden a horse, except one whose rider was like me and never wore spurs or iron on his heel!"],[7,9,"I'm ashamed to look at the holy sun and enjoy its blessed rays, while staying so long a poor nobody."]],"828":[[0,4,"Yes, bloody cloth, I'll keep you, because I wished you to be coloured like this. You married men, if each of you followed my course, how many would murder wives much better than themselves for straying just a little!"],[5,10,"Oh, Pisanio! A good servant doesn't obey every order; he's only bound to obey just ones. Gods! If you'd taken vengeance on my faults, I'd never have lived to do this. You'd have saved the noble Imogen to repent, and struck me, the wretch who deserved your vengeance more."],[11,16,"But alas, you take some people away for small faults; that's love, so they don't sin again. Others you allow to pile sin on sin, each worse than the last, and make them dread it, for their own good. But Imogen is yours now. Do what you will, and make me blessed by obeying."],[17,26,"I've been brought here among the Italian gentlemen to fight against my lady's kingdom. It's enough, Britain, that I've killed your princess. Peace! I won't wound you. So, good heavens, listen patiently to my plan. I'll strip off these Italian clothes and dress myself like a British peasant. That way I'll fight against the side I came with; that way I'll die for you, Imogen, for whom every breath of my life is a death."],[27,32,"And so, unknown, neither pitied nor hated, I'll throw myself in the face of danger. Let me show men more courage in me than my clothes suggest. Gods, give me the strength of the Leonati! To shame the fashion of the world, I'll start a new one: less on the outside and more within."]],"832":[[0,6,"The weight of guilt in my heart takes away my manhood. I've slandered a lady, the princess of this country, and its very air weakens me in revenge. Otherwise, could this peasant, one of nature's drudges, have beaten me at my own profession? Knighthoods and honours worn as I wear mine are only titles of scorn."],[7,9,"If your gentlemen, Britain, are as much better than this lout as he is better than our lords, then the difference is that we're hardly men and you are gods."]],"847":[[0,4,"No blame to you, sir, because all was lost if the heavens hadn't fought for us. The King himself was cut off from his wings, the army broken, and nothing to be seen but the backs of Britons, all fleeing through a narrow lane."],[5,10,"The enemy, full of spirit, tongues hanging out from slaughter, with more work than tools to do it, struck some down fatally, some lightly wounded, some fell from pure fear, so that the narrow way was blocked with dead men wounded in the back and cowards who lived only to die in long shame."]],"849":[[0,9,"Right by the battlefield, with a ditch and turf walls. It gave the advantage to an old soldier, an honest one, I'd swear, who deserved the long life his white beard showed by doing this for his country. Across the lane he, with two youngsters (lads who looked more fit to play country games than to do such slaughter, with faces fit for masks, or fairer than faces covered to keep them pale or hide shame), held the passage and shouted to those running away:"],[10,14,"\"Our Britain's deer die running, not our men. Souls that run backwards fly to darkness! Stand, or we'll act like Romans and give you the death like beasts that you're running from like beasts, and which you can avoid only by turning round to face them. Stand, stand!\""],[15,25,"These three, with the confidence of three thousand, and as good as that many in action (because three who act are a whole army when all the rest do nothing), with this cry of \"Stand, stand!\", helped by the place, and even more inspiring by their own nobility, which could have turned a spinning stick into a lance, brought colour back to pale faces, partly from shame, partly from renewed spirit. Some who had become cowards only by example (oh, a sin in war, damnable in those who start it) began to look the way the three did and to snarl like lions at the hunters' spears."],[26,31,"Then the pursuers paused, then retreated; soon there was a rout, thick confusion. At once they ran like chickens the way they had swooped like eagles; they ran back like slaves over the ground they'd crossed as victors. And now our cowards, like scraps of food saved on hard voyages, became the lifesavers in the crisis."],[32,37,"Finding the back door of the unguarded hearts open, heavens, how they struck! Some at those already dead, some at the dying, some at their own friends knocked down in the first wave. Ten who'd been chased by one now each became the butcher of twenty. Those who'd have died rather than resist became the deadly terrors of the field."]],"856":[[0,9,"Still running? That's a lord! Oh, noble wretch, to be on the battlefield and ask me \"What's the news?\" Today how many would have given their honour to save their skins, ran for it, and still died! I, protected by my own misery, couldn't find death where I heard him groaning, or feel him where he struck. Being such an ugly monster, it's strange he hides himself in fresh cups of wine, soft beds, sweet words, or has more servants there than we who draw his knives in war."],[10,19,"Well, I'll find him. Since I've been fighting for the Britons, I'll be a Briton no more; I'll take up again the part I came in. I won't fight any more, but surrender to the lowest peasant who touches my shoulder. Great is the slaughter the Romans have made here; great must be the revenge the Britons take. For me, death will be my ransom. I came to spend my life on either side, and I won't keep it here or take it home again, but end it somehow for Imogen."]],"869":[[0,5,"Welcome, imprisonment! Because I think you're a way to freedom. Still, I'm better off than a man sick with gout, since he'd rather groan like that forever than be cured by the sure doctor, death, who holds the key to unlock these locks."],[6,10,"My conscience, you're chained more tightly than my ankles and wrists. Good gods, give me the tool of penitence to pick that lock, and then I'm free for ever! Is it enough that I'm sorry? That's how children calm their earthly fathers; gods are more full of mercy."],[11,18,"If I must repent, I can't do it better than in chains I want rather than chains forced on me. If making amends is the main part of my freedom, take nothing less from me than my all. I know you're more merciful than vile men, who take a third, a sixth, a tenth from their bankrupt debtors and let them recover on what's left. That's not what I want."],[19,26,"For Imogen's precious life take mine; and though it's not as precious, still it's a life: you minted it. Between man and man they don't weigh every coin; even if they're light, they accept them for the image stamped on them. Take mine all the more because it's yours. And so, great powers, if you'll accept this account, take this life and cancel these cold chains. Oh, Imogen! I'll speak to you in silence. (He sleeps.)"]],"871":[[0,4,"Thunder-master, stop showing your spite to mortal flies. Quarrel with Mars, argue with Juno, who scolds you for your affairs and pays you back for them."],[5,12,"Has my poor boy, whose face I never saw, done anything but good? I died while he was still in the womb waiting to be born. You, who are said to be the father of orphans, should have been his father then and shielded him from this earthly pain."]],"884":[[0,7,"No more, you petty spirits from the lower world; stop offending my ears. Quiet! How dare you ghosts accuse the Thunderer, whose bolt, as you know, hurled from the sky, smashes every rebellious coast? Poor shadows from Elysium, go back and rest on your banks of flowers that never fade. Don't trouble yourselves with mortal matters: they're not your concern; you know they're ours."],[8,15,"I test those I love best, to make my gift more delightful for being delayed. Be content. My godhead will raise up your fallen son. His comforts will grow; his trials are nearly over. My star, Jupiter, ruled at his birth, and he was married in my temple. Rise and vanish! He'll be husband of Lady Imogen, and much happier for his suffering."],[16,20,"Lay this tablet on his breast; in it I've set out his whole fortune. And so, go; don't show your impatience with any more noise, or you'll stir up mine. Rise, eagle, to my crystal palace. (He ascends.)"]],"888":[[0,9,"(Waking) Sleep, you've been a grandfather and given me a father, and you've created a mother and two brothers. But oh, the mockery: gone! They went as soon as they were born. And so I'm awake. Poor wretches who depend on great men's favour dream as I have done, and wake to find nothing. But alas, I'm going wrong: many don't dream of finding it, or deserve it, and are still soaked in favours. So am I, who have this golden chance and don't know why."],[10,14,"What fairies haunt this place? A book? Oh, a fine one! Don't be, like our showy world, a garment nobler than what it covers. Let what you do be as good as what you promise, quite unlike our courtiers."],[15,20,"(Reading) \"When a lion's cub, unknown to himself, shall find without searching, and be embraced by a piece of tender air; and when from a stately cedar branches are cut which, after being dead for many years, revive, are joined to the old trunk, and grow fresh again; then Posthumus will end his miseries, and Britain will be fortunate and flourish in peace and plenty.\""],[21,26,"It's still a dream, or else the kind of thing madmen say without thinking; either both or nothing; either senseless talk or talk that sense can't untangle. Whatever it is, the course of my life is like it, so I'll keep it, if only because it matches me."]],"894":[[0,7,"A heavy bill for you, sir. But the comfort is, you'll be asked to pay nothing more, and needn't fear any more tavern bills, which are often as sad at parting time as they were cheerful at the start. You come in faint with hunger and leave reeling with too much drink, sorry you've paid too much and sorry you've been paid out too much; purse and brain both empty, the brain heavier for being too light, the purse too light for being emptied of its weight."],[8,11,"Oh, you'll be free of this contradiction now. Oh, the charity of a penny rope! It settles thousands in an instant. It's your only true accountant, settling everything past, present and to come. Your neck, sir, is pen, ledger and counters; so the receipt follows."]],"928":[[0,7,"More, sir, and worse. She confessed she had a deadly mineral poison for you which, once taken, would feed on your life minute by minute and waste you away by inches. Meanwhile she planned, by sitting up with you, weeping, nursing and kissing, to win you over with her show, and in time, when she'd worked on you with her cunning, to get her son named heir to the crown."],[8,12,"But when her plan failed because of his strange absence, she grew shamelessly desperate, revealed her plans in defiance of heaven and men, regretted that the evils she'd hatched hadn't been carried out, and so died in despair."]],"934":[[0,6,"Consider, sir, the fortunes of war. The day was yours by chance. If it had gone our way, we wouldn't have threatened our prisoners with the sword once our blood had cooled. But since the gods want it this way, that nothing but our lives will pay our ransom, let it come. It's enough that a Roman can suffer with a Roman's heart."],[7,13,"Augustus lives to think about it; and that's enough about my own concerns. There's just one thing I'll beg: my boy, a Briton by birth, let him be ransomed. No master ever had a page so kind, so dutiful, hard-working, so attentive to his needs, so loyal, so skilful, so like a nurse."],[14,17,"Let his virtue join with my request, which I'm bold enough to think your Highness can't refuse. He's done no Briton any harm, though he's served a Roman. Save him, sir, and spare no other blood."]],"965":[[0,7,"Once (unlucky was the clock that struck that hour!) in Rome (cursed be the house where it happened!) at a feast (oh, I wish our food had been poisoned, or at least what I lifted to my mouth!), the good Posthumus (what can I say? He was too good to be among bad men, and the best of all among the rarest good ones) was sitting sadly,"],[8,15,"listening to us praise our Italian sweethearts: for beauty that left the boldest boasts of the best speaker empty; for figure, that put to shame the statue of Venus or upright Minerva, shapes beyond what short-lived nature can make; for character, a store of every quality men love women for; and besides that, the hook that catches husbands, beauty that strikes the eye..."]],"967":[[0,5,"I'll get there all too soon, unless you want to grieve quickly. This Posthumus, like a noble lord in love and one who had a royal sweetheart, took his cue, and without criticising those we praised (in that he was as calm as virtue itself),"],[6,9,"he began to paint a picture of his lady. Once his words had drawn it and given it a mind, either our boasts were about kitchen sluts, or his description proved us tongue-tied fools."]],"969":[[0,7,"Your daughter's chastity: that's where it begins. He spoke of her as if Diana had hot dreams and she alone were cold. At that I, wretch that I am, doubted his praise, and bet him gold against this ring, which he wore then on his honoured finger, that I would win my way into her bed and win this ring through her adultery and mine."],[8,12,"He, a true knight, no less confident of her honour than I truly found her, staked this ring; and he would have done so if it had been a jewel from the sun god's chariot wheel, and could have done so safely if it had been worth the whole chariot."],[13,19,"Off to Britain I hurried with this plan. You may well remember me at court, sir, where your chaste daughter taught me the wide difference between a lover and a villain. My hope put out, though not my desire, my Italian brain began to work in your duller Britain, most vilely, but to my great advantage."],[20,30,"To be brief, my scheme succeeded so well that I came back with enough fake proof to drive the noble Leonatus mad, wounding his faith in her good name with such and such tokens: a detailed description of the hangings and pictures in her room, this bracelet of hers (oh, the trick by which I got it!), yes, even secret marks on her body, so that he couldn't help thinking her bond of chastity quite broken and that I'd taken the forfeit. Whereupon (I think I see him now)..."]],"970":[[0,8,"(Stepping forward) Yes, so you do, Italian devil! Oh, I'm the most gullible fool, a monstrous murderer, thief, anything that's due to all villains past, present and to come! Oh, give me a rope, or a knife, or poison, some honest judge! You, King, send for ingenious torturers. I'm the one who makes all the hateful things on earth look better by being worse than they are."],[9,12,"I am Posthumus, who killed your daughter. No, I lie like a villain: who made a lesser villain than myself, a sacrilegious thief, do it. She was the temple of virtue; yes, and virtue herself."],[13,18,"Spit on me, throw stones, throw mud at me, set the street dogs barking at me. Let every villain be called Posthumus Leonatus, and let villainy be less than it was! Oh, Imogen! My queen, my life, my wife! Oh, Imogen, Imogen, Imogen!"]],"986":[[0,4,"The Queen, sir, very often pestered me to mix poisons for her, always claiming she only wanted to increase her knowledge by killing worthless creatures like cats and dogs."],[5,9,"I was afraid her purpose was more dangerous, so I made her a certain substance which, when taken, would stop the powers of life for a while, but in a short time all the body's functions would work again as normal. Did you take any of it?"]],"998":[[0,8,"My lord, now that I've no fear, I'll tell the truth. When my lady went missing, Lord Cloten came to me with his sword drawn, foaming at the mouth, and swore that if I didn't reveal which way she'd gone, I'd die on the spot. By chance I had in my pocket a false letter from my master, which sent him to look for her in the mountains near Milford."],[9,13,"And there he rushed off in a frenzy, wearing my master's clothes, which he forced me to give him, with a lustful purpose and an oath to rape my lady. What happened to him after that I don't know."]],"1022":[[0,7,"As surely as you are your father's. I, old Morgan, am that Belarius you once banished. Your whim was my only offence, my punishment, and all my treason; what I suffered was all the harm I did. These gentle princes (for that's what they are) I've brought up for twenty years. They have all the skills I could teach them, and you know, sir, how I was brought up myself."],[8,15,"Their nurse, Euriphile, whom I married for the theft, stole these children when I was banished. I put her up to it, having already been punished for what I then did. Being beaten for loyalty drove me to treason. The more you felt their loss, the better it served my purpose in stealing them."],[16,20,"But, gracious sir, here are your sons again, and I must lose two of the sweetest companions in the world. May the blessing of the heavens above fall on their heads like dew, because they're worthy to set the sky with stars."]],"1033":[[0,11,"Oh, wonderful instinct! When will I hear it all? This rapid summary has branching details that should be told fully. Where? How did you live? And when did you come to serve our Roman prisoner? How did you part from your brothers? How did you first meet them? Why did you run away from court? And where to? These, and the reasons all three of you went to the battle, and I don't know how much more, should be asked, and all the other connected events, one chance after another. But neither the time nor the place suits our long questioning."],[12,18,"Look, Posthumus anchors himself to Imogen, and she, like harmless lightning, throws her eye at him, her brothers, me, her master, striking each with joy, and each returns it. Let's leave this ground and fill the temple with the smoke of our sacrifices. (To Belarius) You're my brother; we'll always think of you so."]],"1048":[[0,6,"(Reading) \"When a lion's cub, unknown to himself, shall find without searching, and be embraced by a piece of tender air; and when from a stately cedar branches are cut which, after being dead for many years, revive, are joined to the old trunk, and grow fresh again; then Posthumus will end his miseries, and Britain will be fortunate and flourish in peace and plenty.\""],[7,9,"You, Leonatus, are the lion's cub. The proper meaning of your name, Leo-natus, \"lion-born\", says as much."],[10,16,"(To Cymbeline) The piece of tender air is your virtuous daughter. In Latin we call tender air \"mollis aer\", and from \"mollis aer\" we get \"mulier\", woman. That woman, I believe, is this most faithful wife, who just now, fulfilling the words of the oracle, embraced him, unknown to you and unsought, as tender air."]],"1052":[[0,3,"The fingers of the powers above are tuning the harmony of this peace. The vision I told Lucius before this barely cooled battle began is fulfilled at this moment."],[4,10,"For the Roman eagle, soaring high on the wing from south to west, grew smaller and vanished in the sun's beams; which foretold that our princely eagle, imperial Caesar, would unite his favour again with the radiant Cymbeline, who shines here in the west."]],"1053":[[0,7,"Let's praise the gods, and let the curling smoke from our blessed altars climb to their nostrils. Let's announce this peace to all our subjects. Let's set out, with a Roman and a British flag waving together in friendship. So we'll march through London, and in the temple of great Jupiter we'll confirm our peace and seal it with feasts."],[8,9,"Set off! Never did a war end, before the bloody hands were even washed, with such a peace."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"love":["Love and marriage","Imogen and Posthumus, a forbidden match, a parting and a reunion."],"trust":["Trust and jealousy","The wager, the faked proof, and how quickly faith collapses."],"deceit":["Deception and disguise","Lies, poisons, trunks, false letters and borrowed clothes."],"nature":["Birth, nature and breeding","Royal blood, low birth, and whether worth shows through upbringing."],"britain":["Britain and Rome","The tribute, the war, and Britain as a world apart."],"gods":["Gods and providence","Prayers, prophecies, Jupiter, and a design behind chance."],"forgive":["Death, loss and forgiveness","Mourning the not-dead, remorse, and the final pardons."]},"quotes":[{"id":"7","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","text":"So fair an outward and such stuff within Endows a man but he.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Posthumus is introduced as perfect inside and out."},{"id":"11","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","text":"A child that guided dotards.","themes":["nature"],"note":"The orphan was wiser than the old men at court."},{"id":"11","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","text":"By her election may be truly read What kind of man he is.","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"Imogen's choice is the proof of Posthumus's worth."},{"id":"13","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","text":"from their nursery Were stol'n; and to this hour no guess in knowledge Which way they went.","themes":["nature","gods"],"note":"The lost princes are planted in the first scene."},{"id":"22","who":"QUEEN","text":"No, be assur'd you shall not find me, daughter, After the slander of most stepmothers, Evil-ey'd unto you.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"The wicked stepmother denies being one."},{"id":"26","who":"IMOGEN","text":"O dissembling courtesy! How fine this tyrant Can tickle where she wounds!","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Imogen sees through the Queen at once."},{"id":"26","who":"IMOGEN","text":"But that there is this jewel in the world That I may see again.","themes":["love"],"note":"Posthumus as a jewel: the play's language of value."},{"id":"27","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"I will remain The loyal'st husband that did e'er plight troth;","themes":["love","trust"],"note":"A vow he will keep in body but break in faith."},{"id":"27","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"And with mine eyes I'll drink the words you send, Though ink be made of gall.","themes":["love"],"note":"Love that would accept even bitter words."},{"id":"32","who":"IMOGEN","text":"This diamond was my mother's; take it, heart;","themes":["love"],"note":"The ring that becomes the stake in the wager."},{"id":"32","who":"IMOGEN","text":"But keep it till you woo another wife, When Imogen is dead.","themes":["love"],"note":"An ironic gift: he will soon believe she is false and order her death."},{"id":"35","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"It is a manacle of love; I'll place it Upon this fairest prisoner.","themes":["love"],"note":"The bracelet as a loving handcuff, later stolen."},{"id":"39","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"Thou basest thing, avoid; hence from my sight","themes":["nature"],"note":"The King judges Posthumus by his lack of rank."},{"id":"39","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"Thou'rt poison to my blood.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Royal blood must not be mixed with common."},{"id":"42","who":"IMOGEN","text":"There cannot be a pinch in death More sharp than this is.","themes":["love"],"note":"Parting as a kind of death."},{"id":"44","who":"IMOGEN","text":"I am senseless of your wrath; a touch more rare Subdues all pangs, all fears.","themes":["love"],"note":"Love outweighs her father's anger."},{"id":"48","who":"IMOGEN","text":"I chose an eagle, And did avoid a puttock.","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"Imogen judges by worth, not birth."},{"id":"50","who":"IMOGEN","text":"No; I rather added A lustre to it.","themes":["nature"],"note":"She claims Posthumus improves the throne."},{"id":"52","who":"IMOGEN","text":"It is your fault that I have lov'd Posthumus. You bred him as my playfellow,","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"Imogen blames her father's own upbringing of Posthumus."},{"id":"54","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Would I were A neat-herd's daughter, and my Leonatus Our neighbour shepherd's son!","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"The wish for a simple life where love is free."},{"id":"87","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"If it be a sin to make a true election, she is damn'd.","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"The Second Lord sides with Imogen."},{"id":"89","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"She shines not upon fools, lest the reflection should hurt her.","themes":["nature"],"note":"The Second Lord turns the insult round."},{"id":"107","who":"IMOGEN","text":"till he had melted from The smallness of a gnat to air, and then Have turn'd mine eye and wept.","themes":["love"],"note":"One of the play's tenderest images."},{"id":"109","who":"IMOGEN","text":"or I could make him swear The shes of Italy should not betray Mine interest and his honour;","themes":["trust"],"note":"Dramatic irony: the danger in Italy will be a man."},{"id":"109","who":"IMOGEN","text":"comes in my father, And like the tyrannous breathing of the north Shakes all our buds from growing.","themes":["love"],"note":"Cymbeline as a cold north wind."},{"id":"132","who":"FRENCHMAN","text":"more fair, virtuous, wise, chaste, constant, qualified, and less attemptable, than any the rarest of our ladies in France.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus has boasted about his lady before."},{"id":"134","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"She holds her virtue still, and I my mind.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus's confidence before the wager."},{"id":"137","who":"IACHIMO","text":"but I have not seen the most precious diamond that is, nor you the lady.","themes":["deceit","trust"],"note":"Iachimo begins to equate the woman with the jewel."},{"id":"142","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"the other is not a thing for sale, and only the gift of the gods.","themes":["love","gods"],"note":"Posthumus insists Imogen cannot be bought."},{"id":"145","who":"IACHIMO","text":"You may wear her in title yours; but you know strange fowl light upon neighbouring ponds.","themes":["deceit","trust"],"note":"Iachimo's cynical view of marriage."},{"id":"151","who":"IACHIMO","text":"But I make my wager rather against your confidence than her reputation;","themes":["trust","deceit"],"note":"Iachimo claims to test Posthumus, not Imogen."},{"id":"163","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"My mistress exceeds in goodness the hugeness of your unworthy thinking. I dare you to this match: here's my ring.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus's fatal acceptance."},{"id":"190","who":"CORNELIUS","text":"She is fool'd With a most false effect; and I the truer So to be false with her.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Cornelius is honest by being false."},{"id":"196","who":"QUEEN","text":"It is a thing I made, which hath the King Five times redeem'd from death.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"The lie that sends the drug to Imogen."},{"id":"202","who":"PISANIO","text":"But when to my good lord I prove untrue I'll choke myself—there's all I'll do for you.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Pisanio's loyalty in rhyme."},{"id":"206","who":"IMOGEN","text":"A father cruel and a step-dame false; A foolish suitor to a wedded lady That hath her husband banish'd.","themes":["love"],"note":"Imogen sums up her trap."},{"id":"206","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Blessed be those, How mean soe'er, that have their honest wills,","themes":["nature","love"],"note":"Low birth brings freedom to love."},{"id":"211","who":"IACHIMO","text":"She is alone th' Arabian bird, and I Have lost the wager.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Iachimo admits she may be unique."},{"id":"211","who":"IACHIMO","text":"Boldness be my friend! Arm me, audacity, from head to foot!","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Iachimo arms himself with nerve."},{"id":"238","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Since doubting things go ill often hurts more Than to be sure they do;","themes":["trust"],"note":"Imogen prefers certainty to suspicion."},{"id":"243","who":"IACHIMO","text":"Be reveng'd; Or she that bore you was no queen,","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Iachimo tries to turn Imogen's pride against her."},{"id":"248","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Thou wrong'st a gentleman who is as far From thy report as thou from honour;","themes":["trust","love"],"note":"Imogen defends Posthumus instantly."},{"id":"248","who":"IMOGEN","text":"If he shall think it fit A saucy stranger in his court to mart As in a Romish stew,","themes":["britain"],"note":"Britain against Italian corruption."},{"id":"249","who":"IACHIMO","text":"I have spoke this to know if your affiance Were deeply rooted,","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Iachimo's instant cover story."},{"id":"251","who":"IACHIMO","text":"He sits 'mongst men like a descended god:","themes":["nature"],"note":"Iachimo's flattery is also the truth."},{"id":"293","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"That such a crafty devil as is his mother Should yield the world this ass!","themes":["nature","deceit"],"note":"Mother and son contrasted."},{"id":"293","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"The heavens hold firm The walls of thy dear honour,","themes":["trust"],"note":"Prayer for Imogen at the moment of danger."},{"id":"303","who":"IMOGEN","text":"To your protection I commend me, gods. From fairies and the tempters of the night Guard me, beseech ye!","themes":["gods"],"note":"Her prayer is answered strangely."},{"id":"305","who":"IACHIMO","text":"Our Tarquin thus Did softly press the rushes ere he waken'd The chastity he wounded.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Iachimo casts himself as a rapist."},{"id":"305","who":"IACHIMO","text":"O sleep, thou ape of death, lie dull upon her!","themes":["deceit","forgive"],"note":"Sleep as an imitation of death, looking ahead to Act 4."},{"id":"307","who":"IACHIMO","text":"On her left breast A mole cinque-spotted, like the crimson drops I' th' bottom of a cowslip.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"The 'proof' that will destroy Posthumus's faith."},{"id":"307","who":"IACHIMO","text":"She hath been reading late The tale of Tereus; here the leaf's turn'd down Where Philomel gave up.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Ovid's rape story as a warning."},{"id":"307","who":"IACHIMO","text":"Though this a heavenly angel, hell is here.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Iachimo knows what he is."},{"id":"319","who":"MUSICIAN","text":"~Hark, hark! the lark at heaven's gate sings,","themes":["love"],"note":"A lovely song in a crude suitor's service."},{"id":"356","who":"IMOGEN","text":"I care not for you, And am so near the lack of charity To accuse myself I hate you;","themes":["love"],"note":"Imogen's honesty."},{"id":"357","who":"CLOTEN","text":"One bred of alms and foster'd with cold dishes, With scraps o' th' court","themes":["nature"],"note":"Cloten sneers at Posthumus's upbringing."},{"id":"360","who":"IMOGEN","text":"His mean'st garment That ever hath but clipp'd his body is dearer In my respect than all the hairs above thee,","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"The insult that drives Cloten's revenge."},{"id":"365","who":"IMOGEN","text":"I hope it be not gone to tell my lord That I kiss aught but he.","themes":["trust","love"],"note":"Innocent words that come true in a twisted way."},{"id":"382","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Our countrymen Are men more order'd than when Julius Caesar Smil'd at their lack of skill,","themes":["britain"],"note":"Posthumus's patriotism."},{"id":"401","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"If you can make't apparent That you have tasted her in bed, my hand And ring is yours.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus demands proof."},{"id":"411","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"the description Of what is in her chamber nothing saves The wager you have laid.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus still resists."},{"id":"419","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Let there be no honour Where there is beauty; truth where semblance; love Where there's another man.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus's faith collapses."},{"id":"423","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"she hath bought the name of whore thus dearly.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus condemns her."},{"id":"440","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"We are all bastards,","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus's despair spreads to all women."},{"id":"440","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"I thought her As chaste as unsunn'd snow.","themes":["love","trust"],"note":"The image of her purity."},{"id":"440","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Could I find out The woman's part in me!","themes":["trust"],"note":"He wants to tear out what he hates."},{"id":"448","who":"QUEEN","text":"which stands As Neptune's park, ribb'd and pal'd in With rocks unscalable and roaring waters,","themes":["britain"],"note":"The Queen's island defence speech."},{"id":"448","who":"QUEEN","text":"but made not here his brag Of 'came, and saw, and overcame.'","themes":["britain"],"note":"Caesar's defeat in Britain."},{"id":"452","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"Till the injurious Romans did extort This tribute from us, we were free.","themes":["britain"],"note":"Cymbeline's case for independence."},{"id":"464","who":"PISANIO","text":"O master, what a strange infection Is fall'n into thy ear!","themes":["trust","deceit"],"note":"Pisanio sees the poison at work."},{"id":"464","who":"PISANIO","text":"She's punish'd for her truth,","themes":["trust"],"note":"Pisanio's faith in Imogen."},{"id":"464","who":"PISANIO","text":"If it be so to do good service, never Let me be counted serviceable.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Service is not blind obedience."},{"id":"471","who":"IMOGEN","text":"O for a horse with wings!","themes":["love"],"note":"Imogen's joy, with the audience knowing better."},{"id":"475","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Accessible is none but Milford way.","themes":["love"],"note":"The road that leads to danger and to reunion."},{"id":"479","who":"BELARIUS","text":"Stoop, boys; this gate Instructs you how t' adore the heavens,","themes":["nature","gods"],"note":"The humble cave as a church."},{"id":"482","who":"BELARIUS","text":"shall we find The sharded beetle in a safer hold Than is the full-wing'd eagle.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Safety in lowliness."},{"id":"485","who":"BELARIUS","text":"Then was I as a tree Whose boughs did bend with fruit;","themes":["nature"],"note":"Belarius's fall."},{"id":"489","who":"BELARIUS","text":"How hard it is to hide the sparks of nature!","themes":["nature"],"note":"Royal blood shows through."},{"id":"489","who":"BELARIUS","text":"their thoughts do hit The roofs of palaces,","themes":["nature"],"note":"The princes are born to rule."},{"id":"496","who":"PISANIO","text":"No, 'tis slander, Whose edge is sharper than the sword,","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Slander as the real killer."},{"id":"497","who":"IMOGEN","text":"False to his bed? What is it to be false?","themes":["trust","love"],"note":"Imogen's bewildered innocence."},{"id":"499","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Men's vows are women's traitors!","themes":["trust"],"note":"Imogen's turn to anger."},{"id":"501","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Thy master is not there, who was indeed The riches of it.","themes":["love"],"note":"Her heart emptied of him."},{"id":"503","who":"IMOGEN","text":"The lamp entreats the butcher.","themes":["love"],"note":"The lamb begs for death."},{"id":"519","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Hath Britain all the sun that shines?","themes":["britain"],"note":"Imogen imagines life beyond Britain."},{"id":"519","who":"IMOGEN","text":"In a great pool a swan's nest.","themes":["britain"],"note":"Britain as a world apart."},{"id":"522","who":"PISANIO","text":"You must forget to be a woman;","themes":["deceit"],"note":"The disguise begins."},{"id":"525","who":"IMOGEN","text":"This attempt I am soldier to, and will abide it with A prince's courage.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Royal courage in disguise."},{"id":"558","who":"QUEEN","text":"Gone she is To death or to dishonour, and my end Can make good use of either.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"The Queen's ruthless ambition."},{"id":"589","who":"CLOTEN","text":"With that suit upon my back will I ravish her;","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Cloten's ugliest plan."},{"id":"597","who":"PISANIO","text":"Flow, flow, You heavenly blessings, on her!","themes":["trust","gods"],"note":"Pisanio's prayer."},{"id":"601","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Plenty and peace breeds cowards; hardness ever Of hardiness is mother.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Hardship makes courage."},{"id":"623","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Would it had been so that they Had been my father's sons!","themes":["nature"],"note":"Unknowing truth."},{"id":"652","who":"IMOGEN","text":"But clay and clay differs in dignity, Whose dust is both alike.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Rank in life, equality in death."},{"id":"657","who":"ARVIRAGUS","text":"Love's reason's without reason.","themes":["love","nature"],"note":"The brothers' instinctive love."},{"id":"658","who":"BELARIUS","text":"Cowards father cowards and base things sire base. Nature hath meal and bran, contempt and grace.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Belarius on breeding."},{"id":"662","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Th' imperious seas breed monsters; for the dish, Poor tributary rivers as sweet fish.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Small places produce good things too."},{"id":"698","who":"GUIDERIUS","text":"Those that I reverence, those I fear—the wise: At fools I laugh, not fear them.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Guiderius's courage."},{"id":"724","who":"BELARIUS","text":"They are as gentle As zephyrs blowing below the violet,","themes":["nature"],"note":"Royal nature in the boys."},{"id":"724","who":"BELARIUS","text":"valour That wildly grows in them, but yields a crop As if it had been sow'd.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Nature without nurture."},{"id":"735","who":"BELARIUS","text":"Thou diedst, a most rare boy, of melancholy.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Belarius's explanation."},{"id":"740","who":"ARVIRAGUS","text":"Thou shalt not lack The flower that's like thy face, pale primrose;","themes":["forgive"],"note":"The flower speech."},{"id":"747","who":"BELARIUS","text":"Our foe was princely; And though you took his life, as being our foe, Yet bury him as a prince.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Respect for rank even in death."},{"id":"755","who":"GUIDERIUS","text":"~Golden lads and girls all must, ~As chimney-sweepers, come to dust.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"The dirge's refrain."},{"id":"769","who":"BELARIUS","text":"The ground that gave them first has them again.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Earth to earth."},{"id":"773","who":"IMOGEN","text":"These flow'rs are like the pleasures of the world; This bloody man, the care on't.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Imogen wakes beside the body."},{"id":"773","who":"IMOGEN","text":"I know the shape of's leg; this is his hand,","themes":["deceit"],"note":"She mistakes Cloten for Posthumus."},{"id":"773","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Damn'd Pisanio Hath with his forged letters—damn'd Pisanio—","themes":["trust"],"note":"She blames the wrong man."},{"id":"791","who":"LUCIUS","text":"Thy name well fits thy faith, thy faith thy name.","themes":["trust"],"note":"Fidele means 'faithful'."},{"id":"793","who":"LUCIUS","text":"Some falls are means the happier to arise.","themes":["gods","forgive"],"note":"A motto for the play's design."},{"id":"808","who":"PISANIO","text":"Wherein I am false I am honest; not true, to be true.","themes":["trust","deceit"],"note":"Pisanio's paradox."},{"id":"808","who":"PISANIO","text":"Fortune brings in some boats that are not steer'd.","themes":["gods"],"note":"Chance as providence."},{"id":"824","who":"BELARIUS","text":"their blood thinks scorn Till it fly out and show them princes born.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Blood will out."},{"id":"828","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Yea, bloody cloth, I'll keep thee; for I wish'd Thou shouldst be colour'd thus.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Posthumus's remorse begins."},{"id":"828","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"You married ones, If each of you should take this course, how many Must murder wives much better than themselves For wrying but a little!","themes":["forgive","love"],"note":"He forgives before knowing the truth."},{"id":"828","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Every good servant does not all commands;","themes":["trust"],"note":"Posthumus approves Pisanio's (supposed) obedience too late."},{"id":"828","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Let me make men know More valour in me than my habits show.","themes":["nature"],"note":"The opposite of Cloten's use of clothes."},{"id":"832","who":"IACHIMO","text":"The heaviness and guilt within my bosom Takes off my manhood.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Iachimo's conscience."},{"id":"849","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"'Our Britain's harts die flying, not our men.","themes":["britain"],"note":"Belarius's rallying cry."},{"id":"851","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"'Two boys, an old man (twice a boy), a lane, Preserv'd the Britons, was the Romans' bane.'","themes":["britain"],"note":"Posthumus's bitter rhyme."},{"id":"869","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Most welcome, bondage! for thou art a way, I think, to liberty.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Prison as release."},{"id":"869","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"For Imogen's dear life take mine;","themes":["forgive","love"],"note":"Posthumus offers his life."},{"id":"876","who":"SICILIUS","text":"Why did you suffer Iachimo, Slight thing of Italy, To taint his nobler heart and brain With needless jealousy,","themes":["gods","trust"],"note":"The ghosts accuse Jupiter."},{"id":"884","who":"JUPITER","text":"Whom best I love I cross; to make my gift, The more delay'd, delighted.","themes":["gods"],"note":"Jupiter explains the suffering."},{"id":"884","who":"JUPITER","text":"He shall be lord of Lady Imogen, And happier much by his affliction made.","themes":["gods","love"],"note":"The promise of the ending."},{"id":"888","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"'Tis still a dream, or else such stuff as madmen Tongue, and brain not;","themes":["gods"],"note":"Posthumus puzzles over the tablet."},{"id":"924","who":"CORNELIUS","text":"Married your royalty, was wife to your place; Abhorr'd your person.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"The Queen's confession."},{"id":"927","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"Who is't can read a woman?","themes":["deceit"],"note":"Cymbeline learns too late."},{"id":"969","who":"IACHIMO","text":"the wide difference 'Twixt amorous and villainous.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"What Imogen taught him."},{"id":"970","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Ay me, most credulous fool, Egregious murderer,","themes":["forgive","trust"],"note":"Posthumus's self-condemnation."},{"id":"970","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"The temple Of virtue was she; yea, and she herself.","themes":["love"],"note":"Too late, he praises her."},{"id":"972","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Shall's have a play of this? Thou scornful page, There lies thy part.","themes":["deceit"],"note":"He strikes the unrecognised Imogen."},{"id":"977","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"If this be so, the gods do mean to strike me To death with mortal joy.","themes":["gods","forgive"],"note":"Joy as overwhelming as grief."},{"id":"990","who":"IMOGEN","text":"Why did you throw your wedded lady from you?","themes":["love","forgive"],"note":"Imogen's gentle reproach."},{"id":"991","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"Hang there like fruit, my soul, Till the tree die!","themes":["love"],"note":"The reunion."},{"id":"1007","who":"BELARIUS","text":"They were not born for bondage.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Belarius reveals royal blood."},{"id":"1022","who":"BELARIUS","text":"Beaten for loyalty Excited me to treason.","themes":["nature"],"note":"Belarius explains his crime."},{"id":"1022","who":"BELARIUS","text":"for they are worthy To inlay heaven with stars.","themes":["nature"],"note":"The princes praised."},{"id":"1028","who":"IMOGEN","text":"I have got two worlds by't.","themes":["love","forgive"],"note":"Imogen gives up a kingdom gladly."},{"id":"1033","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"Posthumus anchors upon Imogen;","themes":["love"],"note":"The reunited lovers."},{"id":"1041","who":"POSTHUMUS","text":"The pow'r that I have on you is to spare you; The malice towards you to forgive you. Live, And deal with others better.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Posthumus forgives Iachimo."},{"id":"1042","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"Pardon's the word to all.","themes":["forgive"],"note":"Cymbeline's general pardon."},{"id":"1048","who":"SOOTHSAYER","text":"Thou, Leonatus, art the lion's whelp;","themes":["gods"],"note":"The prophecy explained."},{"id":"1051","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"we submit to Caesar And to the Roman empire, promising To pay our wonted tribute,","themes":["britain"],"note":"Peace on Rome's terms."},{"id":"1052","who":"SOOTHSAYER","text":"The fingers of the pow'rs above do tune The harmony of this peace.","themes":["gods"],"note":"Providence behind the plot."},{"id":"1053","who":"CYMBELINE","text":"Never was a war did cease, Ere bloody hands were wash'd, with such a peace.","themes":["britain","forgive"],"note":"The final couplet."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Cymbeline, King of ancient Britain, is ruled by his second wife, the Queen. His daughter Imogen, heir to the throne since her two brothers were stolen as babies, has secretly married Posthumus Leonatus, an orphan brought up at court, instead of the Queen's oafish son Cloten. Posthumus is banished to Rome, where the Italian Iachimo bets him that he can seduce Imogen.","Iachimo fails, so he cheats: hidden in a trunk in Imogen's bedroom, he notes the room and a mole on her breast and steals the bracelet Posthumus gave her. Posthumus believes him and orders his servant Pisanio to kill her. Pisanio instead disguises Imogen as a boy, 'Fidele'. In Wales she is taken in by the banished lord Belarius and two young men who are, unknown to everyone, her lost brothers. Cloten follows her in Posthumus's clothes and is beheaded by Guiderius; Imogen, drugged by a potion the Queen meant as poison, wakes beside the headless body and believes it is her husband's.","Rome invades over unpaid tribute. Posthumus, repentant, fights for Britain in disguise, then gives himself up to die. Jupiter appears to him in prison with a riddling promise. In a final scene of more than twenty revelations Iachimo confesses, husband and wife are reunited, the princes are restored, the dead Queen's plots are exposed, Posthumus forgives Iachimo and Cymbeline makes peace with Rome."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Tragedy of Cymbeline, King of Britain (First Folio title); usually called Cymbeline"],["Written","About 1609 to 1610; first printed in the First Folio of 1623, as the last of its Tragedies"],["Genre","Tragedy (placed last among the First Folio's Tragedies); since the nineteenth century usually called a romance or tragicomedy, with a happy ending reached through near-tragedy"],["Setting","Ancient Britain in the reign of Cymbeline (around the time of Augustus Caesar), Rome, and the mountains of Wales near Milford Haven"],["Protagonist","Imogen, with Posthumus"],["Antagonists","Iachimo, the Queen and Cloten"],["Inciting incident","Posthumus accepts Iachimo's wager on Imogen's chastity (1.4)"],["Turning point","Iachimo convinces Posthumus with the bracelet and the mole, and Posthumus orders Imogen's death (2.4, 3.2)"],["Climax","Imogen wakes beside Cloten's headless body and takes it for Posthumus's (4.2)"],["Resolution","The chain of discoveries and pardons in 5.5"],["Time span","Several weeks, enough for journeys between Britain and Rome and the Roman army's arrival"],["Major conflicts","Imogen and Posthumus against the court; Iachimo's deceit against Imogen's faith; Britain against Rome; birth against worth"],["Themes","Love and marriage, trust and jealousy, deception and disguise, birth and nature, Britain and Rome, gods and providence, loss and forgiveness"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Posthumus is banished for marrying Imogen; they exchange ring and bracelet","id":32},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Cymbeline rages at Imogen for refusing Cloten","id":47},{"s":"a1s4","t":"In Rome, Iachimo and Posthumus agree the wager","id":163},{"s":"a1s5","t":"The Queen gets 'poison' from Cornelius, who secretly gives her a sleeping drug","id":190},{"s":"a1s5","t":"The Queen gives Pisanio the box as a cordial","id":196},{"s":"a1s6","t":"Iachimo slanders Posthumus; Imogen rejects him","id":248},{"s":"a1s6","t":"Iachimo asks Imogen to keep his trunk overnight","id":257},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Iachimo leaves the trunk, notes the room and the mole, and steals the bracelet","id":307},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Imogen tells Cloten Posthumus's meanest garment is worth more than he is","id":360},{"s":"a2s4","t":"Iachimo convinces Posthumus with the bracelet and the mole","id":426},{"s":"a2s5","t":"Posthumus rages against all women","id":440},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Cymbeline refuses Rome's tribute; Lucius declares war","id":455},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Pisanio reads the order to kill Imogen; Imogen sets out for Milford Haven","id":464},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Belarius reveals the princes' identity to the audience","id":489},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Pisanio shows Imogen the letter, refuses to kill her and disguises her as a boy","id":503},{"s":"a3s5","t":"Cloten takes Posthumus's clothes and sets off for Milford","id":589},{"s":"a3s6","t":"Fidele is welcomed at the cave by Belarius and the princes","id":620},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Imogen swallows the Queen's drug","id":662},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Guiderius kills Cloten","id":711},{"s":"a4s2","t":"The brothers mourn Fidele with 'Fear no more the heat o' th' sun'","id":755},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Imogen wakes beside the headless body and takes it for Posthumus","id":773},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Lucius takes Fidele as his page","id":791},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Posthumus repents and changes into British clothes","id":828},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Posthumus defeats Iachimo; the old man and two boys save the King","id":832},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Posthumus gives himself up as a Roman","id":856},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Jupiter appears to Posthumus and leaves a prophecy","id":884},{"s":"a5s5","t":"The Queen's death and confession are reported","id":924},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Iachimo confesses; Posthumus strikes the page, who is Imogen","id":972},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Belarius reveals Guiderius and Arviragus","id":1020},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Posthumus pardons Iachimo; Cymbeline makes peace with Rome","id":1051}],"irony":{"22":"The Queen promises not to be a wicked stepmother; she is trying to poison her stepdaughter.","32":"Imogen tells Posthumus to keep the ring until she is dead; he will lose it to Iachimo and believe her worse than dead.","109":"Imogen wanted Posthumus to swear the women of Italy would not tempt him; it is an Italian man who destroys his faith.","163":"Posthumus stakes the ring to prove his trust, and in doing so invites the test that breaks it.","196":"The Queen calls the box a cordial that has saved the King; it is a harmless drug she thinks is poison.","252":"Imogen gives Iachimo 'my pow'r i' th' court' moments after he tried to seduce her.","256":"Imogen offers to keep the trunk in her bedchamber because her husband has a share in it.","303":"Imogen prays to be guarded from 'tempters of the night' while one is hidden in her room.","365":"Imogen hopes her lost bracelet has not gone 'to tell my lord' anything false; that is exactly where it has gone.","471":"Imogen rejoices at a letter designed to lead her to her death.","623":"Imogen wishes the young men were her father's sons; they are.","662":"Imogen takes the Queen's drug as medicine; the Queen meant it as poison; it is neither.","711":"Guiderius proudly kills 'the Queen's son', his own stepbrother.","773":"Imogen mourns her husband's body; it is the man she most despised.","791":"Lucius praises Fidele's faithful name to a girl whose faith has been doubted by her husband.","828":"Posthumus forgives Imogen for a sin she never committed.","972":"Posthumus strikes the page in his grief for Imogen; the page is Imogen."},"devices":{"jewels":"Jewels, coins and value","clothes":"Clothes and disguise","nature":"Plants, birds and the natural world","myth":"Classical myth and history","sleep":"Sleep, death and dreams","body":"The body, wounds and blood"},"dev":{"26":[["jewels","Posthumus as the one jewel in the world.","this jewel in the world"]],"35":[["jewels","The bracelet as love's handcuff.","It is a manacle of love"]],"48":[["nature","Birds of prey rank husbands: noble eagle, scavenging kite.","I chose an eagle"]],"109":[["nature","Cymbeline's anger as a north wind killing spring buds.","Shakes all our buds from growing"]],"137":[["jewels","Iachimo compares Imogen to a diamond.","the most precious diamond that is"]],"145":[["nature","Wives as ponds visited by strange birds.","strange fowl light upon neighbouring ponds"]],"211":[["myth","Imogen as the phoenix, unique in the world.","th' Arabian bird"]],"217":[["body","Desire as a full yet leaking tub.","that tub Both fill'd and running"]],"243":[["body","Disease imagery for the women Posthumus supposedly visits.","diseas'd ventures"]],"305":[["myth","Iachimo compares himself to the rapist Tarquin.","Our Tarquin thus"],["sleep","Sleep as the ape of death.","O sleep, thou ape of death"]],"307":[["myth","Tereus and Philomel: Ovid's tale of rape.","The tale of Tereus"],["nature","The mole like drops in a cowslip.","like the crimson drops I' th' bottom of a cowslip"]],"360":[["clothes","Posthumus's meanest garment outweighs Cloten.","His mean'st garment"]],"419":[["myth","The ring as a basilisk that kills with a look.","It is a basilisk unto mine eye"]],"440":[["jewels","Men as counterfeit coins.","Made me a counterfeit"],["nature","Imogen as snow untouched by sun.","As chaste as unsunn'd snow"]],"448":[["nature","Britain as Neptune's park, fenced by rocks.","As Neptune's park"]],"482":[["nature","The safe beetle and the exposed eagle.","The sharded beetle in a safer hold"]],"485":[["nature","Belarius as a tree stripped in one night.","Then was I as a tree"]],"499":[["clothes","Imogen as an old-fashioned garment to be ripped up.","a garment out of fashion"]],"501":[["myth","Aeneas and Sinon as false men who spoiled trust for others.","like false Aeneas"]],"519":[["nature","Britain as a swan's nest in a pool.","In a great pool a swan's nest"]],"646":[["clothes","Cloten delights in how Posthumus's clothes fit.","How fit his garments serve me"]],"724":[["nature","The princes as gentle breezes and rough winds.","As zephyrs blowing below the violet"]],"735":[["sleep","Melancholy as a sea without a bottom.","Who ever yet could sound thy bottom"]],"740":[["nature","Flowers matched to Fidele's face, veins and breath.","pale primrose"]],"755":[["sleep","Death as going home with your wages paid.","Home art gone, and ta'en thy wages"]],"773":[["myth","Posthumus's body as a god's: Mercury, Mars, Hercules, Jove.","His foot Mercurial, his Martial thigh"],["body","Imogen smears her face with the dead man's blood.","Give colour to my pale cheek with thy blood"]],"828":[["clothes","He changes his Italian clothes for a peasant's.","I'll disrobe me Of these Italian weeds"]],"869":[["jewels","His life as a coin stamped by the gods.","'tis a life; you coin'd it"]],"991":[["nature","Imogen hangs on him like fruit on a tree.","Hang there like fruit"]],"1050":[["nature","Cymbeline as a cedar whose lopped branches regrow.","The lofty cedar"]]},"stage":{"palace":"Cymbeline's palace in Britain","garden":"The palace garden","house":"Philario's house in Rome","bed":"Imogen's bedchamber, with the trunk","street":"A public place","cave":"Belarius's cave in the Welsh mountains","haven":"The coast near Milford Haven","camp":"The Roman camp","field":"The battlefield","prison":"A British prison","tent":"Cymbeline's tent"},"stagerules":[["garden","garden"],["bedchamber","bed"],["philario","house"],["cave","cave"],["milford","haven"],["field","field"],["camp","camp"],["prison","prison"],["tent","tent"],["public","street"],["palace","palace"]],"place":{"a1s1":"The palace garden","a1s2":"A public place in Britain","a1s3":"Cymbeline's palace","a1s4":"Philario's house in Rome","a1s5":"Cymbeline's palace","a1s6":"Cymbeline's palace","a2s1":"Before the palace","a2s2":"Imogen's bedchamber","a2s3":"An antechamber by Imogen's rooms","a2s4":"Philario's house in Rome","a2s5":"Philario's house in Rome","a3s1":"A hall in the palace","a3s2":"Another room in the palace","a3s3":"Wales: the mountains and the cave","a3s4":"Wales, near Milford Haven","a3s5":"Cymbeline's palace","a3s6":"Wales: before the cave","a3s7":"A public place in Rome","a4s1":"Wales: near the cave","a4s2":"Wales: before the cave","a4s3":"Cymbeline's palace","a4s4":"Wales: before the cave","a5s1":"The Roman camp","a5s2":"The battlefield","a5s3":"Another part of the field","a5s4":"A prison","a5s5":"Cymbeline's tent"},"arc":{"Imogen's danger":[3,2,2,3,4,5,4,8,5,6,6,5,7,9,7,7,6,6,8,9,6,6,6,6,6,6,3],"Iachimo's deceit":[0,0,0,4,4,7,5,9,6,10,10,9,9,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,5,6,7,1]},"arcnote":"Imogen's danger starts at the level of a confined princess at a hostile court, rises when Iachimo arrives and peaks for the first time in 2.2, when he stands over her bed. It climbs again once Posthumus's order to kill her is written (3.2) and reaches its height at Milford Haven (3.4), when she bares her breast to Pisanio's sword, and in 4.2, when she drinks the drug and wakes beside the headless body. Iachimo's deceit begins with the wager (1.4), grows with the slander in 1.6 and the bedroom in 2.2, and reaches full strength in 2.4 when Posthumus believes him. It then works on its own, through Posthumus's letters, until guilt weakens Iachimo in battle (5.2) and he confesses in 5.5, when both lines fall together.","iago":[["a1s4","Iachimo provokes Posthumus and wagers ten thousand ducats against his diamond that he can seduce Imogen."],["a1s4","Posthumus agrees and gives Iachimo a letter of introduction."],["a1s6","Iachimo tells Imogen that Posthumus is unfaithful in Rome and offers himself as her revenge."],["a1s6","Rejected, he claims he was testing her and asks her to keep his trunk."],["a2s2","From the trunk he notes her room and the mole on her breast and steals the bracelet."],["a2s4","In Rome he describes the room; Posthumus resists."],["a2s4","He shows the bracelet and describes the mole; Posthumus gives up the ring."],["a2s5","Posthumus turns against Imogen and all women."],["a3s2","Posthumus writes to Pisanio ordering Imogen's death, and lures her to Milford Haven."],["a3s4","Pisanio refuses to kill her and plans to send false proof of her death."],["a5s1","Posthumus, holding the bloody cloth, repents."],["a5s2","Posthumus, disguised, defeats Iachimo, whose guilt weakens him."],["a5s5","Imogen asks Iachimo how he got the ring."],["a5s5","Iachimo confesses the wager and the trunk."],["a5s5","Posthumus reveals himself and is reunited with Imogen."],["a5s5","Iachimo kneels and returns the ring and bracelet; Posthumus forgives him."]],"rel":{"1":[["IMOGEN","POSTHUMUS","love"],["CYMBELINE","POSTHUMUS","hate"],["QUEEN","IMOGEN","deceive"],["CLOTEN","IMOGEN","love"],["IMOGEN","CLOTEN","hate"],["IACHIMO","POSTHUMUS","deceive"],["PISANIO","POSTHUMUS","trust"],["QUEEN","PISANIO","deceive"],["IACHIMO","IMOGEN","deceive"]],"2":[["IACHIMO","IMOGEN","deceive"],["IACHIMO","POSTHUMUS","deceive"],["POSTHUMUS","IMOGEN","hate"],["CLOTEN","IMOGEN","love"],["IMOGEN","CLOTEN","hate"],["PHILARIO","POSTHUMUS","trust"]],"3":[["CYMBELINE","LUCIUS","hate"],["POSTHUMUS","IMOGEN","hate"],["PISANIO","IMOGEN","trust"],["IMOGEN","POSTHUMUS","love"],["CLOTEN","IMOGEN","hate"],["PISANIO","CLOTEN","deceive"],["BELARIUS","CYMBELINE","hate"],["GUIDERIUS","IMOGEN","love"],["ARVIRAGUS","IMOGEN","love"]],"4":[["GUIDERIUS","CLOTEN","hate"],["ARVIRAGUS","IMOGEN","love"],["LUCIUS","IMOGEN","trust"],["IMOGEN","PISANIO","hate"],["CYMBELINE","PISANIO","hate"],["BELARIUS","GUIDERIUS","love"]],"5":[["POSTHUMUS","IMOGEN","love"],["POSTHUMUS","IACHIMO","trust"],["CYMBELINE","BELARIUS","love"],["CYMBELINE","GUIDERIUS","love"],["IMOGEN","LUCIUS","trust"],["IACHIMO","POSTHUMUS","trust"]]},"context":[["Date","Probably written around 1609 to 1610. The astrologer Simon Forman described a performance he saw, probably in 1611, in notes made before his death that year. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623."],["A Folio tragedy","The Folio's editors placed Cymbeline last among the Tragedies, under the title 'The Tragedy of Cymbeline'. Later critics grouped it with Pericles, The Winter's Tale and The Tempest as 'romances' or tragicomedies: plays where loss and near-death end in reunion."],["Sources","The wager plot comes from a story in Boccaccio's Decameron (Day 2, Story 9), probably through a later English version. The British history, the tribute and Cymbeline (Cunobelinus) come from Holinshed's Chronicles, which also gave Shakespeare the story of a farmer and his two sons holding a lane against invaders."],["Ancient Britain","Cymbeline was a historical British king, Cunobelinus, who ruled in the time of Augustus. The play mixes ancient Britain, Augustan Rome and a Renaissance Italy of courtiers and wagers, and it ignores historical consistency on purpose."],["James I and Britain","James I, king of both England and Scotland from 1603, called himself King of Great Britain and promoted union. Some critics link the play's interest in 'Britain', in reunited royal children and in peace with Rome to his policies. Milford Haven, where Henry VII landed in 1485, was a site of Tudor and British legend."],["Tragicomedy","Beaumont and Fletcher's tragicomedies, such as Philaster, were popular at the same time and share disguise, threatened innocence and sudden reversals with Cymbeline. Tragicomedy brings characters close to death but not to it."],["The masque","Jupiter's descent on an eagle uses the stage machinery of court masques and of the indoor Blackfriars theatre, which the King's Men began using around 1609. The rhymed verse of the vision sets it apart from the rest of the play."],["Imogen or Innogen","Forman's notes and Holinshed have 'Innogen', and some modern editions use that name. The Folio prints 'Imogen', the name used here and in most performances."],["Boy actors","Imogen was played by a boy actor playing a woman who disguises herself as a boy. The jokes about her 'woman's part' and her fear of the sword use this layering."],["Performance","Cymbeline was popular in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, and the actress Ellen Terry made Imogen one of her famous roles. George Bernard Shaw rewrote the final act as 'Cymbeline Refinished' (1937). Modern productions often play up the final scene's improbabilities for comedy as well as joy."],["The final scene","Act 5 scene 5 contains some two dozen discoveries, each releasing the next. Shakespeare draws attention to its complexity: Cymbeline says there is no time to ask all the questions."]],"critics":{"love":[["William Hazlitt, 'Characters of Shakespear's Plays' (1817)","Paraphrased: Hazlitt praised Imogen as one of Shakespeare's most moving heroines and admired the play's tenderness, especially in her devotion to a husband who has wronged her."],["Janet Adelman, 'Suffocating Mothers' (1992)","Paraphrased: Adelman read the play through its anxieties about women and mothers, linking Posthumus's rage against 'the woman's part' to fears of female power, and saw the ending as restoring a family centred on the father."]],"trust":[["Harley Granville-Barker, 'Prefaces to Shakespeare' (1930)","Paraphrased: Granville-Barker argued that the play's sudden shifts and coincidences are deliberate art, and that Posthumus's quick credulity belongs to a story told in a heightened, self-aware style."]],"britain":[["Leah Marcus, 'Puzzling Shakespeare' (1988)","Paraphrased: Marcus read the play alongside James I's project to unite Britain, seeing the reunited family and the peace with Rome as an image of political union."],["Emrys Jones, 'Stuart Cymbeline' (1961)","Paraphrased: Jones argued that the play's choice of the reign of Cymbeline, when Christ was born and the world was at peace under Augustus, gave its ending a sense of universal peace that suited Jacobean ideals."]],"nature":[["Frank Kermode, 'Shakespeare's Language' (2000)","Paraphrased: Kermode found the late verse difficult and compressed, and saw the play's interest in hidden royal nature as part of its fairy-tale pattern."]],"gods":[["G. Wilson Knight, 'The Crown of Life' (1947)","Paraphrased: Knight treated Jupiter's descent as the heart of the play rather than an embarrassment, a moment where divine order breaks into human suffering."]],"deceit":[["Samuel Johnson, notes in his edition of Shakespeare (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson objected to the play's improbable plot and its mixing of times and manners, and thought its absurdities too obvious to need criticism."]]},"symbols":{"Ring and bracelet":"\\bring\\b|\\bdiamond\\b|\\bbracelet\\b|\\bjewel\\b","Clothes":"\\bgarments?\\b|\\bclothes\\b|\\bsuit\\b|\\bweeds\\b|\\bhabits?\\b","Britain":"\\bbritain\\b|\\bbritons?\\b","Rome":"\\brome\\b|\\broman\\w*|\\bcaesar\\b","Gods":"\\bgods?\\b|\\bjove\\b|\\bjupiter\\b|\\bheavens?\\b","Blood":"\\bblood\\w*|\\bbloody\\b"},"symbols2":[["The ring","Imogen's mother's diamond, staked by Posthumus in the wager and won by Iachimo. Its return in 5.5 starts the unravelling."],["The bracelet","Posthumus's 'manacle of love', stolen in the night and used as false proof of adultery."],["The trunk","Iachimo's hiding place in Imogen's bedroom: a Trojan horse that turns a gift into an invasion."],["Posthumus's clothes","Cloten wears them to commit violence; Imogen mistakes his body for her husband's because of them; Posthumus gives up his Italian clothes to fight as a peasant."],["The Queen's box","Meant as poison, given as medicine, it produces a sleep like death: the play's pattern of death that is not final."],["The bloody cloth","Pisanio's false proof of Imogen's death, which Posthumus keeps and which turns him to repentance."],["Jupiter's tablet","The riddling prophecy left on Posthumus's chest, solved only when everyone is reunited."],["The cedar","The tree in the prophecy: Cymbeline, whose lopped branches, his sons, grow again."]],"solo":{"198":1,"206":1,"293":1,"305":1,"307":1,"440":1,"464":1,"489":1,"558":1,"564":1,"589":1,"597":1,"601":1,"646":1,"724":1,"773":1,"808":1,"828":1,"832":1,"856":1,"869":1,"888":1,"907":1},"why":["Sets Posthumus up as the perfect man before his fall.","An orphan raised to wisdom: worth without birth.","Imogen's choice as the measure of her husband.","Plants the lost-princes plot in the first scene.","The stepmother's denial tells us exactly what she is.","Imogen's first words show her sharp judgment.","Introduces the language of jewels and value.","A vow of loyalty Posthumus keeps in body but not in trust.","Love so strong it would drink poison as ink.","The ring that becomes the wager's stake.","A gift whose condition turns bitterly ironic.","The bracelet that will become false evidence.","Cymbeline judges Posthumus by birth alone.","Royal blood imagined as poisoned by the match.","Parting felt as death, the first of many false deaths.","Love outweighs a king's anger.","Imogen ranks men by nature, not rank.","She claims merit adds lustre to the throne.","She turns her father's own choices against him.","A pastoral wish that the Welsh scenes will half grant.","Even Cloten's own attendant sides with Imogen.","Mocking wit at Cloten's expense.","Love's attention to the vanishing ship.","Dramatic irony: the danger in Italy is not its women.","Cymbeline as a killing wind on young love.","Posthumus's habit of boasting about his wife.","His confidence before the test.","Iachimo starts treating the woman as a jewel.","Posthumus insists she cannot be bought.","Iachimo's cynical view of wives as ponds for strange birds.","He pretends to test the husband, not the wife.","The fatal acceptance of the wager.","Cornelius's honest deceit protects Imogen.","The lie that sends the drug on its way to Imogen.","Pisanio's loyalty in a rhyming couplet.","Imogen's trap described in three lines.","Envy of the freedom of the low-born.","Iachimo admits she may be unique.","Iachimo steels himself for a lie.","Imogen prefers knowledge to suspicion.","Iachimo tries to use her royal pride.","Imogen's instant defence of her husband.","Britain's honour against Roman corruption.","The smooth recovery of a skilled liar.","Iachimo's praise of Posthumus is both flattery and truth.","The ass son of a clever mother.","A prayer at the very moment she is in danger.","Imogen prays for protection from tempters of the night.","Iachimo knows the story he is acting in.","Sleep as rehearsal for the false death of Act 4.","The detail that convinces Posthumus.","Ovid's rape story as a warning.","Iachimo's self-knowledge.","The aubade sung for a crude suitor.","Imogen's blunt honesty to Cloten.","Cloten's snobbery about Posthumus's upbringing.","The insult that drives Cloten's revenge.","Innocent words that come true.","Posthumus the patriot in exile.","He demands real proof.","He still resists the evidence of the room.","Posthumus's faith collapses into general cynicism.","The ugliest name, applied to the purest character.","His despair spreads to all humanity.","The purity he now thinks false.","He wants to tear out the 'woman's part' of himself.","The Queen's island speech: patriotism from a villain.","Caesar's defeat in Britain.","Cymbeline's case for independence.","Pisanio sees the poison of slander at work.","Faith in Imogen against his master's word.","Service is not blind obedience.","Imogen's joy at the letter that means to kill her.","The road to danger and to reunion.","The cave as a lesson in humility.","Safety in lowliness.","Belarius's fall from favour in one image.","Royal nature breaking through.","The princes' minds are born to rule.","Slander as the real murderer.","Imogen's bewildered innocence.","Her anger at men's vows.","Her heart emptied of the man she loved.","She begs for death like a lamb.","Imogen imagines life outside Britain.","Britain as a world apart.","The disguise begins.","Royal courage in a page's clothes.","The Queen's ruthless ambition.","Cloten's plan, the play's darkest threat.","Pisanio's prayer for Imogen.","Hardship makes courage.","She wishes for the truth without knowing it.","Rank in life, equality in death.","Instinctive love between siblings.","Belarius's theory of breeding.","Good things from small places.","Guiderius's fearless wit.","Royal gentleness in the boys.","Nature without nurture.","Belarius's explanation of the 'death'.","The flower speech of mourning.","Respect for rank even in an enemy.","The dirge's famous refrain.","Earth to earth.","Imogen wakes to the wrong body.","Clothes deceive her as they deceived Cloten.","She blames the one man who saved her.","Fidele means 'faithful'.","A motto for the play's design.","Pisanio's paradox of loyalty.","Chance as providence.","Blood will show itself.","Posthumus's remorse begins.","He forgives before he knows she is innocent.","He sees too late what Pisanio did.","Clothes used to hide worth, not fake it.","Iachimo's guilt weakens him.","The rallying cry in the lane.","A bitter rhyme on the victory.","Prison as freedom.","He offers his life for hers.","The ghosts accuse Jupiter.","Jupiter explains suffering as love.","The promise of the ending.","Posthumus puzzles over the riddle.","The Queen's confession.","Cymbeline learns too late.","What Imogen taught Iachimo.","Posthumus's self-condemnation.","Praise for Imogen too late.","One last blow before the reunion.","Joy as overwhelming as grief.","Imogen's gentle reproach.","The reunion in one line.","Belarius reveals royal blood.","Wrong drove a loyal man to treason.","The princes praised by their thief-father.","Imogen gladly loses a kingdom.","The lovers reunited.","Posthumus forgives Iachimo.","Pardon for all.","The prophecy explained.","Peace on Rome's terms.","Providence behind the plot.","The closing couplet."],"insults":[{"id":"39","f":"Thou basest thing, avoid","who":"Cymbeline","n":"The King attacks Posthumus's low birth."},{"id":"43","f":"O disloyal thing","who":"Cymbeline","n":"He calls his daughter a 'thing', not a person."},{"id":"48","f":"did avoid a puttock","who":"Imogen","n":"Cloten as a scavenging bird."},{"id":"51","f":"O thou vile one!","who":"Cymbeline","n":"A father's fury at a disobedient daughter."},{"id":"83","f":"Puppies!","who":"Second Lord","n":"Cloten and Posthumus's fight dismissed."},{"id":"88","f":"her beauty and her brain go not together","who":"First Lord","n":"Flattery of Cloten at Imogen's expense."},{"id":"266","f":"a whoreson jackanapes","who":"Cloten","n":"Cloten's crude abuse of a man who rebuked his swearing."},{"id":"274","f":"You are cock and capon too","who":"Second Lord","n":"Cloten as a cockerel and a castrated fool."},{"id":"284","f":"A banish'd rascal","who":"Cloten","n":"Cloten sneers at Posthumus."},{"id":"293","f":"Should yield the world this ass!","who":"Second Lord","n":"The lord's private verdict on Cloten."},{"id":"354","f":"Fools are not mad folks.","who":"Imogen","n":"Imogen calls Cloten a fool to his face."},{"id":"357","f":"A hilding for a livery","who":"Cloten","n":"Posthumus as a servant fit for a uniform."},{"id":"358","f":"Profane fellow!","who":"Imogen","n":"Imogen says Cloten is fit only to be an under-hangman."},{"id":"359","f":"The south fog rot him!","who":"Cloten","n":"A curse on Posthumus."},{"id":"419","f":"O, above measure false!","who":"Posthumus","n":"Posthumus condemns Imogen."},{"id":"440","f":"This yellow Iachimo","who":"Posthumus","n":"Iachimo as sallow or jealous."},{"id":"499","f":"Some jay of Italy","who":"Imogen","n":"Imogen imagines an Italian whore."},{"id":"568","f":"Close villain","who":"Cloten","n":"Cloten threatens Pisanio."},{"id":"683","f":"Some villain mountaineers?","who":"Cloten","n":"Cloten despises the Welsh hill people."},{"id":"688","f":"No, nor thy tailor, rascal","who":"Guiderius","n":"Guiderius mocks Cloten's faith in clothes."},{"id":"694","f":"Cloten, thou double villain","who":"Guiderius","n":"Cloten's name means nothing in Wales."},{"id":"726","f":"Cloten's clotpoll","who":"Guiderius","n":"A pun on Cloten's name: blockhead."},{"id":"773","f":"that irregulous devil, Cloten","who":"Imogen","n":"Imogen curses Cloten over his own body."},{"id":"927","f":"O most delicate fiend!","who":"Cymbeline","n":"The King on his dead Queen."},{"id":"970","f":"Italian fiend!","who":"Posthumus","n":"Posthumus turns on Iachimo, then on himself."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"27","f":"thither write, my queen","n":"Posthumus addresses Imogen as 'thou' and 'my queen', the intimacy of husband and wife."},{"id":"39","f":"Thou basest thing","n":"Cymbeline's 'thou' to Posthumus is contempt from a king to a subject."},{"id":"52","f":"It is your fault that I have lov'd Posthumus.","n":"Imogen keeps the respectful 'you' to her father even while defying him."},{"id":"248","f":"Thou wrong'st a gentleman","n":"Imogen drops to an angry 'thou' when Iachimo insults Posthumus."},{"id":"252","f":"take my pow'r i' th' court for yours","n":"She returns to polite 'you' once he apologises."},{"id":"348","f":"You lay out too much pains","n":"Imogen's cold, formal 'you' to Cloten keeps him at a distance."},{"id":"686","f":"What art thou?","n":"Guiderius 'thous' a prince: in Wales rank means nothing."},{"id":"990","f":"Why did you throw your wedded lady from you?","n":"Imogen's gentle 'you' to her husband at the reunion."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Imogen gives in and marries Cloten","Posthumus is banished; they part with ring and bracelet"],"a1s2":["Cloten challenges Posthumus again","Cloten boasts and is mocked behind his back"],"a1s3":["Posthumus secretly returns","Imogen mourns his departure"],"a1s4":["Posthumus refuses to discuss his wife","Posthumus bets his ring on Imogen's chastity"],"a1s5":["The Queen poisons Imogen at once","The doctor secretly gives the Queen a harmless drug"],"a1s6":["Iachimo seduces Imogen","Imogen rejects him; he gets his trunk into her room"],"a2s1":["Cloten meets and fights Iachimo","Cloten plans to gamble with the Italian"],"a2s2":["Iachimo is discovered in the bedroom","Iachimo gathers false evidence while she sleeps"],"a2s3":["Imogen is won by the music","Imogen insults Cloten and misses her bracelet"],"a2s4":["Posthumus sees through Iachimo","Posthumus believes him and gives up the ring"],"a2s5":["Posthumus calms down","Posthumus rages against all women"],"a3s1":["Cymbeline pays the tribute","Britain refuses and Rome declares war"],"a3s2":["Pisanio obeys his master","Pisanio doubts the order; Imogen sets out for Milford"],"a3s3":["The princes learn who they are","Belarius tells only the audience"],"a3s4":["Pisanio kills Imogen","Pisanio disguises her as a boy"],"a3s5":["Cloten gives up","Cloten follows in Posthumus's clothes"],"a3s6":["The cave dwellers attack the stranger","They welcome Fidele as a brother"],"a3s7":["Rome makes peace","Rome raises more troops"],"a4s1":["Cloten finds Imogen","Cloten boasts and plans violence"],"a4s2":["Cloten kills Guiderius","Guiderius kills Cloten; Imogen wakes beside the body"],"a4s3":["Cymbeline learns the truth","The court falls into disorder as the Romans land"],"a4s4":["Belarius keeps the boys hidden","They all go to fight for Britain"],"a5s1":["Posthumus fights for Rome","Posthumus changes sides in disguise"],"a5s2":["Rome wins easily","The Britons are saved by an old man and two boys"],"a5s3":["Posthumus claims the victory","Posthumus gives himself up as a Roman"],"a5s4":["Posthumus is hanged","The gods intervene and he is summoned to the King"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Posthumus and Cloten","pts":["Both claim Imogen; one is chosen, the other forced on her.","Cloten has birth and no worth; Posthumus worth and no birth.","Cloten wears Posthumus's clothes to destroy; Posthumus changes clothes to sacrifice himself.","The headless body in Posthumus's clothes makes them interchangeable for one terrible moment."],"ids":[360,646]},{"t":"Iachimo and Pisanio","pts":["Both are Italians in a British story (Pisanio's name is Italian).","Iachimo betrays everyone for a bet; Pisanio betrays his master's order to stay loyal.","Iachimo's 'proof' is false; so is Pisanio's bloody cloth, but it saves a life."],"ids":[307,808]},{"t":"The court and the cave","pts":["The court is ruled by a poisoner; the cave by an honest outlaw.","Courtiers flatter; the princes speak plainly.","Royal nature shows more clearly in the cave than at court."],"ids":[485,489]},{"t":"Imogen and the Queen","pts":["Stepmother and stepdaughter: one plots, one suffers.","The Queen's patriotism is political; Imogen imagines a world beyond Britain.","Both handle the same box: one as poison, one as medicine."],"ids":[196,662]},{"t":"Guiderius and Arviragus","pts":["Guiderius is bold and blunt; Arviragus gentle and poetic.","Guiderius kills Cloten; Arviragus mourns Fidele with flowers.","Both love Fidele at first sight."],"ids":[711,740]}],"analysis":{"IMOGEN":["Imogen is the play's centre and, with about a fifth of its lines, its largest part. She chooses her husband for his worth against her father's will, and she defends the choice brilliantly: 'I chose an eagle, And did avoid a puttock.' At court she sees through the Queen's 'dissembling courtesy', handles Cloten with crushing frankness and sends Iachimo packing.","Her faith in Posthumus is total. When Iachimo slanders him she refuses to believe it; when the slander is reversed she believes the reversal just as fast. She is generous to a fault, and the play exploits that: she takes in the trunk because her husband has a share in it.","Faced with Posthumus's letter she moves from bewilderment ('False to his bed? What is it to be false?') to anger at men in general and then to a wish to die. Disguised as Fidele she shows courage, humour and resilience, and her instinctive bond with the brothers shows her royal nature.","Waking beside the headless body she grieves for the wrong man in one of Shakespeare's strangest scenes. Yet she keeps going, takes service with Lucius and, in the final scene, sets the whole chain of discoveries moving by asking about the ring. Her first words to her husband are a gentle reproach, not an accusation."],"POSTHUMUS":["Posthumus is praised by everyone before we meet him, and the play keeps that reputation in view to the end. Born an orphan and raised at court, he is all worth and no rank, the reverse of Cloten.","His fault is a kind of pride in his wife. He has boasted of her before, in France, and in Rome he lets Iachimo turn her into a bet. He resists the description of the bedroom but breaks at the bracelet and the mole, and his soliloquy against women is violent and sweeping.","What redeems him is that he repents before he knows the truth. With the bloody cloth in his hand he says that a husband should forgive a wife's straying, changes into a peasant's clothes and fights for her country. In prison he offers his life for hers.","His final act is forgiveness: 'The pow'r that I have on you is to spare you.' His striking of the unrecognised Imogen in 5.5 is a reminder that his violence never quite leaves him until the end."],"IACHIMO":["Iachimo is a gambler and an improviser. He doubts every woman's virtue as a matter of principle, and the wager is his way of proving the world as cynical as he is.","His first plan fails, and he recovers within a line, turning slander into praise. The trunk scene shows him at his most dangerous and most theatrical: he compares himself to Tarquin, but takes only notes.","Guilt catches up with him in battle, where he is beaten by a man he takes for a peasant. His confession in 5.5 is long, ornate and self-dramatising, but it is complete, and he asks to die. He is forgiven, which makes him unlike Iago, the villain he resembles."],"CYMBELINE":["Cymbeline is a king who has lost control of his family. He banished a loyal soldier, lost his sons, banishes his daughter's husband and is ruled by his wife.","He is not wicked, but weak: he trusts the Queen because 'she was beautiful'. In war he is captured and has to be rescued by men he does not know.","In the last scene he is a father recovering children rather than a king, and his last decision, paying the tribute after winning, shows him choosing peace over pride."],"CLOTEN":["Cloten is a comic figure who becomes a menace. He swears, gambles, brags about his rank and is mocked by his own attendant.","Rejected by Imogen, he turns her insult about Posthumus's garment into a plan to kill Posthumus and rape her while wearing his clothes. The plan shows how dangerous a fool with power can be.","In Wales his rank means nothing; he dies because he insults a stranger. The princely funeral he gets, and Imogen's mistake over his body, make his death both grotesque and strangely moving."],"QUEEN":["The Queen is a fairy-tale stepmother: poisons, flowers and smiling lies. She wants Cloten on the throne and will kill to get it.","Yet she also speaks the play's most stirring patriotic speech, on Britain's island defences. Shakespeare gives the cause of independence to its villain.","She never appears after Act 3. Her death, reported with her confession, frees Cymbeline to make peace."],"BELARIUS":["Belarius is a wronged loyal servant who became a traitor: banished on false evidence, he stole the King's sons for revenge.","He has raised them well, praising the simple, honest life of the mountains against the court, while watching their royal nature show through.","He holds the lane with them in battle and, in the end, gives them back at the risk of his own life."]},"motifs":[["Jewels and value","The ring, the bracelet, the 'jewel in the world': people are valued like precious stones, and the wager treats a wife as a prize.",[6,9,11,27,28,29]],["Clothes","Cloten's 'meanest garment', Posthumus's borrowed clothes, Imogen's boy's disguise and Posthumus's peasant's dress: clothes conceal, reveal and mislead.",[56,89,86,106,116]],["Birth and nature","Royal blood shows in the princes; low birth hides worth in Posthumus; high birth hides folly in Cloten.",[1,16,77,95,99,112]],["Sleep and false death","Imogen sleeps under Iachimo's eyes, drinks a sleeping drug and is mourned alive; Posthumus sleeps in prison and wakes to a vision.",[49,100,103,105]],["Birds","Eagles and puttocks, the Arabian bird, the eagle of Jupiter and the Roman eagle that vanishes into the sun.",[16,37,75,85]],["The gods","Prayers, oaths and finally Jupiter himself: the play suggests a design behind its accidents.",[47,111,123,124,144]]],"bigq":[["Is Posthumus worthy of Imogen?",["Everyone praises him at the start","He enters a wager on her chastity and believes false proof quickly","He orders her murder","He repents before he knows the truth and offers his life","He forgives Iachimo"],[0,31,61,114,140]],["Is Cymbeline a tragedy?",["The Folio places it among the Tragedies","Cloten and the Queen die, and Imogen comes close","But the ending reunites and pardons almost everyone","Later critics call it a romance or tragicomedy"],[103,123,141,145]],["Does nature beat nurture?",["The princes behave like princes in a cave","Cloten is royal but base","Posthumus has worth without birth","Belarius says 'Cowards father cowards'"],[77,78,95,99,112]],["Who is to blame for Imogen's suffering?",["Iachimo's lies","Posthumus's credulity and pride","The Queen's plots","Cymbeline's weakness"],[31,50,61,88,127]],["What does the play say about Britain and Rome?",["The Queen and Cloten refuse the tribute in proud speeches","Britain wins the battle","Cymbeline chooses to pay tribute anyway","The ending unites Roman and British flags"],[66,68,84,143,145]],["How much does the play laugh at itself?",["Cloten's death and the headless body mix horror and farce","The final scene piles revelation on revelation","Cymbeline admits there is no time for all the questions","The prophecy's Latin pun is deliberately strained"],[105,106,139,142]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Imogen?",[5,16,41,80,87,133]],["How does Shakespeare present Posthumus's jealousy and repentance?",[26,31,61,65,114,129]],["How does Shakespeare present deception in the play?",[32,43,50,79,86,131]],["How does Shakespeare present the theme of birth and nature?",[1,12,77,95,99,135]],["How does Shakespeare present the ending as a resolution?",[123,134,138,140,141,145]],["How does Shakespeare present Britain?",[66,68,84,85,118,143]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (imagery, soliloquy, aside, dramatic irony, disguise, structure) and its effect","Linked to context (romance, tragicomedy, James I, the masque) where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Posthumus and the ending","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[16,"Shakespeare presents Imogen as a woman who judges by worth, not rank.","'I chose an eagle, And did avoid a puttock' turns a quarrel about marriage into a hierarchy of birds.","The eagle is the king of birds, so she claims nobility for Posthumus that birth denied him.","The 'puttock', a scavenger, is a crushing image of Cloten.","Her wit under her father's fury shows the courage she will need in Wales."],[65,"Shakespeare presents Posthumus's jealousy as a sickness that spreads from one woman to all.","'Could I find out The woman's part in me!' shows him turning hatred on himself.","The soliloquy lists every vice as 'hers', a catalogue that becomes absurd in its sweep.","The audience knows Imogen is innocent, so the speech exposes him, not her.","His later repentance answers this speech point by point."],[77,"Shakespeare presents royal nature as something that cannot be hidden.","Belarius's 'sparks of nature' suggests fire breaking out of ashes.","The princes act out his war stories and long for the world.","The idea supports the play's ending, where blood is recognised.","Yet the same play shows Cloten, royal by marriage, as base."],[103,"Shakespeare presents death as a leveller in the dirge.","'Golden lads and girls all must, As chimney-sweepers, come to dust' joins rich and poor.","The song is spoken over a girl who is not dead, beside a prince who is.","The audience's knowledge makes the moment both moving and ironic.","It prepares for an ending where the 'dead' return."],[114,"Shakespeare presents repentance in Posthumus before any proof of innocence.","He says husbands who killed wives 'For wrying but a little' would murder their betters.","He forgives Imogen for a fault he still believes she committed.","This moral turn makes his reunion with her deserved.","It contrasts with Othello, who learns the truth only after the murder."],[140,"Shakespeare ends the wager plot with forgiveness rather than revenge.","'The pow'r that I have on you is to spare you' redefines power as mercy.","Posthumus had threatened to fight Iachimo; now he pardons him.","Cymbeline copies him: 'Pardon's the word to all.'","The play turns from the logic of tragedy to that of romance."]],"lang":{"un":[["48","I chose an eagle, And did avoid a puttock.","I picked a noble husband and avoided a scavenger."],["145","strange fowl light upon neighbouring ponds.","Other men visit wives who are not theirs."],["305","O sleep, thou ape of death, lie dull upon her!","Sleep, you imitation of death, lie heavily on her."],["519","In a great pool a swan's nest.","Britain is like a nest in the middle of a great lake: part of the world but apart from it."],["808","Wherein I am false I am honest; not true, to be true.","When I disobey I am being loyal."],["991","Hang there like fruit, my soul, Till the tree die!","Hold on to me as fruit hangs on a tree, until I die."]],"pun":[["726","I have sent Cloten's clotpoll down the stream","clotpoll","pun","Clotpoll means blockhead; it also echoes Cloten's name."],["318","If you can penetrate her with your fingering, so.","penetrate","double meaning","Music reaching her heart, and a crude sexual sense."],["646","a woman's fitness comes by fits.","fit","pun","Clothes that fit, and a woman's moods or 'fits'."],["791","Thy name well fits thy faith, thy faith thy name.","faith","name","Fidele means faithful."],["1048","Being Leo-natus, doth import so much.","Leo-natus","name","Leo-natus: lion-born, the lion's whelp."],["1048","We term it 'mulier'","mulier","false etymology","Latin 'mulier' (woman) said to come from 'mollis aer' (tender air)."]],"st":[["48","I chose an eagle, And did avoid a puttock.",[["eagle","King of birds: nobility."],["puttock","A kite: a scavenger."]]],["440","As chaste as unsunn'd snow.",[["chaste","Pure."],["unsunn'd","Untouched by the sun, as she was untouched by men."]]],["991","Hang there like fruit, my soul, Till the tree die!",[["fruit","Imogen clinging to him."],["tree","Posthumus himself, alive until death."]]]]},"dir":[["305","Our Tarquin thus Did softly press the rushes","How menacing should Iachimo be?",[["Very: he looms over the bed","The audience fears rape."],["Comic: a voyeur taking notes","His purpose is evidence, not violence."],["Torn: he is moved by her beauty","He nearly abandons the plan."]]],["440","Is there no way for men to be, but women Must be half-workers?","How should Posthumus play his soliloquy?",[["Raging and violent","Jealousy as madness."],["Bitter and cold","A man settling into cynicism."],["Close to tears","Grief under the anger."]]],["503","The lamp entreats the butcher.","How should Imogen offer her breast to the sword?",[["Defiantly","She dares Pisanio to obey."],["Calmly","She wants death."],["Desperately","She cannot bear to live."]]],["711","I am perfect what: cut off one Cloten's head,","How should Guiderius enter with the head?",[["Cheerfully","He feels no guilt."],["Defiantly","He expects anger from Belarius."],["Grimly","He knows the danger it brings."]]],["773","These flow'rs are like the pleasures of the world;","How should Imogen's speech over the body be staged?",[["As pure tragedy","The audience shares her grief."],["With dark comedy","The audience knows it is Cloten."],["Both at once","Laughter and tears together."]]],["972","Shall's have a play of this? Thou scornful page, There lies thy part.","How violent is Posthumus's blow?",[["A shocking strike","His violence returns one last time."],["A careless push","He barely notices the page."],["A blow he regrets at once","The reunion follows instantly."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["1033","1053"],["755","1042"],["134","142","151"],["452","519","1053"],["519","1048","1053"],["808","1033","1042"],["884","1044"],["26","48","356"],["522","52","927"],["497","519","970"],["1033","1042","1053"]],"cr":{"love":[["48","497","991"],["440","52","26"]],"trust":[["134","401","411"]],"britain":[["519","1048","1053"],["1048","1052","1053"]],"nature":[["698","724","1007"]],"gods":[["876","884","1052"]],"deceit":[["196","293","1033"]]}}};
const EXT=[["facts","Key facts","The play at a glance: setting, conflict, climax"],["analysis","Character analysis","The main characters in depth, with their key quotes"],["motifs","Motifs and symbols","Jewels, clothes, birth, false death, birds, gods"],["bigq","Big questions","Debates to have about the play, with points to make"],["timeline","Plot timeline","30 key moments, in order"],["map","Character map","Who loves, trusts, hates or deceives whom, act by act"],["iago","The wager plot","Each step of Iachimo's bet, from Rome to the confession, ticked as you read"],["arcs","Danger and deceit","Imogen's danger against Iachimo's deceit, scene by scene"],["context","Context","Boccaccio, Holinshed, ancient Britain, James I, tragicomedy, the masque"],["critics","Critics' views","What critics have said, by theme"],["devices","Imagery","Jewels, clothes, nature, myth, sleep, the body"],["verse","Verse and prose","Beats, prose, shared lines and rhymes, with a verse lab"],["director","Director's choices","Stage six key moments, from the trunk to the blow"],["symbols","Symbol tracker","Every ring, garment, Briton, Roman, god and drop of blood"],["contrast","Contrast pairs","Characters set side by side"],["insults","Insults and oaths","Who abuses whom, and why it matters"],["pronouns","Thou and you","What a pronoun reveals about a relationship"],["glossary","Word list A to Z","Every underlined word and where it's used"],["compare","Compare two quotes","Side by side, with shared themes and essay prompts"]];
